# Design System Specification

> This is the single source of truth for designing and building components in this system.
> **Before building any new component**, read this file completely, then examine the most-recently-built component's CSS + render function as a working template.
> When the user says "read the rules," always consult this file and apply every principle below.

---

## Part 1 — Philosophy & Standing Orders

### 1.1 Design stance
This is a **designer-forward, highly customizable** design system. Every component must look intentional at rest and feel alive on interaction. The system prioritizes:
- **Generous radius** — corners are soft and modern (radius-m = 16 px)
- **Quiet at rest, responsive on interaction** — borders are light, fills are subtle; color intensifies only through hover → focus → pressed escalation
- **Token-driven theming** — a customer picks a brand color and the entire system adapts. No hardcoded hex, no `filter: brightness()` for interactive states
- **Perceptual consistency** — every hover is one palette stop from rest, every focus ring uses the same two-layer formula, every pressed state uses the same brightness filter

### 1.2 Research requirements
Always research and compare against: **Primer** (GitHub), **Apple HIG**, **Material Design 3**, **Radix/shadcn/ui**, **Atlassian Design System**, **Linear**. Justify non-obvious decisions with rationale from these systems.

### 1.3 User preferences (non-negotiable)
These were established through iterative review and must be respected in all future work:

1. **No `filter: brightness()` for hover states.** Every hover color must be a real semantic token mapping to a specific palette stop. `brightness(.92)` is permitted ONLY for momentary pressed/active feedback.
2. **Gradual escalation.** Rest → hover → focus/selected → pressed must always be visually distinct steps. Never jump from rest directly to focus-strength on hover.
3. **Lighter validation at rest.** Error/success borders use the 200-shade `feedback` token at rest (not the 500-shade `border` token). Background gets a subtle tint. The 500-shade appears only on hover/focus.
4. **Inline icons match label color** in three rules, applied per family:
   - **Rule A** (icon = label color): Button, Badge, Nav (active state at full opacity, rest at 0.7), Checkbox-row, Radio-row, Switch-row leading icons. Industry parallel: Apple HIG settings rows, Material list-items, shadcn.
   - **Rule B** (icon = `text-tertiary` chrome): Input/Select leading icon, Input affix, Input action (rest), Select chevron. Industry parallel: shadcn Input/Select, Material outlined fields. Justification: icons inside fields categorize the *field*, not the *content* — they belong to the chrome, not the data the field holds. The trailing action icon goes `tertiary → primary` on hover (chrome → affordance) — the only sanctioned hover foreground change.
   - **Rule C** (icon = brand): Select panel selected checkmark. Industry parallel: shadcn `CheckIcon`, Material menu, Apple menu. Justification: icon is a state indicator (this row is the chosen value), brand color is the state.
5. **Checked controls darken on hover**, never lighten. `brightness(1.1)` is banned — it washes out brand fills.
6. **Radio buttons use solid fill when checked** (matching checkbox), not the old ring-and-dot style.
7. **Switch on = green** (`feedback/success/icon`), switch off = `border/default`. Focus ring adapts to green when the switch is on.
8. **`ICON_CIRCLE` is the universal neutral placeholder** (Phosphor `circle`, Regular weight). Use it anywhere an icon slot exists but the specific glyph isn't the subject of the demo — Buttons, Nav items, Input/Select leading icon, Dropdown items, Checkbox/Radio/Switch row leading icons, Badge with icon, **and Toast tone marker** (across all tones — neutral, info, success, warning, danger; tone is conveyed by color, not by the glyph). Reads as truly neutral, no semantic, no implied affordance, one mental model across the entire system. `ICON_INFO` (Phosphor `info`) remains available as an opt-in for any consumer that wants to express explicit "information" semantics (`<Toast icon={ICON_INFO} ...>`), but is never the default. Never use `ICON_STAR` as a placeholder (implies favoriting).
9. **No native resize handle on textareas.** The component is still resizable via cursor; the handle is hidden.
10. **Horizontal fieldset gap = `space-l` (16 px).** Vertical fieldset gap = `space-3xs` (2 px) because row height provides implicit separation. Horizontal items sit side-by-side at the same height with no implicit separation, so they need an explicit, visibly distinct gap — `space-l` (16) matches the same "siblings of the same kind in a form" gap used for vertical Input → Input stacks (§6.5), keeping the rule axis-agnostic: form siblings share `space-l` regardless of orientation. Earlier rev used `space-m` (12) — the form-row tier from §6.5 which is calibrated for *different things in a row* (Input + Select); selection options are the *same thing repeated*, which needs slightly more breathing to read as a list rather than a packed cluster.
11. **No decorative effects that demand 1-component-only tokens.** Solid buttons do NOT carry a "top-light sheen" (the inset white highlight effect). Linear, shadcn, and modern Apple HIG omit it; M3 omits it. Adding it would require a 1-off `--btn-sheen-light/dark` token that no other component could reasonably consume. The button identity is carried by fill, border, and shadow tokens — that's enough.
12. **Math-first, parameterizable system.** Every numeric value in component CSS MUST trace back to either (a) a base ramp token (`var(--space-*)`, `var(--radius-*)`, `var(--control-*)`, `var(--icon-*)`, `var(--{font/lh-tier})`, color tokens, motion tokens, shadow tokens, z-layer tokens) OR (b) a `calc()` formula composed of base ramp tokens (e.g., `calc((var(--btn-h) − var(--btn-icon))/2)` for optical centering, `calc(var(--outer-rd) − var(--inset))` for concentric corners). **Literal pixel values, magic numbers, and per-component "custom overrides" are forbidden** unless explicitly justified. Designer preferences ("more rounded," "denser," "different brand color") are encoded by adjusting **base tokens**, not by overriding individual components — change `--radius-m` from 16 to 18 once and every consumer (Button M, Input M, RadioCard, Toast, …) reflows through the cascade with no per-component edits. The system's job is to make this propagation **safe**: changing one base token must not break the nested-radius rule (§2.1.2), the padding formula (§6.1.2), or the contrast contracts (§3.3.1). When a designer's preference can't be encoded as a ramp shift (e.g., "Dialog should be 440px wide"), pin the value to a documented container ramp or call it out explicitly as a per-component constant with rationale — but never let a literal value appear in CSS without a documented path back to either a ramp or a formula. See Part 1.4 for the formal parameterizability contract (the base ramps that are "the inputs," the derivation formulas that are "the rules," and the customization levers a customer can pull).
13. **Components default to weight 400 (Regular).** The "label tier" at weight 500 (Medium) was removed in May 2026. Every text node inside a `.ds-*` component class — control text, titles, labels, captions, group headings, active/selected rows — uses weight 400. Hierarchy is carried by **size + color + surface**, never by weight. Weight 500 is reserved for cases that satisfy the three-part necessity test in §5.1.1 (must read as locally dominant AND cannot scale up in size AND `text/primary` cannot carry the contrast); when in doubt, default to 400. Weight 600 (DM Sans) is exclusive to the Display tier (page-level h1–h4 in host chrome — `.h-axis`, `.h-group`, `.h-section`, `.h-item`, `.pal-title`, `.variant-label`) and never appears inside components. `<strong>` / `<b>` HTML elements may step up for inline prose emphasis — that's a content-author tool, not a component-author tool. The rationale is in §5.1.1: this system is mono-everywhere (DM Mono), and weight contrast on monospaced fonts reads weaker than on proportional fonts — modern mono-heavy UIs (Vercel terminal, Berkeley Mono kits, Linear, Material 3 Display) all skew uniform-weight for this reason.

