### 1.4 Parameterizability contract (the math-first system)

The system is two layers: **base tokens** (the inputs, chosen by taste — small, finite) and **derived values** (the outputs, computed by formula — unbounded). Standing Rule #12 enforces that **everything in component CSS is one of these two layers, nothing else**.

#### 1.4.1 The base ramps (the inputs — what a designer is allowed to change)

These are the only places taste lives. Edit one of these and the system reflows:

| Ramp | Where defined | What it controls | Cascade reach |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Spacing** | §2.1 | All padding, gap, margin | Every component |
| **Radius** | §2.1.1 | All `border-radius` | Every rounded surface (Buttons, Fields, Cards, Dialogs, Panels, Badges, Kbd, …) |
| **Control height** | §2.3 | Button/Input/Select/Textarea heights and (via the tier table) padding/icon/font | Every form control + Nav |
| **Icon** | §2.3 | Inline icon glyph size in controls | Every icon wrapper |
| **Type** | §5.1.1 | All `font-size`/`line-height` pairs | Every text node |
| **Color (palette + semantics)** | §3 | All fills, borders, text colors | Every visible surface |
| **Motion** | §4.3 | Transition durations + easings | Every interactive component |
| **Elevation (shadow)** | §4.6 | All `box-shadow` values | Every floating/elevated surface |
| **Z-layer** | §4.7 | Stacking order | Every overlay |

A consumer / designer customizing the system pulls **only** these levers. They never touch component CSS.

#### 1.4.2 The derivation formulas (the rules — what is computed, never chosen)

| Formula | Where defined | What it computes |
|---|---|---|
| **Optical centering** `padding = (container-h − content-h) / 2` | §6.1, §6.1.1 | Padding around icons inside fixed-height controls (Button, Badge, Input, Select, Nav) |
| **Per-edge override (variable-height containers)** `padding-x-override = (container-h − control-h) / 2` | §6.1.2 (corrected) | Padding around hard-edged sub-elements anchoring an edge of a variable-height surface (Tooltip trailing kbd; legacy — Toast/Banner now use uniform 8/8 surface pad + square icon wrappers per §6.1.5 uniform-8/8 family rule) |
| **Concentric corners** `inner_radius = outer_radius − inset` (strict) or "minimize spread" (soft) | §2.1.2 | Border-radius of any rounded element nested inside another rounded surface |
| **Tier-matched icon size** `icon-tier = control-tier` | §2.3 | Icon glyph size in any sized control |
| **Tier-matched type** `font/lh-tier = control-tier` | §2.3, §5.1.1 | Text size in any sized control |
| **Hover step** `hover = rest ± 50 stops` (chromatic) or `+1 palette stop` (neutral) | §3.2, §3.3.3 | Hover background/border on every interactive surface |
| **Chromatic foreground** `text/{role}` = "lowest stop that passes WCAG AA on white AND on role/50" | §3.3.1 | Text color for any chromatic role (danger/warning/success/info/brand foreground) |
| **Stroke job** `stroke = affordance | semantic | definition` | §3.5 | Border choice for any contained surface |

Every component CSS rule that sets a numeric value must compose tokens from §1.4.1 OR apply a formula from §1.4.2. A `calc()` that combines these is fine. A magic number is not.

#### 1.4.3 The customization levers (what a consumer can pull, and what reflows)

| Lever | Designer changes | Components that reflow automatically |
|---|---|---|
| **Re-tonalize** (rebrand) | Brand color hue → palette regenerates via OKLCH (§3) | Every chromatic surface (focus rings, brand buttons, brand text, selected states) |
| **Re-density** | Control height ramp (e.g., compact mode: M from 40 → 32) | All form controls, Nav, Dialog footer, Toast actions |
| **Re-round** | Radius ramp (e.g., flatter: scale all by 0.5) | Every rounded surface; nested-radius math (§2.1.2) revalidates because formulas are relative, not absolute |
| **Re-typography** | Font family OR tier sizes | Every text node (controls inherit via tier-match formula) |
| **Re-spacing** | Spacing ramp (e.g., more breathing room: scale all by 1.5) | Every padding/gap/margin |

The contract: pulling **any** of these levers must not require editing component CSS. The cascade and the formulas do all the work. If a lever change breaks a component, the component had drift — fix the component to use formulas, don't paper over with overrides.

#### 1.4.4 What's allowed in component CSS (the audit-ready rule)

A numeric value in component CSS is **allowed** only if it matches one of these patterns:

```
/* Pattern 1: base ramp token (with sensible fallback) */
padding: var(--space-s, 8px);
border-radius: var(--radius-m, 16px);
height: var(--control-m, 40px);

/* Pattern 2: formula composing base tokens */
padding-left: calc((var(--btn-h) - var(--btn-icon)) / 2);
border-radius: calc(var(--dlg-rd) - var(--dlg-px));

/* Pattern 3: percentage / 100% / 50% / 9999px (literal-but-semantic) */
border-radius: 50%; /* fully round — distinct from the radius ramp by contract */
width: 100%;
```

A numeric value is **forbidden** unless it falls into Pattern 1, 2, or 3:
- ❌ `padding: 5px` (off-ramp, no formula) — drift.
- ❌ `border-radius: 13px` (off-ramp, no formula) — drift.
- ❌ `min-width: 200px` (no formula, no token) — drift unless documented as per-component constant (§1.4.5).

#### 1.4.5 Sanctioned per-component constants (the exceptions that prove the rule)

Some values are necessarily judgment calls — container widths, max content widths — that don't naturally fit a single ramp. These are the documented exceptions; they are NOT drift, but each must be explicitly listed here:

| Value | Component | Rationale | Could it become a ramp? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Dialog max-width: 440px` | §9.6 Dialog | Sits between shadcn 425 / Linear 480; "comfortable confirmation dialog" | Yes — a future "container width ramp" `[narrow=320, default=440, wide=640, full=100%]` would absorb this |
| `Tooltip max-width: 240px` | §9.5 Tooltip | "Comfortable reading width for tooltip prose" — ~30 char-widths at 12/16 mono | Yes — same container ramp |
| `Toast min-width: 280px / max-width: 360px` | §9.8 Toast | "Stackable notification width range" | Yes — same container ramp |
| `Toast min-height: --control-l (40px)` | §9.8 Toast | Floor preserves visual presence; references `--control-l` rather than inlining 40px so it tracks the L tier | Already token-driven, just pinned to L tier |
| `.comp-frame__preview min-height: 300px` | Host chrome (docs preview shell) | "Comfortable preview area" floor — keeps the visible frame at ~398px tall (300 + 48×2 padding + 2 border) even for single-control demos. Earlier 160 (4× --control-l) and 240 (6× --control-l) floors both let smaller demos look cramped; 400 was briefly tried but read as over-tall against the system's denser identity. | Yes — a "preview-frame size" ramp (320 / 400 / 480) would absorb this, or one could shift to 320 (4× --space-3xl 80) for ramp purity. |
| `Composer L --cmp-text-px: 10px` | §9.28 Composer | Tiered H-padding ramp at S/M/L is 6/8/10 — case-B asymmetric breathing requires H = V + 2 at every tier; with V at 4/6/8 from `space-2xs` / `space-xs` / `space-s`, H must be 6/8/10. The spacing ramp has 8 (`space-s`) and 12 (`space-m`) but not 10. Snapping L to 12 would break the H = V + 2 rule (12 − 8 = 4); snapping to 8 would break it inversely (8 − 8 = 0). 10px is the only value that preserves the rule. | Yes — a `space-2s = 10px` step would absorb this (analogous to `radius-2s` (10) and `radius-2m` (14) added for similar in-between needs in the radius ramp per §2.1.1). Defer until ≥3 consumers need it. |
| **Color Picker spectrum gradients** (`#000`, `#fff`, `#ff0000`, `#00ff00`, `#0000ff`, the rainbow hue stops, the `#ddd`/`#fff` checkerboard) | §9.24 Color Picker | These are **color-space endpoints, not theme colors** — a red-channel track MUST run black → pure `#ff0000` regardless of brand/theme; a transparency checkerboard is conventionally light gray on white. Tokenizing them would be semantically wrong (they don't track the palette and must not). | **No.** A color picker's spectrum is, by definition, the full color space — it cannot be a function of the theme. This is the one place raw color literals are correct. |
| **Color Picker handle hairlines** `inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(0,0,0,.2)` (canvas + slider handles) and `inset 0 0 0 3px rgba(0,0,0,.35)` (selected grid cell) | §9.24 Color Picker | Same class as the sanctioned `--shadow-thumb` (§4.6 / §9.2): a contrast affordance for a handle/thumb sitting on a **varying-color surface** that no single semantic token can serve (the handle must stay legible whether it sits on white, black, or saturated brand). A 1-consumer pattern; creating a `--color-handle-hairline` token violates the anti-bloat rule (§2.2.1). | No — `--shadow-thumb`-class special case. If a 2nd "handle on arbitrary color" component ships (e.g., a future Slider with a color-track), extract a shared `--handle-hairline` token then. |
| **Color Picker opacity checker tile 12px** | §9.24 Color Picker | `repeating-conic-gradient(... ) 0 / 12px 12px` — the checker square size. 12 is on the spacing ramp (`space-m`) but is used here as a texture-tile dimension, not a layout inset, so it's documentation-as-demo (the literal IS the texture). | No — texture dimension, not a layout value. |
| **Drop Zone dashed-stroke hexes** `#D0D2D6` (≈ border/default), `#2F65CF` (≈ border/focus + info), `#D13234` (≈ border/danger) inside the SVG data-URL | §9.26 Drop Zone | CSS `var()` **cannot resolve inside a `url("data:image/svg+xml,…")` string** — the SVG is parsed as an opaque resource, so the per-state stroke color must be a literal hex baked into the data-URL. The hexes are kept in lockstep with their semantic-token equivalents by hand. Native `border-style: dashed` (which would accept tokens) doesn't expose dash-length/gap control, so the SVG approach is required to get the `8 6` dash pattern with a preserved `border-radius`. | No — fundamental CSS limitation. Would only become tokenizable if browsers shipped `var()`-in-data-URL resolution (not on any roadmap). |
| `Sidebar width: 180px`, `content max-width: 1000px` | Host chrome (app shell) | App-shell layout constants — the sidebar's comfortable nav width and the content column's max reading width. Neither is a component value; both are one-off shell dimensions. | Yes — a future "container width ramp" `[sidebar=180, content=1000, …]` would absorb both. |
| `Combobox panel max-height: 280px`, `Table min-width: 600px`, `Breadcrumb menu min-width: 200px` | §9.15 Combobox / §9.29 Table / §9.22 Breadcrumb | Overlay/surface sizing minimums and maximums — the combobox panel's scroll ceiling, the table's pre-horizontal-scroll floor (keeps columns legible), the breadcrumb overflow menu's comfortable min. Each is a judgment-call container dimension, not a ramp value. | Yes — same future "container width ramp" would absorb the min-width family; an "overlay max-height ramp" would absorb the combobox ceiling. |

The acceptance test for "is this a sanctioned constant or drift?":
1. Is it documented in this table (or §1.4.5 of any future component)?
2. Is the rationale specific (not "I just like this number")?
3. Could it absorb into an existing ramp eventually?

If all three, it's sanctioned. Otherwise, it's drift and must be ramp-aligned or formula-derived.

#### 1.4.6 Audit policy

When reviewing any component (new or existing):
1. **Search for literal numeric values in CSS.** Every one must resolve to Pattern 1, 2, or 3 from §1.4.4 or be sanctioned in §1.4.5.
2. **Check that scaling a base ramp doesn't break the component.** If the component uses raw ramp tokens, it scales with them. If it uses formulas, the formulas hold. If it uses magic numbers, scaling breaks it — that's the smoke test for drift.
3. **Check that designer-preference values live in tokens, not CSS.** "I prefer a 16px radius" → set `--radius-m: 16px` (already done). "I prefer this specific component's radius" → that's drift; either the component should use `--radius-m`, or a new ramp step is needed (with §2.1.1 justification).

Drift is found and fixed at audit time, not introduced at component-build time. The discipline is: **every value comes from a ramp or a formula, no exceptions outside §1.4.5**.

#### 1.4.7 Known drift to audit

A first-pass scan found ~366 literal-pixel occurrences in `index.html`. Most are fallback values inside `var(--token, Npx)` (allowed by Pattern 1). The remainder cluster into 4 priority tiers: component-CSS drift (Tier 1, breaks Standing Rule #12), playground host-chrome drift (Tier 2), demonstration-only literals (Tier 3, leave inlined), and genuine off-ramp values needing ramp absorption or sanction (Tier 4). **See `design-system-history.mdc` §A.1 for the full drift snapshot.**

The audit is **not blocking** — components work today. Every new component must comply with §1.4.4, and existing components are scheduled for cleanup as they're touched. The principle is more important than fixing everything at once: stop introducing new drift, fix existing drift opportunistically.

