## Part 2 — Token Architecture

### 2.1 Global spacing ramp
All spacing/sizing must land on this ramp. No exceptions.
```
0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 20, 24, 32, 40, 48, 64, 80, 96, 128
```

**Granularity at the small end.** Two "between" steps are available in the ramp for container padding fine-tuning at the typography-row scale:
- `--space-2s` (10) — one step below `s` (sits between `space-s` 8 and `space-m` 12).
- `--space-2m` (14) — one step below `m` (sits between `space-m` 12 and `space-l` 16).

Both intermediate steps are off-ramp for global spacing in most components — they exist so container components with a strong "padding-px vs padding-py" asymmetric breathing requirement can dial in subtler ratios without breaking case-B (§6.1). Neither has a current consumer (Banner used `space-2m` briefly in a prior rev, then compressed back to `space-m` (12) when its outer padding was unified with Toast at 8/12); they stay in the ramp as documented granularity that consumers may reach for as new components ship.

### 2.1.1 Radius ramp
All `border-radius` values MUST land on this 11-step ramp. No off-ramp values (e.g., `5px`, `7px`, `13px` are forbidden).
```
3xs=4, 2xs=6, xs=8, 2s=10, s=12, 2m=14, m=16, l=20, xl=24, 2xl=32, 3xl=48, full=9999
```
Tokens: `--radius-3xs (4px)`, `--radius-2xs (6px)`, `--radius-xs (8px)`, `--radius-2s (10px)`, `--radius-s (12px)`, `--radius-2m (14px)`, `--radius-m (16px)`, `--radius-l (20px)`, `--radius-xl (24px)`, `--radius-2xl (32px)`, `--radius-3xl (48px)`, plus literal `9999px` or `50%` for fully-round. The `2s`, `2m`, `2xs`, and `3xs` names follow the "one step down from {size}" convention — `2s` is one step down from `s`, `2m` one step down from `m`, `2xs` one step down from `xs`, and `3xs` one step down from `2xs`. The +2px increments at the small end (4 → 6 → 8 → 10 → 12 → 14 → 16) give the system finer granularity for differentiating atom-tier chrome (checkboxes, swatches, table cell swatches at 4) from anchored-overlay companion chrome (kbd badges, count chips, panel-nested badges at 6) — see "Why the small end uses +2 increments" below.

`--radius-2xl` (32) and `--radius-3xl` (48) are **reserved tiers** with zero current consumers — documented in the `SHAPE_SEM` constant in code so they're available when a hero / marketing / extra-large-surface component ships (e.g., a future full-page modal, an immersive media card, a marketing hero CTA panel). Reserve them; do not repurpose for "Card felt a bit small at 20" — that's drift. The pattern matches `radius-xl` (24) which was reserved for Sheet / Drawer for ~6 months before any consumer used it.

Mapping by component family:
- **Atoms** (kbd dot, table cell swatch, t-bar, ds-checkbox, ds-radio container fallback, chip dismiss, skeleton, breadcrumb item, tabs underbar, swatch chip, chev-btn): `radius-3xs` (4) — the smallest interactive or decorative chrome. Reads as "tiny rounded rectangle, no extra character."
- **Anchored-overlay companion chrome** (kbd badge in Tooltip / Dropdown, Tabs count chip): `radius-2xs` (6) — sits one step rounder than atoms so the small fill-tinted contained sub-elements (kbd, count chip) read as a hair softer than atom-tier chrome while staying just under Sub-element radius (8). (Nested **Badge** inside a Dropdown / Select panel item stays at the atom-tier `radius-3xs` (4) via `--nested-rd` — see §2.1.2 — because the panel item it sits in is at `radius-xs` (8) with a 4-inset, so the strict concentric target is 4. The kbd badge is the documented +2 exception to that strict target; the nested Badge follows it.)
- **Sub-elements** (standalone Badge, icon-only chrome): `radius-xs` (8) — small inert chrome sized to read as "tightly contained." Badge differentiates from Button XS via the absence of a stroke and via tonal soft fill — see §3.5.1.
- **Small-element family — interactive 24/28px-tall controls** (Button-XS/S, Input-S, Select-S, Textarea-S, Nav-vertical S/M, Nav-horizontal S): `radius-xs` (8)
- **Small-element family — interactive 32px-tall controls** (Button-M, Input-M, Select-M, Textarea-M, Nav-vertical L, Nav-horizontal M): `radius-2s` (10)
- **Anchored-overlay panel items** (Dropdown panel item `.ds-menu__item`, Select panel item `.ds-select-panel__item`): `radius-xs` (8) — same tier as equivalent Nav rows. The 4-inset (panel padding `space-2xs`) means `inner = outer − inset = 8 − 4 = 4` for any corner-adjacent inner element (nested Badge), which is `radius-3xs` (4). The kbd badge inside an item is the documented +2 companion-chrome exception at `radius-2xs` (6) — see §2.1.2.
- **Anchored-overlay panel outer surfaces** (Dropdown panel, Select panel, Combobox panel, Tooltip): `radius-s` (12) — derived per the §2.1.2 inverse rule (`outer = inner item + inset = 8 + 4 = 12`). Sits cleanly above Nav-row radius (8) and below Card radius (16/20). Locked at one value across all panel sizes so menu surfaces feel quieter than form fields — see §9.3.
- **Medium controls** (40px-tall) (Button-L, Input-L, Textarea-L, Select-L, Nav-horizontal L, Nav-vertical XL, Segmented-L, InputGroup-L, Avatar-square-L): `radius-s` (12)
- **Large controls** (48px): Button-XL, Input-XL, Textarea-XL, Select-XL, Nav-horizontal XL, Segmented-XL, InputGroup-XL, Avatar-square-XL: `radius-2m` (14)
- **Card-style surfaces** (variable-height cards that visually align with the M-tier control radius family but aren't strictly height-tiered) (RadioCard, Toast, inline doc cards `.comp-frame` / `.ds-frame` / `.sem-item`): `radius-m` (16) — these are not 40-tall controls; their radius is set by the "card chrome" identity, intentionally a hair larger than M-tier controls so the eye reads them as containers, not big buttons. Also derived per the §2.1.2 inverse rule: outer = inner button radius (8) + inset (8 for Toast `--tst-pad`) = 16.
- **Primary container surfaces** (Dialog, Card): `radius-l` (20) — derived per the §2.1.2 inverse rule: outer = inner button radius (`radius-xs` 8 for S-tier buttons) + inset (`space-m` 12 padding) = 20. Both Dialog and Card share this tier as peer surfaces in the same shape family, differentiated by behavior (modal vs inline) rather than radius.
- **Hero / spacious surfaces** (future Sheet / Drawer): `radius-xl` (24) — reserved tier per the §2.1.2 inverse rule: outer = inner button radius (`radius-xs` 8) + inset (`space-l` 16, the spacious-tier padding for larger surfaces) = 24. No current consumer; documented destination for the tier so it isn't repurposed before a real Sheet/Drawer ships.
- **Fully round (pill)**: Switch track/thumb, Badge dot, ds-radio dial: `9999px` or `50%`. Reserved for shape-coded interactive primitives whose round shape IS their identity (Switch track's binary off-pill / on-pill iconography is the canonical visual language for toggle switches; the Radio dial is by definition circular; the Badge dot is the small circular notification primitive nested inside or beside a Badge body).

#### Why the small end uses +2 increments — granularity at the atom/sub-element/companion-chrome boundary (May 2026)

The original radius ramp jumped +4 between `2xs` (4) and `xs` (8) — a wide gap that forced every "between" use case (a kbd badge that wanted to feel slightly rounder than an atom-tier checkbox, a nested badge inside a panel item that needed to satisfy `inner = outer − inset` with a non-integer-multiple result, a tabs count chip that wanted to read as quieter chrome than a standalone Badge) to choose between two stops that didn't fit. Three independent observations triggered the May 2026 ramp refactor:

1. **Kbd badges read squarer than the panel chrome around them.** The Dropdown / Tooltip kbd badges sat at `radius-2xs` (4) — the smallest atom-tier stop — while the panel items above them sat at `radius-xs` (8) and the panel outer at `radius-s` (12). The "atom inside sub-element inside surface" stack read as `4 → 8 → 12` (steps of +4 / +4), but the eye perceived the kbd badge as visually crowded against the rounder panel item because the +4 step was a hard visual break. A +2 step would bridge the gap.
2. **Tabs count chip lived in the same visual role as kbd but on a different surface.** Both are "small fill-tinted contained sub-elements" (per §3.5 stroke audit), both at 20-tall, both with a 1px `border/subtle` definition stroke — yet one sat at 4 (kbd) and one had been at 4 (count) just because that was the only sub-atom stop the ramp offered. Same role, same chrome, same height, same desired aesthetic — they should share the same radius.
3. **No "round but not sub-atom" tier existed for companion chrome.** With the panel item at radius 8 and a 4px per-edge collapse, the §2.1.2 nested-radius rule produces inner = 4 for a corner-adjacent nested Badge — fine, the nested Badge stays at 4 (`radius-3xs`). But the kbd badge and the Tabs count chip aren't strictly corner-pinned the way a nested Badge is; they're free-floating small fill-tinted chrome that the eye wanted to read as a hair softer than atom-tier checkboxes. At the old ramp, 4 was the only sub-8 stop, so they were stuck at atom radius. The +2 stop (6) gives them their own "companion chrome" tier without disturbing panel items (8) or panel outers (12).

The fix introduced two new tokens — `radius-3xs` (4) and a redefined `radius-2xs` (6, previously 4) — to close the +4 gap with two +2 steps. The naming follows the established "one step down from {size}" convention: `2xs` is one step down from `xs` (was true before, still true after — only the value moved); `3xs` is one step down from `2xs` (newly named). The token value rename is the only churn: atom-tier consumers that should stay at 4 migrate from `--radius-2xs` to `--radius-3xs` (token rename, value unchanged); anchored-overlay companion chrome moves from `--radius-2xs` (4) to `--radius-2xs` (6) (value bumped, token name preserved).

The resulting ramp now reads as a clean +2 progression at the small end: **4 → 6 → 8 → 10 → 12 → 14 → 16**, with the +4 jumps reserved for the larger-element transitions (16 → 20 → 24). This gives the system the granularity to distinguish atom from companion-chrome from sub-element from small-control from soft-rectangle within a 12-pixel total range — important because that's exactly the range where the eye reads the most variation between adjacent stops.

#### What actually shipped in the May 2026 ramp refactor (scope clarification)

The May 2026 work was a **ramp-granularity** change, NOT a component-radius cascade. What changed:
- The ramp gained `radius-3xs` (4) and redefined `radius-2xs` from 4 to 6 — closing the old +4 gap between 4 and 8 with two +2 steps.
- **Companion chrome moved up one stop**: kbd badges (Tooltip / Dropdown) and the Tabs count chip moved from the atom-tier 4 to the new `radius-2xs` (6).
- **Atom-tier chrome stayed at 4** (token renamed `radius-2xs` → `radius-3xs`, value unchanged): checkbox, radio dial fallback, table swatch, chev-btn, nested Badge inside a list row.
- **Panels, panel items, and controls did NOT move.** Dropdown / Select / Combobox / Tooltip panels stay at `radius-s` (12); their items stay at `radius-xs` (8); Nav items stay at `radius-xs` (8). An earlier draft of this spec proposed a "+2 cascade" that would have bumped panels to 14 and items to 10; that cascade was **not implemented** — it would have made panel items (10) collide with the M-tier control radius (Button M is also 10) and confused the size-tier reading. The panels-stay-at-12 decision is final; the cascade narrative was removed (it survives in `design-system-history.mdc`).

The split that IS encoded in code:
- The `.ds-menu` / `.ds-select-panel` / `.ds-combobox__panel` / `.ds-tooltip` outer radii at `radius-s` (12) and the `.ds-menu__item` / `.ds-select-panel__item` item radii at `radius-xs` (8) — unchanged from before the refactor.
- The kbd badge (`.ds-tooltip__kbd` / `.ds-menu__kbd`) and Tabs count chip at `radius-2xs` (6); nested Badge inside any list row at `radius-3xs` (4) via the `--nested-rd` parent variable (§2.1.2).

#### The subtle radius-to-height curve — ratios held near 29–33% (the rectangular-identity stance)
The control tiers live on a 5-step height ramp (24/28/32/40/48). The radius curve scales **subtly** with height — never more than +2px per height step — so corners stay in the system's rectangular-identity band (29–33% radius-to-height) at every tier instead of ballooning into soft-pill territory at the larger sizes. The canonical control→radius mapping:

| Tier | Height | Radius | Ratio | Reads as |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| XS | 24 | 8 (xs) | 33% | rectangle |
| S | 28 | 8 (xs) | 29% | rectangle |
| **M** | **32** | **10 (2s)** | **31%** | **rectangle** |
| L | 40 | 12 (s) | 30% | soft-rectangle |
| XL | 48 | 14 (2m) | 29% | soft-rectangle |

The ratio is held nearly flat (29–33%) across the entire ramp — the corner reads as the *same family* at every size, just scaled. This is the **subtler ramp** adopted in June 2026, replacing the earlier curve that ramped L/XL to 35%/42% (radius-2m 14 at 40, radius-l 20 at 48) and made the large tiers read as noticeably rounder "soft-pill" surfaces out of step with the small tiers. The +2px-per-step scaling matches the system's rectangular brand identity (Standing Rule §1.1: "generous rounded rectangles, not pills") and industry norms for dense product UIs (Linear / Vercel / Primer all hold their control radii under ~33% of height). **See `design-system-history.mdc` §A.2 for the rejected steeper curves and the rev history (the prior 35%/42% L/XL ramp and why it was flattened).**

Card-class surfaces (Toast, RadioCard, inline cards) intentionally STAY at `radius-m` (16) — set by the "card chrome" identity, not the control-height curve — so the eye reads them as *containers* rather than *big buttons*. Primary container surfaces (Dialog, Card) sit at `radius-l` (20) and anchored-overlay panels at `radius-s` (12), both derived from the §2.1.2 inverse rule (`outer = inner + inset`) rather than the control-height curve. Sub-elements (Badge, Dropdown panel item, Select panel item) stay at `radius-xs` (8) — they live inside a panel at `radius-s` (12) with `space-2xs` (4) padding, so the nested-radius rule (`outer − padding = inner`) locks them at 8.

#### Current tier utilization — keep this honest before adding any new ramp step

This is the single source of truth — the control→radius table above (XS 8 / S 8 / M 10 / L 12 / XL 14) is the canonical mapping. Before claiming a discontinuity exists or proposing a new ramp value, consult this table.

#### When to add a new ramp step (rule)

Adding a new ramp value is justified only when **all four** are true:
1. **A new height tier is being introduced** that doesn't fit any existing ratio band cleanly (e.g., a 36px tier with `radius-xs` lands at 22% and reads visibly more rectangular than the rest of the system's small-element family at 33%, OR a 56px tier where `radius-l` at 20 lands at 36% and reads less rounded than the M/L tiers).
2. **At least 3 components in production use the new tier** (or are committed to use it) — adding a token for one consumer is bloat (see Anti-bloat rule, §2.2.1).
3. **No combination of existing tokens with the nested-radius rule (`outer − padding = inner`) can produce the desired value** — most "in-between" needs are actually nested-element artifacts that should follow the parent's padding math, not invent a new ramp step.
4. **The new value lands on the global `RADII` palette** (`[0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16,20,24,28,32,40,48,56,64,80,9999]`) — never invent off-palette values.

Candidates evaluated and rejected / adopted under this rule are documented in `design-system-history.mdc` §A.2 (do not re-litigate without new data). Adopted candidates: `radius-2m = 14` (smooths the S→M ratio jump) and the May 2026 small-end refinement that shipped `radius-3xs = 4` + redefined `radius-2xs` from 4 to 6 (companion-chrome tier — see "Why the small end uses +2 increments" above). Rejected for lack of consumer: `radius-2l = 22`, `radius-2xl- = 28`.

#### 2.1.2 Concentric corners (nested radius rule)

> **Invocation phrase**: "concentric corners" → invokes the nested-radius rule (`inner = outer − inset`). Parallel to "uniform visible inset" (which invokes the optical-padding rule §6.1 / §6.1.2). Both phrases are short visual descriptions of system invariants.

For any rounded backplate that contains another rounded backplate, the inner element's `border-radius` MUST satisfy:

```
inner_radius = outer_radius − inset
```

Where `inset` is the **visible** distance from the outer backplate's edge to the inner backplate's edge — i.e., the padding value AFTER the §6.1.2 per-edge collapse, not raw `padding-px`. For most cases the inset is the same on both axes; if it isn't, use the smaller value (concentricity must hold on the corner the inner element approaches).

This produces **concentric corners**: the outer and inner curves are visually parallel, like a perfect frame around a frame, sharing a curve center. Mismatched radii break the concentric track and read as one of two failure modes:

- `inner > outer − inset`: inner curve protrudes into the concentric zone, looks "stuck on."
- `inner < outer − inset`: inner curve is squarer than the concentric track, looks "tucked in."

##### Two regimes — strict vs. soft

The rule has two regimes determined by the inner element's distance to the outer corner:

| Regime | When it applies | What's required |
|---|---|---|
| **Strict** | Inner is corner-adjacent: `inset ≤ ½ outer_radius` (typically inset ≤ 4 in this system, since outer rows are at `radius-xs` 8) | `inner_radius = outer_radius − inset` exactly. Eye reads both curves at the same focal area; any deviation looks "stuck on" or "tucked in." |
| **Soft** | Inner is deep-nested: `inset > ½ outer_radius` (typically Dialog → button at 12-16 inset, where outer is `radius-l` 20 or larger) | `inner_radius ≤ outer_radius − inset` ideally; if not feasible, minimize the **radius spread** (`outer_radius − inner_radius`). Eye doesn't strictly read concentric track at this distance, but reads the *spread*: inner shouldn't be substantially more rounded than outer or it looks like a different design language. |

The 4px-inset case is by far the most common in this system because list rows (Nav item, Dropdown item, Sidebar row) all converge on a 4px per-edge collapse around 20-tall hard-edged trailing badges (kbd cluster, counter badge, button-like sub-element). Combined with the row's `radius-xs` (8) outer radius, the rule resolves to `inner = 4 = radius-3xs` for nav badges and chev-btns — that's the strict regime in action (the kbd cluster is the documented +2 exception, sitting at `radius-2xs` (6) for companion-chrome family feel rather than the strict 4).

**Why two regimes, not one absolute rule**: the human eye reads concentric corners by tracking the parallel curve offset between two adjacent rounded edges. When the inner edge sits in the outer's curve transition zone (close to the corner), the eye sees both curves simultaneously and demands they share a track. When the inner edge sits well into the outer's straight zone (far from the corner), the eye reads them as independent shapes — only the *radius spread* between them registers, not the strict track. Modern systems behave this way in practice: shadcn / Apple HIG / Linear / Atlassian all enforce concentric for corner-adjacent elements (badges, chips, sub-element backplates) but use a softer pairing rule for deeply-nested elements (dialog buttons). Material 3 is the only outlier — they pair their generous 28px dialog with full-pill 20px buttons to maintain the spread relationship even at large inset. Our system follows the more common pattern: strict near corners, soft for deep-nested.

**Enumerated nesting pairs in the system** (must stay correct as the system evolves):

| Outer | Outer radius | Inset | Regime | Required / target | Inner element | Inner radius |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Dropdown panel | `radius-s` (12) | 4 (panel pad) | strict | 8 | Dropdown item | `radius-xs` (8) ✓ |
| Select panel | `radius-s` (12) | 4 | strict | 8 | Select panel item | `radius-xs` (8) ✓ |
| Dropdown item M | `radius-xs` (8) | 4 (per-edge collapse, §9.3) | strict | 4 | Each kbd in trailing cluster | `radius-2xs` (6) — deliberate +2 deviation from the strict target (4) so the kbd reads as anchored-overlay companion chrome, not atom chrome; see §2.1.1 "Anchored-overlay companion chrome" |
| Nav item M | `radius-xs` (8) | 4 (per-edge collapse) | strict | 4 | Counter badge (Badge S) | `radius-3xs` (4) ✓ — overridden via the `--nested-rd` parent variable |
| Sidebar row (`.nav-node__title`) | `radius-xs` (8) | 4 (after §6.1.2 fix) | strict | 4 | `.chev-btn` | `radius-3xs` (4) ✓ |
| Nav item M | `radius-xs` (8) | 4 | strict | 4 | Trailing kbd cluster | `radius-2xs` (6) — same +2 companion-chrome deviation as the Dropdown kbd |
| Select panel item | `radius-xs` (8) | 4 | strict | n/a | Trailing checkmark | wrapper, no own radius |
| Dialog | `radius-l` (20) | 12 (`--dlg-px`) | soft | ideal 8 (`outer − inset`) | Footer buttons (Button S) | `radius-xs` (8) ✓ — soft-regime ideal hit exactly; classification is soft (inset 12 > ½ outer 10) but the value lands on a ramp step, so no compromise |

**Why two of these were drift before this section was written.** The dropdown panel → item pair was designed with the rule in mind (§9.3 already documented `12 − 4 = 8`). The kbd at the atom-tier 4 (then named `radius-2xs`, now `radius-3xs`) coincidentally satisfied the rule when nested in a row at radius 8 with 4-inset, so it was correct by accident. **Badge** and **`.chev-btn`** were sized for their **standalone** appearance (Badge as a "soft chip" at radius 8; chev-btn as a "small icon button" at radius 8) and never re-checked when nested. Both are now overridden in list-row contexts to honor the rule.

**Implementation pattern: parent-defined `--nested-rd` variable (now shipped).** Rather than redefining the standalone radius (which would lose the soft-chip / small-button feel of these components in their solo use), the rule is enforced via a parent-declared CSS variable: every list-row parent declares `--nested-rd: radius-3xs (4)` once on its base class, and the inner Badge / chev-btn reads `--nested-rd` with a fallback to its own standalone radius. The inner element picks up the parent's intent without the parent naming each inner class:

```css
/* Inner element: read the parent's intent, fall back to standalone radius */
.ds-badge  { --bdg-rd: var(--nested-rd, var(--radius-xs, 8px)); border-radius: var(--bdg-rd); }
.chev-btn  { border-radius: var(--nested-rd, var(--radius-3xs, 4px)); }

/* Parent rows declare --nested-rd once */
.ds-nav-item,
.nav-node__title,
.ds-menu__item,
.ds-select-panel__item,
.ds-bottom-nav__item { --nested-rd: var(--radius-3xs, 4px); }
```

This is the "forward path" the earlier rev deferred — it shipped once the consumer count (Badge + chev-btn across Nav, Sidebar, Dropdown, Select panel, Bottom Nav) crossed the ≥3-types / ≥3-contexts threshold. The descendant-selector enumeration it replaced is documented in `design-system-history.mdc`. **Note**: the kbd badge does NOT route through `--nested-rd` — it pins `radius-2xs` (6) directly, the deliberate +2 companion-chrome deviation (above), so a kbd cluster sitting inside a Dropdown item still reads as panel chrome rather than dropping to the atom-tier 4 that `--nested-rd` would impose.

**Audit checklist for any new rounded-inside-rounded pair**: (1) measure the visible inset on the corner-approaching axis; (2) compute `outer_radius − inset`; (3) set the inner element's `border-radius` to that value (or override its `--*-rd` variable in the nesting selector). Whenever a row's height OR padding changes such that the inset changes, re-derive every inner element's radius. Whenever a component changes its own outer radius, re-derive its inner elements' radii.

##### The inverse rule — inner components define the outer radius (May 10, 2026)

The forward formula `inner = outer − inset` is what you apply when the **outer container's radius is given** and you need to fit inner elements inside it. But many components in this system are designed the other way around — the **inner elements come from a tier ramp** (e.g., Button XS/S/M/L/XL each ship with a fixed radius from §2.1.1) and the outer container's radius is a free parameter. In that case, the natural design move is to invert the formula:

```
outer_radius = inner_radius + inset
```

Under this rule, the outer radius is **derived**, not chosen. Pick the inner element tier (which is usually decided by content density, affordance hierarchy, and the §1.4 customization levers — not by visual taste at the surface level) and the outer radius falls out as a consequence. The result is mathematically guaranteed strict-regime concentric corners: `inset = outer − inner` holds by construction.

**Why this matters as a system design rule.** The forward formula is a *check* — does this surface satisfy concentric corners given its outer choice? The inverse formula is a *generator* — what outer radius MUST this surface have to satisfy concentric corners with its known inner elements? When you build new components, the inverse rule prevents picking outer arbitrarily and discovering after the fact that no inner-tier radius hits the strict target. It also exposes when a surface's outer radius is "designer taste at the surface level" vs "consequence of inner content" — most modern, math-first design systems converge on the latter.

**System-wide audit (May 10, 2026)** — the inverse formula resolves cleanly for every primary surface in the system:

| Surface | Corner-adjacent inner | Inner radius | Inset to corner | Derived outer | Token | Match? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Tooltip | kbd cluster | `radius-2xs` (6) | 8 (`--tt-pad`) | derived 14 vs actual `radius-s` (12) | `radius-s` (12) | soft — kbd's +2 companion-chrome deviation (6 not 4) makes the strict inverse derive 14; the tooltip outer stays at the anchored-overlay-family `radius-s` (12), spread 6, accepted |
| Dropdown panel | first/last item | `radius-xs` (8) | 4 (`--mnu-pad`) | 12 | `radius-s` (12) | ✓ |
| Select panel | first/last item | `radius-xs` (8) | 4 panel pad | 12 | `radius-s` (12) | ✓ |
| Toast | close Button XS | `radius-xs` (8) | 8 (`--tst-pad`) | 16 | `radius-m` (16) | ✓ |
| Banner (single-line) | n/a — action button vertically centered, NOT corner-adjacent | — | — | — | `radius-m` (16) | n/a (family identity, not derived) |
| Banner (multi-line, top-aligned) | action Button S | `radius-xs` (8) | 8 (`--bn-pad`) | 16 | `radius-m` (16) | ✓ (uniform 8/8 surface → strict-exact) |
| Dialog | footer Button S | `radius-xs` (8) | 12 (`--dlg-px`) | 20 | `radius-l` (20) | ✓ |
| **Card** (post-fix) | footer buttons + media | `radius-xs` (8) | 12 (`--cd-pad`) | 20 | `radius-l` (20) | ✓ |
| (future) Sheet / Drawer | inner buttons | `radius-xs` (8) | 16 (Sheet's natural pad) | 24 | `radius-xl` (24) | reserved for this case |

The audit shows **9 of 9 surfaces are derived-consistent**. The one near-exception:

1. **Banner single-line mode** is genuinely n/a — the action button is vertically centered with `align-items: center`, so it doesn't approach any banner corner. Banner's outer radius (16) is set by the "card-class surface family" identity (Toast at 16, inline cards at 16) rather than by inner-radius math. This is the correct trade-off because Banner's behavior is "match the family" not "satisfy nested-radius math." Documented exception.
2. **Banner multi-line mode** has the action button top-aligned (`align-items: flex-start`), making the button's TOP-RIGHT corner corner-adjacent. Under the uniform-8/8 surface padding (§6.1.5), the inset is 8, so the inverse rule derives outer = 8 + 8 = 16 = Banner's actual `radius-m` (16) — strict-exact ✓. (This was a soft-regime anomaly under the old asymmetric `--bn-py` 12 scheme, which derived 20; unifying the surface padding to 8/8 resolved it.)

**Card pre-fix was the only un-derived surface.** Card was at `radius-xl` (24) by an old "general-purpose container = largest tier" rationale that predated the math-first stance. Fixed to `radius-l` (20) on May 10, 2026 to align with the inverse rule. Card now sits in the same surface family as Dialog (both at 20), differentiated by behavior (inline vs modal) and content density (Card has up to 4 regions including media; Dialog has up to 3) rather than by shape.

**`radius-xl` (24) tier is now reserved** for future Sheet / Drawer. A Sheet would naturally have inset 16 (Sheet's larger surface justifies the spacious-tier padding), and 8 + 16 = 24 — the inverse rule places Sheet at `radius-xl`. Until that consumer ships, the tier is sanctioned reserved space (per §2.1.1's "rejected new ramp steps need 3 consumers" rule, this isn't a new tier — it's an existing tier whose intended consumer is documented).

**Customization implications.** When a designer pulls one of the §1.4.3 levers, the inverse rule keeps the system consistent automatically:
- **Re-density** (e.g., compact mode: Button S → XS at radius-xs 8): inner radius unchanged (still 8), inset unchanged (12), outer unchanged (20). System still consistent. ✓
- **Re-spacing** (e.g., scale all spacing by 0.75: --cd-pad 12 → 9): inner 8 + inset 9 = 17 (off-ramp). Either the scaled spacing token rounds to 8 (giving outer 16), or a ramp absorption is required. Documented as a known scale-coupling case — designers spacing-down a system should expect outer-radius shifts that follow.
- **Re-round** (e.g., scale all radii by 1.25: radius-xs 8 → 10, radius-l 20 → 25): inner 10 + inset 12 = 22 (off-ramp). Closest token: radius-l-scaled = 25. Spread = 3 — within soft-regime tolerance. ✓ approximately.

The takeaway: the inverse rule is **a generator, not just a checker**. New components should default to deriving outer from inner, and existing components should be audited periodically for consistency. The forward rule (§2.1.2 strict/soft regime) is then used as a verifier on the result.

**When the inverse rule does NOT apply.** If the inner element is NOT corner-adjacent (e.g., centered button in a variable-height surface, or media slot in an off-edge position), the surface's outer radius is determined by surface-family identity instead. Banner's single-line mode is the canonical example. Document the exception per §1.4.5 (sanctioned per-component constants).

### 2.2 Semantic token format
Always reference with fallback: `var(--space-s, 8px)`, `var(--radius-m, 16px)`, `var(--control-m, 32px)`.

### 2.2.1 Token creation rules (non-negotiable)
- **Always use an existing semantic token first.** Check `COLOR_SEM_LIGHT` / `COLOR_SEM_DARK` before creating anything new.
- **Use global token references, not raw hex.** Semantic tokens map to global palette stops like `"blue/500"`, `"neutral/200"`, never to `#hex` values.

#### CRITICAL: Update all consumers, not just the component definition
When a component's tokens, states, or rules change, you MUST also update every **instance of that component used in the product itself** — including the playground's own sidebar nav, header, and other host chrome that reproduce component patterns. The component's CSS in the system layer and the consumer's CSS in the application layer can drift when this is forgotten. Specific places that have historically drifted:
- `.nav-leaf`, `.nav-node__title`, `.nav-node__link` — playground sidebar nav. Functionally Nav M items, MUST share the Nav state matrix (default/hover/pressed/focus/active/disabled), the §6.1.2 per-edge padding override, and the §9.3 list-of-rows trailing-element formulas. With a trailing `.chev-btn` (hard-edged 20×20) or `.chev` (20-wide icon wrapper), `.nav-node__title` MUST set `padding-right: calc((var(--control-s, 28px) - 20px) / 2)` for uniform 4×4 inset around the chev-btn. Per §2.1.2 nested-radius rule, `.chev-btn`'s `border-radius` MUST be `radius-3xs` (4) (read via `--nested-rd`), NOT `radius-xs` (8) — row at radius 8 with 4px inset requires inner radius 4 for concentric corners. Any future host-chrome row mimicking a Nav row with a trailing hard-edged sub-element MUST follow both formulas.
- `.seg-ctrl`, `.token-badge`, `.chev-btn` — host chrome that mimics component patterns. If you change a corresponding component's hover/active treatment, audit these. (`.icon-btn` was retired — use `Button XS icon-only` or `Button S icon-only` instead, as the topbar theme toggle does.)
- Any `<section data-page="…">` content that hand-rolls a control instead of using its `*El()` helper.

When any list-row treatment changes (Nav, Select panel item, future ActionMenu/Combobox), grep for `.nav-active`, `.nav-leaf`, `.nav-node`, `.ds-nav-item`, `.ds-select-panel__item`, and any future list-row classes, and update them all in the same pass.
- **If a new semantic token is needed**, it MUST:
  1. Map to a real global palette stop (e.g., `"green/350"`, `"neutral/300"`)
  2. Exist in BOTH `COLOR_SEM_LIGHT` and `COLOR_SEM_DARK`
  3. Have a clear, justified reason (e.g., "hover intermediate between 200 rest and 500 focus")
  4. Follow the naming convention: `{category}/{role}` (e.g., `"feedback/danger/border-hover"`, `"action/solid/brand/hover-bg"`)
- **`color-mix()` is a last resort**, only when no semantic token fits and a perceptual blend is required (e.g., focus ring halo at 30% opacity). Document why a token wasn't created instead.
- **Anti-bloat rule: do not add a semantic token that only one component uses unless that component family will plausibly grow consumers** (see Part 3.1 reserved-tokens policy). If the value is a one-off decorative effect (a button sheen, a single component's special shadow), inline the raw value AND document it as a sanctioned inline exception with rationale. The two sanctioned inline exceptions today are `color-mix()` for the focus halo (perceptual blend, no token captures it cleanly) and `--shadow-thumb` (lives as a global token but is contractually a Switch-only special case). Anything else with raw values is drift.
- **Anti-duplicate rule: never define two tokens with the same value and overlapping role.** Historically the system had `focus/ring` (= `border/focus`) and `focus/ring-danger` (= `border/danger`) — duplicates that were removed. Before adding any new token, search for existing tokens of the same value.
- **Never use `--t1`, `--t2`, `--t3`, `--ctn-bg`, `--ctn-border`, `--bg`, `--nav-sel`, or any other chrome alias** ANYWHERE in the file — not in component CSS, not in playground host chrome (sidebar, topbar, page sections, segmented controls, font cards, type cards, palette cards, tables, badges, doc descriptions). Every color reference in the entire stylesheet MUST resolve through a semantic design-system token (`--color-text-*`, `--color-surface-*`, `--color-border-*`, `--color-action-*`, `--color-feedback-*`). The only exceptions are: `--font` and `--font-display` (font-family references, not colors), and the three scoped syntax-highlighter aliases `--code-tag` / `--code-attr` / `--code-str` (rationale below). All three syntax aliases are set at theme-apply time from `palettes.blue[600]` / `palettes.indigo[600]` / `palettes.pink[600]` respectively. Earlier revs cycled through two failed mappings: (a) `palettes.{blue,red,green}[500]` via JS — read like a 1990s editor and the saturated 500 stops on letter glyphs at 14px landed below the AA contrast floor for green; (b) routing through `text/info` / `text/danger` / `text/success` semantic tokens — got the contrast right but the red+green pairing felt like "validation message" not "code highlight," and shifting `text/danger` for any reason would silently drift the highlighter. The current blue/indigo/pink mapping (May 2026) is **scoped private** rather than semantic because the three syntax slots — tag (`<Component>`), attribute (`placeholder`), string (`"value"`) — carry no semantic meaning the system already has tokens for. All three are at the calibrated-band stop 600 (per §3.3.1 the 600 stops on blue/indigo/pink pass WCAG AA on white at ≥4.5:1; pink in particular sits comfortably warm without crossing into the "this is an error" register that red carries). If a chrome alias is encountered during edits, replace it with the matching semantic token: `--bg → --color-surface-default`, `--t1 → --color-text-primary`, `--t2 → --color-text-secondary`, `--ctn-bg → --color-surface-subtle`, `--ctn-border → --color-border-subtle`. If a chrome alias is encountered during edits, replace it with the matching semantic token: `--bg → --color-surface-default`, `--t1 → --color-text-primary`, `--t2 → --color-text-secondary`, `--ctn-bg → --color-surface-subtle`, `--ctn-border → --color-border-subtle`.

### 2.3 Component scale
Components share a common scale. Changing one token resizes everything at that tier. All font/line-height pairs MUST come from the type ramp — no one-off sizes.

| Size | Height | Padding | Radius | Icon | Gap | Font | Line height |
|------|--------|---------|--------|------|-----|------|-------------|
| XS | control-xs (24) | space-xs (6) | radius-xs (8) | icon-xs (12) | space-2xs (4) | 12px | 16px |
| S | control-s (28) | space-xs (6) | radius-xs (8) | **icon-2s (14)** | space-2xs (4) | 12px | 16px |
| M | control-m (32) | space-s (8) | radius-2s (10) | icon-s (16) | space-2xs (4) | 12px | 16px |
| L | control-l (40) | space-m (12) | radius-s (12) | icon-m (20) | space-xs (6) | 14px | 20px |
| XL | control-xl (48) | space-l (16) | radius-2m (14) | icon-l (24) | space-s (8) | 16px | 24px |

**S-tier icon = 14, not 12 — closing the icon-to-control ratio gap (May 2026 ramp addition).** An audit of icon-to-control ratios revealed an outlier: XS = 12/24 = 50%, **S = 12/28 = 43%**, M = 16/32 = 50%, L = 20/40 = 50%, XL = 24/48 = 50%. Every other tier sits at exactly 50%, but S was 7pp lower because the icon ramp (12, 16, 20, 24, 32) skipped over 14 — the value that would put S at the same proportion. Visually this read as "the icon in the small button is *small*" — exactly the symptom that surfaced on the banner dismiss button (S-tier ghost-icon-only with a 12px X glyph). Adding `--icon-2s: 14` to the global icon ramp and routing every S-tier control's `--*-icon` token to it restores the 50% target across the entire control ramp. Same naming convention as `--space-2s` (10) and `--radius-2s` (10): "one step below the named tier" — `icon-2s` is one step below `icon-s` (16). Industry alignment: Apple HIG SF Symbols at 14pt for compact controls, Material 3 uses 14dp icons in their dense Filled Button, shadcn `size="sm"` button defaults to 14×14 icons. The system was the outlier before this rev; now it matches the convergent industry pattern.

S-tier components routed through `--icon-2s`: Button S, Input S, Select S, Segmented Control S, Tabs S, **Chip base (S default — XS still pins to icon-xs 12)**, Nav vertical M (28-tall row — Nav vertical's "one step denser" rule means its M-tier dimensions = Button S dimensions), Nav horizontal S (28-tall), Nav base (defaults to M-tier dimensions), Breadcrumb M (28-tall row).

**Five tiers, dense-default ramp.** The ramp was redesigned (May 2026) from a 4-tier 24/32/40/48 to a 5-tier 24/28/32/40/48 by adding a 28px tier at S and renaming the existing tiers down by one position (old S=32 → new M, old M=40 → new L, old L=48 → new XL). The design rationale follows industry research:

- **Linear** uses 28 (S) and 32 (M) as their core tiers; we now match Linear M at our M.
- **VSCode** uses 24 (small a11y floor) and 28 (standard); we now match VSCode standard at our S.
- **Primer (GitHub)** uses 28 (S), 32 (M), 40 (L); we match exactly.
- **Atlassian / Apple HIG** use 32 as `md` / `regular`; we match.
- **shadcn / Material 3** use 40 as `default` / `md`; we match at L.
- **shadcn / Material 3** use 48 as `lg`; we match at XL.

Each tier in the new ramp lands on a real industry size, which makes the names directionally correct across all major systems: a "Button M" in our system reads as "the dense-default M" that Linear/Primer/Atlassian/Apple use, not the larger 40px M that shadcn/Material use. The `<Button>` (no modifier → M = 32) default is dense by design.

**Every tier accepts every configuration.** XS through XL all support text labels, icon-only configurations, and any combination of leading/trailing icons. There is no "containment requirement" or "icon-only chrome" restriction — every tier is fully flexible.

#### Tier use-by-density (recommendation, not enforcement)

The same 5-tier ramp serves products at very different visual densities. Pick a default tier per product context, then escalate or de-escalate per affordance importance.

| Product context | Default tier | Industry parallel | When to escalate |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Ultra-dense / IDE chrome / table row actions** | **XS** (24) | VSCode small (a11y floor), Atlassian compact, Apple HIG mini | Use S for primary inline actions |
| **Desktop-dense / power-user tools / toolbars** | **S** (28) | Linear S, VSCode standard, Primer S | Use M for default form actions |
| **General compact apps / dense dashboards (system default)** | **M** (32) | Linear M, Primer M, Atlassian md, Apple HIG regular | Use L for primary CTAs in form-heavy flows |
| **Consumer apps / standard web** | **L** (40) | shadcn `default`, Material 3 md, Primer L | Use XL for hero CTAs |
| **Marketing / hero CTAs / onboarding** | **XL** (48) | shadcn `lg`, Material 3 `lg`, Apple HIG `large` | Use L for everything secondary |

**The system's default is M = 32 (dense-default).** This matches Linear / Primer / Atlassian / Apple HIG conventions for "the default size" in dense product UIs. Consumers building consumer-app density (shadcn-default / Material-md territory) should explicitly use `Button size="l"` (40) for primary CTAs and `Button size="m"` (32) for secondary actions, OR override the project's `.ds-btn` default to point to L-tier values.

Note: the radius curve is intentionally **non-linear** with height (see §2.1.1 for the full rationale). The current mapping XS=8 / S=10 / M=14 / L=20 produces ratios 33% / 31% / 35% / 42% — a smoothed gradient where the largest jump (+7pp) sits at the M→L boundary, not at S→M. Sub-elements (Badge, Dropdown panel item, etc.) follow the nested-radius rule (parent radius − parent padding = child radius) and don't track the height curve.

**Gap is non-negotiable**: at every tier, the icon-to-text gap MUST come from this row. Components have historically drifted — Input and Select once used `space-2xs`/4px at M tier instead of the required `space-xs`/6px, which made icon + text optically merge. When you build or audit any component with `display: flex` + `gap`, verify the gap value against this table for the correct tier. The "one step denser" rule for Nav vertical applies to height/font/icon size, NOT to gap — Nav's bigger gaps are for breathing room in a denser frame and must be documented per-component if they intentionally diverge.

**Interpretation note — gap = visible target, declared = token − 2px wrapper offset (May 2026, paired with §6.1.5)**: the values in this table represent the **visible** icon-to-text distance, NOT the declared `gap` token. When a component has both an icon wrapper and a text wrapper (the universal §6.1.5 rule), the text wrapper's 2H padding adds 2px to the visible distance from the icon glyph to the text-wrapper edge. To land the visible value on the §2.3 target, the declared `gap` is compensated: `gap: calc(var(--space-*) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))`. So a Button M reads from the §2.3 table as "icon-to-text gap = 4" (= `space-2xs`), declares `gap: calc(var(--space-2xs, 4px) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` = 2px in CSS, and renders 2px declared + 2px wrapper = **4px visible** = §2.3 target. Components without a text wrapper (Input/Textarea native value text, Bottom Nav vertical-stacking icon-and-label, Banner/Toast row-architecture body-padded layout, etc.) declare the table value directly without subtraction. See §6.1.5 "The 2px gap-compensation rule" for the full in-scope and exclusion list.

### 2.4 Component role → scale offset
- **Button, Segmented Control, Input, Select, Textarea**: Direct scale (XS=24, S=28, M=32, L=40, XL=48). Default M = 32.
- **Navigation (vertical)**: One step denser (Nav M = Button S dimensions = 28). Nav uses 12/16 font through L; the body-tier bump only happens at XL=40.
- **Navigation (horizontal)**: Matches Button scale (XS through XL).
- **Badge**: Sub-control (S=20, M=24). Both use 12/16 font; differentiated by height and padding only. Independent of the control ramp. **Rounded-rectangle at `radius-xs`** (8) — see §3.5.1. Badge differentiates from Button (including Button XS at 24-tall) by **never carrying a stroke** (`border: 0`); the absent 1px edge plus the read-only-no-hover-no-focus behavior makes it unambiguously a label even when the height and tonal fill match a Button.
- **Tooltip**: Uses 12/16 font (Body S from the type ramp). Single size.
- **Icons inside supplementary controls**: One step smaller than primary affordances (Input icon = icon-s at M=32).

