## Part 3 — Color System

### 3.1 Semantic tokens (use these, not raw values)

**Surfaces**
- `--color-surface-page` — **app-shell page background** (`neutral/50` light, `neutral/1200` dark). The outermost surface — the canvas behind every contained surface. Intentionally one stop off `surface/default` (white) in light mode so white cards / dialogs / inputs read as "lifted off the page"; in dark mode it's the darkest surface (`neutral/1200`, below `surface/default`'s `neutral/1100`) so the same lift direction holds. Used by the playground host chrome (`.layout`, `.main`). Consumers building an app shell paint the page with this; everything contained sits on `surface/default` or `surface/raised`.
- `--color-surface-default` — primary (white light / dark-gray dark)
- `--color-surface-raised` — elevated cards, popovers
- `--color-surface-sunken` — **disabled fill across all components**. Light = `alpha/black/2` (~`#FAFAFA` on white). Dark = `alpha/white/4` (slight lift over dark page bg). Alpha approach self-adapts to substrate. Used by Textarea, Checkbox/Radio (unchecked), RadioCard, Switch thumb. Previously `neutral/100` (opaque) — duplicated `surface/subtle`'s value; remap fixed both issues.
- `--color-surface-quiet` — **very subtle in-component tint** (`neutral/50` light, `neutral/1000` dark). The quietest section-divider fill in the system, lighter than `surface/subtle` (0.020 ΔL vs 0.050 ΔL in light). Used by Table header rows (§9.29), Segmented Control track (§9.23), and the `seg-ctrl` host-chrome topbar control. Reserved for future "very-subtle section tint inside a contained surface" consumers (grouped-row dividers, alternating rows, Dialog/Card section headers). In dark mode 0.070 ΔL vs `surface/default` fixes a latent bug where `surface/subtle` mapped to the same `neutral/1100` as default — Table headers were invisible. Same-stop overlap with `surface/raised` (also `neutral/1000` in dark) sanctioned because the roles never co-exist on the same surface (raised = anchored overlays floating; quiet = in-component tints). Added May 2026 when Table promotion surfaced that `surface/subtle` couldn't be lifted globally without compromising 11 dependent consumers.
- `--color-surface-subtle` — subtle container fill (`neutral/100` light, `neutral/1100` dark) — Avatar fallback bg, Tabs count chip, Skeleton base, Progress track, Spinner track, Card media slot, Drop-zone icon circle, RadioCard visual, Badge neutral, token chips, Switch-disabled track. (Previously also Card `filled` variant + Segmented Control track; both moved out in May 2026 — Card retired the variant for tones per §9.13, Segmented Control moved to `surface/quiet` per §9.23.) Stayed at `neutral/100` (not lifted to `neutral/50`) because the remaining consumers need the 0.050 ΔL — Avatar against `surface/page` (`neutral/50`) needs visible mass; Progress/Spinner/Skeleton tracks need contrast against the indicator atop them; Tabs count chip needs its tinted-chip identity. `surface/quiet` is the targeted answer for the lighter cases that don't depend on chromatic contrast for state. Distinct from `surface/sunken` which is alpha-based and reserved for disabled.
- `--color-surface-overlay` — modal/dialog backdrop surface

**Text**
- `--color-text-primary` — body, headings (NOT `--t1`)
- `--color-text-secondary` — supporting, descriptions
- `--color-text-tertiary` — decorative icons inside inputs, muted labels
- `--color-text-placeholder` — input placeholders
- `--color-text-disabled` — disabled everything
- `--color-text-danger` / `success` / `warning` / `info` — validation text

**Borders**
- `--color-border-default` — rest state (neutral/200 light, neutral/900 dark)
- `--color-border-subtle` — lighter dividers
- `--color-border-hover` — intermediate hover (neutral/300 light, neutral/800 dark)
- `--color-border-strong` — high contrast (neutral/500 light)
- `--color-border-focus` — focus ring (blue/500)
- `--color-border-danger` / `success` / `warning` — full-strength validation (500 shade)

**Action (buttons and interactive fills)**
- `--color-action-hover` — generic overlay for unfilled surfaces (alpha/black/4)
- `--color-action-{solid|soft|outline|ghost}-{neutral|brand|danger|success}-{bg|text|border}` — rest tokens
- `--color-action-{solid|soft}-{tone}-{hover-bg|hover-border}` — hover tokens (one palette step from rest)

**Feedback (badges, alerts, validation tints)**
- `--color-feedback-{danger|warning|success|info}-{bg|border|text|icon}` — universal across all four severities
- `--color-feedback-{danger|success}-border-hover` — **danger and success only.** Used on hover of form fields in `is-error`/`is-success` validation states. Warning and info do NOT have `border-hover` because they are informational severities (badges, banners, alerts), never validation states attached to interactive form controls. This asymmetry is intentional and the system MUST NOT add unused symmetry tokens just to look symmetric.
- `--color-feedback-success-icon-hover` — **success only.** The "track-turns-green" pattern is unique to Switch (and any future component with a success-themed always-visible track surface, e.g., progress bar at 100%). No equivalent danger/warning/info exists because no component has a "track turns red on hover" pattern. If one is built, add the matching token at that time — do not add it speculatively.

**Reserved tokens** (defined for future use, currently 0 consumers — keep, do not remove):
- `--color-overlay-scrim`, `--color-overlay-scrim-heavy`, `--color-overlay-surface` — Dialog backdrop and Drawer scrims.
- `--color-selection-{bg,bg-strong,text,border,indicator}` — Table row selection, text selection, multi-select. **Currently 0 consumers — fully reserved.** Pagination's active page was the sole consumer of `--color-selection-border` through early May 2026, but the May 2026 brand-color audit moved Pagination's active state to a **neutral** treatment (`action/hover` fill + inset 1px `border/default` ring — same selected-row chrome as Nav and Table rows; see §9.16), so `selection/border` no longer has a live consumer. The whole `selection/*` family stays reserved for a future Table multi-select / text-selection feature that wants a dedicated blue selection tint distinct from the neutral row-selection chrome.
- `--color-text-link`, `--color-text-link-hover` — Sanctioned exception in 4.3.1 for inline anchor hover when a hover affordance is needed and bg/border can't carry it.
- `--color-text-warning`, `--color-text-info` — Symmetric with `text-danger`/`text-success` (used in form validation captions); same surface family will eventually need warning/info variants for non-form alert text.
- `--color-border-strong` (neutral/500) — Completes the border ramp between `border-hover` (n/300) and `border-focus`. Reserved for high-contrast outline buttons or "press to confirm" affordances.
- `--color-surface-brand`, `--color-surface-brand-subtle`, `--color-text-brand`, `--color-text-on-brand`, `--color-border-brand` — Symmetry with neutral/danger/success "soft surface" patterns. Used today by docs UI (`.ds-table .t-bar-label`, `.motion-dot`) for brand-tinted token visualizations; will be consumed by any future "branded surface" component.

**Tokens that are 1-component currently but NOT 1-off** (justified, do not extract or rename):
- `--color-surface-inverse`, `--color-text-inverse` — used today by Tooltip; reserved for any high-contrast overlay surface (Toast in dark mode, Snackbar, future code-block highlight).
- `--color-feedback-success-icon-hover` — see asymmetry note above.

The rule: a token is "1-off" only when (a) only one component uses it AND (b) no other component could plausibly need it under the documented system patterns. If both conditions hold, the value should be inlined and documented as a component-internal special case (like `--shadow-thumb`). If condition (b) fails, keep the token.

### 3.2 Hover treatment by surface type

Every hover color is a real semantic token. Never use `filter: brightness()` for hover.

| Surface | Rest | Hover | Focus |
|---------|------|-------|-------|
| Text input / textarea / select | `border/default` (200) | `border/hover` (300) | `border/focus` (blue/500) + halo |
| Selection control (unchecked) | `border/default` + surface fill | `action/hover` overlay | Focus ring |
| Selection control (checked) | `action/solid/brand/bg` (500) | `action/solid/brand/hover-bg` (600) | Focus ring |
| Switch off | `border/default` (200) | `border/hover` (300) | Focus ring |
| Switch on | `feedback/success/icon` (green/500) | `feedback/success/icon-hover` (green/600) | Green focus ring |
| Button solid-neutral | `action/solid/neutral/bg` (1200) | `hover-bg` (1100) | Focus ring |
| Button solid-brand | `action/solid/brand/bg` (500) | `hover-bg` (600) | Focus ring |
| Button soft-* | `action/soft/{tone}/bg` (50) | `hover-bg` (100) | Focus ring |
| Button outline/ghost | transparent | `action/hover` overlay | Focus ring |
| Validation rest | `feedback/{role}/border` (200) + bg tint | `feedback/{role}/border-hover` (350) | `border/{role}` (500) + halo |
| Card / panel / badge | `border/default` | `border/hover` (300) for interactive cards; none for static | — |

### 3.3 Validation color pairing
- Rest border: `feedback/{role}/border` (200 shade) — soft
- Hover border: `feedback/{role}/border-hover` (350 shade) — intermediate
- Focus border: `border/{role}` (500 shade) — full strength + halo
- Background: `feedback/{role}/bg` (50 shade) — subtle tint, always present
- Placeholder text: `text/{role}` (per §3.3.1)
- Icons: `text/{role}` — matches placeholder

### 3.3.1 Chromatic foreground — one token, one stop per role (non-negotiable)

**Every place a chromatic color appears as foreground** — text, label, icon-on-tinted-bg, dot indicator — resolves to a single token: **`text/{role}`**. Badge text + icon, toast tone marker, soft button label, validation field icon, validation caption, dialog title, required asterisk, menu destructive item, banner text. One token, one rule.

The stop per role is the **lowest stop (most saturated)** that passes WCAG AA body text (4.5:1) on **both** the surface where the foreground lives and the role's `50` tint background. That gives us "saturated AND safe everywhere":

| Role | Light | Dark | Hex (light) | Contrast: white / role/50 (light) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `danger` | `red/600` | `red/300` | `#D13234` | 4.98 / 4.68 |
| `success` | `green/700` | `green/300` | `#05721D` | 6.12 / 5.81 |
| `warning` | `dune/700` | `dune/300` | `#7C5504` | 6.65 / 6.28 |
| `info` | `blue/600` | `blue/300` | `#2F65CF` | 5.41 / 5.12 |
| `brand` | `blue/600` | `blue/300` | `#2F65CF` | 5.41 / 5.12 |

**Why three roles land at 600 and two at 700** (asymmetry is forced by hue-perceptual-lightness, not chosen):
- **`green` at 600** = `3.39:1` contrast on white → **fails AA**. Green is naturally the brightest hue at any given OKLCH lightness, so the saturated stops fall below the contrast floor faster than the other hues. `green/700` is the first stop that lands AA-safe.
- **`dune` (amber) at 600** = `3.72:1` on white → **fails AA**. Same dynamic — yellow-adjacent hues read bright. `dune/700` is the first AA-safe stop. (Going to `dune/800` desaturates the chroma below 0.075 and starts reading as muddy brown, losing the "this is amber/warning" signal — so 700 is also the visual *floor*, not just the AA *floor*.)
- **`red`, `blue`** are denser hues; their saturated-600 stops pass AA comfortably. They get to be in the "500–600 vibrant range" the system prefers.

**Three palette regimes, why they matter for the contrast math**:
1. **Stops 500/550/600** — each palette uses its own calibrated `ref.L` (red `0.657`, green `0.710`, blue `0.620`, dune `0.710`). These stops carry *hue identity* — they're tuned for each palette's "saturated brand feel."
2. **Stops 700+** — every palette drops to a *shared* `L_BY_STOP` curve (`L=0.480` at 700 across all hues). Lightness is auto-normalized at this tier.
3. **What this means**: `red/700`, `green/700`, `blue/700`, `dune/800` are perceptually matched in lightness (all sitting at L≈0.48). At the 600 stop they're *not* matched — green/600 is at L=0.620 while red/600 is at L=0.567, which is exactly why green/600 is too bright for AA on white.

**Tokens that resolve to `text/{role}`** (all aliases at the global-stop layer; kept as separate names for component-author forward flexibility, but they MUST never diverge from the canonical `text/{role}` value):
- `text/{role}` — the canonical token
- `action/soft/{role}/text` — soft button label + icon (Dialog "Delete" CTA, `Button soft-danger`, `Button soft-brand`)
- `action/outline/{role}/text` — outline button label + icon (currently brand only)
- `action/ghost/{role}/text` — ghost button label + icon (currently brand only)

**Components that use `text/{role}` directly via CSS**:
- `.ds-badge--{role}` `--bdg-fg` and `--bdg-dot` — badge text, badge icon, badge dot indicator
- `.ds-toast--{role}` `--tst-icon-color` — toast tone-marker icon
- `.ds-input.is-{error|success}` `__icon`, `__field::placeholder`, `__placeholder` — validation chrome
- `.ds-textarea.is-{error|success}::placeholder` — validation chrome
- `.ds-select.is-{error|success}` `__icon`, `__placeholder`, `__chevron` — validation chrome
- `.ds-field__caption--{error|success}`, `.ds-fieldset__validation--{error|success}` — validation captions
- `.ds-field__required`, `.ds-fieldset__required` — required asterisks (use `text/danger`)
- `.ds-menu__item--destructive` — destructive menu items (use `text/danger`)

**Rule of last resort — only color the foreground when the element *is* the role signal**. A chromatic foreground is a strong, attention-grabbing carrier of role meaning. Use it only when *that element* is what tells the user "this is danger / success / warning / info." If something else nearby already carries the role (a colored CTA button, a colored surface fill, a colored icon next to it), do not stack a second chromatic carrier on top — pick the strongest one and keep the rest neutral.

**Worked examples**:
- ✅ Soft-danger button label = red. The button IS the destructive action.
- ✅ Validation caption ("Email is required") = red. The caption IS the explanation; nothing else carries it.
- ✅ Required asterisk = red. A single glyph that means "required" — it IS the indicator.
- ✅ Toast tone-marker icon = red. In a toast, the icon is the only chromatic carrier of tone; the title and body stay neutral.
- ✅ Badge text + dot = red. The badge IS the role indicator.
- ❌ **Dialog Danger title was previously red — removed.** The soft-danger CTA at the bottom of the dialog already carries the tone signal; coloring the title was a redundant second carrier. Apple HIG, Material 3, Atlassian, Primer, Radix/shadcn, Linear, Vercel, Stripe, and Notion all keep destructive-dialog titles neutral for this exact reason. The dialog's `--danger` variant now affects only the footer's default button set, not the title color.
- ❌ Coloring a banner/alert description when the banner already has a colored bg + icon — same redundancy.

**`text/{role}` is the ceiling for chromatic body-text contrast in this system**. If a context needs *deeper* (e.g., a future high-contrast accessibility mode), it adds an opt-in token (`text/{role}-strong`); it does not redefine `text/{role}`. If a context needs *lighter* (e.g., a placeholder shade), it adds an opt-in token; it does not redefine `text/{role}`. The whole system pivots off this one stop per role, so changing it touches every chromatic surface — which is the point.

**Banned**:
- ❌ `red/500` / `green/500` / `blue/500` for badge text or button labels — fails WCAG AA body-text on tinted bg, even though it looks vibrant. The 500 stop in the chromatic palettes is for *surface/border* roles, not foreground.
- ❌ Different stops per component for the same role (e.g., `Badge danger` at `red/500` while a hypothetical "danger title" is at `red/700`). The previous system did this via the now-deprecated `feedback/{role}/icon` token; that token has been deleted for danger/warning/info and badge/toast now route through `text/{role}` like everything else.
- ❌ Component-local overrides like `--ds-some-comp-danger-color: var(--red-700)`. If the canonical stop doesn't work for a specific context, the fix is to raise the discussion at the rule level, not to escape locally.
- ❌ Stacking chromatic carriers — e.g., red title + red CTA in the same dialog (see "rule of last resort" above). Pick one carrier per local context.

### 3.3.2 The one exception — `feedback/success/icon` is now exclusively for the Switch on-state surface

The Switch component's on-state track is a **chromatic surface fill** (a saturated green pill that the white thumb sits on). Conceptually it's the same role as `action/solid/brand/bg = blue/500` — a brand-strength colored surface, not a foreground. The CSS uses `var(--color-feedback-success-icon)` (= `green/500`) for the track, and `var(--color-feedback-success-icon-hover)` (= `green/600`) for hover.

The token name is a historical accident (it was originally meant for badge tone markers, before the Standing-Rule-#8 unification). Renaming it to `action/solid/success/bg` would be more accurate but would affect only this one consumer; left in place for now.

`feedback/danger/icon`, `feedback/warning/icon`, `feedback/info/icon` were deleted (no consumers after the badge/toast repoint). If a future component needs a "saturated chromatic surface fill" for a non-success role, add the corresponding token at the 500 stop with explicit rationale; do not co-opt foreground tokens.

### 3.3.3 Soft button border — rest/hover step parity (non-negotiable)

Every `action/soft/{role}` border pair steps **+50 stops** from rest to hover (light) and **−50 stops** from rest to hover (dark — going lighter to feel "raised" on dark surfaces).

| Role | Light rest → hover | Dark rest → hover |
|---|---|---|
| `neutral` | `neutral/100 → neutral/150` (+50) | `neutral/1000 → neutral/950` (−50) |
| `brand` | `blue/100 → blue/150` (+50) | `blue/1000 → blue/950` (−50) |
| `danger` | `red/100 → red/150` (+50) | `red/1000 → red/950` (−50) |
| `success` | `green/100 → green/150` (+50) | `green/1000 → green/950` (−50) |

Previously `danger` stepped +100 light (`red/100 → red/200`) and −100 dark (`red/1000 → red/900`) — twice the others. There was no design reason for it; the bigger jump made `Button danger` hover feel suddenly more aggressive than `Button brand` hover, breaking the "rest → hover → focus → pressed" gradual escalation rule (Standing Rule #2). Now unified at +50/−50 across all chromatic roles.

### 3.3.4 Solid button border — luminous-edge rim, calibrated by ΔL not by stop-step

`action/solid/{role}` carries a **lighter-than-fill border** in every state. The effect is a subtle "luminous edge" rim — a few percent of perceptual lightness above the fill — that gives solid buttons a slightly elevated, more crafted feel without crossing into the "top-light sheen" territory that Standing Rule #11 forbids. (Sheen = inset white highlight that lives inside the fill; rim = faint outline above the fill. Different mechanisms, different aesthetics. The rim doesn't require any 1-component-only token; it's just a different stop choice for the existing `border` slot every soft/outline/ghost button also has.)

**The math constraint that drives the stop choices.** A naïve "border = bg minus 50 stops" rule (the same +50/−50 pattern soft buttons use, §3.3.3) fails for solid buttons because the perceptual-lightness curve isn't smooth across the global palette. Three regimes interact:

1. **Stops 50–450** sit on the auto-generated `L_BY_STOP` curve. Adjacent stops have ΔL ≈ 0.020–0.085, depending on where you are on the curve.
2. **Stops 500/550/600** drop into per-palette **calibrated** lightness (`ref.L`, `ref.L − 0.04`, `ref.L − 0.09`). Adjacent stops within this band have ΔL ≈ 0.040–0.050 — much flatter than the curve.
3. **Stops 700+** rejoin the shared `L_BY_STOP` curve at L=0.480 and step uniformly down.

The calibrated 500/550/600 band exists for *hue identity* (each chromatic palette gets its own "saturated brand feel"), but the side effect is that any rule of the form "border = bg minus N stops" produces wildly different perceptual contrast depending on which side of the 500-band boundary the bg lives on. Concretely for `blue`:

| Pair | Stop step | Actual ΔL |
|---|---|---|
| 500 → 450 | −50 | **0.115** (auto-curve has a big jump at the 450↔500 boundary) |
| 600 → 550 | −50 | **0.050** (calibrated band, flat) |
| 400 → 350 | −50 | **0.015** (auto-curve, but we're high up where it's nearly flat) |
| 500 → 450 | −50 | **0.115** (same as light-mode rest) |

So a uniform "step by −50" rule gave solid-brand a clearly visible rim at light-mode rest (ΔL=0.115), an almost invisible rim at light-mode hover (ΔL=0.050), an *invisible* rim at dark-mode rest (ΔL=0.015), and a clearly visible rim at dark-mode hover (ΔL=0.115). User-reported as "the brand button's stroke disappears on hover" — which was the most obvious of four consistency failures.

**The rule:** `action/solid/{role}` border stops are picked by **target ΔL ≈ 0.10**, not by a fixed stop-step. The stops listed below were chosen to land in the 0.090–0.130 ΔL band across all four states (rest/hover × light/dark), which is the perceptual range where the rim reads "subtle but visible" — past 0.05 it disappears, past 0.20 it starts to feel like a real outline button.

| Role | Mode | bg | border (rim) | ΔL |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `brand` | Light rest | `blue/500` | `blue/450` | 0.115 |
| `brand` | Light hover | `blue/600` | `blue/500` | 0.090 |
| `brand` | Dark rest | `blue/400` | `blue/100` | 0.130 |
| `brand` | Dark hover | `blue/500` | `blue/450` | 0.115 |
| `neutral` | Light rest | `neutral/1200` | `neutral/800` | 0.265 |
| `neutral` | Light hover | `neutral/1100` | `neutral/700` | 0.290 |
| `neutral` | Dark rest | `neutral/50` | `neutral/300` | 0.130 |
| `neutral` | Dark hover | `neutral/100` | `neutral/350` | 0.115 |

`neutral` lands above the chromatic 0.10 target (0.115–0.290) for two reasons: (a) the achromatic palette has no calibrated band so all stops sit on `L_BY_STOP` where ΔL between adjacent stops is wider, and (b) a near-black `neutral/1200` button against a white page reads "tonal," and a stronger rim helps it feel like a deliberate dark surface rather than a flat black blob. The chromatic rim is intentionally *more subtle* than the achromatic rim — chromatic fills already carry "this is the action" through hue saturation, so the rim only needs to whisper.

**Why this is allowed under Standing Rule #11 (no 1-component decorative tokens).** The rim doesn't require any new token. `action/solid/{role}/border` and `action/solid/{role}/hover-border` already exist for symmetry with `soft`/`outline`/`ghost` — every button fill style has both `bg` and `border` slots so the button CSS can blindly resolve `--btn-br: var(--color-action-{fill}-{role}-border)` regardless of which fill is in play. The rim approach uses the existing slot; it just picks a stop that's slightly off the bg instead of equal to it.

**The alternative we rejected.** Setting `border = bg` always (a "no rim" approach) is what most modern systems do (Linear, shadcn, Material 3, Apple HIG, Vercel — Stripe is the notable exception with its inset-highlight pattern). It would have removed the math constraint entirely. We kept the rim because (a) the system's character is "designer-forward, highly customizable" rather than "minimum-viable modern" and the rim adds craft, (b) the rim is a useful place for the system to expose a customization knob later (consumer can crank rim ΔL up for a more "vintage industrial" look or down to zero for a "modern flat" look without any code change), and (c) `solid-neutral` already had a stronger rim for tonal-button perception, so removing it from `solid-brand` would have created two solid buttons with different stroke profiles for no design reason.

**For any future solid-* role added** (e.g., `solid-danger`, `solid-success` if the case arises), pick stops that land in the 0.09–0.13 ΔL band for chromatic roles, 0.11–0.30 for achromatic. Validate against this section before merging — do not reach for the `−50 stops` pattern from §3.3.3.

### 3.4 Feedback tone → palette mapping (with rationale)

Each feedback tone maps to one global palette via the `feedback/*` and related semantic tokens. The mapping is **not arbitrary**: tones must be perceptually distinguishable at every stop they're used at (especially the 50/200 stops used for soft tints in Toast, Badge, Validation backgrounds).

| Tone | Palette | OKLCH hue | Δ from danger (red, h=25.4°) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `info` | `blue` | 262.2° | 236.8° |
| `success` | `green` | 145° | 119.6° |
| **`warning`** | **`dune`** (amber) | **78°** | **52.6°** |
| `danger` | `red` | 25.4° | 0° (reference) |

**Why warning = dune (amber), not orange**: Earlier the system mapped `warning → orange` (h=48°), only **22.6° from danger's red** (h=25.4°). At the soft 50/200 tint stops used for Toast / Badge / Validation backgrounds, both palettes rendered as a pale warm wash and were perceptually indistinguishable. Almost no modern design system uses pure orange for warning for this reason — Material 3, shadcn / Tailwind, Atlassian, Apple HIG, Sentry, Primer all use amber or yellow. Our `dune` palette (h=78°) is the amber equivalent. The remap moved warning ~30° farther from red on the OKLCH wheel, which is enough to read as a distinct hue at every stop. Yellow (h=95°) would be even more distinct but has accessibility issues at the 500 stop on light backgrounds (yellow icons on white surfaces fail WCAG contrast); amber/dune at L=0.71 stays readable.

**Rule for any future tone-to-palette mapping**: minimum 50° hue separation from any other tone used in the same surface family. If a new tone needs to be added (e.g., `notice`, `pending`), validate hue distance against all existing tones at the 50, 200, and 500 stops before adopting.

**Why we don't rename palettes after a remap**: `dune` keeps its palette name even though it's now the warning color. The palette layer (`dune`, `red`, `blue`, etc.) is the *physical* color identity (hue + chroma + lightness curve); the semantic layer (`warning`, `danger`, `info`) is the *role*. Roles can move between palettes without renaming the palettes — preserving the option to remap again later (e.g., if `warning → yellow` becomes preferred) without churn at the palette level. This is how Material 3 keeps its tonal palettes named `tertiary` / `secondary` separately from semantic roles.

### 3.5 Stroke logic — when borders apply, when they don't, and which token

A stroke does exactly **one of three jobs**. Pick the job for the surface, then use the matching token. A surface never carries multiple stroke jobs — if you're tempted to stack them, you're using the wrong token for at least one.

| Job | Token | Purpose | Behavior across states |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A. Affordance** | `border/default` (200) | "This is interactive — here's its boundary" | Escalates to `border/hover` (300) on hover, `border/focus` (blue/500) on focus + halo |
| **B. Semantic** | `feedback/{tone}/border` (200) | "This surface carries a tone meaning (validation, notification severity)" | Escalates to `feedback/{tone}/border-hover` (350) on hover, `border/{tone}` (500) on focus + halo |
| **C. Definition** | `border/subtle` (150) | "This surface is similar in value to its background — the hairline keeps the edge crisp" | **Never escalates.** Definition strokes are ambient; they're always the same weight. |

**Shadows are complementary, not redundant.** A shadow does one job: **elevation**. It signals "this floats above the page." Elevated surfaces still get a definition stroke when their fill is similar in value to the background — the shadow handles depth, the stroke handles edge crispness. Generously rounded surfaces (our `radius-xl` Dialog, `radius-m` Toast) blur shadow boundaries faster than sharp edges, which makes the definition stroke load-bearing.

**The "fill carries the boundary" exception.** When a surface fill has enough contrast against its background to act as its own edge, the stroke is omitted because the fill IS the boundary. The clearest case: dark inverse Tooltip on a light page (the dark fill is the boundary). The dark/light contrast is doing the work; adding a stroke would be redundant edge-coding.

**Surface-by-surface mapping (current state, post-audit):**

| Component / surface | Job | Token | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Input, Textarea, Select trigger | A — Affordance | `border/default` | Escalates on hover/focus per Part 3.2 |
| Outline Button | A — Affordance | `action/outline/{tone}/border` | Escalates per Part 3.2 |
| Checkbox / Radio (unchecked) | A — Affordance | `border/default` | Escalates on hover |
| RadioCard | A — Affordance | `border/default` | Escalates on hover/checked |
| Toast / Banner (info / success / warning / danger) | B — Semantic | `feedback/{tone}/border` | Bg + border + icon together carry the tone — **identical between Toast and Banner**, see §9.10 for why chrome is intentionally shared |
| **Toast / Banner (neutral)** | **C — Definition** | **`border/subtle` (150)** | Both Toast and Banner neutral use `surface/default` bg + `border/subtle` border. Toast neutral was demoted from `border/default` (200) in the May 2026 audit; Banner inherits the same chrome because shadcn / Sonner / modern Vercel all share Toast-Banner chrome and differentiate via shadow + width + position |
| Dialog | C — Definition | `border/subtle` | Shadow-4 carries elevation; subtle stroke crisps the 20px-radius edge against the modal scrim |
| Dropdown panel (`.ds-menu`) | C — Definition | `border/subtle` | Shadow-3 + subtle stroke; affordance lives on the trigger, not the panel |
| Select panel (`.ds-select-panel`) | C — Definition | `border/subtle` | Same as Dropdown |
| Combobox panel (`.ds-combobox__panel`) | C — Definition | `border/subtle` | Same as Select panel |
| Card / `.comp-frame` / chrome containers | C — Definition | `border/subtle` | Always-on hairline |
| **Badge (all tones)** | **None — fill carries the boundary** | `border-color: transparent` (1px reserved for layout parity with outline-bearing surfaces) | Uniformly borderless across every tone. Neutral fill `surface/subtle`; tonal fills `feedback/{tone}/bg` (50 stop). Stroke-presence is the load-bearing differentiator from Button XS soft-{tone} at the height-collision case (Badge M = Button XS = 24): Badge has zero stroke, Button has one. Matches shadcn / Atlassian Lozenge / Vercel Geist Tag / Linear / Apple HIG. See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.13 for the "added stroke / neutral-only stroke / current uniform borderless" rev path. |
| Tabs list (`.ds-tabs__list`) | None | — | The list itself is borderless (gap-only); the active 3px rounded underbar is its own absolutely-positioned `::after`, not a list-border overpaint. Earlier rev had a `border-bottom` on the list that the underline crossed; the current underline-only treatment removed it because the underbar reads more cleanly without a competing full-width hairline behind it. |
| Tabs count chip (`.ds-tabs__count`) | C — Definition | `border/subtle` (150) | Same family as Badge / kbd — small fill-tinted contained sub-element. The May 2026 Badge audit explicitly fixed Badge for this exact missing stroke; the count chip was the next instance of the same drift and is now aligned. |
| Kbd badge in tooltip / menu | C — Definition | `border/subtle` | Tiny element — needs a crisp edge |
| Separator | The stroke IS the component | `border/subtle` (1px) | — |
| Tooltip (light mode) | None — fill carries the boundary | — | `surface-inverse` near-black fill against light page; stroke would be redundant |
| Tooltip (dark mode) | TBD — verify visually | `border/subtle` if surface-inverse lacks contrast against dark page | — |
| Solid Button (every tone) | Fill carries the boundary | `border-color: transparent` reserved 1px so layout doesn't shift | The bg's saturated fill IS the edge against neutral surfaces |
| Soft Button (every tone) | Fill carries the boundary | Same pattern as Solid; tinted fill IS the edge | — |
| Ghost Button | None | — | Transparent until hover, then `action/hover` overlay carries the affordance |
| Switch track (off / on), Checkbox checked, Radio checked | Fill carries the boundary | Filled chromatic surface | The selected/on state IS a colored shape |

**Rules for new components:**

1. **Pick the job first, the token follows.** Don't reach for `border/default` reflexively for non-interactive surfaces — it's an affordance token, not a definition token. Definition surfaces use `border/subtle`.
2. **One job per surface.** Don't combine affordance + definition strokes. If a surface needs both, the affordance stroke is sufficient (it already provides definition by being there).
3. **Shadow + stroke is normal**, not double-coding. Shadow = elevation, stroke = edge definition. Both can co-exist.
4. **Skip the stroke only when the fill IS the boundary.** Dark fill on light surface, brand-colored solid button on neutral page — the contrast is the edge. Adding a stroke is noise.
5. **Definition strokes never escalate.** `border/subtle` stays at 150 across rest/hover/focus. Only affordance and semantic strokes change with state.
6. **For tinted/contained sub-element surfaces** (Badge, future Chip-soft, future Tag, future Pill-style status), the default treatment is **uniformly borderless across all tones** — fill carries the boundary. Neutral reads softer-edged than tonal (which is the correct hierarchy for "quietest variant"); tonal fills carry their own edge through chroma. Stroke-presence is the load-bearing differentiator from interactive Button soft-{tone} (which always carries a 1px stroke). Chromatic strokes (Job B at 200, semantic-strong at 500) stay reserved for surfaces where the tone IS the entire message — Toast, Banner, Validation field outlines. (Badge-family consistency was prioritized over neutral edge-crispness; mixing neutral-with-stroke and tonal-borderless within the same Badge family read as inconsistent — see §3.5 audit table for the full rule history.)

#### Stroke necessity & customization (the "no strokes" theme)

Strokes in the system fall into three removability categories. This matters for theme customization — a consumer who says "I don't want strokes anywhere" needs a clear rule about which strokes are structural (cannot be removed without breaking the component) vs which are decorative (definition strokes that can be themed away).

| Removability tier | Components | What happens if you remove the stroke |
|---|---|---|
| **Structural — cannot be removed** | Input, Textarea, Select trigger, Outline Button, Checkbox unchecked, Radio unchecked, RadioCard | The control becomes invisible. An Input on `surface/default` with no border is just a blank rectangle — there's no way for the user to know "type here." A Radio without its dial circle is a blank space. Removing these strokes deletes the component's primary affordance. |
| **Communicative — should not be removed** | Toast tonal, Banner tonal, Validation field outlines | The stroke carries tone meaning that bg-tint alone can convey on a white page but FAILS to convey on a similar-tone page (e.g., a `success` Toast on a `surface/subtle` page). Removing these strokes leaves the component dependent on bg contrast that may not hold across all page types. |
| **Definitional — fully removable** | Card, Dialog, Dropdown panel, Select panel, Combobox panel, Toast (neutral only), Tabs list border-bottom, **Badge**, kbd badge, Separator (it IS the stroke, but the consumer can choose not to use the component) | These strokes provide edge crispness when the surface fill is similar in value to the page. On a page where the fill IS strongly different from the page (white card on dark page, dark Tooltip on light page, etc.), the stroke is already redundant. A consumer who wants a "flat fill" aesthetic can drop these without breaking any component's primary identity. |

**Customization mechanism — single-token override for the entire definitional tier:**

```css
/* Theme: no Job C strokes — Card/Dialog/panels/Badge/Toast-neutral all become flat fill */
:root, .theme-light, .theme-dark {
  --color-border-subtle: transparent;
}
```

That's it. Because every Job C stroke in the system resolves to `var(--color-border-subtle)`, overriding the token to `transparent` (or `var(--color-surface-default)` if you want to be explicit) drops the entire tier in one rule. Job A and Job B strokes use different tokens (`border/default`, `feedback/{tone}/border`, `border/{tone}`) and are NOT affected — so Inputs, Outline Buttons, Toasts (tonal), and Banners stay structurally functional.

**What you should NOT do** (anti-patterns that emerge when consumers try to "remove all strokes"):
1. ❌ Setting `border-width: 0 !important` globally — kills Job A strokes too, breaking interactive controls.
2. ❌ Setting `--color-border-default: transparent` to "soften" Inputs — kills the field affordance, leaving users with no visual cue for "type here."
3. ❌ Setting `--color-feedback-{tone}-border: transparent` to "calm" Toasts — kills tone communication, leaving Toasts dependent on bg-tint contrast that may not hold on tonal pages.

The single sanctioned override is `--color-border-subtle: transparent`. Anything broader is breaking the system, not customizing it.

**Why this works as a one-knob model**: Job C is the only stroke job that's purely about edge crispness. Job A is about affordance ("this is interactive"); Job B is about tone communication. Both A and B carry information that can't be replaced by adjusting the surface or the fill. Job C, by contrast, only fires when the surface fill is similar to the page bg — and the consumer who wants a flat fill is implicitly saying "I'm OK with edges being soft." The token consolidation makes the whole tier toggleable as one decision.

### 3.5.1 Shape language — pill is reserved, Badge differentiates by stroke absence (non-negotiable)

The system uses **shape itself as a semantic carrier** alongside size, color, and stroke. Two shape families:

| Shape family | Reads as | Components |
|---|---|---|
| **Rounded-rectangle** (`radius-2xs` through `radius-xl`, 4–24px) | The default identity for everything in the system — interactive controls, surfaces, sub-element labels | Buttons (every tier), Inputs, Textareas, Selects, Cards, Dialogs, Toasts, RadioCards, Dropdown panels, Nav items, **Badge** |
| **Fully round / pill** (`9999px` or `50%`) | Reserved for **shape-coded primitives** whose round shape IS their identity, not a generic "this is a label" cue | Switch track + thumb (the binary off-pill / on-pill iconography), Radio dial (a circle by definition), Badge dot (the small circular notification primitive) |

**The rule for new components**: pick the height-tier rounded-rectangle radius from §2.1.1. Use fully-round ONLY when the round shape is the canonical visual language for the affordance (Switch, Radio, notification dot) — never as a generic "this is a label" cue. Use other differentiators (stroke absence, size, tonal fill) for label-vs-control distinctions.

#### Why Badge is `radius-xs` rounded-rectangle, not pill — and how it differentiates from Button

The 5-tier height ramp puts Button XS at 24px and Badge M at 24px, raising the question: how does a user tell a read-only Badge from a clickable Button XS soft-{tone} when both are 24-tall with the same radius and the same tonal fill?

We considered making Badge fully-pill (industry pattern at shadcn / Linear / Atlassian / Primer) and shipped it briefly. The pill differentiation worked at the rule level but produced an unwanted side-effect in real product contexts: **at small heights with single-character content** (e.g., a counter Badge holding "3" or "8" inside a nav item), the pill's rounded ends collapse into a complete circle, making the Badge read as a notification dot rather than a label chip. That's correct iconography for iOS / Material 3 notification badges (the "5" red circle on an app icon), but it's the wrong identity for our Badge — we want a rectangular label chip, not a circular notification glyph.

The decision: **roll back the pill rule and differentiate Badge from Button via stroke weight + size hierarchy + tonal fill convention + interaction-state absence.**

| Differentiator | Badge | Button (any tier) |
|---|---|---|
| **Stroke** | 1px `border/subtle` (150 stop) — Job C definition (added in May 2026 audit) | Always 1px `border/default` (200) for outline / `action/{tone}/border` for soft (200) — Job A affordance (50 stops darker than Badge) |
| **Stroke meaning** | "I have an edge" (decorative) | "I am interactive" (functional affordance, escalates with state) |
| **Default size** | S = 20, M = 24 | XS = 24, S = 28, M = 32, L = 40, XL = 48 |
| **Hover state** | None — Badge is read-only | Always darkens or shifts |
| **Focus state** | None | Two-layer halo |
| **Default fill** | Tonal soft (`feedback/{tone}/bg`) by convention | Can be any of solid / soft / outline / ghost |

The **stroke absence** is the load-bearing differentiator. Even at the same height with the same tonal fill (the worst case: Badge M info vs Button XS soft-info, both 24-tall with `feedback/info/bg`), the eye picks up the missing 1px edge on the Badge instantly. shadcn, Notion, Linear, Atlassian, and Primer all run their badges without strokes for exactly this reason — a stroke around a tonal chip is redundant edge-coding and visually conflates the chip with a clickable button.

The **size hierarchy** is the secondary signal. At Badge S (20) vs Button XS (24), the 4px height difference plus the missing stroke makes the distinction unambiguous. Only at Badge M (24) vs Button XS (24) does size collapse and the system relies entirely on stroke + behavior for the read.

The **tonal fill convention** is the tertiary signal. Badges almost always carry a tonal hue (info / success / warning / danger / brand) whereas Buttons can be neutral. A neutral Badge is the edge case; even there, the absent stroke distinguishes it from `Button XS soft-neutral` or `Button XS ghost-neutral`.

#### Why the pill rule was wrong despite industry alignment

shadcn / Linear / Atlassian / Primer all use pill Badges and we initially shipped to match. The problem isn't that "industry is wrong" — it's that **most of those systems don't have a 24-tall Button tier** that conflicts with their 20–24-tall Badge. shadcn's smallest Button (`size="sm"`) is 36; Linear's S is 28; Atlassian's compact is 24 but their Lozenge is 16. The Badge-vs-Button confusion case we faced is partly a consequence of our denser-than-most ramp. Pill resolves the case at the cost of the counter-collapses-to-circle aesthetic problem.

The fix that respects both is: **drop the stroke** (cribbing shadcn's actual treatment, which is borderless) **AND keep rounded-rectangle** (preserving the chip-not-circle shape at small content widths). That's what the system uses now.

#### Neutral Badge — special case

When the Badge is `neutral` toned (no semantic tonal color), the bg is too subtle without a stroke to read on a white surface. `--color-action-soft-neutral-bg` (= `neutral/50`) is essentially transparent against `surface/default` (`alpha/white/100`). For neutral Badge specifically, the bg is overridden to `--color-surface-subtle` (= `neutral/100`), which produces a visible chip without needing a border. All other tonal variants (info / success / warning / danger / brand) carry their `feedback/{tone}/bg` which is colorful enough to read borderless.

#### What this rule does NOT mean

- **Switch track is fully round, but Switch is interactive.** Not a contradiction. Switch's track shape *is its identity* — the binary off-pill / on-pill iconography is canonical for toggle switches across iOS, Material 3, Apple HIG, every modern system. Breaking the convention would reduce recognizability.
- **Notification dots and Badge.dot stay fully-round.** The small circular `.ds-badge__dot` (8×8) inside a Badge is the canonical "unread" / "active" / "live" indicator and stays at `9999px`. Same for the Radio dial. These are circles by definition, not "labels" applying the pill rule.
- **Buttons never become fully-pill** to "look softer." A consumer who wants softer buttons should bump the height-tier radius (e.g., M from `radius-2s` 10 → `radius-2m` 14), not switch shape families.
- **A future Chip is rounded-rectangle, not pill.** Chip is interactive (filter chip, toggleable tag); per the same rule it stays in the rounded-rectangle family. Differentiation from Button comes from chip-specific affordances (selected state with check icon or filled tonal bg, optional dismiss icon at trailing edge) and from size (Chip typically lives at 24–28 tall, matching Button XS/S).

