## Part 4 — Interaction States

### 4.1 State order (mandatory for every interactive component)
```css
.ds-comp { /* rest */ }
.ds-comp:hover:not(:disabled):not(.is-disabled):not(.is-focused):not(:focus-within), .ds-comp.is-hover { /* hover */ }
.ds-comp:focus-visible, .ds-comp.is-focused { /* focus */ }
.ds-comp:active:not(:disabled), .ds-comp.is-pressed { filter: brightness(.92); }
.ds-comp.is-error { /* validation error */ }
.ds-comp.is-success { /* validation success */ }
.ds-comp:disabled, .ds-comp.is-disabled { opacity: .5; cursor: not-allowed; pointer-events: none; }
```

### 4.2 Focus ring — two-layer translucent halo
Every focus ring is: **1px solid brand stroke + 2px translucent halo**, no gap.

**Components with own border that turns brand on focus** (inputs, textareas, radio cards):
```css
border-color: var(--color-border-focus);
box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-border-focus) 30%, transparent);
```

**Components without own border** (buttons, selection controls):
```css
box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--color-border-focus), 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-border-focus) 30%, transparent);
outline: 2px solid transparent; /* Windows High Contrast Mode */
```

Error/success swap `--color-border-focus` for `--color-border-danger`/`--color-border-success`. Switch uses green ring when on.

### 4.3 Motion tokens
The system uses a 4-step duration scale and a 4-step easing scale. Component CSS may use the literal `.15s ease` shorthand (the historical default) OR reference the tokens directly — both are valid since the tokens map to the same default. Use named tokens whenever a value departs from the default.

| Duration token | Value | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `--motion-fast` | 100ms | Micro-interactions, immediate feedback (segmented control press) |
| `--motion-base` | 150ms | **Default.** All hover/focus/color/border swaps |
| `--motion-medium` | 250ms | Position/scale changes (drawer slide, accordion expand, dialog enter) |
| `--motion-slow` | 400ms | Page transitions, full-screen overlays |

| Easing token | Value | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `--easing-default` | `ease` | Default for all hover/focus state changes |
| `--easing-out` | `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)` | Entrances (toast slide-in, dialog enter, dropdown open) |
| `--easing-in` | `cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0)` | Exits (toast dismiss, dialog close, dropdown close) |
| `--easing-in-out` | `cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)` | Bidirectional movement (drawer toggle, position swap) |

**Rules:**
- Only transition properties that change. Never `transition: all`.
- Default state transition pattern: `transition: border-color .15s ease, box-shadow .15s ease, background .15s ease`.
- For component-driven motion (entrance/exit, position, transform), prefer named tokens: `transition: transform var(--motion-medium) var(--easing-out)`.
- **Always respect `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.** The system already includes a global override that reduces all transition/animation durations to 0.01ms when the user requests reduced motion. Do not bypass this with `!important`. Components needing motion-essential affordances (like switch thumb position) should prefer `transform` over `left/top` so the static layout still reads correctly even when transitions are disabled.

### 4.3.1 Foreground stability rule (non-negotiable)
**Foreground (text/icon color) does NOT change on hover.** Hover changes background, border, or fill — never the foreground. This is a system-wide invariant that keeps the hover language uniform: "hover means *this surface is now darker (light mode) or brighter (dark mode)*", not "this text is changing."

The single sanctioned exception is `.ds-input__action:hover` where a decorative tertiary icon becomes a real affordance (color: `text/tertiary → text/primary`). That role change is justified; pure visual hover never changes foreground.

Concrete prohibitions, all of which break the rule:
- `a:hover { opacity: .X }` — opacity affects foreground perception (text fades toward bg). Forbidden.
- `:hover { color: ... }` on any component-level rule unless it represents a *role change* (chrome → affordance) and is documented as such.
- `:hover { filter: ... }` — same problem; filters mutate foreground.

If a hover affordance is needed and the bg/border can't carry it (e.g., an inline link inside body text), use the link tokens (`text/link → text/link-hover`) instead of opacity. The link tokens already encode the correct contrast direction per mode (in light mode they get darker; in dark mode they get brighter — same direction as `action/hover`).

### 4.4 Pressed/active
`filter: brightness(.92)` — the only permitted use of brightness filters. Applied via a single shared rule on the base class.

### 4.5 Disabled

Disabled state is conveyed by **three coordinated changes**, applied per component family. There is no single "opacity .5 over everything" rule — that approach was used for some components (Button, Nav item) and is fine when the component has no inner fill that needs to communicate the disabled state, but components with **fillable interiors** (Input, Textarea, Checkbox/Radio container, RadioCard, Switch thumb) get an explicit lighter fill so the "disabled" cue is perceptible even at rest without dimming the entire control.

| Layer | Token | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| **Fill** | `--color-surface-sunken` (light: `alpha/black/2`, dark: `alpha/white/4`) | Very subtle alpha tint that reads as "this control is here, but inactive." Self-adapts to the substrate — on a white Card surface the disabled fill composes to ~`#FAFAFA` (lighter than the previous opaque `neutral/100`); on the page bg it composes to a slightly distinct tint that remains visible alongside the disabled border. |
| **Border** | `--color-border-disabled` (light: `neutral/200`, dark: `neutral/900`) | Same intensity as `border/default` so the control's shape is preserved at rest. Disabled cue does NOT come from a softer border — it comes from the lighter fill + dimmer text. |
| **Text** | `--color-text-disabled` (light: `neutral/400`, dark: `neutral/700`) | Muted text/icon that's still WCAG AA against `surface/default` but visibly less saturated than `text/primary`/`text/secondary`. |
| **Cursor + interactivity** | `cursor: not-allowed; pointer-events: none;` | Required on every disabled component — kills hover/focus and signals "you can't act on me." |

**Components without a fillable interior** (Button, Nav item, Tabs trigger, Dropdown menu item, Select panel item, Tooltip-trigger element) use the opacity-or-text-color shortcut because they don't have an inner surface to lighten:

```css
/* Button family */
.ds-btn:disabled { opacity: .5; cursor: not-allowed; }

/* Nav family */
.ds-nav-item.is-disabled { color: var(--color-text-disabled); cursor: not-allowed; pointer-events: none; }
```

These don't need the `surface/sunken` fill because their default fill is either transparent (Ghost button, Nav item) or carries the affordance via tone (Solid/Soft button) — applying an even lighter fill would compete with the affordance signal. The `opacity: .5` shortcut dims fill + text + border together, achieving the disabled read in one rule.

**Components WITH a fillable interior** explicitly set `--color-surface-sunken` as the disabled fill:

```css
/* Textarea */
.ds-textarea:disabled { background: var(--color-surface-sunken); color: var(--color-text-disabled); cursor: not-allowed; }

/* Checkbox/Radio (unchecked) */
.ds-checkbox.is-disabled { background: var(--color-surface-sunken); border-color: var(--color-border-disabled); ... }

/* RadioCard */
.ds-radio-card.is-disabled { background: var(--color-surface-sunken); border-color: var(--color-border-default); ... }

/* Switch (thumb) */
.ds-switch.is-disabled::before { background: var(--color-surface-sunken); ... }
```

For Switch (and any future "filled selection control"), the **track** uses `border/disabled` (= `neutral/200` light) as a fill alias — this is the legacy pattern from when checkbox-checked-disabled needed a darker visible "off-switch" surface. The thumb uses the new lighter `surface/sunken`, which lifts cleanly off the slightly darker track. This visual pairing (lighter thumb on darker track) preserves the on/off iconography even in disabled state.

**Anti-patterns**:
- ❌ Setting `background: transparent` on a disabled Input/Textarea — fill disappears, control becomes a floating outline that reads as "empty placeholder," not "disabled."
- ❌ Setting `background: var(--color-surface-default)` (white) on a disabled control — fill matches enabled exactly, the only disabled cue is text color, which is too subtle for many users.
- ❌ Adding `opacity: .5` ON TOP OF the explicit `--color-surface-sunken` fill — double-dims the control to the point of unreadability. Pick one or the other per component family.

### 4.5.1 Stroke profile rule (non-negotiable)
**A component's stroke profile is decided at rest and is invariant through state.** Hover, pressed, and disabled NEVER add or remove a stroke; they may only shift the color of an existing stroke by exactly one palette stop. The only state allowed to introduce a new stroke is **selected**, applied as `box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-border-default)`. Focus is layered on top via `box-shadow` halo (4.2) and does not alter the underlying stroke profile.

**Why:** prevents flicker on hover, keeps hit-targets the same size before and after interaction, and makes a "selected" visual primitive distinct from a "hover" visual primitive (one is persistent intent, the other is transient).

**Stroke profiles by component family:**

| Family | Stroke at rest | Hover changes stroke? | Selected adds inset ring? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Field controls (Input, Textarea, Select trigger, RadioCard) | 1px `border/default` | yes — color shifts to `border/hover` | n/a |
| Selection controls unchecked (Checkbox, Radio) | 1px `border/default` | NO — only bg overlay added | n/a |
| Selection controls checked (Checkbox, Radio, Switch on) | 1px filled with brand/feedback | yes — shifts one stop with the fill | n/a |
| Switch off | 1px filled with `border/default` | yes — shifts one stop | n/a |
| Solid / Soft buttons | 1px (border = bg, effectively invisible) | yes — color tracks the bg shift | n/a |
| Outline button | 1px `action/outline/border` (Job A) | NO — only bg overlay added | n/a |
| Ghost button | none | n/a — only bg overlay added | n/a |
| List rows (Nav item, Menu item, Select panel item) | NONE | NO — only bg overlay added | yes — inset 1px `border/default` |
| Tooltip (light mode) | none — fill carries the boundary | n/a | n/a |
| Dialog | 1px `border/subtle` (Job C — definition) | n/a (passive) | n/a |
| Overlay panels (Select panel, Dropdown panel) | 1px `border/subtle` (Job C — definition) | n/a (passive) | n/a |
| Inline cards / chrome containers (`.comp-frame`, `.font-card`, etc.) | 1px `border/subtle` (Job C — definition) | n/a | n/a |

**Border on overlay surfaces** is governed by Part 3.5 (Stroke logic): every contained surface that sits on a similar-value background gets a `border/subtle` definition stroke, regardless of whether a backdrop is present. The shadow handles elevation; the stroke handles edge crispness. The two are complementary. The only exception is when the surface fill carries enough contrast against its background to act as its own boundary (dark Tooltip on light page).

### 4.6 Elevation tokens
Drop shadows MUST use one of these 5 tokens. Never write raw `rgba(0,0,0,…)` shadow recipes inline. Focus halos and active-state inset rings use the dedicated patterns in 4.2 and 9.3 respectively — they are NOT elevation.

| Token | Recipe | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `--shadow-1` | `0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.06)` | Tactile micro-lift (segmented control pressed button); barely-there elevation |
| `--shadow-2` | `0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.12)` | Small floating element (Tooltip) |
| `--shadow-3` | `0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.08), 0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.04)` | Anchored overlay (Select panel, future Dropdown/ActionMenu/Popover) |
| `--shadow-4` | `0 2px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.06), 0 8px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.12)` | Modal (Dialog, future Drawer/Sheet) |
| `--shadow-thumb` | `0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.18)` | **Special-case only**: Switch thumb. Darker (.18 opacity) because it sits on a varying-color track and needs more contrast. Do not use elsewhere. |

The 4-step elevation scale maps cleanly to the layer scale (4.7): tooltips on `--z-tooltip` use `--shadow-2`, anchored overlays on `--z-anchored` use `--shadow-3`, modals on `--z-modal` use `--shadow-4`. Each shadow recipe also intentionally pairs with that layer's expected vertical movement (light shadows for things close to the surface, heavier shadows for things far from it).

### 4.7 Z-index / Layer scale
Stacking is governed by 10 named layer tokens. Components MUST reference these tokens, never raw integers.

| Token | Value | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `--z-base` | 0 | Default content layer |
| `--z-content` | 10 | Sticky table headers, in-flow elevated content |
| `--z-sidebar` | 20 | Application sidebar (chrome) |
| `--z-topbar` | 30 | Application top bar (chrome) |
| `--z-anchored` | 40 | Anchored overlays: Select panel, Dropdown, ActionMenu, Popover, Combobox |
| `--z-tooltip` | 50 | Tooltips — always above anchored overlays |
| `--z-modal` | 60 | Modal layer: Dialog, Drawer, Sheet |
| `--z-modal-tooltip` | 70 | Tooltips that originate inside a modal — must outrank the modal |
| `--z-toast` | 80 | Toast / Snackbar — always above modals so notifications during a modal are visible |
| `--z-critical` | 90 | Reserved for system-critical overlays (rare; e.g., session-expired) |

**Rules:**
- Never use raw `z-index: 999` or `z-index: 1000`. Reference the token.
- The scale leaves room (10-unit gaps) so stacking conflicts within a tier can be resolved with `+1`/`+2` without reaching the next tier.
- Local stacking contexts (e.g., a card with `z-index: 0` and an inner `z-index: 1`) are unaffected — the global scale only governs cross-component stacking.

