## Part 5 — Typography & Icons

### 5.1 Font usage
- `var(--font)` — primary (currently DM Mono). The system's identity is **mono-for-body** — body text, labels, controls, descriptions all render in DM Mono. This is intentional and gives the system a technical, code-adjacent feel.
- `var(--font-display)` — headings (currently DM Sans). Display tier ONLY (page-level h1-h4).
- Monospace variant: `font-family: var(--font-monospace, ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace)` — used inside `<code>` and code-style affordances; the system's `--font` is already mono so this is mainly for fallback safety.

### 5.1.1 Type ramp (7 named tiers)
All text in the system MUST use one of these 7 tiers. No off-ramp font/line-height pairs. Sizes are documented as `font-size/line-height`.

**Display tier** — DM Sans, weight 600 (page-level headings only — h1–h4 in the playground/host chrome, never inside components):

| Token | Size/LH | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `display/2xl` | 32/40 | Page hero / h1 (currently `.h-axis`) |
| `display/xl` | 24/32 | Section group / h2 (currently `.h-group`) |
| `display/l` | 20/28 | Subsection / h3 (currently `.h-section`) |
| `display/m` | 16/24 | Small heading / h4 (currently `.h-item`, `.pal-title`, `.variant-label`) |

**Body tier** — DM Mono (`var(--font)`), weight 400 (Regular). The default for everything inside components — control text, titles, labels, captions, descriptions:

| Token | Size/LH | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `body/l` | 16/24 | Dialog title, Card title, Banner title (when present at L tier), Dialog body content, large row labels (Checkbox/Radio/Switch row L), Tabs L trigger, Button XL text |
| `body/m` | 14/20 | Default body and control text (Input M, Button M/L, Select M, Textarea M), Toast title + description, Card description, Banner title + description, Tabs M trigger, Radio-card label, Checkbox/Radio/Switch row M label, Dialog body, descriptions |
| `body/s` | 12/16 | Caption, badge text, dense list rows, code preview, Tooltip body, dialog description, validation message, field label, fieldset legend, table header, dropdown/select panel group label, nav group heading, switch/checkbox/radio row XS+S, nav vertical, Tabs S/XS trigger, Button XS/S text |

**Standing rule — Components default to weight 400 (Regular). The "label tier" is collapsed.**

The system previously had a separate "label tier" at weight 500 (Medium) for control titles, form labels, and metadata headings. That tier was removed. Every component now uses weight 400 — Buttons, Tabs, Badges, field labels, fieldset legends, group labels, Toast/Banner titles, Radio-card labels, Avatar initials, Table headers, Segmented Control buttons, and active/selected state rows in Nav and Select panels (which previously got a 400→500 bump on `is-active` / `is-selected`). Hierarchy is carried by **size + color + surface**, never by weight.

**The two sanctioned weight-500 carve-outs** (both pass the three-part necessity test below): **Dialog title** (`.ds-dialog__title`, 16/24) and **Card title** (`.ds-card__title`, 14/20). In both, the title is locally dominant, cannot scale up (Dialog title is already at `body/l`; Card title shares the 14/20 tier with its body by design), and color cannot carry the contrast (title and body share `text/primary`). These are the only two weight-500 nodes inside `.ds-*` component classes. Toast and Banner titles stay at 400 — their description sits at the same size but a different color (`text/secondary`), so color carries the hierarchy and the necessity test fails (criterion 3).

**Rationale.** This system is mono-everywhere (DM Mono). On monospaced fonts, weight contrast reads weaker than on proportional fonts because fixed character widths make the perceived stroke difference subtler — DM Mono 500 is only mildly heavier than 400. Modern mono-heavy UIs (Vercel terminal, Berkeley Mono kits, JetBrains command palettes) almost universally skew uniform-weight and lean on size/color/surface for hierarchy. Material 3's Display and Headline tiers default to Regular (400) for the same reason. Vercel ships Buttons at 400 (Regular). Linear keeps body and most UI labels at 400. Apple Notes/Mail keep list rows at 400 for both default and selected states (color/bg carries the change, not weight).

The previous 500-heavy treatment was pulling the system *away* from its stated "technical, code-adjacent" identity (§5.1) and conflicting with the Standing Rule "quiet at rest" stance (§1.1).

**When weight 500 (Medium) MAY be used — necessity criteria (all three must be true):**

1. The element must read as visually dominant within its **local** context (not just the page).
2. The element **cannot** scale up in size (already at the `body/l` tier max within its container, or sizing-up would break layout — e.g., a Toast title that's already 14/20 because the toast body is 14/20).
3. **Color cannot carry the contrast** — `text/primary` is already in use and there is no darker neutral text token.

If any of the three is false, the answer is "drop to 400." When in doubt, default to 400 and let size/color do the work.

**Rules:**
- Never invent an off-ramp combination (e.g., `13/19`, `15/22`).
- Weight 600 is exclusive to display tier (DM Sans, page headings only). Weight 400 is the default for every text node inside a component. Weight 500 is reserved for the necessity-criteria cases above and is justified per-instance.
- Do NOT use weight 600 inside any `.ds-*` component class. Display-tier weight is for `.h-*`, `.pal-title`, `.variant-label`, etc. (page chrome).
- `<strong>` / `<b>` HTML elements may use weight 500 or 600 for inline emphasis within prose — that's a content-author tool, not a component-author tool.
- Component scale tier XS/S/M/L picks from `body/s`, `body/m`, `body/l` for control text — same tiers used for everything else.
- The system intentionally has no `body/xl` — anything bigger is a heading and belongs in the display tier with the display font.

### 5.2 Icon sizing in controls
All system icons are inline SVG (Phosphor Regular weight — see §5.3 / §5.3.1). Two dimensions matter inside an icon wrapper:
- **Icon frame** (reserved space): `width: var(--{comp}-icon)` × `height: var(--{comp}-lh)` on the wrapper, so the wrapper occupies one text-line of vertical space and aligns naturally with adjacent text in the same flex row.
- **Glyph size** (drawn symbol): the SVG itself is sized to `var(--{comp}-icon) × var(--{comp}-icon)` via the wrapper's `> svg { width / height }` rule (§6.1.3). The SVG file ships with `width="1em" height="1em"` as a fallback for any non-wrapper context.

**NEVER** set the wrapper's `font-size` to the icon token. SVGs sized via `1em` would scale up to icon-frame size, but the wrapper would gain a tall ascent/descent box that would push adjacent text out of alignment. Always size the SVG via the `> svg` child selector, and keep wrapper `line-height: 1` so the wrapper's height is purely controlled by `height: var(--{comp}-lh)`.

```css
.ds-comp__icon {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: var(--comp-icon);
  height: var(--comp-lh);
  line-height: 1;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}
.ds-comp__icon > svg { width: var(--comp-icon); height: var(--comp-icon); }
```

Icon color flows from the wrapper's `color` token through the SVG's `fill="currentColor"`. No per-component icon-color tokens are needed; the wrapper sets `color` and the SVG inherits.

### 5.3 Icon reference

The system uses **[Phosphor Icons](https://phosphoricons.com)** at **Regular weight** for every icon constant. Each constant is an inline SVG string (Phosphor's source SVG) with `xmlns`, `viewBox="0 0 256 256"`, `width="1em"`, `height="1em"`, `fill="currentColor"`, and a defensive `style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:middle"` for any non-wrapper context.

| Constant | Phosphor name | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `ICON_CIRCLE` | `circle` | Universal neutral placeholder, including Toast tone marker (Standing Rule #8) |
| `ICON_INFO` | `info` | Opt-in only, when explicit "information" semantics is wanted (Standing Rule #8) |
| `ICON_CHEVUP` | `caret-up` | Functional |
| `ICON_CHEVDOWN` | `caret-down` | Functional (disclosure, select, sidebar) |
| `ICON_CHEVLEFT` | `caret-left` | Functional |
| `ICON_CHEVRIGHT` | `caret-right` | Functional (submenu, nav) |
| `ICON_PLUS` | `plus` | Functional |
| `ICON_MINUS` | `minus` | Functional (also indeterminate checkbox) |
| `ICON_XMARK` | `x` | Functional (close, dismiss) |
| `ICON_CHECKMARK` | `check` | Functional (selection, checkbox) |
| `ICON_EYE` | `eye` | Functional (password reveal) |
| `ICON_EYE_SLASH` | `eye-slash` | Functional (password hide) |
| `ICON_TRASH` | `trash` | Functional (delete) |
| `ICON_STAR` | `star` | Functional (favorite) |
| `ICON_SUN` | `sun` | Functional (light theme) |
| `ICON_MOON` | `moon` | Functional (dark theme) |
| `ICON_GEAR` | `gear` | Functional (settings) |
| `ICON_COPY` | `copy` | Functional (copy) |
| `ICON_RESET` | `arrow-counter-clockwise` | Functional (reset) |
| `ICON_LIST` | `list-bullets` | Functional (list view) |
| `ICON_GRID` | `squares-four` | Functional (grid view) |
| `ICON_USER` | `user` | Avatar fallback when no image and no initials are provided (§9.12) |
| `ICON_MAGNIFYING_GLASS` | `magnifying-glass` | Search-style Input leading icon (e.g., Polaris-parity search field). NOTE: Combobox dropped this leading icon in May 2026 — see `ICON_CARET_UP_DOWN` and §9.15. |
| `ICON_CARET_UP_DOWN` | `caret-up-down` | Combobox trailing affordance — canonical "popup value picker" iconography shared with Apple HIG popup buttons, Tailwind Catalyst Combobox/Select, Headless UI Listbox/Combobox, and shadcn Combobox (lucide `chevrons-up-down`). Stacked-caret glyph reads as "click to open a list and the chosen value swaps in place," semantically distinct from a single `ICON_CHEVDOWN` which reads as a disclosure. See §9.15. |
| `ICON_CALENDAR` | `calendar-blank` | Date-style Input leading icon; Date picker trigger glyph (§11 Input row) |
| `ICON_IMAGE` | `image` | Card media-slot placeholder for empty-state "image goes here" (§9.13). Explicit-semantic glyph per Standing Rule #8 — the icon's role IS to communicate "image content here," so it's the right opt-in over the universal `ICON_CIRCLE` placeholder. |
| `ICON_EYEDROPPER` | `eyedropper` | Color Picker eyedropper-tool button glyph — sample a color from the canvas surface (§Color Picker). Explicit-semantic glyph per Standing Rule #8 — the icon's role IS to communicate "sample a color from elsewhere," matching the convention across Figma / Sketch / Adobe Color / Apple ColorPanel where the eyedropper is the canonical visual for the pick-from-canvas affordance. |
| `ICON_COMMAND` | `command` | Kbd-badge glyph (⌘) — see §9.9 |
| `ICON_OPTION` | `option` | Kbd-badge glyph (⌥) — see §9.9 |
| `ICON_BACKSPACE` | `backspace` | Kbd-badge glyph (⌫) — see §9.9 |

**Usage rules (per Standing Rule #8):**
- **Universal neutral placeholder** (anywhere an icon slot exists but the glyph isn't the subject of the demo, including Toast tone marker): `ICON_CIRCLE`. Reads as truly neutral — Phosphor's `circle` is a perfectly empty ring. One mental model across the entire system, no per-component placeholder split.
- **Toast tone**: tone is conveyed by **color** (background tint, border, icon stroke), never by the glyph. The default icon is `ICON_CIRCLE` for all tones (neutral, info, success, warning, danger). Consumers can opt into a different glyph per call (`<Toast icon={ICON_INFO} ...>`, `<Toast icon={ICON_CHECKMARK} tone="success" ...>`) when explicit semantic reinforcement is desired, but the system default is neutral.
- **Functional icons** must use the glyph that names the action: `ICON_XMARK` for close, `ICON_CHECKMARK` for selection, `ICON_EYE`/`ICON_EYE_SLASH` for password, `ICON_CHEVDOWN` for disclosure, `ICON_CHEVRIGHT` for submenu, `ICON_TRASH` for delete, `ICON_GEAR` for settings, `ICON_COPY` for copy.
- **Never use `ICON_STAR` as a placeholder** — it implies favoriting.

### 5.3.1 Why Phosphor Regular, not SF Symbols

The system originally shipped icons as SF Symbols PUA codepoints (`String.fromCodePoint(0x100185)` for checkmark, etc.) loaded through the SF Pro font. We migrated to Phosphor for these reasons:

- **PUA codepoints aren't stable across SF Symbols versions.** Apple ships SF Symbols 4 / 5 / 6 with different Private Use Area layouts; the same codepoint can render as a completely different glyph depending on which SF Pro font version the user's OS has installed. We hit this in production: `0x100262` rendered as `paperclip` instead of `info.circle` on a fresh install. There's no way to pin "the codepoint that means info.circle" — Apple owns the registry and changes it between releases.
- **SF Symbols isn't license-clean for the web.** Apple's SF Symbols license restricts use to "Apple platforms" — a strict reading excludes a web design system intended for any browser/OS. Phosphor is MIT-licensed.
- **Cross-platform coverage matters.** SF Pro renders SF Symbols only on macOS / iOS / browsers with SF Pro installed. Windows, Linux, and any browser without SF Pro show codepoint replacement boxes (`?`) or wrong glyphs from a fallback font. Phosphor SVGs render identically everywhere because the geometry travels with the markup.
- **Special-key glyphs were a font-fallback bug.** DM Mono (our body font) lacks `⌫` (U+232B Erase to the Left), so kbd badges fell back to a system font with a different stroke weight than `⌘` next to it — visually inconsistent. Solved by routing Mac modifier glyphs (`⌘` / `⌥` / `⌫`) through Phosphor SVG icons via the unified `kbdGroup` renderer (see §9.9). Letter keys and multi-letter keys (Shift, Esc) still render as text in the same badge for cross-platform readability.
- **Why Regular weight specifically — system-wide invariant.** Phosphor ships 6 weights (Thin / Light / Regular / Bold / Fill / Duotone). Regular is optically tuned at small sizes — strokes don't visually thin out at 12px (Dropdown menu) the way Light would. At 24px (Toast) Regular still looks balanced, not heavy. DM Mono is a 400-weight body font; Regular icons match it for visual weight across text + icon pairs. **Every icon in the system uses Regular, with no exceptions** — including the kbd modifier glyphs (⌘ ⌥ ⌫). Bold was briefly trialled for kbd icons on the rationale that they "stand in for printed keycap labels"; rejected on review because the heavier weight read as visually noisy in the menu context (where the surrounding rows are quiet 12/16 mono text and a chevron at Regular). One weight, one rule, no drift. Bold / Fill / Duotone weights are reserved for potential future active-state treatment of icons that need to escalate, not for stroke-weight matching with adjacent text.
- **Why we picked Phosphor over Lucide.** Both are excellent open-source MIT-licensed icon sets with similar visual language. Phosphor's "Regular" weight has a slightly softer, friendlier geometry than Lucide's stricter line work, and ships more weight options (Lucide has one). For a designer-forward system that may add fill/duotone/bold for states later, Phosphor's multi-weight catalog is the more durable foundation. Both render identically through our wrapper system, so the choice is reversible if needed.

**SVG conventions (used by every `ICON_*` constant in `index.html`):**
```
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 256 256"
     width="1em" height="1em"
     fill="currentColor"
     style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:middle">
  <path d="..." />
</svg>
```
- `viewBox="0 0 256 256"`: Phosphor's canonical canvas. All weights/icons share it.
- `fill="currentColor"`: color flows from the wrapper's `color` token. No `stroke` — Phosphor Regular is a filled icon (paths use internal cutouts via even-odd fill-rule to create the outlined look). One-line color override; no per-icon stroke-width tuning.
- `width="1em" height="1em"`: scales the SVG to parent `font-size` as a defensive default for any non-wrapper context. The wrapper's `> svg { width / height }` rule (§6.1.3) overrides this to the canonical icon-frame size.
- `style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:middle"`: defensive default for non-flex contexts. In our flex wrappers it's a no-op.

**Updating an icon:** Fetch the SVG from `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@phosphor-icons/core@latest/assets/regular/{name}.svg`, paste the `<path>` into the existing constant, and ensure the wrapping `<svg>` keeps the conventions above. Do not change weight without updating the spec — mixed weights would break the visual rhythm.

