## Part 6 — Layout & Spacing

### 6.0 Invocation phrases (cheat-sheet)

When auditing a component's layout, three short phrases invoke the three system-wide rules. Each maps to one section. If you're checking visual layout and don't know which rule applies, this table is the entry point:

| Phrase | Section | What it checks | Typical question that triggers it |
|---|---|---|---|
| **"uniform visible inset"** | §6.1, §6.1.2 | Hard-edged sub-elements (icons, badges, kbd clusters, buttons) inside a backplate have **corner-symmetric visible inset** to the surface. Formula: `padding-x-override = (container-h − control-h) / 2`. Priority rule: when text and hard-edged share an alignment edge, hard-edged wins. | "Does this icon have equal padding on all four sides relative to the surface corner?" |
| **"concentric corners"** | §2.1.2 | A rounded element nested inside another rounded surface follows `inner_radius = outer_radius − inset` (strict regime when `inset ≤ ½ outer_radius`; soft regime minimizes radius spread otherwise). | "Does this rounded thing sitting inside another rounded thing look right at the corner?" |
| **"leading-induced inset"** (or "cap-top inset") | §6.1.4 | The CSS line-box reserves leading above the first line's cap-top. This creates a 2-4px gap between `padding-top` and visible cap-top on every text-bearing surface. The phenomenon is system-wide, accepted, and not compensated. Surfaces when a hard-edged shape (icon glyph) sits adjacent to text — the icon's edge gives the eye a reference and the leading offset becomes visible. | "Why does the icon look slightly higher than the title?" |

These three rules are **independent** — they govern different geometric relationships and can fire in combination on the same component. Example: in a multi-line Toast with leading icon, all three apply: padding satisfies "uniform visible inset" (icon glyph 12V/12H around centered, 10V/12H multi-line); the 16-radius surface contains 14-radius footer buttons satisfying "concentric corners"; and the icon glyph appears 2-3px above title cap-top via "leading-induced inset." All three are correct simultaneously; understanding which rule governs which observation is what unblocks the audit.

### 6.1 Optical padding

**The single rule.** For any content inside a backplate, the goal is **uniform _visible_ inset to the content's _visible_ edge on all sides**. The implementation falls into two cases that are both expressions of this same goal:

| Case | Content | Visible edge | Padding rule |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A. Hard-edged content** | Standalone icon, backplated badge (kbd, counter, status pill), button-like sub-element | DOM edge of the element (its visible edge equals its bounding box) | **Equal padding all sides**: `padding = (container − element) / 2`. For bordered containers, subtract 1px. |
| **B. Text content** | Single-line text in a fixed-height control (centered), or multi-line text in a flexible container (top-aligned) | **Cap-top** (top of capital letters), which sits ~3–6px below the line-box top because of half-leading + ascent-above-cap | **Slightly more horizontal padding** (raw): `padding-py = padding-px − leading_per_side`. The line-box already provides the missing vertical breathing for free; horizontal has no equivalent buffer, so raw padding-px must be larger. |

**Why this is one rule, not two**: both cases produce the same outcome — uniform visible inset around the content. They differ only in what counts as the "visible edge" and how much of the padding is implicit vs explicit. Hard-edged content has no leading, so all padding is explicit and must be equal. Text has leading, so raw padding-py is reduced by the leading amount and the leading does the rest.

**Two flavors of Case B by layout style** — same rule, different mechanics:

- **Single-line text in a fixed-height control** (Button, Nav item, Input value, Badge, Tooltip): text is vertically centered. `padding-py` is **implicit** (= `(container-height − line-height) / 2`). The visible cap-top inset becomes `(container-height − cap-height) / 2`, which lands close to `padding-px`. You only set `padding-px`; the height + line-height + flex-centering does the vertical work. This is why Nav, Button, and Input "just work" without manual padding-py tuning.
- **Multi-line text in a flexible-height container** (Textarea, Card body, Banner body, Dialog body): text is top-aligned. `padding-py` is **explicit** and must be tuned to `padding-px − leading_per_side`.

**Computing leading_per_side.** For any font:
```
leading_per_side ≈ (line-height − cap-height) / 2 + (ascent − cap-height)
                ≈ (line-height − 0.7 × em) / 2 + 0.1 × em
```
For DM Mono (used as `var(--font)` body in this system):

| Font tier | em | line-height | cap-height (~0.7 em) | ascent (~0.8 em) | leading_per_side |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 12/16 (`body/s`, `label/s`) | 12 | 16 | 8.4 | 9.6 | ~3.2 |
| 14/20 (`body/m`) | 14 | 20 | 9.8 | 11.2 | ~4.4 |
| 16/24 (`body/l`, `label/l`) | 16 | 24 | 11.2 | 12.8 | ~5.6 |

**How to apply Case B (multi-line) — Textarea worked through.** For each tier, pick `padding-py` from the spacing ramp closest to `padding-px − leading_per_side`. Targeting **slight V dominance** (visible V > H by ~1–1.5px) is the canonical pattern across the system because that's what Nav single-line produces naturally — see "match nav" below.

| Tier | font | leading | px | ideal py | ramp py | visible H | visible V | Δ (V − H) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| xs | 12/16 | 3.2 | 6 | 2.8 | 4 | 6 | 7.2 | +1.2 |
| s | 12/16 | 3.2 | 6 | 2.8 | 4 | 6 | 7.2 | +1.2 |
| m | 12/16 | 3.2 | 8 | 4.8 | **6** (was 4) | 8 | 9.2 | +1.2 |
| l | 14/20 | 4.4 | 12 | 7.6 | 8 | 12 | 12.4 | +0.4 |
| xl | 16/24 | 5.6 | 16 | 10.4 | 12 | 16 | 17.6 | +1.6 |

All five tiers now produce **visible V > H by 0.4–1.6px** — the same direction and same order of magnitude as Nav single-line text (Δ +1.8 at the M tier). Earlier `--txa-py: 4` at the M tier was the only outlier (Δ −0.8, H dominant), introduced because the formula coincidentally aligned with the smaller XS/S `--txa-py` at the same font tier and was never re-derived for the larger px. Now corrected.

**The "match Nav" sanity check** — for any text tier, `visible V` should be approximately what Nav produces for the same font in its naturally-centered row. For 12/16 in Nav M (h = 28): visible V = `(28 − 8.4) / 2 ≈ 9.8`. Textarea M after fix produces visible V = 9.2 — within 0.6px of Nav. For 14/20 in Nav L (h = 32): visible V = 11.1 vs Textarea L 12.4 (within 1.3). For 16/24 in Nav XL (h = 40): visible V = 14.4 vs Textarea XL 17.6 (within 3.2 — the one looser case, see ramp-rounding note).

**Ramp-rounding note**: at the XL tier, the ideal `padding-py` is 10.4, which falls between the ramp's 8 and 12. Picking 8 yields visible H by 2.4 (clearly H dominant) — wrong direction. Picking 12 yields V by 1.6 (matches the Δ pattern of every other tier). 12 is the right choice even though it's slightly off from "perfectly equal" — uniformity of _direction_ across tiers matters more than minimizing the absolute deviation at any single tier. If a tier ever needed an exact match, the path is to add a `--space-2s = 10` step to the spacing ramp (analogous to `--radius-2s = 10` already added for the same kind of in-between need); the ramp-extension rule is in §2.1.1.

**Auto-apply with `:has()`**:
```css
/* Case A: hard-edged content auto-centers via per-edge override */
.ds-comp:has(> .ds-comp__icon:first-child) { padding-left: calc((var(--h) - var(--icon)) / 2 - 1px); }
.ds-comp:has(> .ds-comp__keys) { padding-right: calc((var(--h) - 20px) / 2); /* 20 = kbd height */ }
```

**Universal scope — applies to small badges and labels too, not only "controls."** This rule is the *most violated* one in the system and the failure mode is always the same: a small bordered container (kbd badge, tag, dot label, indicator) gets a single `padding: 0 Xpx` value chosen for the text case, and then renders an icon glyph inside that same padding. The icon's intrinsic width plus L/R padding plus border exceeds the box's `min-width`, the box expands horizontally, and the visible inset around the glyph becomes more horizontal than vertical — the exact bug this section forbids.

**Sanity-check checklist for any container that may hold either an icon OR text** (kbd badge, tag, indicator dot label, future Chip, future status pill):

1. **Compute the horizontal inset around an icon glyph at the smallest content size.** The formula with `box-sizing: border-box`, padding `0 P`, border `B` per side, content (icon) width `I`, declared `width` or `min-width` `W`:
   - Required intrinsic width = `I + 2P + 2B`.
   - If required > `W`: box expands; visible L/R inset = `P + B`. Visible T/B inset = `B + (W − 2B − I) / 2` (flex-centered).
   - For inset to be uniform: `P + B = B + (W − 2B − I) / 2` → `P = (W − 2B − I) / 2`.
2. **If the math doesn't yield uniform inset for both icon and text content with a single padding value, split the rule** via `:has()`:
   - Default rule: `padding: 0 P` tuned for the text case (so multi-character text doesn't crowd the edge).
   - Icon override: `:has(> .icon-wrapper) { width: W; padding: 0 }` — explicit `width` (not just `min-width`) clamps the box to a square, and `padding: 0` lets flex-centering produce uniform inset.
3. **Don't try to make the "letter" case and the "icon" case visually identical at the geometry level** (e.g., 20×20 box with same padding for both). They have different intrinsic widths; matching geometry forces matching padding, which then violates one of the two cases. Match them at the **inset-around-the-glyph level** instead — uniform around icons, horizontal-dominant around text. The container can be different sizes for different content; that's correct, not a bug.

**Cases where this rule has been (mis)applied historically**:
- ✅ Nav item leading icon: `padding-left: calc((--ni-h − --ni-icon) / 2)` — produces uniform inset around icon at every Nav tier. Canonical.
- ✅ Button leading/trailing icon: same formula. Canonical.
- ✅ Input/Select leading icon: `calc((--inp-h − --inp-icon) / 2 − 1px)` (the `-1px` accounts for the border). Canonical.
- ✅ Toast: per-edge override per §6.1.2 collapses padding to `--py` on icon-anchored corners. Canonical.
- ❌ → ✅ **Kbd badge** (Tooltip + Dropdown): originally `padding: 0 6px` produced 24×20 icon kbds (more horizontal than vertical inset around the 12px ⌘ glyph); shrunk to `padding: 0 4px` made it 22×20 (still asymmetric); finally fixed with `:has(> .ds-kbd__icon) { width: 20px; padding: 0 }` → 20×20 with 4px uniform inset. See §9.9 for the math. The lesson: **shrinking the padding number doesn't fix the asymmetry, it just reduces it.** The fix is to remove horizontal padding for the icon case entirely and clamp the box to a square via explicit `width`.

#### 6.1.1 Horizontal-vertical padding ratio for text-bearing surfaces
The `space/*` ramp gives us a small set of valid padding pairs that satisfy "horizontal > vertical." The common pairings used in the system:

| Component class | Padding (vert / horiz) | Tokens | Ratio |
|---|---|---|---|
| Tight overlay (Tooltip) | **uniform 8/8 surface + 2V/4H body wrapper** → visible ~12H / ~12V | `space-s` surface + `space-3xs / space-2xs` wrapper | see §6.1.5 (uniform-8/8 family) |
| Floating notification (Toast) | **uniform 8/8 surface + 0V/2H body wrapper** → visible 10H / ~12V | `space-s` surface + `0 / space-3xs` wrapper | see §6.1.5 (uniform-8/8 family, compact-identity body) |
| Banner inline notice | **uniform 8/8 surface + 2V/4H body wrapper** → visible 12H / ~12V | `space-s` surface + `space-3xs / space-2xs` wrapper | see §6.1.5 (uniform-8/8 family) |
| Card | 12 / 12 | `space-m / space-m` | 1.0 (generous surface pad; 0V/2H wrapper for leading-delta only) |
| Dialog | 12 / 12 | `space-m / space-m` | 1.0 (exception — see below) |
| Textarea XS | 4 / 6 | `space-2xs / space-xs` | 1.5 |
| Textarea S | 4 / 6 | `space-2xs / space-xs` | 1.5 |
| Textarea M | 4 / 8 | `space-2xs / space-s` | 2.0 (visibly ~uniform — see note) |
| Textarea L | 8 / 12 | `space-s / space-m` | 1.5 |
| Textarea XL | 12 / 16 | `space-m / space-l` | 1.33 |

**Textarea per-tier mapping — why these specific pairs.** Textarea is the only "text fills the surface" component that comes in five tiers. Each tier picks a (vertical, horizontal) pair from this table such that the **visible top inset to the cap-top of the first line** (= `--txa-py + (line-height − cap-height) / 2`) lands close to or slightly above `--txa-px`. The line-box leading does most of the work — at S/M (font 12/16, leading ≈ 3.8 each side) the visible top inset is ~`--txa-py + 4`, so a small `--txa-py` is enough; at XL (font 16/24, leading ≈ 6.4 each side) the leading is bigger but so is `--txa-px` (16), so `--txa-py` has to grow with it. Concretely after fix:

| Tier | font | leading (per side) | `--txa-px` | `--txa-py` | Visible H | Visible V |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| xs | 12/16 | 3.8 | 6 | 4 | 6 | 7.8 (V dominant) |
| s | 12/16 | 3.8 | 6 | 4 | 6 | 7.8 (V dominant) |
| m | 12/16 | 3.8 | 8 | 4 | 8 | 7.8 (~uniform) |
| l | 14/20 | 5.1 | 12 | 8 | 12 | 13.1 (V dominant) |
| xl | 16/24 | 6.4 | 16 | 12 | 16 | 18.4 (V dominant) |

The earlier XL value (`--txa-py: 8`) gave **16H × 14.4V** (H dominant by 1.6) — the only tier that visibly violated the rule. The fix bumps `--txa-py` from `space-s` (8) to `space-m` (12), bringing the visible inset to **16H × 18.4V** (V dominant, matches the leading-induced offset documented in §6.1.4 and consistent with every other tier).

**Why M is acceptable at the 2.0 raw ratio.** It's the only tier where `--txa-py` and `--txa-px` are two ramp steps apart, but the visible inset (8H × 7.8V, ~uniform) is fine because at the 12/16 font tier the leading happens to absorb exactly the gap. We could theoretically promote M to `6/8` (raw ratio 1.33), but the visible result would be 8H × 9.8V (V dominant by 1.8), introducing more downward shift than M needs — the M tier is the dense form-row tier, where compactness matters more than maximal text breathing.

**Exceptions (when 1:1 surface padding is acceptable)**:
- **Large containers with internal vertical structure** (`Dialog`, `Card`): surface padding is uniform (Dialog 12/12, Card 12/12) because the generous ≥12 pad provides full breathing and the section gaps consume the vertical rhythm, so 1:1 reads balanced. Text inside gets only a 0V/2H wrapper for leading-delta absorption (§6.1.5 tier).
- **Uniform-8/8 family** (`Toast`, `Banner`, `Tooltip`): surface padding is uniform 8/8 (NOT asymmetric) — so buttons living as direct flex children pick up corner-symmetric 8V/8H inset per §6.1.2 with no per-edge override. The visible horizontal > vertical text inset is produced by the **stacked body wrapper** (2V/4H for Banner/Tooltip → 12H visible; 0V/2H for Toast → 10H visible), not by the surface padding. See §6.1.5 for the full architecture and why the old per-component asymmetric-surface schemes (Toast 8/12, Banner 12/16, Tooltip 8/12) were retired.

For text-bearing surfaces, the **visible** text inset is horizontal > vertical. The mechanism differs by family: large containers (Dialog/Card) get it from generous uniform surface pad + line-box leading; the uniform-8/8 family gets it from the stacked body wrapper. If a corner control (close button, badge) sits as a direct child of the surface, the uniform 8/8 surface pad already gives it corner-symmetric inset — no per-edge override needed (see Part 6.1.2 below).

#### 6.1.2 Per-edge padding override — extra horizontal padding only when text sits at the edge

> **Invocation phrase**: "uniform visible inset" → invokes the per-edge padding rule (`padding-x-override = (container-h − control-h) / 2` per §6.1.2; priority rule for shared-alignment cases per §6.1.2). Use this when verifying that hard-edged sub-elements (icons, badges, kbd clusters, buttons inside a backplate) have corner-symmetric visible inset to the surface they sit in. Parallel to "concentric corners" (§2.1.2 nested-radius rule) and "leading-induced inset" (§6.1.4 cap-top phenomenon).
The horizontal > vertical rule (Part 6.1, 6.1.1) is about giving **text** the breathing room it needs because the line-box doesn't provide horizontal buffer. That rationale **does not apply to control-anchored edges**: an icon, badge, button, or close affordance is self-contained chrome that doesn't need extra horizontal padding to feel balanced — it needs **symmetric corner inset** so it doesn't read as floating further from one edge than the other.

**The rule (universal — applies to any container with text and controls inside it)**:
> When a side of a text-bearing surface is anchored by a control (icon, badge, button, close, chevron, action), that side's padding must collapse to the value that makes the control's **corner inset symmetric with the perpendicular (vertical) inset**. Apply the override via `:has()` selectors on the surface, never on the control.

This is a refinement of Part 6.1, not a contradiction. Part 6.1 says "horizontal > vertical for text"; 6.1.2 says "but only on edges where text actually sits."

**Two formula variants** for the same principle, picked based on surface shape:

| Surface shape | Formula | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Fixed-height** (Button, Badge, Input, Select, Nav) | `calc((var(--h) - var(--icon)) / 2 - 1px)` | Icon is vertically centered by flexbox in a known height, so its top/bottom inset is `(h - icon) / 2`. Matching horizontal inset gives the icon equal padding on all four sides. The `-1px` accounts for the 1px border so the visual icon-to-edge distance matches top/bottom. (Drop the `-1px` for borderless surfaces like Button.) |
| **Variable-height** (Toast, future Banner / Card / popover bodies) | `var(--py)` | No single "height" to compute against; the vertical padding is the explicit `--py` token. Match it directly. |

**Audit — components that already implement this rule**:

| Component | Trigger | Override (left / right) |
|---|---|---|
| `Button` | leading icon, trailing icon | `padding-left/right: calc((var(--btn-h) - var(--btn-icon)) / 2)` |
| `Badge` | leading icon | **no override needed** — `--bdg-px` is derived per tier from `(--bdg-h - --bdg-icon) / 2` so the base padding already equals the corner-symmetric value (S: 4 = `space-2xs`; M: 6 = `space-xs`). Default text-only padding and icon+text padding match at every tier; L/R raw values are uniform. The earlier rev set `--bdg-px` to a "case-B asymmetric for text" value (S: 6, M: 8) and used a `:has()` override to pull padding-left into the corner-symmetric value when an icon was present — producing visibly asymmetric raw L/R (S: 4L/6R; M: 6L/8R). The fix collapsed the two paths into one: `--bdg-px` now lands on the icon-formula value at every tier, so the override is redundant and is removed. Text-only Badges become 4px tighter overall (matches shadcn / Linear / Atlassian Lozenge density). |
| `Input` | leading icon, trailing icon, trailing action | `padding-left/right: calc((var(--inp-h) - var(--inp-icon)) / 2 - 1px)` |
| `Select` | leading icon, trailing chevron | `padding-left/right: calc((var(--sel-h) - var(--sel-icon)) / 2 - 1px)` |
| `Nav item` | leading icon, trailing badge, trailing chevron | `padding-left/right: calc((var(--ni-h) - var(--ni-icon)) / 2)` |
| `Toast` / `Banner` (uniform-8/8 family) | any direct-child control (icon wrapper, close button, actions) | **no override** — surface padding is uniform `space-s` (8) on all sides per §6.1.5, so every direct child gets corner-symmetric 8V/8H inset automatically. Text inset (H>V) is produced by the stacked body wrapper, not the surface. |
| `Tooltip` | trailing kbd cluster (rare: leading kbd) | **no override** — uniform 8/8 surface (§6.1.5); kbd cluster as a direct child gets corner-symmetric 8/8 |
| `Tooltip` (multi-row, `--keys-below`) | leftmost kbd anchors left edge in bottom row | uniform 8/8 surface gives 8/8 around the leftmost kbd; title text accepts the same 8H raw (visible V > H by ~3 from leading) per priority rule below |

Any new component that fits "text + optional inline controls" must follow this pattern. The `:has()` selector lives on the surface (e.g., `.ds-comp:has(>.ds-comp__icon:first-child)`) and overrides the surface's own padding for that side.

##### Generalized formula — the override matches *visible V inset around the centered control*, not raw V padding

The Button / Badge / Input / Select / Nav formulas all compute `(container-h − control-h) / 2`. This is the **visible V inset around the control after vertical centering**, which equals the raw V padding only when the control fills the row (control-h ≈ container-h − 2·padding-y). For tightly-fitting controls (Button leading icon, Nav item leading icon), the row IS the control's height plus padding, so visible V = raw padding-y, and the simpler shorthand `var(--py)` works.

For variable-height containers where the row is **driven by a different (taller) child**, the formula must NOT be shortcut to `var(--py)`. The visible V inset around a smaller child is `(container-h − child-h) / 2`, which is *larger* than `--py`. Using `--py` as the override under-shoots and produces visible V > H around the smaller child.

**Corrected formula (universal)**:
```
padding-x-override = (container-h − control-h) / 2
```

When the control fills the row, this collapses to `--py`. When the control is smaller than the row, it equals more.

**The uniform-8/8 family (Toast / Banner / Tooltip) sidesteps this entirely** — see §6.1.5. Because their surface padding is uniform `space-s` (8) on all sides and every inner control (icon wrapper, body, buttons cluster) is a **direct flex child**, each control gets corner-symmetric 8V/8H inset from the surface padding alone, with no per-edge override. The leading icon is wrapped in a **square** (Toast 20×20, Banner 24×24); the square wrapper as a direct child gets 8/8 around its bounding box, and the glyph centered inside reads ~10/10 from the surface edge — visually centered on the title-line in single-line mode. This replaces the old asymmetric-surface + per-edge-override scheme the previous spec rev documented (`--tst-px` 12 / `--tst-py` 8 with a `padding-left` override), which had produced visible V>H around the glyph. The override formula above is still the canonical tool for any *future* variable-height surface that reintroduces asymmetric surface padding.

**Multi-line caveat (Toast / Banner title + description)** — see §6.1.5 for the canonical icon-wrapper architecture: the wrapper is a **square** (Toast 20×20 around the 16-icon, Banner 24×24 around the 20-icon) with **2px symmetric internal padding** (`--tst-icon-pad: space-3xs` = 2). With `align-items: flex-start` in multi-line, the square wrapper top-aligns at the surface padding while the body's first-line row carries line-box leading, leaving the glyph 2-3px above the title cap-top. That residual is the system-wide leading-induced inset per §6.1.4 — accepted across every modern toast/banner reference (Apple HIG, M3, shadcn, Sonner, Atlassian, Primer Flash). The earlier V-compensated wrapper (Toast 20W×24H, Banner 24W×24H — internal V-padding > H to land 0px glyph-to-cap alignment) was **dropped** because it produced asymmetric internal chrome and a 44px toast min-height; the 2-3px square-wrapper residual is the accepted trade. See `design-system-history.mdc` for the full rev-by-rev (Rev D/E V-compensated → square successor).

##### Priority rule — when text and hard-edged sub-elements share an alignment edge

When a backplate's content includes BOTH text and hard-edged sub-elements (icons, badges, kbd clusters, button-like backplates) that **share an alignment edge** — meaning both anchor the same edge of the surface — the hard-edged sub-element's uniform-visible-inset takes priority. Text accepts slightly uneven raw-vs-visible padding.

**Why hard-edged wins**: hard-edged backplates have their visible edge equal to their bounding box, so any asymmetry between H and V inset around them is immediately visible — the corner reads as "stuck on" or "off-balance." Text has a line-box that provides ~2–4px of vertical leading "for free" above the cap-top and below the baseline, so text can accept raw padding equal to the hard-edged element's corner-symmetric value AND still read as having balanced visible inset (the leading absorbs the V/H difference). The system-wide stance: when forced to choose, prioritize the more-visible failure mode (hard-edged corner asymmetry) over the less-visible one (text visible-V slightly > visible-H).

**Concrete consequence** — multi-row Tooltip (`--keys-below` mode):
- Title text and trailing kbd cluster both anchor the LEFT edge (shared alignment).
- Surface padding is uniform 8/8 (§6.1.5), so the leftmost kbd gets corner-symmetric H=8, V=8 ✓ for free — no override needed.
- The title text in the top row carries the body wrapper's 4H (uniform-8/8 family → 12H visible from the outer left); against the kbd's 8H below it the title reads slightly more inset, which is the accepted shared-edge trade (hard-edged kbd wins corner-symmetry, text leans on leading + wrapper).

**Where the priority rule mattered historically** — it was written for the old asymmetric-surface model (Tooltip `--tt-px` 12 / `--tt-py` 8) where a per-edge override had to collapse padding-left to 8 for kbd corner-symmetry. Under the uniform-8/8 architecture the surface is already 8/8, so the override is moot — the rule is preserved here for any future surface that reintroduces asymmetric surface padding with shared-edge content.

**How padding resolves for `Toast` under the uniform-8/8 architecture** (40-tall toast, surface pad uniform 8/8 per §6.1.5):

| Variant | Surface padding | Visible inset around each element |
|---|---|---|
| Default (icon + body + close) | uniform 8/8 all sides | Icon (20-square wrapper, direct child) 8V/8H ✓; close (24, direct child) 8V/8H ✓; text gets 0V/2H body wrapper → 10H visible from outer left, V≈12 from leading |
| No icon (body + close) | uniform 8/8 | Text 10H visible (8 surface + 2 body wrapper); close 8V/8H ✓ |
| No close (icon + body) | uniform 8/8 | Icon 8V/8H ✓; text 10H visible |
| Bare text (no icon, no close) | uniform 8/8 | Text 10H visible (8 surface + 2 body wrapper) |

Every control is a direct flex child of the 8/8 surface, so each picks up corner-symmetric 8/8 inset automatically — no per-edge override anywhere. The body's horizontal text inset comes from its own 0V/2H wrapper (§6.1.5 Toast tier), giving 10H visible. This replaces the old `--tst-px(12)/--tst-py(8)` override model entirely.

Text in the body of any variant gets its breathing room from its **0V/2H wrapper** plus the **`--gap`** to its sibling controls (icon and close) — not from any surface horizontal-padding override.

**When this rule does NOT apply**:
- Surfaces that already use **uniform padding by design** (Dialog 12/12, Card 12/12, RadioCard 12/12, and the uniform-8/8 family Toast/Banner/Tooltip per §6.1.5) — there's no asymmetric surface padding to override in the first place. The corners are already symmetric, so direct-child controls pick up corner-symmetric inset for free.
- Surfaces where the **control IS the entire content** at the system level — e.g., a Switch track (the thumb is the only thing inside; there's no "text vs control" distinction to make). Note: Button is NOT one of these; its icon-text duality means the rule applies to it.

**Reverse direction — what to do if you find a system component that violates this rule**:
1. Add `:has()` selectors on the surface to detect the control-anchored side.
2. Pick the formula based on surface shape (fixed-height → optical-centering formula; variable-height → match `--py`).
3. Add a row to the audit table above so the next person can verify coverage.

#### 6.1.3 The universal icon-wrapper pattern (SVG-in-text-context)
**Every icon wrapper in the system uses this pattern.** All system icons are inline Phosphor SVGs (§5.3); there are no codepoint glyphs anywhere in the wrapper world. The "character-style wrapper" (`font-family: "SF Pro"; font-size: <text-fs>; line-height: <text-lh>; text-align: center;` with no flex centering and no `> svg` sizing rule) is **deprecated** and must not be used.

**The pattern:**
```css
.ds-comp__icon {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: var(--comp-icon);             /* icon frame width — owns horizontal extent */
  height: var(--comp-lh);              /* matches text line-height — same vertical extent as a text line */
  line-height: 1;                       /* collapses any inherited line-box; flex centering owns vertical */
  flex-shrink: 0;
  color: var(--comp-icon-color);        /* or inherit, depending on Standing Rule #4 (rule A/B/C) */
}
.ds-comp__icon > svg {
  width: var(--comp-icon);              /* SVG fills the icon frame width */
  height: var(--comp-icon);             /* SVG is square, matches icon frame */
}
```

**Why it works:** the wrapper occupies one text line's worth of vertical space (`height: var(--comp-lh)`), which makes its visual center coincide with the adjacent text's visual center in the same flex row. The `> svg` rule forces the SVG to `var(--comp-icon) × var(--comp-icon)` regardless of its intrinsic `width="1em" height="1em"`, and the flex container centers it inside the wrapper's box. Color flows from the wrapper's `color` token through the SVG's `fill="currentColor"` (Phosphor Regular is filled, not stroked — see §5.3.1).

**Why the explicit `> svg` sizing matters:** Phosphor SVGs ship with `width="1em" height="1em"`, which would render them at parent `font-size`. But icon frames in this system are tier-aligned (`--icon-{xs,s,m,l}` = 12/16/20/24) and decoupled from text size (`--{comp}-fs` = 12/14/16). Without the `> svg` rule, an icon at `font-size: 14px` would render as 14×14 inside a 16-wide frame, leaving a 1px horizontal letterbox — a small but visible asymmetry against adjacent text and other icons. Sizing the SVG directly via the child selector pins the icon to the canonical frame size and keeps every icon in the system optically uniform.

**Why `line-height: 1` on the wrapper, not `var(--comp-lh)`:** inside a flex container, `align-items: center` controls vertical alignment. `line-height: 1` collapses any inherited line-box so it doesn't fight the flex centering. The wrapper's external vertical extent is owned by `height: var(--comp-lh)`, not by any internal line-box.

