#### 6.1.4 Leading-induced cap-top inset (system-wide)

> **Invocation phrase**: "leading-induced inset" (or shorter: "cap-top inset") → invokes this section's rule that the gap between a surface's `padding-top` and its first line of text's cap-top is a CSS line-box property (not drift), it's accepted system-wide, and three documented reasons in this section explain why we don't optically compensate. Use this when verifying icon-vs-text alignment or when somebody asks "why does the icon look slightly higher than the title?" Parallel to "uniform visible inset" (§6.1.2 padding rule) and "concentric corners" (§2.1.2 nested-radius rule).

**The phenomenon.** Every text-bearing surface in CSS has a non-zero gap between its declared `padding-top` and the visible cap-top of its first line of text. The line-box reserves leading above the cap (and a corresponding descent below the baseline); `padding-top` measures to the line-box edge, but the eye reads the cap-top. Approximate leading above the cap, by tier:

| Tier | Font / LH | Leading above cap | Cap inset when `padding-top = 0` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `body/s` | 12/16 mono | ~2px | 2 |
| `body/m` | 14/20 mono | ~3px | 3 |
| `body/l` | 16/24 mono | ~3-4px | 3-4 |
| `display/m` | 16/24 sans | ~3-4px | 3-4 |
| `display/2xl` | 32/40 sans | ~4-6px | 4-6 |

So a Dialog with `padding-top: 16` and a `body/l` title has its title cap-top at ~19-20 from the surface edge. A Toast with `padding-top: 8` and a `body/m` title has its cap-top at ~11. The horizontal padding has no analogous offset — text starts exactly at `padding-left`. The result is a small visible asymmetry on every text-bearing surface in the system.

**Why it's not a bug.** Leading is invisible on plain text — the eye accepts the line-box top as the surface top because there's nothing to compare against. It only surfaces when **a discrete shape sits at the corner**: an icon glyph, an SVG, a checkbox indicator, a leading badge dot. The shape's hard edge gives the eye a reference point, and the leading-induced offset becomes visible.

This is also why the per-edge override in 6.1.2 collapses padding to `--py` for icon-anchored corners: it makes the **wrapper box** corner-symmetric (8/8 in the Toast case). But the wrapper is letterboxed to text line-height (6.1.3) so the **glyph inside** sits offset by the wrapper's leading. The wrapper-corner asymmetry is zero; the glyph-corner asymmetry equals the wrapper's leading; and the title-cap-corner asymmetry equals the line-box's leading. **All three trace to the same CSS line-box leading**, expressed at three different surfaces.

**Why we don't optically compensate.** The natural fix is to subtract leading from `padding-top` and add it to `padding-bottom` — so a uniform `--tst-pad: 8` would become `padding: 6 8 10 8`; a uniform `--dlg-px: 12` would become `padding: 10 12 14 12`. This makes visible cap-top sit flush with horizontal padding. We reject it for three reasons:

1. **It couples padding tokens to typography.** Any line-height change (e.g., bumping `body/m` from 20 to 22) ripples through every surface's padding. The padding ramp would no longer be a pure spacing ramp — it would encode font metrics. That cross-coupling is exactly what the token system is built to avoid.
2. **It breaks bottom-corner symmetry.** A Button (or any control) sitting at the bottom edge of an optically-padded surface would have a 19px corner inset on bottom and 13px on top. The asymmetry now lives between two control corners, which is *worse* than between a control corner and a text cap — controls are equally hard-edged on both ends.
3. **The cost outweighs the perceptual gain.** The asymmetry is 2-4px. Outside of side-by-side comparison the eye accepts it, and the system already accepts it on every text-bearing surface today.

**Industry alignment.** Apple HIG, Material 3, shadcn/Radix, Atlassian, Linear, Sonner, Primer all carry this same asymmetry. None optically compensate. Their padding ramps are spacing ramps, not text-metric ramps — same stance as this system.

**Where it shows up here** (descending visibility):

| Surface | Visible asymmetry | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Toast multi-line with icon | ~2px (glyph corner: 8/10) | Most visible — icon ring is hard-edged at the left corner |
| Dialog title cap-top vs left edge | ~3-4px | Visible when title is short |
| Button leading icon vs top edge | ~2-3px | Mostly hidden by line-box centering in the control height |
| Input leading icon | ~2-3px | Hidden by line-box centering in 32/40/48 control |
| Body description first line | ~2-3px | Invisible — no adjacent hard edge to compare against |

**Forward note: `text-box: trim-both` / leading-trim.** New CSS that crops the line-box so cap-top sits flush with the box top. With one declaration on every text container, all five rows above collapse to zero asymmetry, and the icon letterbox auto-follows because `--lh` would equal cap-height. Status as of this writing: Safari 18.2+ ships it, Chrome 133+ behind a flag, Firefox not yet shipped — roughly 75-80% of users today. Plan: revisit when Chrome ships unflagged and Firefox lands support; at that point migrate text-bearing surfaces to `text-box: trim-both` and shrink icon wrappers from `--lh` height to `--icon-size` height (the letterbox is no longer needed when the cap and the line-box top coincide). Until then, the leading-induced inset is system-wide and accepted; this section is the canonical reference, and per-component notes should link here rather than re-explaining it.

#### 6.1.5 Nested text-wrapper padding — the minimal-offset rule (system-wide)

> **Invocation phrase**: "minimal text offset" → invokes this rule. **Every text node in every component carries a minimal H/V padding** — just enough to absorb the line-box leading delta between cap-height and line-height. Parallel to "uniform visible inset" (§6.1.2), "concentric corners" (§2.1.2), and "leading-induced inset" (§6.1.4) — together these are the four system-wide geometric invariants.

**The rule (universal — applies to EVERY text-bearing element in EVERY surface, including standalone text outside contained surfaces):**

> Pick a vertical padding V for the text from the smallest space-ramp steps (0 or 2). Then set horizontal padding H = **V + one ramp step**. Adjacent icons (when present as siblings to the text wrapper) get their own wrapper at the same V/V padding — same V as the text, with symmetric horizontal padding equal to V (not H). Whether the padding lives on a wrapper `<div>` or directly on the text element (`<label>`, `<legend>`, `<span>`, `<p>`) is an implementation detail; the visible padding is what counts.

The pairing tiers, in ramp order:

| V | H | Icon wrapper | When to use | Components |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **0** (`space-0`) | **2** (`space-3xs`) | 0V / 0H | (a) Standalone surfaces with stacked sections **and generous surface padding ≥ 12** (Dialog/Card), where the surface's own padding provides full breathing and the text only needs the 2H leading absorption. (b) Standalone text **outside** contained surfaces (Field labels, captions, legends, validation messages) — no surface padding to lean on, but 2H still absorbs leading delta and gives a tiny consistent buffer across every text node in the system. | **Dialog**, **Card**, **Field label/caption/required asterisk**, **Fieldset legend/caption/validation** |
| **2** (`space-3xs`) | **4** (`space-2xs`) | 2V / 2H | **Uniform-8/8 family** — small overlay surfaces with tight 8/8 surface padding. Text wrapper supplements with cell-level V breathing because 8/8 alone is too tight against the surface corners; H goes one step higher to maintain the case-B asymmetric ratio system-wide. Surfaces in this family share architecturally (8/8 surface + square icon wrapper aligned to text line-height + 2V/4H body wrapper) even though their row/stack arrangements differ. | **Toast**, **Banner**, **Tooltip** |

The choice of tier follows the surrounding architecture, not preference. If the surface is a member of the uniform-8/8 family (Toast/Banner/Tooltip — small overlay surfaces with tight 8/8 surface padding), text uses 2V/4H to supplement the tight surface pad. If the surface has generous padding ≥ 12 (Dialog, Card) or text stands alone outside any surface (Field label, Fieldset legend), 0V/2H is enough — surface padding (when present) does the V breathing.

**The uniform-8/8 family — surface architecture rule (May 2026, system-wide)**:

Toast, Banner, and Tooltip all share **one architectural pattern**:
1. **Surface padding** = uniform `space-s` (8) on all sides — derived from §2.1.2 inverse rule against the inner-button radii (Button XS/S radius 8 + surface pad 8 = surface radius 16 = `radius-m`). Same 8 on both axes so buttons living as direct flex children of the surface (Toast close, Banner dismiss) pick up corner-symmetric 8V/8H inset automatically per §6.1.2 — no per-edge override needed.
2. **Icon wrapper** (when present — Toast/Banner; absent on Tooltip) = **square**, dimensions equal text line-height in both axes. Toast: 20×20 around the 16-icon (`--tst-lh` = 20). Banner: 24×24 around the 20-icon (`--bn-lh` = 20, accepting a 4px ascent buffer from line-box leading). Internal padding 2px symmetric on all sides. Square per the family rule: dimensions match text line-height so icon-glyph shares a vertical center with the title cap-line in single-line layouts. Replaces earlier V-compensated wrappers that used asymmetric internal padding (V > H) to achieve 0px multi-line glyph-to-cap alignment; the V-compensation was correct math but produced asymmetric internal chrome and added complexity. The 2-3px multi-line residual is within §6.1.4 leading-induced inset tolerance.
3. **Body wrapper** = 2V/4H (`--{comp}-body-py: space-3xs` 2, `--{comp}-body-px: space-2xs` 4). Tooltip uses the same tier on its text wrapper (no icon to pair with) so it visually matches Banner's text inset (12H from outer left). Toast's body is the one outlier — uses 0V/2H because Toast's compact-overlay identity (40px min-height floor, 360px max-width) favors tighter horizontal text inset (10H) vs Banner's in-flow comfortable breathing (12H).

**Why one family for three components**: Toast, Banner, and Tooltip are all "small overlay or in-flow notification surfaces" with similar dimensions, similar density requirements, and similar inner-content patterns (text + optional icon + optional buttons/kbd). Sharing one architecture means: (a) consumers learn the chrome once and recognize it across all three, (b) the §6.1.2 corner-symmetric-inset rule fires identically for each, (c) future changes to the family (e.g., a tighter surface pad ramp) reflow cleanly across all three. The earlier separate per-component padding schemes (Toast: 8V/12H surface, Banner: 12V/16H surface, Tooltip: 8V/12H asymmetric) created three distinct chrome rhythms that competed for visual identity; the uniform-8/8 family rule resolves them into one.

**Tier exclusivity — Card/Dialog stay in the 0V/2H tier**: Card and Dialog are explicitly NOT in the uniform-8/8 family. Card uses `space-m` (12) surface padding; Dialog uses 12/12. Both are larger, content-heavy surfaces where the 12px surface pad alone provides comfortable text breathing. Adding a 2V/4H text wrapper on top of 12/12 would push the text 14H from the outer surface edge — too generous for the surface's compact identity. Card/Dialog's 0V/2H wrapper supplements the 12/12 surface only for leading-delta absorption; the 12px surface pad does the bulk of the H/V breathing.

**Standalone text — why 0V/2H matters outside a contained surface (May 2026 extension).** Field labels, fieldset legends, captions, and validation messages historically rendered with no horizontal padding — text glyphs hugged the field's left edge exactly. That looked correct against most adjacent controls (an Input with its own `--inp-px` padding starts text well-inset from its border, so the label sitting flush with the Input's outer border edge read as roughly aligned with the visible chrome above). But it failed in two contexts:
1. **When a label sits inside a contained surface with its own text-wrapper** (Dialog body containing a Field), the label's text starts 2px to the LEFT of the surrounding body text — visually inconsistent.
2. **When two labels stack vertically and one carries a different DOM ancestry** (a Field inside a Fieldset, a Field inside a Card body), the per-element padding diverged unless every label happened to inherit the same zero.

Adding `padding: 0 2px` to every Field/Fieldset text member makes the rule local-and-uniform: regardless of where the label sits, it carries the same 2H buffer. The text's visible left edge is now consistently `parent-content-edge + 2`, which composes cleanly with whatever surface padding sits above it. Industry alignment: this is a niche choice (most modern systems leave Field labels at 0 horizontal padding — Material 3, shadcn, Radix, Atlassian, Apple HIG), but the consistency benefit across nested surfaces outweighs the 2px shift.

**Universal direct-wrapper padding (May 2026 ratification).** The earlier "two satisfaction paths" framing (direct wrapper vs parent-padding satisfaction) was retired. Across the system, **every text-bearing element gets a direct wrapper carrying 0V/2H padding (or 2V/4H for row-architecture surfaces)** — including atomic inline-flex labels (Button, Badge, Chip, Nav item, Menu item, Select panel item, Tabs trigger, Segmented Control item, Breadcrumb, Bottom Nav, Select trigger value). The exception list is small and explicit (see "Exclusions" below).

**Why universal direct wrappers, not parent-padding satisfaction.** Three reasons converged in May 2026 after side-by-side review of Button + Badge labels with and without the wrapper:

1. **Consistency reads stronger than per-component gap exceptions.** The old objection ("a wrapper inflates the icon-to-text gap by 2px, breaking the §2.3 control-gap table") is real, and is now resolved by **mechanical compensation** rather than acceptance: every component that has both an icon wrapper and a text wrapper subtracts 2px from its declared `gap` via `calc(<§2.3 token> − var(--space-3xs, 2px))`. The result is that the §2.3 control-gap table represents the **visible** icon-to-text rhythm exactly, declared gaps are 2px tighter on every compensated component, and the §2.3 calibration survives the wrapper change intact. See "The 2px gap-compensation rule" below for the in-scope component table.
2. **Removes the cognitive cost of "which path does this component use?"** The earlier two-path framing meant every new component required a judgement: "is this atomic enough that parent-padding satisfies?" Two-path systems drift over time because the judgement criteria are subjective. Universal direct wrappers remove the question — every text node gets a wrapper, period.
3. **The wrapper enables tier scaling without per-component code.** When a future density variant ships (compact mode, comfortable mode, etc.), the wrapper's `var(--space-3xs, 2px)` token re-flows automatically. Atomic elements without wrappers would need per-component re-tuning to track the density shift.

**The exact rule (universal):**

> Every text-bearing element in every component MUST carry direct horizontal padding via a wrapper element (or `padding` directly on the text element when it's already a `<label>` / `<legend>` / `<span>`-style atom). Pick the tier from the table below based on the surrounding architecture.

| Tier | Padding | When | Components |
|---|---|---|---|
| **0V / 2H** | `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` | Default — applies to most text. Stacked layouts with generous surface pad ≥ 12 (Dialog/Card body), standalone text (Field labels/captions/legends), row-control labels (Checkbox/Radio/Switch row, RadioCard, DropZone, DatePicker title), Toast body (uniform-8/8 family but compact-identity exception — see family rule above), AND atomic inline-flex labels (Button, Badge, Chip, Nav item, Menu item, Select panel item, Tabs trigger, Segmented Control item, Breadcrumb, Bottom Nav item, Select trigger value) | Most |
| **2V / 4H** | `padding: var(--space-3xs, 2px) var(--space-2xs, 4px)` | Uniform-8/8 family text wrappers — supplements the tight 8/8 surface pad with cell-level V breathing. Whether the wrapper lives on a `__body` flex column (Banner, has icon+text+buttons row siblings) or directly on a text element (Tooltip, no icon) is an implementation detail; the visible 2V/4H inset on the wrapper is what counts. | Banner, Tooltip |

**The icon wrapper rule** (paired with text wrapper, when icon is a glyph sibling to text):

| Text tier | Icon wrapper | Why |
|---|---|---|
| 0V / 2H | 0V / 0H (icon glyph sits flush at parent edge) | Text has 0V → surface→cap-top = leading delta only; icon needs 0V to match. No H on icon — its left edge anchors at parent content-edge. |
| 2V / 4H | 2V / 2H (symmetric inset around icon glyph) | Text has 2V → wrapper gives visible cell structure; icon mirrors with 2V. Symmetric 2H around icon keeps corner-symmetric inset on the icon's leading-edge side. |

**Exclusions — when direct wrapper padding is NOT applied:**

1. **Icon-only controls** (close buttons, chevrons, dismiss icons) — no text to wrap.
2. **Avatar initials** — 1–2 characters centered in a square/circle. Adding 2H padding would push the initials off-center.
3. **Pagination ellipsis** — `…` glyph centered in a square slot. Adding padding doesn't help (centered char already has letterboxed breathing on both sides).
4. **Pagination status text** (`Page X of Y`, etc.) — already has 8px `padding` on the status element itself (parent), which is > 2px and satisfies the inset directly on the same element.
5. **Native `<input>` / `<textarea>` value text** — browsers don't allow wrapper elements inside form controls; the input's parent (`.ds-input`, `.ds-textarea`) carries `--inp-px` ≥ 6px which is well over 2px, satisfying the inset on the parent.
6. **Table cell content** — `<td>` and `<th>` already carry `padding: var(--tbl-py) var(--tbl-px)` (8/12 at M); the cell IS the wrapper.

**The 2px gap-compensation rule (May 2026, universal, no exceptions).** When an atomic inline-flex control has both an icon-wrapper sibling and a text-wrapper sibling, the text wrapper's 2H adds 2px to the visible icon-to-text distance relative to the `--*-gap` declared in the parent flex. To preserve the optical icon-to-text rhythm calibrated in the §2.3 control-gap table, **the declared `gap` is compensated by subtracting 2px from the tokenized value** via `calc(var(--space-*) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))`. Example at Button M: original `gap: var(--space-2xs, 4px)` becomes `gap: calc(var(--space-2xs, 4px) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` = 2px declared, + 2px from the label wrapper = **4px visible glyph-to-text-edge** — exactly the §2.3 target. The compensation lands the visible gap on the §2.3 target every time; declared gap = §2.3 token − 2px wrapper offset.

**Scope of compensation** (in lockstep across the system):

| Compensated (declared = token − 2) | Component | Variable |
|---|---|---|
| ✓ | Button | `--btn-gap` |
| ✓ | Badge | `--bdg-gap` |
| ✓ | Nav (vertical + horizontal) | `--ni-gap` |
| ✓ | Chip | `--chip-gap` |
| ✓ | Dropdown menu item | `--mnu-item-gap` + inline `.ds-menu__item` rule |
| ✓ | Select panel item | inline `.ds-select-panel__item` rule |
| ✓ | Select trigger | `--sel-gap` |
| ✓ | Tabs trigger | `--tab-gap` |
| ✓ | Segmented Control item | `--seg-gap` |
| ✓ | Breadcrumb inner-gap (icon ↔ label inside one segment) | `--bc-inner-gap` |
| ✓ | Tooltip inline gap (content ↔ kbd cluster) | inline `.ds-tooltip` rule |
| ✓ | Row controls (Checkbox / Radio / Switch row) | inline row rules |
| ✓ | RadioCard | `--rc-gap` |
| **Not compensated** — Banner / Toast | spacing between icon ↔ body ↔ buttons is from `__body` own H padding, not from a flex `gap` (`.ds-toast__row` has `gap: 0`). Wrapper math already balanced. | — |
| **Not compensated** — Bottom Nav | icon + label stack vertically; no horizontal icon-text `gap` to compensate. | — |
| **Not compensated** — Breadcrumb inter-segment `--bc-gap` | gap is between segments (chevron-separated), not between icon and text inside one segment. The wrapper sits inside one segment and doesn't affect inter-segment spacing. | — |
| **Not compensated** — Tooltip `--tt-keys-gap` | gap between adjacent kbd badges inside a cluster (kbd-to-kbd), not text-to-kbd. Kbd is a chrome atom with its own internal padding, not a text wrapper. | — |
| **Not compensated** — Input / Textarea | native form control; no text wrapper to offset. Parent `--inp-px` already provides the inset. | — |
| **Not compensated** — Composer `--cmp-gap` / `--cmp-cluster-gap` | gaps are between inter-row sections (`--cmp-gap` vertical) and between sibling Buttons inside a cluster (`--cmp-cluster-gap` horizontal). Neither is an icon-to-text-wrapper pair. | — |
| **Not compensated** — Dialog `--dlg-gap` | vertical gap between header / body / footer sections. Not an icon-to-text pair. | — |

The compensation is mechanical: subtract 2px wherever a horizontal icon-wrapper and text-wrapper sit as flex siblings with a declared `gap` that's calibrated against the §2.3 control-gap table. The result is that **the §2.3 control-gap table now represents the visible target, not the declared token** — declared values are 2px tighter on every compensated component to land the visible value on §2.3. This restores the §2.3 calibration the wrapper would otherwise have inflated.

**Why subtraction, not gap-inflation acceptance** (May 2026 revision of the May 2026 ratification): the earlier May 2026 ratification accepted +2px gap inflation across atomic inline-flex controls as the trade for universal wrapper consistency. On side-by-side review, the inflated rhythm read slightly looser than intended — Button M visible gap landed at 6 vs the §2.3 target of 4, which produced a noticeable density shift across icon+text controls. Mechanical subtraction via `calc()` lets us keep BOTH the universal text wrapper AND the §2.3 target. The previous "treat the table as minimum gaps" framing is **superseded** — the table now represents the visible icon-to-text gap exactly, with declared gaps offset by −2 wherever a text wrapper is present.

The audit table below documents every text-bearing element in the system after the May 2026 universal-wrapper pass.

**Why text needs ANY wrapper even when it sits in a stacked layout (Dialog, Card, Tooltip)**. Before May 2026 Dialog/Card/Tooltip text rendered as bare text nodes inside the surface — no wrapper, surface padding flowed directly to the text. That made surface padding do two jobs at once: outer breathing AND text-leading absorption. The result was that **surface padding had to be larger than its "outer chrome" role alone justified**, because if you tightened the surface pad to match the surrounding chrome (e.g., dropping Dialog from 16 to 12), the text's leading-induced offset (§6.1.4) became visibly off — text appeared to "stick up" relative to the horizontal pad. Adding a 0V/2H text wrapper separates concerns: surface padding becomes purely about outer chrome (matching the §2.1.2 inverse-rule cascade against the inner content), and the text wrapper absorbs leading delta independently. Now Dialog can tighten to 12/12 surface pad and the text still reads optically flush.

**Why icons need a wrapper that's SQUARE in the uniform-8/8 family**. Previously (Rev D/E) Toast and Banner used V-compensated icon wrappers — wrapper V matched the text wrapper's V (so visible top/bottom inset stayed uniform across the row), with wrapper H smaller (just the symmetric icon-cell H). That math was correct but produced asymmetric internal chrome (V > H around the icon glyph) and added complexity. In the uniform-8/8 family (May 2026 redesign), icon wrappers are **SQUARE — V = H = text line-height**:

| Surface | Text wrapper | Icon wrapper outer | Icon glyph | Wrapper internal pad |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Toast (8/8 surface) | 0V / 2H | 20 × 20 (= `--tst-lh` 20) | 16 | 2V / 2H symmetric |
| Banner (8/8 surface) | 2V / 4H | 24 × 24 (rounded up from `--bn-lh` 20 + ~4 ascent buffer) | 20 | 2V / 2H symmetric |
| Tooltip (8/8 surface) | 2V / 4H | n/a (no icon) | n/a | n/a |
| Dialog (12/12 surface) | 0V / 2H | 0V / 0H wrapper when icon present (icon glyph flush at surface-pad edge) | n/a | n/a |

The square-wrapper rule makes the icon-glyph share a vertical center with the title cap-line in single-line layouts. The 2-3px multi-line residual (when content stacks and the icon stays top-aligned) is within §6.1.4 leading-induced inset tolerance and accepted system-wide (Apple HIG, M3 snackbar, Radix, shadcn, Sonner, Atlassian Banner, Primer Flash all share this residual).

**The math (worked out)**:

*Dialog (0V/2H tier, surface 12/12, no icon)*:
- Visible H from surface edge to text glyph: 12 + 2 = **14**
- Visible V from surface edge to text cap-top: 12 + 0 + ~3 (leading per §6.1.4) = **15**
- Ratio: 14H / 15V — V dominant by 1, matches the canonical leading-induced inset pattern

*Toast (uniform-8/8 family, 0V/2H text tier exception, surface 8/8)*:
- Visible H from surface edge to text glyph: 8 + 2 = **10**
- Visible V from surface edge to text cap-top: 8 + 0 + ~3 (leading) = **11**
- Visible inset around icon glyph (16-icon in 20×20 wrapper, surface 8 around wrapper): 8 + 2 = **10V** and 8 + 2 = **10H** — uniform 10/10 around the icon glyph in single-line layouts
- Ratio: 10H / 11V text — V dominant by 1 from leading

*Banner (uniform-8/8 family, 2V/4H text tier, surface 8/8)*:
- Visible H from surface edge to text glyph: 8 + 4 = **12**
- Visible V from surface edge to text cap-top: 8 + 2 + ~3 (leading) = **13**
- Visible inset around icon glyph (20-icon in 24×24 wrapper, surface 8 around wrapper): 8 + 2 = **10V** and 8 + 2 = **10H** — uniform 10/10 around the icon glyph in single-line layouts
- Ratio: 12H / 13V text — V dominant by 1 from leading, same pattern as Dialog

*Tooltip (uniform-8/8 family, 2V/4H text tier, surface 8/8, no icon)*:
- Visible H from surface edge to text glyph: 8 + 4 = **12** (matches Banner)
- Visible V from surface edge to text cap-top: 8 + 2 + ~3 (leading) = **13** (matches Banner)
- Ratio: 12H / 13V — V dominant by 1, same pattern as Banner

In every case the visible ratio lands V-slightly-dominant-from-leading. The uniform-8/8 family share **identical surface architecture** (8/8 surface pad + square icon wrappers when icons are present); only the body wrapper tier differs (Toast at 0V/2H for its compact identity, Banner/Tooltip at 2V/4H for their comfortable in-flow / floating identity).

**Why one ramp step is universal across font tiers — not "match the leading exactly."** A naïve reading would say "horizontal padding should equal leading-per-side" (~3px for 14/20, ~4px for 16/24, ~2px for 12/16). That's correct as a target inset, but it requires per-tier off-ramp values (3, 4, 2 are not all on the spacing ramp). The "one ramp step up from V" pattern is the closest on-ramp approximation for the 14/20 case (target ~3, actual 4 vs base 0 — off by 1px), and degrades gracefully at other tiers (16/24 target ~4, actual 4 — exact at the 2V/4H tier). Industry alignment: Primer Banner / Material 3 Snackbar / the Figma reference (node 306-805 for Dialog, 278-1813 for Banner) all carry a small consistent H>V delta on their nested text wrapper. None compute per-tier leading values.

