**The two-tier choice rule (load-bearing)**:

- **2V / 4H tier (uniform-8/8 family — Banner/Tooltip)**: invoked by **tight 8/8 surface padding** where the surface pad alone is too tight against the corners for comfortable text breathing. The 2V supplements the surface pad with cell-level V breathing; the 4H absorbs leading delta plus a little extra for cluster cohesion (matches the case-B asymmetric ratio system-wide). Visible inset is 12H / 13V (slightly V-dominant from leading).
- **0V / 2H tier (everything else, including Toast body)**: invoked by **stacked architecture with generous surface pad ≥ 12 (Dialog/Card), standalone text, atomic inline-flex labels, AND Toast body (compact-identity exception within the uniform-8/8 family)**. The 0V relies on the surrounding surface's outer pad (or parent control's `--*-px`) to provide V breathing; the wrapper's 2H absorbs leading delta and adds a uniform horizontal buffer. Toast body specifically uses this tier despite being in the uniform-8/8 family because Toast's compact-overlay identity (40px min-height floor, 360px max-width) favors tighter text inset (10H visible vs Banner's 12H) — the family rule prioritizes shared **surface** architecture (8/8 pad + square icon wrapper) over text-wrapper-tier uniformity.

**Icon wrapper rule for the uniform-8/8 family (Banner/Toast — Tooltip has no icon)**:

The icon wrapper is **square**, with outer dimensions equal to the text line-height of the surrounding row, regardless of the body wrapper's V/H tier. This is INDEPENDENT of the text wrapper rule above:

- **Toast** icon: 20×20 wrapper around 16-icon (`--tst-lh` = 20, internal padding = `(20 − 16)/2` = 2 symmetric on all sides).
- **Banner** icon: 24×24 wrapper around 20-icon (`--bn-lh` ≈ 20, wrapper rounded up to 24 to accept a 4px ascent buffer from line-box leading, internal padding = `(24 − 20)/2` = 2 symmetric on all sides).

Square per the family rule: icon wrapper dimensions equal text line-height in both axes, letting the icon-glyph share a vertical center with the title cap-line in single-line layouts. Replaces an earlier V-compensated wrapper that used asymmetric internal padding (V > H) to achieve 0px multi-line glyph-to-cap alignment at the cost of asymmetric internal chrome. The 2-3px multi-line residual is within §6.1.4 leading-induced inset tolerance and accepted system-wide.

**System-wide rule (closed for review)**:
- Any future surface in the uniform-8/8 family (Snackbar, inline alert, future Drawer/Sheet header at the same tight 8/8 tier) → 8/8 surface pad + **2V / 4H text wrapper** + **square icon wrapper sized to text line-height** (with internal padding `(wrapper_dim − icon)/2` symmetric).
- Any future stacked-architecture surface with generous surface pad ≥ 12 (settings panel, content card, popover with vertical sections) → **0V / 2H** text + **0V / 0H** icon wrapper (when icons are present as glyph siblings to text).
- Any future standalone text outside a container (helper text, page heading, form prompt) → **0V / 2H** on the text element itself.
- Any future atomic inline-flex control (a new Pill, Tag, Toggle, Stepper number) → **0V / 2H** wrapper around the text node inside the control AND **`gap: calc(<§2.3 token> − var(--space-3xs, 2px))`** on the parent flex per the gap-compensation rule above. The visible icon-to-text distance then lands exactly on the §2.3 target.
- The rule is universal across the system: every text-bearing element gets a direct wrapper with 0V/2H (or 2V/4H in uniform-8/8 family surfaces). Exclusions are listed in the §6.1.5 audit table and limited to elements that can't take a wrapper (native inputs, table cells) or where adding padding would harm the layout (centered initials, centered ellipsis).

### 6.2 Form spacing rhythm
Title → content → footer reads as three zones, not a flat stack.

| Zone | Gap to next | Implementation |
|------|-------------|----------------|
| Header (label/legend) | 12px before content | label `margin-bottom: var(--space-s, 8px)` + parent gap 4px |
| Content (controls) | 8px before footer | Parent gap 4px compounds with margin |
| Footer (caption/validation) | — | Single line, no extra margin |

### 6.3 Fieldset/group spacing
- Legend → items (no caption): 12px (`margin-top: var(--space-m, 12px)` on items)
- Caption → items: 8px (gap 4px + margin 4px)
- Items → validation: 8px
- Legend is NOT a flex item in fieldsets — always use `margin-top` on the next element

### 6.4 Row alignment
- Default: `align-items: center`
- With description: `align-items: flex-start` + 2px `margin-top` on control to center with first text line

### 6.5 Layout gap rule (between separately-instantiated controls)
Internal control gaps (icon ↔ text inside one Button) are governed by spec 2.3. Inter-section gaps inside a complex component (Dialog header ↔ body ↔ footer) are owned by that component (`--dlg-gap`, `--mnu-gap`, etc.). **External gaps** — the space between two separately-instantiated controls in a row or column — were historically ad hoc (`gap: 16px`, `gap: 8px` inline). They MUST now resolve through the existing `space/*` tokens per the table below. **No new tokens are introduced** for layout gaps; the `space/*` ramp is the single source of truth.

| Layout pattern | Gap | Token | Examples |
|---|---|---|---|
| Joined controls | 0 | n/a | Button group, segmented control inner buttons |
| Tight cluster | 4 | `space-2xs` | Toolbar of icon-only buttons, kbd badge cluster, badge dot row |
| Default cluster | 8 | `space-s` | Row of 2-3 labeled buttons, filter chip row, page-action toolbar |
| Form row (horizontal) | 12 | `space-m` | Input + Select side-by-side in one form row |
| Form field stack (vertical) | 16 | `space-l` | Vertical column of Input → Input → Input |
| Form section | 24 | `space-xl` | Between two fieldsets in the same form |
| Page section | 32–48 | `space-2xl` / `space-3xl` | Between major content zones on a page |

**Two design principles behind the table:**
1. **The external gap MUST be ≥ the larger control's internal gap.** A row of M buttons (internal gap = 6) cannot be packed tighter than 6px. `space-s` (8) is the safe floor for default clusters, which also matches the L-tier's internal gap.
2. **Vertical stacks of "the same kind of thing" want exactly one full step (`space-l` = 16).** Anything tighter looks crowded; anything looser breaks the perception of "one form."

**Drift policy**: any inline `gap: Npx` in CSS or HTML/JS template strings is drift. Replace with `var(--space-X, Npx)` from the table. The only sanctioned raw `gap` exceptions are visualization elements that are *demonstrating* a literal pixel value (e.g., a swatch card showing what `space/6` looks like — even there, `var(--space-xs, 6px)` is preferred for consistency).

