## Part 7 — Component Anatomy

### 7.1 Documentation structure
Every `render{Name}()` produces:
1. `<h2 class="h-group">` + description
2. **Default** — compFrame with single instance
3. **Sizes** — `<h3>Sizes</h3>` + S/M/L
4. **States** — `<h3>States</h3>` + all states
5. **Variants** — `<h3>Variants</h3>` + `variantBlock()` per concept

### 7.1.1 Demo layout — comp-frame previews
`.comp-frame__preview` is the surface that holds rendered component instances inside docs. It uses `flex` with `gap: var(--space-2xl, 32px)` so any **direct children** (sibling component instances) are separated by 32px. This is the canonical "between variants" spacing — never override it locally.

Three demo intents map to three layout modes:

| Intent | What it shows | Layout | API |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Variant comparison — row** | Side-by-side configurations of one component (Tones: 5 toasts with different `tone` props; horizontal Avatar sizes) | Direct children of `.comp-frame__preview`, 32px gap | `compFrame(html, code)` |
| **Variant comparison — column with shared left edge** | Vertically stacked variants of one component where each instance has a different intrinsic width (Checkbox states: "Unchecked" vs "Disabled checked"; Input sizes XS-XL with varying control heights; Pagination tiers; horizontal toolbars at different tiers). Without a wrapper, each instance would center-align individually in the frame and the group would visually "shimmer" left and right based on each row's content width. | **Wrap in an inline-styled flex column** with `gap: var(--space-2xl, 32px)` (matches the frame's direct-children gap), `align-items: flex-start` (children share a left edge), and an **explicit width** from the canonical demo-width table below (or no width when the contents naturally size — used for `align-items: stretch` cases like Banner that fill 100% of frame inner content) | `compFrame(html, code)` — the wrapper itself is one centered child of the frame, no `{ layout: "column" }` needed |
| **Production behavior** | What the component looks like in its real product context (Stacked toasts in a viewport, dialog inside a backdrop) | Wrapped in a production container class (`.ds-toast-stack`, etc.) which is a single child of the preview and provides its own internal gap | Standard `compFrame(html, code)` |

**Canonical demo-width table** (use one of these widths when wrapping a variant-comparison column; matches what the system already uses for state/sizes stacks across components):

| Width | Components using it | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| **200px** | Nav vertical Sizes | One step below 220 — Nav vertical is "one step denser" than form controls per Standing Rule §2.4, so its demo width follows the same denser-by-one rule |
| **220px** | Input, Select, Checkbox, Radio, Switch, Field demos | The system's canonical "field state stack" width. Tight enough to look like a discrete documentation unit (not a production form), comfortable enough to fit longest typical state labels like "Disabled checked" (~140px content + ~70px breathing). Industry production form widths are 280-320 (shadcn / Material 3 / Atlassian / Apple HIG settings); ours is intentionally tighter for docs density |
| **240px** | InputGroup, Combobox | One ramp step wider than 220 (220 → 240) because joined-chrome components carry multiple segments and need room for each to read without crowding |
| **280px** | Progress, Segmented Control | Wider than Input because horizontal-stretch components (progress bar, segmented toggle) benefit from more horizontal real estate to read clearly |
| **320px** | Textarea, Card horizontal | Multi-line text needs more horizontal room than single-line; Textarea's natural width is taller and wider than a single-line Input |
| **360px** | RadioCard, BottomNav | Card-style selection controls and bottom nav need room for their richer content (description, multiple items) |
| **480px** | Tabs | The widest tier because a Tab row at L tier with 3+ triggers + counts naturally consumes more horizontal space |
| **No width (content-width)** | Pagination, Segmented Control, Breadcrumb, Tabs sizes/states demos where the components carry their own intrinsic widths and use `align-items: flex-start` so the wrapper auto-sizes to the widest variant and narrower variants left-align inside it | When the component's own width is large enough that an explicit wrapper width would be redundant or would force wrapping, omit the width and let the flex container size to its widest child |

**Rules:**
- **Never wrap variant-comparison demos in a production container** to get vertical stacking — the production container's internal gap (e.g., `ds-toast-stack` uses 8px to match Sonner) will hide the 32px variant gap.
- **Never inline-style a flex column with a custom gap on demo content** other than `var(--space-2xl, 32px)`. The wrapper's gap MUST match the frame's gap — that's the entire point of the wrapper pattern (children read as if they were direct children of the frame, just with their left edges anchored).
- **`align-items: flex-start`** is the canonical alignment for variant-comparison columns. Children share a left edge regardless of their content width, which makes the group read as a discrete unit centered in the frame instead of a scattered set of independently-centered rows.
- **`align-items: stretch`** is acceptable for components whose natural identity is full-width (Banner, Progress, Toast variants — each stretches to fill the wrapper). The wrapper width then sets the actual rendered width.
- **`align-items: center`** (the frame default for `{ layout: "column" }`) is wrong for variant-comparison columns where children have varying widths — it produces the "off-centered" look where the longest variant defines the visible center and shorter variants visually drift left-right.
- **The previous `{ layout: "column" }` API on `compFrame`** is now used only for production-behavior demos where there's a single centered child (or for legacy demos that haven't been migrated). New code should prefer the inline-styled flex column wrapper documented above.
- **Production-behavior demos are exceptions**, not the default. Only use them when documenting how the component sits in its real surface (toast viewport, drawer, sheet, popover anchor). Label the variant after the behavior (`Stacked`, `Anchored`), not after the layout.

**Why this pattern**: a variant-comparison column without a wrapper has the bug "each row centers individually in the frame, so the visible width of the group varies row-by-row." The longest row anchors the eye to a center, shorter rows drift left of center, and the group reads as visually inconsistent. The wrapper fixes this by making the group itself a single centered unit and anchoring all children to a shared left edge. The wrapper width then becomes the "documentation footprint" of this state stack — tight enough to look like spec content, loose enough to fit longest typical content with breathing room. Industry parallel: shadcn / Material 3 / Atlassian docs all render state demos with implicit "demo container" widths (~280-320 in their production form contexts; ours is tighter at 220 because we're docs-density first).

**Inline-styled flex rows for variant comparison (default: responsive wrap)**: when several large fixed-width component instances (Cards, Avatars, Color Pickers, Date Pickers, Composers, etc.) are placed side-by-side as a variant-comparison row, an inline-styled inner flex IS allowed. **Default behavior (May 2026): row wrappers inside `.comp-frame__preview` wrap responsively.** A system-level CSS rule `.comp-frame__preview > div:not([style*="flex-direction:column"]) { flex-wrap: wrap }` extends `flex-wrap:wrap` to every row wrapper automatically, so demos do NOT need to add `flex-wrap:wrap` to their inline style. The catchall preserves the column-direction state-stack convention (see "variant comparison — column with shared left edge" above) because column wrappers are excluded via the `:not([style*="flex-direction:column"])` predicate — wrap on a column flex creates a multi-column overflow layout that breaks the state-stack rhythm.

When a viewport is too narrow to fit the row, the components break to the next line instead of overflowing the preview's right edge. This matches the system's broader stance that "components compose into the available space rather than overflow it" — same instinct as `min-width: 0` on text-bearing flex children. **Inline `flex-wrap:nowrap` overrides the catchall** (specificity 1,0,0,0 > 0,0,2,1) and is reserved for the rare case where a demo genuinely needs to refuse to wrap (e.g., showing how a row would scroll horizontally at very narrow viewports). The Card Tones demo previously shipped with `flex-wrap:nowrap` (the canonical reference for the convention prior to May 18, 2026) and is now `flex-wrap:wrap` along with every other variant-comparison row.

The inner flex's `gap` MUST be **one of these two values**, no exceptions:

| Case | Gap | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Independent component instances being compared (Card tones, Avatar sizes, Button sizes laid out side-by-side) | `var(--space-2xl, 32px)` | Matches the comp-frame__preview's canonical 32px direct-children gap (§6.5 "Page section" tier — also the default for "between separately-instantiated components in a frame"). The wrapping div is a transparent layout helper, not a semantic group, so the gap reads as "frame gap" not "intra-group gap." |
| A defined group (button group, dropdown menu items, fieldset radio rows, kbd cluster, breadcrumb segments) | The group's own token (e.g., `space-s` 8 for default cluster, `space-2xs` 4 for tight cluster, `space-l` 16 for form-row vertical) | Follows the group's documented internal gap from §6.5 |

**The two-value rule:** if the inner flex is showing variants of one component, gap is **always 32px**. If it's showing a defined group (a real product cluster), gap is the group's own token. There is no "in-between" case. An inline `gap: 16px` on a variant-comparison row is drift unless it's a documented form-field-stack (vertical column of related form fields) — and form-field-stacks should use the column modifier on the preview, not an inner inline-flex.

**Audit:** any `style="display:flex;gap:var(--space-l,16px)"` (or any non-32px gap) wrapping a row of independent component instances is drift. The Card Tones row had this drift in May 2026 (used `space-l` 16) before being corrected to `space-2xl` 32 in lockstep with the square-shape rev.

**State demo consolidation rule (non-negotiable, May 2026)** — when a component documents state escalation (Default / Hover / Focused / Pressed / Error / Disabled / etc.), **all states MUST be stacked inside a single `compFrame` using the variant-comparison-column pattern above**. Do NOT split states across N separate `variantBlock` calls or N separate `compFrame` instances. The split layout forces the reader to scroll between disconnected blocks and breaks the perceptual side-by-side comparison the state matrix exists to enable.

This rule was added after the Drop Zone audit (May 2026 — see `design-system-history.mdc` §B.35) found that Drop Zone was the only component with split state demos at audit time; every other state-bearing component (Composer, Input, Select, Button, Checkbox, Radio, Switch, RadioCard, Tabs, etc.) already followed this pattern. Drop Zone was corrected to stack its five states (Default / Drag over / Focused / Error / Disabled) in one comp-frame with a flex column wrapper at `align-items: flex-start; gap: var(--space-2xl, 32px); width: 480px` (matching the RadioCard/BottomNav demo-width tier because Drop Zone is a card-class layout primitive, not a tier-aligned control).

**Variants** (icons, sizes, specific behaviors) can still occupy separate `compFrame` instances or `variantBlock` calls — the rule fires only for **state matrices** where the comparison value is the per-state visual delta against a baseline.

### 7.2 Naming
- Component titles: **PascalCase** (`RadioGroup`, `TextArea`, `CheckboxGroup`). This is the universal standard across Primer, Radix, shadcn, and React conventions. Never camelCase for component names.
- CSS classes: `ds-{name}` with BEM sub-elements (`ds-nav-item__icon`)

### 7.2.1 Navigation labels (non-negotiable)
- **1-2 words max.** No exceptions.
- **No parentheses.** Ever. Use a distinct short name instead.
- **Sentence caps** (capitalize first word only).
- Drop filler words: "With caption" → "Caption", "With validation (error)" → "Error", "Resize behavior" → "Resize"
- Pattern labels use the concept name directly: "Label first" not "Checkbox after"
- NAV nodes with `children` MUST use `label` (not `t`). Leaf nodes use `t`.

### 7.3 Placeholder copy (non-negotiable)

**Rule of intent**: demo copy exists to make the **visual treatment** legible, not to ship product copy. Default to generic labels that mirror the slot or concept under demonstration; reach for realistic copy only when realism is *itself* the subject of the demo (e.g., a currency input is demonstrating the `$ / 0.00` pattern, not the input chrome). When in doubt, generic.

**Hard rules across the system** (May 2026):
1. **Sentence case only — no ALL CAPS.** No `text-transform: uppercase`, no all-caps glyph strings, no `letter-spacing` tracking that signals a caps treatment. Section dividers, group labels, kbd badges, and "OR"-style separators all read in sentence case (`"Label"`, not `"OR"` or `"LABEL"`). The only sanctioned exception is **Avatar initials**, where uppercase is the typographic convention for name-abbreviation primitives across every modern system (Apple Photos, Material 3, Atlassian, Linear, Notion); the CSS enforces uppercase via `text-transform: uppercase` so consumers don't have to think about case.
2. **Numerals, not number-words.** When a placeholder needs to enumerate interchangeable instances (Option 1 / Option 2 / Option 3, Tab 1 / Tab 2 / Tab 3, Toast 1 / Toast 2 / Toast 3), use **digits** (`1`, `2`, `3`), never spelled-out words (`one`, `two`, `three`). Digits read as "sequence position" at a glance; spelled words read as proper-noun labels. This was changed system-wide May 2026 (previous convention was `"Option one"`, `"Item one"`, `"Tab one"` etc.).
3. **Limit unique placeholder words.** The system uses a small, deliberate vocabulary for placeholders. Components that demo "interchangeable rows / instances" reuse the same generic noun (`Option` for selection groups, `Item` for list rows, plus the component-name + numeral pattern for stacked-instance demos like Tabs/Toasts). New placeholder words are added only when the existing vocabulary genuinely fails to communicate the demo's role; "I'd like a different word here" is not justification.

**Canonical placeholders by demo category**:

| Category | Placeholder | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Default preview | Component name (`"Button"`, `"Input"`, `"Toast"`, `"Dialog"`) | First view of the component — name = identity |
| Size demos | `"Small"`, `"Medium"`, `"Large"`, `"Extra small"`, `"Extra large"` (spelled out, not abbreviated) | The size IS the subject; spelling out matches the system's full-word style across other size labels |
| Role / variant / tone demos | Role name (`"Primary"`, `"Secondary"`, `"Tertiary"`, `"Danger"`, `"Neutral"`, `"Info"`, `"Success"`, `"Warning"`) | The role IS the subject; visual carries via fill/border, label echoes the role |
| State demos | State name (`"Default"`, `"Hover"`, `"Pressed"`, `"Focused"`, `"Disabled"`, `"Destructive"`) | The state IS the subject |
| Selection control groups | `"Option 1"`, `"Option 2"`, `"Option 3"` | Numerals = "these are interchangeable instances by sequence position" |
| List rows (Dropdown items, Nav items, Chip groups) | `"Item 1"`, `"Item 2"`, `"Item 3"` | Same logic as selection groups; rows are interchangeable instances |
| Stacked-instance demos (Tabs, Toasts, Sections) | Component name + numeral: `"Tab 1"`, `"Tab 2"`; `"Toast 1"`, `"Toast 2"`; `"Section 1"`, `"Section 2"` | Stacked-instance demos benefit from echoing the component identity; numerals carry the "position in stack" |
| Inline-position labels (separator vertical demo) | Bare numerals: `"1"`, `"2"`, `"3"` | When the demo's subject is a divider between same-class peers, a numeral is enough — no noun prefix |
| Slot labels (Dialog/Toast/Card subcomponents) | Slot name with sentence caps: `"Title"`, `"Description"`, `"Body"`, `"Label"`, `"Caption"`, `"Placeholder"` | Slot name = the role of that text in the layout |
| Button slots inside compound components | `"Button"` (component name, plural rendered as repeated `"Button"`) | Slot is "a Button"; per Default-preview rule, that resolves to the component name |
| Counters | `"8"` | Single representative integer |
| Required asterisk | `"*"` | The glyph is the marker |
| Validation messages | Slot-named generic: `"Caption"`, `"Error message"`, `"Helper text"` | Treat as captions (slot label) |
| Separator labeled variant | `"Label"` (slot-name convention) | Per the slot-label rule above; replaces the deprecated `"OR"` convention. `"OR"` was removed for two reasons: (a) it violated the no-ALL-CAPS rule, and (b) it implied a specific use case (binary-choice auth pattern) when the labeled separator is a generic primitive |

**Sanctioned exceptions (realistic copy is the subject)** — use realistic copy ONLY when the demo's pedagogical purpose requires it:

| Demo | Allowed realistic copy | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Input — currency pattern | prefix `"$"`, placeholder `"0.00"` | The pattern (currency) is the subject |
| Input — domain pattern | prefix `"https://"`, suffix `".com"`, placeholder `"domain"` | The pattern (URL/domain) is the subject |
| Input — password pattern | placeholder `"Password"`, value `"password123"`, reveal toggle | The pattern (masked → revealed) is the subject |
| Dropdown — default demo | Realistic items (e.g., Edit / Duplicate / Move to folder / Copy link / Delete) | First view of the component shows the production pattern; matches industry convention (shadcn, Radix, Material 3, Atlassian). The realistic copy IS the demonstration of "what dropdowns look like in real apps." |
| Dropdown — shortcuts variant | Realistic action labels paired with their canonical kbd (`Cut ⌘X`, `Copy ⌘C`, `Paste ⌘V`) | Label and shortcut form a single semantic unit; abstracting the label breaks the pairing |
| Dropdown — groups variant | Realistic group labels and items (e.g., `Account` group with `Profile`, `Settings`) | The grouping IS the subject; generic group labels (`"Group one"`) lose the pedagogical value |
| Dropdown — destructive variant | Keep `"Delete"` on the destructive item; abstract siblings to `"Item 1"`, `"Item 2"` | `Delete` is the canonical destructive action across the industry; the destructive item-label IS the convention |
| Tooltip — placement demo | Position name (`"top"`, `"right"`, `"bottom"`, `"left"`) on the trigger button | Position IS the subject |
| Dropdown trigger | `"Actions"` (slot-name convention, not strict component-name) | `"Actions"` is the cross-system slot name for "this opens a dropdown of actions" — used by Atlassian, Material, Radix, shadcn |

**Apply the rule of last resort**: every other demo defaults to generic. If a demo wants to make a "this is the production look" argument, it must justify why generic doesn't carry the visual treatment.

**Failure mode this rule prevents**: realistic copy that *looks* educational but isn't. Demos like Dialog `title="Delete project"` / `description="This action cannot be undone."` LOOK production-ready, but the spec text already documents *when* to use the danger variant; the demo's only job is to show *what it looks like*. Realistic copy in those slots adds noise without adding signal, and creates a copy-edit burden every time the system grows.

### 7.3.1 Page architecture
Content is split into 7 pages (Color, Typography, Density, Shape, Surface, Motion, Components). Each page is a `<section data-page="name">` that is shown/hidden via `.page-active`. Clicking a top-level nav item switches pages; clicking a child nav item scrolls within the active page.

- Each render function's output is wrapped in its page section in `mount()`
- The `pageForId()` function maps any section ID to its page name
- `highlightNav()` calls `showPage()` automatically when the active section changes
- A `navScrollLock` prevents the IntersectionObserver from flickering the active indicator during programmatic scrolls

### 7.4 Description copy rules
- **1 sentence only.** No exceptions. If you need a second sentence, the first one is wrong.
- Complete sentence with subject + verb. No fragments.
- No em dashes, no semicolons, no parenthetical asides.
- No implementation footnotes in prose.
- Reference siblings by PascalCase in `<code>`: "use `CheckboxGroup`"

