## Part 9 — Selection Control Rules

### 9.1 Universal patterns
- Default form is **labeled** (control + text). Bare control is the primitive, not a variant.
- Variants are additive: description, leading icon, label-first, group
- Validation on the **group**, not individual controls
- Wrap entire row in `<label>` for extended click area
- `:active` pressed: `filter: brightness(.92)`
- Touch target: `min-height: var(--control-s, 28px)`
- Indeterminate (checkbox only): must set `aria-checked="mixed"` AND `input.indeterminate = true`

### 9.2 Switch-specific
- Track:thumb = ~1.6:1 width:height, thumb contained (no overhang)
- On = green track, off = `border/default` track, thumb always `surface-default`
- Thumb shadow: `var(--shadow-thumb)` — the only sanctioned use of the special-case thumb elevation token (darker .18 alpha for contrast on a varying-color track)
- Thumb animates with `transform: translateX()`, never `left:` — also keeps the static layout correct under `prefers-reduced-motion`
- HTML: `<input type="checkbox" role="switch">`

### 9.3 Dropdown panel items
Applies to Select, future Combobox, and ActionMenu panels. Dropdown panels are **vertical lists of selectable rows**, the same role family as Nav vertical, and they MUST share Nav vertical's "one step denser" sizing logic — dimensions taken from the control ramp, font from the compact 12/16 tier at S/M/L, stepping up to 14/20 only at XL. This rule is non-negotiable: any list-of-rows component (Nav, Select panel, ActionMenu, Combobox) uses the same height+font matrix below.

| Tier | Item height | Font / line-height | Icon | Justification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| S | `control-xs` (24px) | 12/16 | `icon-xs` (12px) | Densest list density |
| M | `control-s` (28px) | 12/16 | `icon-xs` (12px) | Default — matches Nav vertical M |
| L | `control-m` (32px) | 12/16 | `icon-xs` (12px) | Comfortable list density |
| XL | `control-l` (40px) | 14/20 | `icon-s` (16px) | Step up to body/m text at XL only |

- Item horizontal padding: `space-s` (8px) default. **Per-edge inset rule (universal across all list-of-rows components — non-negotiable)**. Every leading or trailing element inside a row gets a `:has()` override that sets the item's padding on that side to the value that produces uniform glyph/badge inset against the row's hover backplate. The formulas:

| Element | Selector | Padding override |
|---|---|---|
| Leading icon (icon-wrapped, letterboxed to `--lh`) | `:has(> .ds-{comp}__icon:first-child)` | `padding-left: calc((var(--item-h) - var(--icon)) / 2)` |
| Trailing icon (chevron, checkmark — icon-wrapped) | `:has(> .ds-{comp}__chev:last-child)` / `:has(> .ds-{comp}__check:last-child)` | `padding-right: calc((var(--item-h) - var(--icon)) / 2)` |
| Trailing hard-edged badge (kbd cluster, counter, status pill — NOT letterboxed) | `:has(> .ds-{comp}__keys)` / `:has(> .ds-{comp}__badge:last-child)` | `padding-right: calc((var(--item-h) - <badge-h>) / 2)` (badge-h = 20 for kbd, `--bdg-h` for counter badges) |

**Why these formulas, restated**: an icon-wrapper is letterboxed to `--lh` (so it occupies one text-line of vertical space) and contains a smaller glyph centered inside it. The visible **glyph** lands at horizontal inset = `padding-left/right` (since wrapper width = icon size, glyph fills wrapper width) and vertical inset = `(item-h − lh) / 2 + (lh − icon) / 2` = `(item-h − icon) / 2`. So setting padding to `(item-h − icon) / 2` makes the glyph have **uniform inset on all sides**. A hard-edged badge (kbd, counter) is NOT letterboxed; its visible top edge equals its DOM top edge. Vertical inset = `(item-h − badge-h) / 2` from flex-centering. So padding-right = `(item-h − badge-h) / 2` makes the badge corner-symmetric.

**Coverage table — every list-of-rows component must implement these. Non-coverage is drift, not a feature.**

| Component | Leading icon | Trailing chevron | Trailing badge (counter/kbd) | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Nav vertical (`.ds-nav-item`) | ✓ line 213 | ✓ line 215 | ✓ line 214 | complete |
| Nav horizontal (`.ds-nav--horizontal .ds-nav-item`) | inherits from base | inherits | inherits | complete (same rules apply) |
| Dropdown (`.ds-menu__item`) | ✓ added in lockstep | ✓ added in lockstep | ✓ kbd cluster (20-tall) added | complete |
| Select panel (`.ds-select-panel__item`) | n/a (no leading icon today) | ✓ trailing checkmark uses chev formula | n/a (no trailing badge today) | complete for current shape |
| Future Combobox, ActionMenu, command-palette rows | must follow this table | must follow | must follow | TBD |

**Worked numbers — Dropdown all tiers** (formula `(item-h − icon) / 2` for icon, `(item-h − 20) / 2` for kbd):

| Tier | Item h | Icon | Default `--item-px` | Leading-icon padding | Trailing-chev padding | Trailing-kbd padding |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `--s` | 24 | 12 | 8 | **6** (was 8) | **6** (was 8) | **2** (was 8) |
| default | 28 | 12 | 8 | 8 (no change) | 8 (no change) | **4** (was 8) |
| `--l` | 32 | 12 | 8 | **10** (was 8) | **10** (was 8) | **6** (was 8) |
| `--xl` | 40 | 16 | 8 | **12** (was 8) | **12** (was 8) | **10** (was 8) |

**Three tiers of override correctness**: (1) the override is the default value at the M tier — that's why the bug went unnoticed when the spec was first written for M-only; (2) the override moves padding **inward** at the S tier (default 8 was too much for a 24-tall row holding a 12 icon); (3) the override moves padding **outward** at the L/XL tiers (default 8 was too little for a 32/40-tall row). All three corrections trace to the same formula — there's no special case at any tier.

No vertical padding — height is set explicitly via `min-height` and content centers via flex.
- Item border-radius: `radius-2s` (10px), fixed at all sizes. Nests one step inside the panel (panel 14 − panel padding 4 = inner radius 10) per §2.1.2 inverse rule. Bumped from `radius-xs` (8) in May 2026 — see §2.1.1 "Why the +2 cascade hit the anchored-overlay family" for rationale. Locked to one value because the panel is locked — see panel rules below.
- **Panel border-radius: `radius-2m` (14px), fixed at all sizes** (do NOT inherit from trigger). Bumped from `radius-s` (12) in May 2026 in lockstep with the item radius cascade — outer 14 = inner item 10 + panel padding 4 (§2.1.2 inverse rule). Locked because menu surfaces should feel like one cohesive rounder family, with each nesting step +2 below the next: outer 14 → item 10 → kbd badge / nested Badge 6 → atom 4. The previous 12 sat awkwardly between Nav-row (8) and Card (16); the cascaded 14 reads cleanly above Nav and below Card, giving the anchored-overlay family its own identity rather than borrowing radii from neighboring families. Industry alignment for the cascade direction: shadcn 8 (kept flatter — they don't share our system stance of "generous rounds"), Atlassian 4 (also flatter), Material 3 8 (M3 corner radius is generally tighter), Linear 10 — most modern systems sit in the 8-12 band; we sit at 14 because the system-wide brand stance (Standing Rule §1.1: "Generous radius — corners are soft and modern") pushes the panel family one stop rounder.
- Panel padding: `space-2xs` (4px). Panel inter-item gap: `space-2xs` (4px). Gap from trigger: `space-2xs` (4px).
- Panel surface: `surface/raised` background, **`border/subtle` hairline** (Job C — definition stroke per Part 3.5; affordance lives on the trigger, not the panel), `var(--shadow-3)` elevation, `var(--z-anchored, 40)` layer.
- **All list-style rows (Nav vertical, Select panel item, future ActionMenu, future Combobox) MUST share an identical state matrix**, not just identical hover/selected colors:

| State | Treatment |
|---|---|
| Default | text `text/primary`, transparent bg |
| Hover (excluded when selected/active or disabled) | bg `action/hover` |
| Pressed (`:active`) | `filter: brightness(.92)` (the only permitted `brightness()` use) |
| Focus (`:focus-visible`) | `outline: 2px solid border/focus; outline-offset: -2px` (inset outline — these rows are full-width within their container, so the standard outset two-layer halo would clip) |
| Selected / active | bg `action/hover` + `inset 0 0 0 1px border/default` ring + (optionally) trailing checkmark when role is "selection" not "navigation". **No weight bump.** The bg fill + inset ring + (Select) checkmark already carries the state — the previous 400→500 weight bump was a redundant fourth signal that made the row feel heavier than its siblings. Per §5.1.1 weight rule: hierarchy comes from size + color + surface, not weight. Apple Notes/Mail/Reminders, Linear, and Vercel all keep selected list rows at the same weight as default rows for the same reason. |
| Disabled | `text/disabled` color, `cursor: not-allowed`, `pointer-events: none` |
| Transition | `background .15s ease, color .15s ease` |

The trailing checkmark is the dropdown-specific reinforcement that Nav doesn't need; everything else (bg, ring, focus, press, disabled, transition) is identical across both controls.
- Group label: `text/tertiary` color, weight 400 (per §5.1.1 — components default to Regular), 12px/16px (fixed — group labels are metadata, not content, and are kept small at every tier). Height matches the item height of its tier so the row rhythm is unbroken. The previous 500-weight on group labels was redundant with the `text/tertiary` color shift; color carries the "metadata vs content" distinction on its own.
- Separator: 1px `border/subtle`, **true edge-to-edge full-width** via negative horizontal margin equal to the panel's padding: `margin: 0 calc(-1 * var(--mnu-pad))` for Dropdown / `margin: 0 calc(-1 * var(--space-2xs, 4px))` for Select panel. All list-of-rows panels use this pattern because text-alignment is impossible to satisfy across all row variants (items with leading icons start at a different x-offset than items without, group labels at a third, etc.) — the only consistent rule is "stretch to the panel's inner border edge." Vertical spacing comes from the panel `gap` (`space-2xs` = 4 px above and below). Matches shadcn / Radix `DropdownMenu.Separator` (`-mx-1`), Linear, Material 3 menu divider, Apple HIG menus. See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.14 for the three-iteration rev path ("match item padding" inset → `margin: 0` → current negative-margin edge-to-edge).
- Panel: `surface/raised` background, `border/subtle` hairline (Job C — definition stroke per Part 3.5), `radius-s` (12) locked at all sizes — does NOT inherit from trigger; menu surfaces feel quieter than form fields and proportionate to the kbd badges they contain (3-step ramp 12 → 8 → 6). `--shadow-3` elevation, `--z-anchored` (40) layer.
- Panel padding: `space-2xs` (4px)
- **Item gap**: `space-2xs` (4px) — equal to panel padding so each item sits in a uniform 4px envelope (top edge, between siblings, bottom edge), giving the rounded items a "floating tile" rhythm consistent with the system's generous-radius philosophy
- Gap from trigger: `space-2xs` (4px)

### 9.4 Radio card
- One size (M), selected = brand border + halo
- Inner radio focus halo suppressed (card is the focus surface)
- `align-items: center` for label-only; `flex-start` + 2px margin for description

### 9.5 Tooltip
- **Border-radius**: `--radius-2m` (14px) — sits in the system's generous-rounded-rectangle band, smaller than Banner/Toast/Card's `radius-m` (16) tier because Tooltip is a smaller floating surface and the radius-to-height ratio reads more naturally one ramp step down. (Previously `radius-s` (12); see `design-system-history.mdc` for the radius ramp history.)
- **No arrow**: Clean floating labels without arrows, matching Apple HIG, Primer, and M3 (default off). Spatial proximity via `--tt-offset` (4px) provides directional association
- **Offset**: `--tt-offset: var(--space-2xs, 4px)` — tight gap from trigger to tooltip body, consistent across all four placements
- **Size**: 12px/16px font (`body/s` from type ramp), max-width 240px.
- **Internal architecture — uniform 8/8 surface + Banner-tier 2V/4H text wrapper (Rev B, May 2026)**. Tooltip is a member of the **"Toast/Banner/Tooltip uniform-8/8 family"** (see §9.8 Toast and §9.10 Banner for the shared architecture and §6.1.5 for the family rule). Tooltip has no leading icons, so the icon-wrapper slot of the family is absent; the only family-tier slot present is the body wrapper.
  - **SURFACE padding** (`--tt-pad: space-s` 8) — uniform 8/8 outer ring; applied to `.ds-tooltip` directly. Replaces the prior 8V/12H asymmetric padding. With uniform surface padding, the kbd cluster (`.ds-tooltip__keys`, 20-tall hard-edged backplates) sitting as a direct flex child picks up 8V/8H corner-symmetric inset from this padding alone — same §6.1.2 derivation Toast and Banner use for their buttons. The previous `:has()` per-edge overrides for trailing/leading kbd clusters become no-ops (right/left padding already = `--tt-pad`) and are removed.
  - **BODY wrapper padding** (`--tt-body-py: space-3xs` 2, `--tt-body-px: space-2xs` 4) — Banner-tier 2V/4H wrapper (§6.1.5 row-architecture tier) applied to `.ds-tooltip__text`. The prior 0V/2H scheme produced visible 14H / ~11V (visible H = 12 surface + 2 body; visible V = 8 surface + 0 body + ~3 leading) — H-dominant past the §6.1.1 case-B ceiling of ~1.5. The new uniform-8/8 surface + 2V/4H text wrapper produces visible **12H / ~13V** (V-slightly-dominant from leading) — same pattern as Banner, aligning Tooltip's text inset with Banner exactly.
- **Inline gap compensation** (per §6.1.5 universal 2px gap-compensation rule): `.ds-tooltip` declares `gap: calc(var(--space-s, 8px) - var(--tt-body-px))` so the visible distance between the text wrapper's right edge and the trailing kbd cluster lands on the §6.5 default-cluster target of 8px (declared 4 + text wrapper 4H pad = visible 8).
- **Multi-row override** (`--keys-below`): when keys wrap to a separate row (kbd cluster ≥ 3 keys), the title text and the kbd cluster share the left edge at the outer tooltip's `padding-left`. With uniform 8/8 surface padding, no override is needed — the family rule produces the right answer automatically. The previous `.ds-tooltip--keys-below { padding-left: var(--tt-py) }` rule is removed.
- **Shadow**: `var(--shadow-2)` — small floating elevation
- **Stroke**: None in light mode. The `surface-inverse` near-black fill against the light page IS the boundary — adding a stroke would be redundant edge-coding (Part 3.5 "fill carries the boundary" exception). Dark mode behavior: TBD; if the inverse surface lacks contrast against the dark page, add `border/subtle` (Job C — definition stroke)
- **Layer**: `var(--z-tooltip, 50)` — always above anchored overlays so a tooltip on a Select item floats above the panel
- **Shortcut keys**: Always decomposed into individual badges (never combined like "⌘C" in one badge), built via the unified `kbdGroup(keys, "ds-tooltip__kbd")` helper — see §9.9 for the shared key-cap rendering contract. Keys stay inline when 1-2 keys, wrap to a row below when 3+. Inline gap between text and keys: `var(--space-s, 8px)`. Below-row gap: `var(--space-s, 8px)`. Inter-key gap: `var(--space-2xs, 4px)`
- **Multiline clamp**: Max 3 lines via `-webkit-line-clamp: 3`. Tooltips should be brief; if content exceeds 3 lines, use a different pattern (popover, inline help)
- **Demos**: default, shortcut (inline + multi-key side by side), multiline
- **Trigger icons**: Always use the generic placeholder icon (`ICON_CIRCLE`). Do not use functional icons (gear, trash, copy) in demos.

### 9.6 Dialog
- **Border-radius**: `--radius-l` (20px) — DERIVED from the §2.1.2 inverse rule (`outer = inner_radius + inset = Button S radius 8 + surface pad 12 = 20`). Outer is a consequence of inner content (S-tier footer buttons + 12px surface pad), not a free taste choice. Card sits at the same tier with the same math; Dialog and Card are peer surfaces in the same shape family, differentiated by behavior (modal vs inline) rather than radius.
- **Padding**: `--space-m` (12px) — uniform on all sides via `--dlg-px`. Pure outer-chrome role: the close button sits inside the header row at this inset; footer buttons sit at this inset from the bottom. The 0V/2H text wrapper (§6.1.5) absorbs the line-box leading delta independently, so the surface pad doesn't have to do two jobs.
- **Gap**: `--space-s` (8px) — gap between header, body, and footer via flex column gap (`--dlg-gap`). **Intentionally tighter than padding** (3:2 pad:gap ratio, 12/8). Padding owns the *outer chrome*; gap owns *internal separation* between groups. A 1:1 ratio would flatten the visual hierarchy. Industry alignment for compact dialogs: shadcn 16/12 (4:3), Atlassian 16/12 (4:3), Linear 20/16 (5:4) — all keep gap < padding. Our 12/8 (3:2) sits at the tighter end of the band.
- **Text-wrapper padding**: `--dlg-text-px` (`space-3xs` = 2px) — 0V / 2H per §6.1.5 standalone-surface tier. Applied to `.ds-dialog__header-text` (wraps title + description) and `.ds-dialog__body`. The 0V relies on the surface pad (12) to provide V breathing; the 2H absorbs the line-box leading delta between text cap-top and the horizontal chrome edge. Without this wrapper, surface padding had to do two jobs (outer breathing AND text-leading absorption); separating concerns lets the surface stay at 12 (matching the inverse-rule cascade) while text reads optically flush.
- **Shadow**: `var(--shadow-4)` — modal elevation token
- **Layer**: `var(--z-modal, 60)` — modal layer in the global z-index scale
- **Border**: `1px solid border/subtle` (Job C — definition stroke per Part 3.5). The dialog surface (`surface/raised`) is near-white in light mode sitting on a near-white scrim; shadow softens the edge but doesn't crisp it. The subtle hairline tightens the corner.
- **Width**: Single width `440px` max-width. No size variants. Matches Primer's "medium" dialog and sits between shadcn (425) and Linear (480).
- **Structure** (May 2026 architecture):
  - **Header** = `.ds-dialog__header`, a flex-row containing `.ds-dialog__header-text` (text wrapper with title + description) on the left and an inline `.ds-dialog__close` button on the right. The close button is **a sibling of the text wrapper inside the header row**, not absolutely positioned. This lets the row participate in the dialog's column flex layout (header → body → footer) and respects the `--dlg-px` outer pad uniformly.
  - **Header-text** wrapper carries 0V/2H text padding (§6.1.5) and stacks title + description vertically with `--space-2xs` (4) inter-line gap.
  - **Body** = `.ds-dialog__body` with 0V/2H text padding (§6.1.5) — the message content sits in its own wrapper independent of the header and footer.
  - **Footer** = `.ds-dialog__footer`, right-aligned button row with `--space-s` (8) inter-button gap; sits as a direct child of the dialog, picking up the outer `--dlg-px` ring naturally.
- **Title**: 16/24 Medium (DM Mono, **500**, 16/24) in `text/primary`. Weight 500 is justified under the §5.1.1 three-part necessity test: (1) title is locally dominant in the header, (2) at `body/l` it already sits one tier above body (14/20), but the type ramp has no larger tier inside the body family, (3) color cannot carry contrast (both title and body use `text/primary`). All three criteria pass → weight 500 is sanctioned.
- **Description**: 12/16 Regular (DM Mono, 400, 12/16) in `text/secondary`. Sits inside the header-text wrapper below the title with `--space-2xs` (4) gap.
- **Body**: 14/20 Regular (DM Mono, 400, 14/20) in `text/primary`. Matches Input / Button / Select text size so prose and embedded controls share the same baseline. Body MUST be smaller than the 16/24 title — same-size body and title flattens hierarchy. Default has `flex:1 1 auto` and NO overflow rules — focus halos on inputs/textareas would otherwise be clipped horizontally (CSS rule: setting `overflow-y:auto` implicitly computes `overflow-x:auto`, clipping `box-shadow` halos). The `--scroll` modifier adds `overflow-y:auto` only when the dialog has constrained height.
- **Close button**: Real `Button S icon-only ghost-neutral` containing an `ICON_XMARK` glyph (28×28, `radius-xs` 8px, 12px icon at the `icon-xs` tier). **Inline in the header row** as a flex-end sibling of the header-text wrapper (not absolutely positioned). **Tier-by-context rule**: Toast = XS (24, compact float); **Dialog = S (28, primary modal surface)**; Banner = S (28, persistent inline). S is the system's standard inline close tier for primary container surfaces.
- **Stacked footer**: `flex-direction: column-reverse`. Full-width buttons (via `--stacked` modifier).
- **Danger variant**: Title and description stay neutral (`text/primary`, `text/secondary`). The variant's only effect is to swap the footer's right-hand button **fill** from `solid-brand` to `soft-danger`; the layout `[soft-neutral | <primary>]` is unchanged. The destructive CTA is the sole carrier of the danger tone — see Part 3.3.1 "rule of last resort." Industry-standard pattern (Apple HIG, M3, Primer, Radix/shadcn, Linear, Vercel, Stripe, Notion all keep destructive titles neutral).
- **Alert dialog**: Same layout, just `role="alertdialog"` + no close button for forced confirmation.
- **All left-aligned** — no centered variant.
- **Demo presentation**: Shown as standalone cards with drop shadow, no backdrop.
- **Demos**: default (with description), without description, danger, form, scrollable, stacked footer.

### 9.7 Dropdown
A floating list of **actions** anchored to a button trigger. Each item runs a callback; nothing is bound or persisted. Distinct from Select (which picks a value and persists it). The shared list-row primitive (Part 9.3) means Dropdown reuses Select panel's dimensions, state matrix, and dense-list font tier — these are NOT redefined per-component.

**Cross-system equivalents** (so we know which patterns we're inheriting and which we're NOT):

| Role | Ours | Primer | Radix / shadcn | Atlassian | Material 3 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Form value picker | `Select` | `Select` (native) | `Select` | `Select` | `Menu` (filled / outlined) |
| Action menu | **`Dropdown`** | `ActionMenu` | `DropdownMenu` | `DropdownMenu` | `Menu` (action role) |
| Searchable / multi-select / rich panel | (future `Combobox`) | `SelectPanel` | `Combobox` | `Combobox` / `PopupSelect` | (component-specific) |

**Important**: Primer's `SelectPanel` is NOT equivalent to our `Dropdown`. SelectPanel is an *advanced Select* with a built-in search field, multi-select, and richer panel content (footer, etc.). It maps to a future `Combobox` in our system, not to Dropdown. Primer's `ActionMenu` is the action-menu pattern — that's what `Dropdown` maps to.

**Trigger ownership**: Dropdown owns the binding contract (`aria-haspopup="menu"` / `aria-expanded` / `aria-controls`) but does NOT own the trigger's visual style. The trigger is any Button — typically `soft-neutral` with a trailing chevron, or icon-only (kebab) for compact toolbars. The default demo MUST show trigger + open panel together (matching Atlassian/Radix/M3/shadcn) so the binding is visible.

- **Trigger**: a Button or icon-only button (often kebab/three-dots). The component does not own its own trigger styling.
- **Panel**: `surface/raised` background, **`border/subtle` border** (Job C — definition stroke per Part 3.5; affordance lives on the trigger, not the panel, so the panel only needs an ambient hairline against the page), `var(--radius-m)` radius (M default), `var(--shadow-3)` elevation, `var(--z-anchored, 40)` layer, `var(--space-2xs, 4px)` padding, `var(--space-2xs, 4px)` item gap. Identical to Select panel's panel rules.
- **Sizes**: S (24px), M (28px, default), L (32px), XL (40px) — matches the dropdown panel size matrix in 9.3 exactly.
- **Item state matrix**: identical to 9.3 (default / hover / pressed / focus / disabled). **No `is-selected` state** — actions don't persist. **No checkmark** — there's no chosen value to mark.
- **Item content order**: leading icon (optional) → label → trailing affordance (optional, mutually exclusive: kbd shortcut OR submenu chevron, never both)
- **Keyboard shortcut badge**: shares the unified key-cap renderer with Tooltip — see §9.9 for the contract. API: `kbd: ["⌘", "E"]` (atomic array, one badge per key — same rule as Tooltip per §9.5). Mac modifier glyphs `⌘`, `⌥`, `⌫` render as Phosphor icons inside the badge for stroke-weight consistency; letter keys (E, K, C) and multi-letter keys (Shift, Esc, Tab) render as text. Visual: solid `surface/default` bg, `border/subtle` border, `radius-2xs`, 12/16 mono text, `text/secondary`. Use real Unicode glyphs (`⌘`, `⌫`, `⇧`, `⌥`) in the array, not "Cmd+Z" prose.
- **Submenu chevron**: `ICON_CHEVRIGHT` glyph in `text/tertiary`, sized at the panel's icon tier. Mutually exclusive with kbd shortcut on the same item.
- **Destructive variant** (`.ds-menu__item--destructive`): foreground `text/danger` at rest; hover bg `action/soft/danger/bg`; focus outline color swaps to `border/danger`. Used for Delete, Remove, Discard, etc. The trailing kbd badge keeps its standard styling — destructive state lives on text + bg only, not on every nested element.
- **Group label** (`role="presentation"`): same height as items so the row rhythm is unbroken; `text/tertiary`, 500 weight, 12/16 (fixed at all sizes — group labels are metadata, not content). When a group label is present, do NOT also wrap items in a `<fieldset>` or extra container — the label is a sibling row.
- **Separator** (`role="separator"`): 1px `border/subtle`, true edge-to-edge full-width via negative horizontal margin (`margin: 0 calc(-1 * var(--mnu-pad))`) so the line escapes the panel's 4px padding and reaches the inner border edge. Vertical spacing comes from the panel `gap` (4px above and below). See the rule rationale in §9.3.
- **a11y wiring**:
  - Panel: `role="menu"`
  - Items: `role="menuitem"` + `tabindex="-1"` (roving focus pattern; the panel itself or one item has `tabindex="0"` at any time when focus is inside)
  - Disabled items: `aria-disabled="true"` (NOT `disabled` — `disabled` removes them from the focus order, but menu items should still be focus-traversable so users can read why they're unavailable)
  - Trigger: `aria-haspopup="menu"`, `aria-expanded="true|false"`, `aria-controls="<panel-id>"`
  - Closing: Esc, click outside, or selecting an item; focus returns to trigger
- **Keyboard contract**: ↑/↓ to move between items, Home/End for first/last, Enter or Space to activate, Esc to dismiss, →/Enter on a submenu item to open it, ← to close a submenu, type-ahead by first letter
- **Demos**: default (with everything), sizes (S/M/L), states (default / hover / focused / disabled / destructive / destructive hover), and variants (icons, shortcuts, groups, submenu, destructive)

### 9.8 Toast
A brief, non-blocking notification that appears at the screen edge, optionally auto-dismisses, and conveys tone through color + icon. Distinct from `Dialog` (modal, blocks input) and `Tooltip` (anchored to a trigger, no semantic message). Owns the highest overlay layer (`--z-toast: 80`) because toasts must be visible above modals.

**Cross-system equivalents**:

| Role | Ours | Primer | Radix / shadcn | Atlassian | Material 3 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Ephemeral notification | **`Toast`** | `Toast` (Flash) | `Toast` | `Flag` | `Snackbar` |
| Persistent inline notice | (future `Banner`) | `Banner` | `Alert` | `Banner` / `InlineMessage` | `Banner` |

**Important**: Toast and Banner share visual anatomy (icon + body + actions) but differ in behavior: Toast is **overlay + ephemeral + animated**; Banner is **inline + persistent + in document flow**. They are separate components in our system, not variants of one.

**Tone** — naming convention matches `tone` used elsewhere in the system (`action/soft/{tone}/bg`, Badge `tone`, Button `tone`). Five tones: `neutral` (default), `info`, `success`, `warning`, `danger`. NOT "severity" — that term implies an emergency hierarchy, but a `success` toast isn't more severe than `info`; they're different *intents*.

**Treatment** — uses the same soft feedback color treatment as Validation states (Part 3.3): tinted background (50 shade), feedback border (200 shade), feedback icon (500/600 shade), neutral primary text. Tone reads at a glance via icon + color, not via inverted dark fills (we leave dark inverted to Tooltip).

- **Surface**: `surface/raised` background for neutral; `feedback/{tone}/bg` for non-neutral tones. Border 1px **`border/subtle`** for neutral (Job C definition stroke per §3.5 audit; demoted from `border/default` 200 in the May 2026 audit so that "Job C = `border/subtle`, period" is a clean rule across the system) or `feedback/{tone}/border` (200) for non-neutral tones (Job B semantic — tonal communication). Radius `var(--radius-m, 16px)` — same tier as Card / RadioCard, because a toast is a small floating card not a panel anchored to a trigger.
- **Elevation**: `var(--shadow-3)` — same recipe as Dropdown / Select panel. Lighter than Dialog's `shadow-4` because toasts already carry strong visual separation from the bg tint + border; a heavier shadow would compete.
- **Z-layer**: `var(--z-toast, 80)` — fixed for the component.
- **Dimensions**: `min-width: 280px`, `max-width: 360px`, `min-height: var(--control-l, 40px)` (40px floor preserved via `--control-l` after the May-2026 ramp shift — "M=32" would fight Toast's "small floating card with comfortable breathing" identity).
- **Internal architecture — uniform 8/8 surface + square icon wrapper + minimal body wrapper (Rev F, May 2026)**. Toast is a member of the **"Toast/Banner/Tooltip uniform-8/8 family"** — see §6.1.5 for the family rule. Structure is `.ds-toast` (surface, flex-column with gap) containing `.ds-toast__row` (the main flex row) and optionally `.ds-toast__footer` (sibling row below). Inside the row, three sibling elements each own their own padding:
  - **SURFACE padding** (`--tst-pad: space-s` 8) — uniform 8/8 outer ring; applied to `.ds-toast` directly. The XS close button living as a direct flex child of the row picks up corner-symmetric inset from this padding alone — 40-tall toast minus 8/8 surface padding = 24-tall content row, which is exactly the XS close button's height, so 8V/8H around the close ✓ per §6.1.2.
  - **ICON wrapper internal padding** (`--tst-icon-pad: space-3xs` 2, symmetric on all sides) — wrapper becomes **square 20×20** around the 16-icon (icon-s + 2 + 2). Square per the family rule: icon wrapper dimensions equal text line-height in both axes (`--tst-lh` 20 = wrapper W = wrapper H), letting the icon-glyph share a vertical center with the title cap-line in single-line layouts. Replaces an earlier V-compensated 20W×24H wrapper (Rev D/E) that used asymmetric internal padding to achieve 0px multi-line glyph-to-cap alignment at the cost of asymmetric chrome and added complexity. The 2-3px multi-line residual is within §6.1.4 leading-induced inset tolerance and accepted system-wide (Apple HIG, M3 snackbar, Radix, shadcn, Sonner, Atlassian Banner, Primer Flash all share this residual). The square-wrapper architecture matches Banner and unifies the family. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.18 for the full rev history including the dropped V-compensated wrapper.**
  - **BODY wrapper padding** (`--tst-body-py: 0`, `--tst-body-px: space-3xs` 2) — **minimal stacked-architecture tier per §6.1.5**. Toast's compact identity (40px min-height floor, 360px max-width) favors tighter horizontal padding than Banner; the 0V/2H scheme produces visible **10H** (8 surface + 2 body) and ~12V (8 surface + 0 body + ~4 line-box leading) — surface-pad dominates and provides the bulk of the breathing. Tighter than Banner's 2V/4H (12H visible) — Toast's small-overlay identity vs Banner's in-flow identity. Applied to `.ds-toast__body`.
  - **Buttons cluster** (`.ds-toast__buttons`) — flex container with `--tst-buttons-gap: space-2xs` (4) between the optional inline action button(s) and the close button, no padding of its own. Sits as a direct flex child of `.ds-toast__row` alongside icon and body.
  - **Footer variant** (`.ds-toast--footer`) — adds `.ds-toast__footer` as a sibling of `.ds-toast__row` inside the surface. Because the surface is `flex-direction: column` with `--tst-section-gap: space-s` (8) gap, the footer appears as row 2 below the main row automatically. The footer has its own `--tst-footer-gap: space-xs` (6) between its buttons but **no padding** — the surface's 8px padding alone handles the footer's outer inset on all four sides. No CSS Grid, no per-area padding overrides.
- **Why uniform 8/8 + square icon wrapper, not asymmetric or V-compensated**: aligns Toast with Banner and Tooltip into one architectural family — see §6.1.5 family rule for the full rationale. Three padded slots (surface + icon + body), each owning a different kind of padding, replaces the cluster-based approach (Rev C: `.ds-toast__content` + `.ds-toast__actions` with their own paddings) which hid the "button is anchored to surface corner" relationship behind named clusters. The current three-slot approach makes corner-inset derivation explicit: surface padding alone defines where buttons sit relative to the toast's edges; text and icon each carry their own internal padding. See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.7 for the cluster-based Rev C history and §B.18 for the V-compensated icon wrapper history.
- **Type — title and description at the same size, hierarchy via color**: title `14/20` regular primary (`body/m`), description `14/20` regular secondary (`body/m`). Hierarchy comes from **color alone** (primary vs secondary) — not weight, not size. Per §5.1.1 the system collapsed the label tier; both lines are at the same `body/m` tier (matching M3 snackbar). Single-line messages use the same `.ds-toast__title` class — semantically "the headline," DOM/A11y benefit from consistency, and any future §5.1.1 weight escalation (e.g., danger toasts) lives in one selector. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.7 for the dropped weight-500 differentiation rev.**
- **Icon — `circle` (universal placeholder), on by default**: icon is **ON by default** and renders `ICON_CIRCLE` (Phosphor `circle`, Regular weight — see §5.3 / §5.3.1). Pass `icon: false` to opt out, or `icon: <glyph>` to override with a custom symbol (e.g., `icon: ICON_INFO` for explicit "information" semantics, `icon: ICON_CHECKMARK` paired with `tone: "success"`). Per Standing Rule #8, **tone is conveyed by color** (background tint + border + icon stroke), never by the glyph — so the system default stays neutral and consumers escalate to a semantic glyph when they specifically want one. Rationale for default-on: feedback components benefit from a stable left-hand visual anchor — even when text is short, the icon creates a consistent left edge across stacked toasts and gives tone-color a place to live as a third signal (in addition to bg + border) for `success` / `warning` / `danger`. The "no icon" variant is documented as a deliberate compact-look choice, not the default.
- **Vertical alignment** (applied via `:has()`):
  - **Single-line and title-only / description-only content**: `align-items: center` on the toast — icon, body, and the actions cluster all vertically center on the text line. This covers the most common case (single-line message) and keeps title-only / description-only toasts compact and centered.
  - **Title + description (true multi-line)**: `align-items: flex-start` — icon and actions cluster anchor to the top so they align with the title line, never floating in space between title and description. The selector is `.ds-toast:has(.ds-toast__title):has(.ds-toast__description)` — both must be present.
  - **Footer variant**: always uses `flex-start` for icon, even with title-only content, because the grid layout makes top-anchoring read as the deliberate structure.
- **Two action layouts — by content density, not purpose**:

  Toast supports two button arrangements as layout variants of the same component, chosen by content density.

  | Pattern | API | Layout | Typical content shape | Button styling |
  |---|---|---|---|---|
  | **Inline actions** | `inlineActions: ["Label"]` or `["Label", "Label"]` (1–2 items; back-compat alias `action: "Label"`) | Single row: body + buttons + close share one row. Toast height stays at 40 floor. | Short single-line + 1–2 quick buttons (Undo, Allow/Block) | **1 button**: `soft-neutral XS`. **2 buttons**: `soft-neutral XS` + `solid-brand XS` — last = primary. |
  | **Footer actions** | `footerActions: ["Label", "Label"]` (2–3 items) | Multi-row: top row = body + close, dedicated row below = buttons. Toast grows vertically. | Title + description + 2–3 buttons (Cancel + Confirm, Discard + Save) | `soft-neutral S` + `solid-brand S` — last = primary. Same convention as inline, larger tier. |

  **Hierarchy is the same; size differs**: every "2 buttons in a row" pattern in the system — Dialog footer, Toast footer, Toast inline, Banner — uses `soft-neutral` (secondary) + `solid-brand` (primary, rightmost). Toast inline runs XS (shares row with body+close, must stay compact); Toast footer runs S (dedicated row, more vertical space). Industry alignment: Sonner ships `action` as a filled/branded CTA + `cancel` as ghost, the same primary+secondary convention. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.7 for the dropped peer-only-styling rev for single inline buttons.**

  **The rule of thumb**:
  - Short content + 1–2 short buttons → **inline actions**
  - Title + description + 2–3 buttons → **footer actions**
  - Needs blocking semantics, scrim, or focus trap → **Dialog**, not Toast
  - Needs to span full viewport width (cookie consent, system alert) → **Banner**, not Toast

  **Mutual exclusion**: passing both `inlineActions` and `footerActions` is undefined behavior — helper picks `footerActions`. If you need both, you're describing Dialog complexity.

  **Width**: `max-width: 360` is on the conservative end of the stacked-corner toast tier (Sonner ~400, M3 compact Snackbar ~344, Atlassian Flag ~400, Polaris Toast ~500). Loosening to 400–480 if needed is fine; the *anchor* (corner-stacked, fixed-width, vertical stacking) is what defines a Toast vs a Banner. A toast that spans the full viewport width is a Banner, not a Toast variant.

- **Action + close — both at the XS tier, hierarchy expressed by fill (chip vs ghost)**:
  - Inline action buttons (1–2) and the close button are siblings inside `.ds-toast__buttons` so they share a horizontal cluster at the right edge. Wrapper uses `align-items: center` so all buttons share a vertical center. All are 24-tall (XS), so they align on the same baseline naturally and the toast height stays at its 40 floor.
  - **Inline action buttons**: 24px tall (XS tier), 12/16 text, `radius-xs` 8px. **Single button**: `.ds-btn--soft-neutral .ds-btn--xs` (soft fill, secondary chip — no primary peer to compare against, so primary fill is unnecessary weight). **Two buttons**: first `.ds-btn--soft-neutral .ds-btn--xs`, last `.ds-btn--solid-brand .ds-btn--xs` — primary appears once there's a peer to compare against (rightmost-is-primary convention shared across Dialog footer / Toast footer / Banner). **Soft-neutral, not ghost** for the secondary — ghost on a toast surface reads as "did this slot have a button?" and creates visual ambiguity. **Cap at 2** — three inline buttons compete with the close X and crowd the 360px max-width; use `footerActions` for three. Industry: Sonner (`action` + `cancel`), M3 Snackbar (one inline), Atlassian Flag (up to 2–3 if short labels), Polaris (one inline). **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.7 for the dropped S-tier inline rev (forced toast min-h up to 48).**
  - **Close button**: `.ds-btn .ds-btn--ghost-neutral .ds-btn--xs .ds-btn--icon-only` containing an `ICON_XMARK` glyph. **Sized at XS (24×24) — same icon-only chrome tier as Dialog close.** Both action and close at XS keeps the cluster at one tier, with hierarchy carried by *fill* (soft-neutral chip vs ghost-neutral transparent) rather than *size*. Industry alignment: Sonner `size="sm"`, M3 Snackbar, Atlassian Flag, Polaris — all use single-tier-with-fill-hierarchy in compact mode. The close glyph is intentionally smaller than the leading icon (12 vs 16) because leading icon is *content* and close X is *chrome* — Sonner / shadcn / Radix all keep this content-vs-chrome size distinction. Close glyph sits 14px from the right edge (8px collapsed padding + 6px button internal centering: 24-button minus 12-glyph / 2); leading icon glyph sits 12px from left.
  - **Toast height stays at its 40 floor** in single-line cases regardless of whether an action is present — cluster's tallest control is 24, plus 8/8 padding = 40. Title + description grows the toast vertically; inline controls do not. Matches Sonner's compact-mode (~40–44).
  - Under the Rev F uniform-8/8 architecture (see Internal architecture above), icon wrapper, body, and the `.ds-toast__buttons` cluster are all **direct flex children of the surface**, which carries uniform `--tst-pad` (8) on all sides — so each picks up corner-symmetric 8V/8H inset automatically with **no `:has()` per-edge override**. The XS close button (24-tall) fills the 24-tall content row of a 40-tall toast, landing 8V/8H ✓. The square 20×20 icon wrapper (direct child) also gets 8V/8H around its bounding box; the 16-glyph centered inside the 20-square reads ~2px in from the wrapper edge, so the glyph itself sits ~10V/10H from the surface edge — visually centered on the title-line in single-line mode, with the §6.1.4 leading-induced residual accepted in multi-line. The body's text inset (10H visible) comes from its 0V/2H wrapper (§6.1.5), not from any surface override.
  - **Inline actions are single-line by default**: `inlineActions` is paired with **title only**, not title + description. If you have multi-line content (title + description) AND need a response affordance, use `footerActions` instead — the inline-with-multi-line layout was awkward (actions floated top-right, disconnected from the description they should be acknowledging). Single-line inline keeps the toast compact and the action visually adjacent to the message. The renderer doesn't reject `inlineActions` + `description` together (consumers can still pass both if they really want), but the demo and recommended pattern is title + inline actions only.
- **Footer variant** (`.ds-toast--footer` modifier, triggered by `footerActions: [...]`):
  - Use when the toast's content needs **more vertical room than a single row affords** — typically title + description + 2–3 buttons. The dedicated button row below the body lets buttons carry primary/secondary hierarchy without competing with the close X for attention. This pattern naturally fits decision-style notifications (Discard + Save, Skip + Install, Cancel + Confirm) but is not exclusive to them — any multi-button toast with a description is a footer-actions candidate. See "Two action layouts" above.
  - Layout: CSS grid with two rows. Top row: `[icon] [body] [close]`. Bottom row: `[. footer footer]` — footer spans the body + close columns and right-aligns its buttons. This keeps the close button anchored top-right (consistent with the inline pattern) while giving the buttons a full row to themselves.
  - Footer buttons use real system `Button`s following the Dialog footer convention: secondary actions are `.ds-btn--soft-neutral .ds-btn--s`, the right-most primary action is `.ds-btn--solid-brand .ds-btn--s`. Same buttons that appear in Dialog footers — single source of truth, single visual language for "respond to this." **Why footer scales up to S (vs inline at XS) and gains primary/secondary hierarchy**: the dedicated row gives buttons more vertical breathing room, so the larger 32-tall hit-target reads as "this is its own zone" without crowding; the hierarchy (soft-neutral + solid-brand) reads naturally because the row isolates the buttons from the close X. Both are layout consequences of having more vertical space, not purpose claims about the user's mental state.
  - **Cap at 3 buttons** — same as Dialog footer. Two is the typical pattern; three is the maximum before the row crowds.
  - **Mutual exclusion with `inlineActions`** — pass one or the other, never both. The helper picks `footerActions` if both are passed, but consumers should never rely on this. If you need both, you're describing Dialog complexity.
- **Stacking**: multiple toasts stack vertically inside a `.ds-toast-stack` container with `gap: var(--space-s, 8px)`. Newest toast appears at the bottom (or top, product-defined). Max 3 visible at once is a sensible product convention.
- **Animation**: enter `var(--motion-medium, 250ms) var(--easing-out)` with translate from offset toward final position; exit `var(--motion-base, 150ms) var(--easing-in)` fade + small translate. Honors `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` by skipping translate (fade only).
- **Auto-dismiss timing**: short 4s (single-line, no action), default 6s (multi-line or with action), persistent ∞ (tone = `danger`, or whenever an action button is required to acknowledge). Hovering or focusing the toast pauses the timer; leaving resumes it.
- **a11y wiring**:
  - Container (region wrapper, app-wide singleton): `role="region"` + `aria-label="Notifications"` so it appears in landmark navigation
  - Each toast: `role="status"` + `aria-live="polite"` for neutral / info / success; `role="alert"` + `aria-live="assertive"` for warning / danger
  - Close button: `aria-label="Dismiss"` (icon-only)
  - Toast itself is NOT focusable; focus goes to the action button or the close button
  - Esc dismisses the toast that has focus inside
- **Keyboard contract**: Tab moves focus into the toast (action → close); Enter/Space activates the focused button; Esc dismisses
- **Demos** (per Part 7.3 placeholder copy convention — abstract labels reflecting the demo name, not realistic example text):
  - default — `title="Toast"`, `description="Description"` (with icon — matches default behavior)
  - tones — five toasts each labeled with their tone name (`Neutral`, `Info`, `Success`, `Warning`, `Danger`); icon included so the tone color flows through bg + border + icon for the at-a-glance read
  - all variants below use neutral tone so the variant feature is the subject, not the color
  - message — single-line `title="Message"` (the prominent line of any toast uses `title`; the legacy `text` alias produces identical output but `title` makes the role explicit)
  - title and description — `title="Title"`, `description="Description"`
  - inline actions — `title="Title"`, `inlineActions=["Button","Button"]` (two buttons in the body's row; demos the 2-button cap and the rightmost-is-primary convention — first button is `soft-neutral XS`, last button is `solid-brand XS`, mirroring Dialog footer / Toast footer hierarchy at the XS tier. Single-line per the rule above. Per Part 7.3 placeholder copy: button slots in compound components resolve to `"Button"`)
  - footer actions — `title="Title"`, `description="Description"`, `footerActions=["Button","Button"]` (multi-row toast with a dedicated footer button row below body; demos the canonical secondary + primary pairing — fill differentiates `soft-neutral S` from `solid-brand S`, mirroring Dialog footer. The fill *is* the demo subject; identical labels keep the eye on the fill differentiation. Per Part 7.3 placeholder copy: button slots in compound components resolve to `"Button"`)
  - no icon — `title="Title"`, `description="Description"`, `icon=false` (compact opt-out)
  - stacked — three single-line toasts: `title="Toast 1"`, `title="Toast 2"`, `title="Toast 3"`

### 9.9 Kbd badge — shared key-cap renderer
The kbd badge is a **sub-element** consumed by Tooltip (§9.5) and Dropdown (§9.7), not a top-level component with its own page. Both consumers share one rendering contract so a Cmd+Shift+E shortcut looks and reads identically whether it appears in a tooltip or a menu.

**API: atomic key-cap arrays.**
```js
kbd: ["⌘", "E"]            // Cmd+E
kbd: ["⌘", "Shift", "E"]   // Cmd+Shift+E
kbd: ["⌫"]                  // Backspace alone
```
Each array entry becomes one badge. Combined strings (`"⌘E"`) are forbidden — they break the atomic-key-cap rule that Linear, shadcn, Radix, and Apple HIG all follow. A legacy string fallback exists in the renderer (`kbdGroup` splits unknown strings character-by-character) but every demo and call site must use arrays.

**Glyph → icon map.** Mac modifier glyphs that have a clean Phosphor equivalent render as **inline SVG icons** inside the badge instead of as font characters. The icons ship at **Phosphor Regular** weight, same as every other icon in the system (§5.3.1 — one weight, one rule). Anything not in this map renders as text.

| Glyph | Unicode | Renders as | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| ⌘ | U+2318 | Phosphor `command` SVG (`ICON_COMMAND`) | Most common Mac modifier; consistent stroke weight |
| ⌥ | U+2325 | Phosphor `option` SVG (`ICON_OPTION`) | Second most common |
| ⌫ | U+232B | Phosphor `backspace` SVG (`ICON_BACKSPACE`) | DM Mono lacks this glyph and falls back to a system font with thinner stroke — the original bug that triggered this whole pattern |
| ⇧ Shift | U+21E7 | Text "Shift" (no Phosphor equivalent at matching aesthetic) | Phosphor's `arrow-fat-up` reads more like a key-shape arrow than a Shift modifier; text label is unambiguous |
| ⌃ Control | U+2303 | Text "Control" or "Ctrl" | No Phosphor equivalent |
| ⎋ Esc | U+238B | Text "Esc" | No Phosphor equivalent; text is the cross-platform convention anyway |
| ⇥ Tab | U+21E5 | Text "Tab" | No Phosphor equivalent |
| ↵ Return | U+21B5 | Text "Return" or "Enter" | Phosphor `arrow-elbow-down-left` is close but reads ambiguously |
| Letters (A-Z, 0-9) | — | Text | Always text |

The "icon-only-when-Phosphor-has-a-real-match" rule keeps the badge visually consistent without forcing a square peg. Adding new glyph→icon entries requires (a) a Phosphor icon that visually reads as that key, and (b) updating both `KBD_GLYPHS` and `KBD_LABELS` in `index.html`.

**Renderer (`index.html`):**
```js
function kbdKeyEl(key, baseClass) {
  var s = String(key);
  var iconHtml = KBD_GLYPHS[s];
  var label = KBD_LABELS[s] || s;
  if (iconHtml) {
    return '<kbd class="' + baseClass + '" aria-label="' + escAttr(label) + '">'
      + '<span class="ds-kbd__icon" aria-hidden="true">' + iconHtml + '</span></kbd>';
  }
  return '<kbd class="' + baseClass + '">' + esc(s) + '</kbd>';
}
function kbdGroup(keys, baseClass) {
  var arr = Array.isArray(keys) ? keys : (keys != null ? String(keys).split("") : []);
  return arr.map(function(k){ return kbdKeyEl(k, baseClass); }).join("");
}
```
- `baseClass` is the consumer-scoped badge class — `"ds-tooltip__kbd"` for Tooltip, `"ds-menu__kbd"` for Dropdown. Both are styled identically (12/16 mono, `surface/default` bg, `border/subtle` border, `radius-2xs`, `min-width: 20px`, `height: 20px`, `padding: 0 4px`, with a `:has(> .ds-kbd__icon)` override that swaps to `width: 20px; padding: 0` for icon-only badges). The two-class split is purely so each consumer can adjust if its surface tier ever needs a different font scale; today they're aligned.

  **Padding is conditional on content type — this implements the universal rule from §6.1.** For text content (letters, multi-character keys), the badge keeps `padding: 0 4px` so horizontal padding exceeds vertical (text's line-box already provides ~4px of vertical breathing above and below the cap-height; horizontal needs explicit padding to match optically). For icon content (a single inline SVG glyph), the badge switches to `width: 20px; padding: 0`, which produces a 20×20 square box with the 12×12 icon centered via flex → exactly **4px uniform inset on all sides**. Both rules are the same as Nav item icon centering: `(container-height − icon-width) / 2`.

  **The geometry, worked out.** With `box-sizing: border-box` and `border: 1px` on each side, an icon kbd needs `width = icon (12) + 2× padding + 2× border = 12 + 0 + 2 = 14px` of intrinsic width before clamps; the explicit `width: 20px` then overrides to 20×20, with the icon centered by flex giving (20 − 2 border − 12 icon) / 2 = 3px from content area + 1px border = **4px uniform** all around. A letter kbd "C" (≈ 7px wide) needs `7 + 8 padding + 2 border = 17px`; `min-width: 20px` clamps to 20×20, with letter centered giving 4px padding + 1px border + 1.4px flex = **~6.4px L/R, ~5.5px T/B** — more horizontal than vertical, correct for text. A multi-character "Shift" (≈ 30px wide) needs `30 + 8 + 2 = 40px`, expanding past min-width naturally with **~5px L/R, ~5.5px T/B**, comfortably read.

  **Earlier spec text claimed `padding: 0 4px` produced a 20×20 icon kbd; that math omitted the 1px border on each side.** The actual icon-kbd box at `padding: 0 4px` is 22×20 (12 icon + 8 padding + 2 border). The visible inset around the glyph at that size is 5px L/R vs 4px T/B — more horizontal than vertical, exactly the bug §6.1 forbids. The fix is the `:has(> .ds-kbd__icon)` override above; do not re-introduce horizontal padding on icon kbds in pursuit of "consistency with letter kbds." The single-char letter and single-icon kbd are NOT supposed to be visually identical at the geometry level — they're supposed to be visually identical at the **inset-around-the-glyph** level, which requires different padding because the content has different intrinsic widths.
- Output uses the **`<kbd>` HTML element** (not `<span>`) — semantically correct for keyboard input, and screen readers can name the key from `aria-label` when the visible content is an icon.
- The icon wrapper `.ds-kbd__icon` is sized to `1em` (matches the badge's `font-size: 12px`) so the SVG renders at the same visual height as adjacent text characters in a multi-key shortcut like `⌘ E` or `⌘ Shift E`.

**Sizing math.** Badge is `20px` tall. Inside, `font-size: 12px` and `line-height: 16px` give the text glyph a cap-height of roughly 9–10px. The `.ds-kbd__icon > svg { width: 1em; height: 1em }` rule renders the Phosphor icon at 12×12 — slightly taller than the text cap-height, but Phosphor Regular's filled paths optically match a 12px text glyph's visual weight (verified at the rendered size). Don't reach for `0.85em` to match cap-height exactly — that makes the icon look anemic next to letter keys; the small height excess reads as "icon as a glyph" not as "icon imposed on text," which is the right metaphor for a modifier-key indicator.

**Accessibility (`<kbd>` + `aria-label`).** When the badge contains an icon, the `<kbd>` carries `aria-label="Command"` (or "Option" / "Backspace"); the inner span gets `aria-hidden="true"` so the SVG isn't double-announced. When the badge contains text, no `aria-label` is needed — the text reads naturally. Screen readers in a dropdown like "Edit ⌘ E" announce it as `Edit, Command, E`, which matches a user's spoken expectation.

**When to extend.** If a future Banner, Snackbar, or Command Palette wants kbd badges, it gets its own `--{comp}__kbd` base class (same dimensions and styling) and calls `kbdGroup(keys, "ds-{comp}__kbd")`. The icon map and `<kbd>` semantics are shared.

### 9.10 Banner — persistent in-flow notification (Toast's anchored counterpart)

Banner is the **persistent in-flow** counterpart to Toast (§9.8). Toast and Banner share **the same chrome treatment** — same `surface/default` bg for neutral, same `feedback/{tone}/bg` + `feedback/{tone}/border` for tonal, same `text/{tone}` icon. Differentiation lives entirely in **layout + behavior**:

| Property | Toast | Banner |
|---|---|---|
| Position | Floating overlay, anchored to viewport edge | Inline in document flow |
| Width | `min-w 280 / max-w 360` | `width: 100%` of its container |
| Persistence | Auto-dismisses (4–6s) | Persistent — only user/programmatic close |
| **Shadow** | **`var(--shadow-3)` — floats above the page** | **None — sits flat in flow** |
| Layer | `--z-toast` (80) | `--z-content` (10) or base |
| Stacking | Multiple in a stack | Singleton per surface |
| Entry animation | Slide-in (`var(--motion-medium)`) | None — appears with parent |
| Action prominence | Optional small inline action | Often prominent action button(s) |

In production the eye reads "this floats" (shadow + max-width + viewport-anchored) vs. "this is part of the page" (no shadow + full-width + in-flow). Toast and Banner are **never seen side-by-side in actual product use** — Toast floats at the viewport corner, Banner sits inline at full width. The "they look identical in static docs" concern is a docs-presentation artifact, not a system flaw.

This follows the shadcn / Sonner / modern Vercel / Chakra / Primer approach: chrome shared, behavior + position differentiate. **Material 3 is the only major system that strongly differentiates** Toast (dark) vs Banner (light) — Android tradition, not a universal modern principle. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.1 for the full cross-system audit and dropped Banner revisions (stripe pattern, forced-different bg).**

#### Banner anatomy

- **Surface**: same as Toast — `surface/default` bg + `border/subtle` for neutral (Job C definition stroke); `feedback/{tone}/bg` + `feedback/{tone}/border` for tonal (Job B semantic stroke). Radius `var(--radius-m, 16px)`. **No shadow.** The Banner surface carries its own padding (`--bn-pad: space-s` 8) which both buttons (corner-symmetric inset) and inner wrappers (icon + body, each with their own internal padding) compose against. Matches Primer's Banner component structure exactly.
- **Internal architecture — uniform 8/8 surface + square icon wrapper + Banner-tier body wrapper (Rev G, May 2026)**. Banner is a member of the **"Toast/Banner/Tooltip uniform-8/8 family"** — see §6.1.5 for the family rule. Banner's HTML is `.ds-banner` (the surface, also the flex row) containing three sibling slots, each owning a different kind of padding:
  - **SURFACE padding** (`--bn-pad: space-s` 8) — uniform 8/8 outer ring; applied to `.ds-banner` directly. The S-tier dismiss button sitting as a direct flex child of the row picks up corner-symmetric inset from this padding alone (no separate `__actions` cluster). 28-tall S button + 8/8 surface padding = 44-tall banner with 8V/8H ✓ uniform inset around the button per §6.1.2.
  - **ICON wrapper internal padding** (`--bn-icon-pad: space-3xs` 2, symmetric on all sides) — wrapper becomes **square 24×24** around the 20-icon (icon-m + 2 + 2). Square per the family rule: icon wrapper dimensions equal text line-height in both axes (`--bn-lh` 20 ≈ wrapper W = wrapper H = 24, accepting a 4px ascent buffer from the line-box leading). Replaces an earlier V-compensated 24W×24H wrapper that used asymmetric internal padding (`padding-top: 4`, `padding-bottom: 4`, `padding-left: 2`, `padding-right: 2`) to achieve 0px multi-line glyph-to-cap alignment at the cost of asymmetric internal chrome. The 2-3px multi-line residual is within §6.1.4 leading-induced inset tolerance and accepted system-wide. The square-wrapper architecture matches Toast and unifies the family. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.18 for the full rev history including the dropped V-compensated wrapper.**
  - **BODY wrapper padding** (`--bn-body-py: space-3xs` 2, `--bn-body-px: space-2xs` 4) — **stacked-architecture tier per §6.1.5** (one ramp step H above V to absorb the line-box leading delta; surface-pad 8 carries the bulk of the breathing). Visible **12H** (8 surface + 4 body) vs **13V** (8 surface + 2 body + ~3 leading) — V dominant by ~1 from leading, matching the system-wide leading-induced-inset pattern (§6.1.4). More generous horizontal than Toast (10H) — Banner's in-flow identity favors comfortable text breathing vs Toast's compact-overlay identity. Applied to `.ds-banner__body`.
  - **Buttons cluster** (`.ds-banner__buttons`) — flex container with `--bn-buttons-gap: space-2xs` (4) between the optional action button and the dismiss button, no padding of its own. Sits as a direct flex child of `.ds-banner` alongside icon and body.
- **Actions**: Banner uses `Button S` (28-tall) for both action and close — the larger surface area (vs Toast's compact 40px floor) lets buttons carry their full S-tier identity. With the May 2026 `icon-2s: 14` ramp addition (§2.3), the close button's X glyph lands at 14px — matching the M / L / XL tiers' 50% icon-to-control ratio.
- **Vertical alignment**: when both `title` and `description` are present, the surface switches to `align-items: flex-start` via a `:has()` rule. Single-line content stays `align-items: center`.
- **Why three padded slots, not cluster-based __content + __actions**: matches Primer's Banner component (the dismiss button sits as a direct child of the banner row, picking up the surface's overall padding for its corner inset) and the Figma component (node 278:1813) exactly. The Rev D cluster-based approach (`.ds-banner__content` carrying its own 8/16 padding, `.ds-banner__actions` carrying its own 8/8 padding, surface with NO padding) hid the "the button is anchored to the surface corner" relationship behind two named clusters and forced the actions cluster to invent its own padding values that duplicated the surface's intent. The Rev E three-slot approach makes the corner-inset derivation explicit: surface padding alone defines where buttons sit relative to the banner's edges, and text/icon get their own internal padding without competing with the button's anchor relationship.

**Rule for future container components**: when a surface needs **different padding for text vs icons vs buttons**, prefer **per-wrapper internal padding** (each inner element owns its own breathing) plus **surface padding** (anchors button corner inset) over **named clusters with their own padding** (Rev D approach). The per-wrapper approach makes each padding decision local to the element it affects; the cluster approach forces the cluster to encode two different padding intents (surface anchor + text breathing) into one box, which then conflicts with the button's anchor relationship. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.11 for the full rev history including dropped 16/12, 12/14, `:has()`-on-surface, and cluster-based Rev D revs.**

#### Why `--full-bleed` keeps top/bottom borders but drops left/right

Structural rule from Primer's `flush` modifier: viewport edges carry the L/R boundary, page-content edges (above and below) need the 1px tone border to mark separation. Secondary reason specific to our system: our tonal bg tints are subtle 50-stop tints, so without top/bottom borders a tonal full-bleed banner reads as a faint colored wash with soft edges bleeding into surrounding content. For neutral full-bleed (`surface/default` bg = page color), the borders are load-bearing — without them the banner is invisible. (Apple iOS, Vercel, Linear use stronger bg saturation and drop borders entirely; we use subtler tints consistent with the soft-tone-tint system, which is why top/bottom borders are kept.)

#### Tones — kept on Banner for cross-component vocabulary consistency

Material 3 ships Banner without tones (severity through icon and content). We keep tones because the system already uses tone as a semantic language across Toast and Validation; a `success` toast and a `success` banner share the same green tint + green border + green icon so users learn the vocabulary once.

#### a11y wiring
- `role="status"` + `aria-live="polite"` for `neutral` / `info` / `success`.
- `role="alert"` + `aria-live="assertive"` for `warning` / `danger`.
- Dismiss button: `aria-label="Dismiss"`.
- Keyboard-tabbable: action button → close button.

#### Variants documented
- default (title + description, info tone)
- tones (neutral / info / success / warning / danger)
- message (single-line title only)
- title and description (the canonical 2-line content)
- action (single inline action button at the right)
- dismissible (close X at the right)
- full bleed (`radius: 0` + edge-anchored top/bottom borders for top-of-screen system messages)
- no icon (compact opt-out)

### 9.11 Tabs — horizontal trigger row + content panel

Tabs switches between parallel content panels under one heading. The active indicator is a **rounded underbar**, not a filled pill — non-negotiable differentiator from Segmented Control per §12.1. The hover state uses the **same underbar shape in a lighter color** (not a backplate fill), so the gradient from "candidate hover target" → "selected" reads as one continuous visual language with only the bar color changing from `border/default` (gray) to `text/primary` (near-black).

| Pattern | Active indicator | Hover indicator | Track | Role |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Tabs** | 3px rounded **`text/primary` (near-black)** underbar (`::after`, `radius-2xs`, indented from trigger edges by `--tab-px`, sitting 2px below the trigger) | **Same underbar shape** in `border/default` (gray) — *no backplate* | Transparent — triggers separated by `space-s` (8px) gap; list has **NO border-bottom** | Navigate between content panels — switching changes WHAT IS SHOWN |
| Segmented Control | Filled pill (active gets `surface/default` + `shadow-1`) | Subtle bg on the trigger | Tonal track (`surface/quiet` bg + `border/subtle` inset) | Pick a value — switching changes a SETTING |

**Sizing**: follows the standard control ramp (§2.3) at S / **M (default)** / L. Default is M (32) so Tabs share density with Segmented Control and Button at the same tier — three horizontal-trigger families compose naturally in the same toolbar.

**State matrix** (mandatory):

| State | Treatment |
|---|---|
| Default | `text/secondary`, transparent bg, `border-radius: radius-xs` (8) on all four corners |
| Hover (excluded when `aria-selected="true"` or disabled) | `text/primary` + **rounded underbar in `border/default`** (gray) appears under the content. Same `::after` shape as the active indicator — only the color differs. NO background fill. |
| Pressed (`:active`) | `filter: brightness(.92)` |
| Focus (`:focus-visible`) | Inset 2px outline at `border/focus` with `outline-offset: -4px` |
| Selected (`aria-selected="true"`) | `text/primary` + **3px rounded underbar in `text/primary`** (near-black) at the trigger's bottom edge. The selected trigger does NOT receive any hover treatment — once selected, the bar is stable. The underbar dropped brand color in the May 2026 brand-color audit (§12.1) — Tabs are navigation between content panels, not a value-selection set, and the system-wide "brand for values, neutral for navigation" stance puts Tabs in the neutral camp alongside Nav vertical/horizontal/bottom. Industry alignment: Vercel Geist, Linear, Notion, Apple HIG, and shadcn all run Tabs with neutral active indicators; Material 3 is the only major holdout with brand-colored underbars. |
| Disabled | `text/disabled`, `cursor: not-allowed`, `pointer-events: none` |

**Underbar geometry** (single shape, two colors):
- `position: absolute; left: var(--tab-px); right: var(--tab-px)` — indented by trigger's horizontal padding so the bar sits *under the content*, not under the entire trigger box. Reads as "the underline marks the word, not the click target."
- `bottom: calc(-1 * var(--space-3xs, 2px))` — sits 2px below the trigger's content-box edge, which is why the list reserves `padding-bottom: var(--space-2xs, 4px)` (room for the bar without clipping).
- `height: 3px; border-radius: var(--radius-2xs, 4px)` — rounded ends, not square corners. Soft enough to feel like part of the rounded-rectangle family, not so rounded that it reads as a pill.
- `transition: background .15s ease` — only the color animates between hover/active states; the bar's geometry is identical, so movement between states feels like a single bar shifting color rather than two different bars appearing.

**Why no list `border-bottom`**: Earlier iterations and a stale spec rev had a 1px `border/subtle` line running under the entire tab row, with the active bar overpainting it. The line had two failure modes: (1) it visually competed with the active bar (eye registered "row above a line, with one tab pushing through" instead of "tabs with one bar below them"), and (2) it forced the Tabs to be a structurally bordered surface even when the parent was already structured (a Tab row inside a Card, inside a Dialog, inside a Page) — adding a fourth divider in a stack of three. The fix: drop the line entirely. The active and hover underbars are sufficient to mark "where the tab row ends" because they protrude into the same 4px reserved gutter; the line was redundant. Industry alignment: Linear, Vercel Geist, Notion, and Stripe Dashboard all run their tabs without a continuous bottom line; Material 3 and shadcn keep one but their visual systems also lean more "structural divider." Our quieter stance matches the modern dense-product set.

**Why hover is an underbar, not a backplate**: An earlier rev briefly used `action/hover` background pill for hover (top-rounded `border-radius: 8 8 0 0`, "browser tab silhouette"). Two problems: (1) the eye reads two different shapes — pill on hover, then bar on active — and the transition feels like "different state, different visual idiom" instead of "one indicator escalating from gray to near-black." (2) The hover backplate visually competed with Segmented Control's filled-pill aesthetic, blurring the §12.1 differentiation. The current treatment — same bar shape, only color changes — keeps Tabs' identity unmistakably "underline navigation" and makes the hover→active transition read as one continuous escalation. Industry consensus is more split here (shadcn / Atlassian use backplate hover; Linear / Vercel / Stripe / older Apple HIG use underbar hover); we sit with the underbar camp because it makes the system's three horizontal-trigger families (Tabs, Segmented Control, Button) read as three distinct visual languages with no cross-contamination.

**Note on the hover foreground change** (text secondary → primary): deliberate, audited exception to §4.3.1's "foreground stable on hover" rule. The whole point of an unselected tab is to invite a click; the strongest affordance is "the inactive tab text gets darker on hover." Every modern Tabs system does this. The exception is local to Tab triggers and does NOT extend to other components.

**Style variants — deferred (May 2026)**: An earlier rev shipped a `.ds-tabs--soft` modifier that swapped the underbar for a `action/soft/brand/bg` filled active pill. Dropped from the base design system because (a) it visually overlapped Segmented Control's filled-pill identity (§12.1) at small surface widths and forced a cross-component grammar reread; (b) the base system stance is "one canonical default per component, variants only when a documented consumer ships them" (§2.2.1 anti-bloat rule). The filled-tabs aesthetic is a legitimate option that future projects may want — when a real consumer ships and asks for it, rebuild it as an explicit `style="filled"` API alongside the default underline; until then, Tabs has one visual treatment (3px `text/primary` underbar) and Segmented Control owns the filled-pill territory.

**Optional content per trigger**:
- **Leading icon** — `ds-tabs__icon` wrapper sized by line-height letterbox per §6.1.3.
- **Trailing count** — small chip-style badge: 20px tall, `min-width: 20px`, `radius-2xs` (6) — companion-chrome tier shared with the kbd badge (§2.1.1), `surface/subtle` bg, `text/secondary` text, **`1px border/subtle` Job C definition stroke** (per §3.5 audit table — same family as Badge and kbd, all three are fill-tinted contained sub-elements that take the same definition stroke for edge crispness; the count was historic drift through May 2026 and is now aligned). When the trigger is selected, the count's bg shifts to `action/hover` and its text to `text/primary` so the count reads as "lit" along with the underbar; the border stays `border/subtle` (definition strokes never escalate per §3.5).

**Keyboard contract** (per §14.6):
- ←/→ to move between triggers (roving `tabindex`, only the focused trigger has `tabindex="0"`; others have `tabindex="-1"`)
- Home / End for first / last trigger
- Enter or Space to activate (manual mode — recommended; default automatic mode would activate on focus, which can be jarring with content panels that load expensive data)
- Disabled triggers stay focus-traversable (so users can read why they're unavailable) but skip activation

**Surface for content panel**: `ds-tabs__panel` with `space-l` (16) vertical padding above/below content, `body/m` (14/20) text in `text/primary`. Each panel's `role="tabpanel"` is wired to the trigger's `aria-controls`.

### 9.12 Avatar — user/entity representation

Avatar represents a person or entity with image, initials, or icon fallback. Round by default (the universal "avatar" mental model); rounded-square modifier available for product chrome that prefers a less personal/more institutional shape (e.g., team avatars, organization logos).

**Sizes**: follows the standard control ramp (§2.3) at XS / S / **M (default)** / L / XL. Round avatars always use `9999px`; font / line-height comes straight from §2.3. The only Avatar-specific value is the square-modifier radius, which **matches the same-tier Button radius** so a square avatar lines up visually with adjacent same-height controls:

| Tier | Square radius (matches Button at this tier) |
|---|---|
| XS (24) / S (28) | `radius-xs` (8) |
| **M (32, default)** | `radius-2s` (10) |
| L (40) | `radius-s` (12) |
| XL (48) | `radius-2m` (14) |

**Content priority** (helper picks one):
1. `src` provided → render `<img>`.
2. `initials` provided → render 1–2 uppercase letters in mono, weight 400 (per §5.1.1 — components default to Regular).
3. Fallback → render `ICON_USER` (Phosphor `user`, Regular weight) at 60% of the avatar size, in `text/secondary`.

**Background**: default avatars use `surface/subtle` (neutral chip surface). For consumers wanting a deterministic per-name color, the system exposes a future `tone` prop that resolves to a soft tonal background (`feedback/{tone}/bg` + `text/{tone}`), but defaults to neutral.

**Status indicator** — an absolute-positioned dot at bottom-right, 25% × 25% of the avatar size, with a 2px ring of `surface/default` (so the dot reads cleanly against any backdrop). Four states:

| Status | Color token |
|---|---|
| `online` | `feedback/success/icon` (green/500) |
| `away` | `border/warning` (dune/500 — derives from the warning border ramp) |
| `busy` | `border/danger` (red/500) |
| `offline` | `text/tertiary` (neutral muted) |

**Group stacking** (`.ds-avatar-group`): horizontal flex row; each subsequent avatar overlaps the previous by 25% of its width (`margin-left: calc(var(--av-size) * -0.25)`). Each avatar gets a 2px `surface/default` ring (`box-shadow`) to separate from its neighbor. Use a "+N" initials avatar at the end to summarize overflow.

**a11y wiring**: `role="img"` + `aria-label="<full name or description>"`. The `aria-label` is required; the avatar is inert otherwise (decorative `aria-hidden="true"` is acceptable when the avatar appears next to a visible name in the same surface, but the helper defaults to providing a label).

### 9.13 Card — surface-level container

Card groups related content into a contained surface with optional media + header + body + footer regions. It's the system's default "container" primitive — anything that needs a visible boundary around content uses Card.

**Sizing & shape** (matched to Figma node 295-672 — the canonical Card baseline):
- Default radius: `radius-l` (20) — **derived from the inner footer buttons via the §2.1.2 inverse rule** (`outer = inner radius + inset = radius-xs 8 + space-m 12 = 20`), not chosen by surface-family taste. Card sits in the same surface family as Dialog (both at `radius-l` 20), differentiated by behavior (inline vs modal) and content density (Card has up to 4 regions including media; Dialog has up to 3) rather than by shape. An earlier rev had Card at `radius-xl` (24) on the deprecated "general-purpose container = largest tier" rationale; fixed to `radius-l` (20) May 10, 2026 to align with the inverse rule (full derivation in "Card outer radius" below + §2.1.2). `radius-xl` (24) is now reserved for future Sheet / Drawer.
- Default padding: `space-m` (12) — applied to the **card itself** (one source of truth), not to each region. Unchanged from the May 2026 rev.
- Default region gap: `space-m` (12) — vertical breathing between sibling regions. **Equal to padding (1:1 ratio)** matching Figma 295-672. The earlier rev sat at `space-s` (8) on the rationale that gap < pad establishes a hierarchy step between "outer chrome" and "internal separation" (3:2 ratio matching Dialog). The 1:1 rev reflects Card's smaller and squarer dimensions: at 264 × 264 with 4 regions (media / title / body / footer), a tighter gap creates compositional crowding; the equal-space rhythm reads as "deliberate and proportional" at this scale. Dialog at 440px width keeps the 12/8 (3:2) hierarchy step because Dialog's wider container and 3-zone explicit structure benefit from a step; Card's narrower square benefits from equal-space. **Both ratios are sanctioned per surface; the choice is per-component, not universal.**
- Default media radius: `radius-xs` (8) — for the inset media slot. Derived per the §2.1.2 inverse rule (`inner = outer − inset = 20 − 12 = 8`); soft-regime ideal hit exactly, see "Media slot — inset" below.

**No internal dividers** — Card is a single padded surface, not a stack of bordered cells. Padding lives on the card root (`padding: var(--cd-pad)` on `.ds-card`); regions separate via `gap: var(--cd-gap)` on the card's flex column with no chrome of their own (header, body, footer are bare layout helpers). Industry alignment: shadcn / Vercel Geist / Linear / Notion all ship dividerless.

**Gap-to-padding ratio is 1:1 (12/12) for Card, 3:2 (12/8) for Dialog — per-surface choice, not universal policy**. Card at 264×264 with 4 visible regions benefits from equal-space rhythm; Dialog at 440 wide with 3 zones benefits from a hierarchy step. Both ratios are sanctioned. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.2 for the gap-rev history (gap < pad → gap = pad), the dropped `--spacious` modifier, the internal-dividers removal, the three-fill-styles → three-tones rev, the `color-mix()` rationale for tertiary description, and the no-footer / no-media decisions for the tertiary tone demo.**

**Three tones** (brand-emphasis axis, derived from §3.1 reserved brand-surface tokens):

| Tone | Background | Foreground | Border | Stroke job |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **primary** (default) | `surface/default` (white) | `text/primary` for title + body, `text/secondary` for description | `1px solid border/subtle` | Job C — definition stroke crisps the edge against same-value page bg |
| **secondary** | `surface/brand-subtle` (blue/50 light, blue/1100 dark) | `text/primary` for title + body, `text/secondary` for description | `1px solid border/subtle` | Job C — definition stroke against the soft tint |
| **tertiary** | `surface/brand` (blue/500 light, blue/400 dark) | `text/on-brand` (white) for title + body; description uses `color-mix(in oklch, text/on-brand 80%, transparent)` for hierarchy | `border-color: transparent` (border space reserved for layout consistency) | None — fill carries the boundary (per §3.5 "fill carries the boundary" exception, saturated brand fill creates strong visual separation against any neutral page bg). |

**Internal structure** — optional media + header (with optional leading avatar) + body + footer (single or split), gap-spaced (no dividers):

```html
<div class="ds-card">
  <!-- Optional: inset media slot (sits inside the card's padding) -->
  <div class="ds-card__media" aria-hidden="true"><!-- Phosphor `image` icon, or <img> --></div>

  <!-- Optional: leading avatar + title + description -->
  <div class="ds-card__header">
    <div class="ds-card__avatar"><!-- avatarEl() output: image / initials / icon --></div>
    <div class="ds-card__header-text">
      <div class="ds-card__title">Title</div>
      <div class="ds-card__description">Description</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Optional: free-form body content -->
  <div class="ds-card__body">Body content</div>

  <!-- Optional Mode A: right-aligned button row (single cluster) -->
  <div class="ds-card__footer">
    <!-- secondary + primary buttons -->
  </div>

  <!-- Optional Mode B: split footer (two clusters anchored to opposite edges) -->
  <div class="ds-card__footer ds-card__footer--split">
    <div class="ds-card__footer-group"><!-- text actions (left) --></div>
    <div class="ds-card__footer-group"><!-- icon-button cluster (right) --></div>
  </div>
</div>
```

- Header is a 2-zone flex row: `[avatar (optional, leading)] [text-block (grows)]`. Uses `display: flex` with `space-m` (12px) gap between zones; `align-items: flex-start` so a leading avatar pins to the title's cap-top and a multi-line description doesn't push the avatar off the baseline. Header carries no padding or border of its own — the card's `--cd-pad` and the column `--cd-gap` handle spacing.
- **Avatar slot** (`__avatar`) — a positional wrapper for the leading-avatar pattern (Material 3 standard card with author identity). `flex-shrink: 0` so the avatar keeps its declared size when the text block needs to wrap. Card doesn't style the avatar itself; consumers pass full `avatarEl()` output (any Avatar size / image / initials / icon variant). Use this slot when the card represents an entity with identity (a user post, a comment, a profile preview); skip it for entity-less content (a feature card, a stat card).

- **No trailing actions slot in the header.** Card header is title + optional description (and optional leading avatar) only. An earlier rev (May 10, 2026) shipped a trailing icon-button slot at the S tier, derived from the inverse-rule audit that picked Card's outer radius. It was removed (May 10, 2026, same day) on the rationale that header chrome buttons compete for attention with the title and don't compose well with the small-square card geometry — the icon button at 28 tall against a 20-tall title pulled the eye even at `ghost-neutral`, and the affordance value of "things you do TO this card" was better served by the footer slot where buttons have dedicated space and can carry primary/secondary hierarchy via fill. Industry alignment: shadcn cards / Linear cards / Vercel Geist cards / Notion blocks all default to bare headers; Material 3 standard-card's "more icon" is a content-specific affordance pattern that doesn't generalize to a system's default Card chrome. Consumers needing per-card affordances should put them in the footer (`Button soft-neutral S` or `solid-brand S`) or above/beside the card in the consumer's own layout, not inside the header.
- **Title typography** — `body/m` (14/20) at **weight 500** in `text/primary`. **Same size as body**, differentiated by weight only. This is the canonical example of the §5.1.1 weight-500 escalation case — every part of the three-part necessity test fires:
 1. **Locally dominant within its context** ✓ — title is the headline of the card; nothing else inside Card is meant to read more prominently.
 2. **Cannot scale up in size** ✓ — title and body now share the 14/20 tier by design (per the user's "same size, different weight" directive). Scaling title back up to `body/l` (16/24) would re-create the size hierarchy that the user explicitly rejected.
 3. **Color cannot carry the contrast** ✓ — both title and body use `text/primary` (`--cd-fg`). Description carries the muted-color step, but title↔body share the primary color. Neither can lighten/darken the other.
 All three criteria satisfied → weight 500 is justified, not drift. **Card title is one of exactly two weight-500 consumers inside a `.ds-*` component class today; the Dialog title (§9.6) is the other** — both pass the same three-part necessity test (§5.1.1). If a future component proposes weight 500, it must satisfy this same three-part test.
 An earlier rev (May 2026, Figma 295-672 1:1 implementation) ran title at `body/l` 16/24 weight 500 + body at `body/m` 14/20 weight 400 — that combination only used weight 500 because body was at 16/24 too in the Figma source, and once body dropped to 14/20 the size step alone could carry hierarchy. A subsequent rev then dropped title to weight 400 + body to weight 400 with size step 16→14 — calmer but lost the "title and body same size" parity the user later asked for. The current rev (May 10, 2026) makes title and body the SAME size (14/20) and brings back weight 500 for title only — so the hierarchy is carried entirely by weight, with size now being a uniform shared property. The Figma's weight 500s preserved in the design source as a documented reference but not implemented; the system's "weight 400 default" rule wins where they conflict.
- **Description typography** (when present) — `body/m` (14/20) at weight 400 in `text/secondary`. Same size as body but `text/secondary` color provides the metadata-vs-content distinction. Description is optional; the canonical baseline omits it (per §7.3 placeholder copy: small secondary text adds visual weight without adding signal in most demo contexts).
- **Body typography** — `body/m` (14/20) at weight 400 in `text/primary`. **Same size as title** (per the user's "size-uniform, weight-differentiated" directive); title and body share the 14/20 tier and differ only by weight (500 vs 400). Same color as title (so body remains "the content," not metadata — the muted-color step is reserved for description). Matches Dialog body which also runs at `body/m` for the same content-prose role. The 14/20 tier was chosen earlier (May 2026) to give cards more breathing room within the 264 square shell; the latest rev keeps body at 14/20 and brings title down to match (rather than scaling body back up to title), preserving the calmer, less-busy read the user asked for.

- **Inter-region gap collapse — text↔text adjacency removes the 12px gap (§9.13.1)** — when the card root's flex column places the header (which contains the title and optional description) directly above the body (text content), the inter-region `--cd-gap` (12) is collapsed to 0 via a CSS adjacent-sibling rule:
 ```css
 .ds-card__header + .ds-card__body { margin-top: calc(-1 * var(--cd-gap)) }
 ```
 The flexbox `gap` property still adds 12px between the two regions; the `-12` margin-top on `.ds-card__body` cancels it exactly. Net visible spacing between the title's baseline and body's cap-top is **only the line-box leading** (~5px below title baseline + ~5px above body cap-top = ~10px visible) instead of `10 + 12 = 22` with the explicit gap.
 The header-text inner wrapper also runs `gap: 0` (was `space-2xs` 4 in earlier revs), so title + description (when both present) read as a single text block with hierarchy carried by weight (500 vs 400) and color (primary vs muted), again with line-box leading providing the visible separation.
 **The collapse applies ONLY to text↔text adjacency** — header→body. Other inter-region pairs keep their `--cd-gap` (12) because they cross content modes:
 - **Media↔header** keeps the gap — graphic-to-text boundary, the 12px breathing helps the image read as a distinct visual zone before the typographic content.
 - **Body↔footer** keeps the gap — text-to-controls boundary, buttons need explicit space from body text or they read as crammed against it.
 - **Header↔footer** (title + footer with no body) keeps the gap — text-to-controls, same rationale as body↔footer.
 Industry alignment: Material 3 cards stack headline + supporting text with line-height-only separation (no explicit gap between consecutive text lines), Apple HIG list rows do the same, Linear stacks title + description with `0` margin between them and lets line-height carry the breathing. Our system's collapse rule encodes the same pattern declaratively.
- **Footer — three layout modes, default button tier is S (28-tall)**:
  - **Stretched action row** (`__footer--stretch`) — buttons stretch to fill the row equally via `flex: 1 1 0` on each button (the modifier rule `.ds-card__footer--stretch > .ds-btn` does this). Canonical default: a 50/50 row with secondary on the left (`Button soft-neutral S` with icon + label) and primary on the right (`Button solid-brand S` with icon + label). Use when both affordances are peer responses ("Cancel" / "Confirm", "Decline" / "Accept", "Skip" / "Continue"). The stretched layout reinforces "these are equally weighted choices" visually; the fill differentiation (soft vs solid) carries the primary/secondary hierarchy.
  - **Single cluster** (`__footer`) — right-aligned (`justify-content: flex-end`) with `space-s` (8px) gap between buttons. Dialog-style "Cancel | Confirm" pattern (`Button soft-neutral S` then `Button solid-brand S`). Use when buttons should NOT span the full width — a quieter, more deliberate look than the stretched action row.
  - **Split cluster** (`__footer--split`) — two clusters anchored to opposite edges via `justify-content: space-between`, each cluster wrapped in `__footer-group` (a flex row with `space-s` gap). Use for the Material 3 standard-card pattern: text actions on the left ("Action 1", "Action 2"), icon-button cluster on the right (favorite, share). The split signals a hierarchy split — left = "things you do with this content," right = "things you do TO this content (save, share, dismiss)" — the same convention iOS Mail / Gmail / Twitter / Material 3 cards / Slack messages all follow. Pairs with the avatar+description header for the canonical M3 standard-card identity.

**Default button tier — S (28), not Figma's M (32) — the system's "card-class footer" convention**: Figma 295-672 ships the Card footer at M-tier (32-tall) with icon + label per button. A 1:1 implementation read as overcrowded in the 264-square shell — 4 distinct visual elements (2 icons + 2 labels) packed into a 32-tall row competing with title + body for attention. The system departs from Figma here and uses S-tier (28-tall), which:
- Aligns Card with the **Dialog footer / Toast footer / Banner action row** convention. Every "card-class notification surface" in the system uses S in its footer; bringing Card into this family keeps the cross-component vocabulary consistent.
- Reduces visual weight by ~13% per button (28 vs 32 tall) without sacrificing legibility — both at the same `body/s` (12/16) text tier, so the only thing that changes is the chrome around the label.
- Lets the smaller card "breathe" — at 264 wide with 12 padding, each footer button is ~116 wide. M (32) makes that 116×32 (3.6:1 ratio); S (28) makes it 116×28 (4.1:1) — the more horizontal proportion reads as a button row, not a button stack.
- The Figma's M-tier is preserved as a documented reference but not implemented. Consumers who specifically want Figma 1:1 fidelity can pass `Button solid-brand M` directly; the Card's `--footer--stretch` modifier doesn't lock the size.

**Card outer radius — DERIVED from inner buttons + padding (inverse rule, §2.1.2)**:

Card's outer radius is **`radius-l` (20)**, derived per the §2.1.2 inverse rule (`outer = inner_radius + inset`):

```
outer = button-radius + --cd-pad
      = radius-xs (8) + space-m (12)
      = 20
      = radius-l
```

Every corner-adjacent inner element sits at `radius-xs` (8) — footer buttons (S, 28-tall) and media (full-width, 16/9). Both are inset by `--cd-pad` (12). The inverse rule resolves to outer 20 for all of them, so the entire card's geometry becomes a single mathematical statement: every corner-adjacent nested element shares the strict-regime/soft-ideal target. (The header has no trailing affordance, so the top corners' concentric pair is media↔surface when media is present, or just the surface itself when media is absent; either way the math holds.)

**Why this matters more than picking 24 by surface-family taste.** An earlier rev (May 2026) had Card at `radius-xl` (24) on the rationale that "Card is the system's general-purpose container primitive, give it the largest tier." That choice was **arbitrary at the surface level** — it didn't trace back to inner-component math, and it produced spread-16 corners on every sub-element (forward-rule strict target was 12, but no tier hits 12 exactly, so all sub-elements landed at 8 with spread 16). The May 10, 2026 retcon to `radius-l` (20) puts Card on the same math-first footing as Toast (16 = 8+8), Tooltip (12 = 4+8), Dialog (20 = 8+12), and the panel family (12 = 8+4) — every primary surface in the system now derives its outer radius from its inner content rather than picking it freely.

**Card and Dialog are now peers** at `radius-l` (20). They share the same outer, the same `--*-px` (12), and the same S-tier inner buttons. The shape language is identical; what differs is **behavior** (Card inline, Dialog modal + scrim) and **content density** (Card has up to 4 regions including media, Dialog has up to 3). This is the correct answer — distinct surfaces shouldn't differ in shape unless their inner components differ. Industry alignment: shadcn / Linear / Atlassian all run their card and dialog surfaces at the same or near-same radius, distinguishing by behavior + content.

**`radius-xl` (24) is now reserved** for future Sheet / Drawer where the larger surface justifies inset 16 (the spacious-tier padding) and 8 + 16 = 24 places it at `radius-xl` exactly. This gives the radius ramp a cleaner slot-to-tier mapping: `radius-m` (16) for compact surfaces (Toast / Banner), `radius-s` (12) for anchored panels (Dropdown / Select / Tooltip), `radius-l` (20) for primary container surfaces (Card / Dialog), `radius-xl` (24) for hero surfaces (Sheet / Drawer when shipped).

**Media slot — INSET (sits inside the card's padding)**:

The media slot is a sibling region in the card's flex column with no negative margins — it occupies the same horizontal indent as title, description, body, and footer. Each media slot carries its own `border-radius: var(--cd-media-rd)` to round its corners cleanly within the padded zone:
```css
.ds-card__media {
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  background: var(--color-surface-subtle);
  border-radius: var(--cd-media-rd);   /* default: radius-xs (8) — derived from inverse rule */
  /* + flex centering for the placeholder icon, overflow:hidden for inner img clipping */
}
```

**Why inset, not edge-to-edge**: an earlier rev had the media bleed to the card's inner border edges using `margin: calc(-1 * var(--cd-pad)) ...` to escape the padding. That "hero card" pattern (bleed image + content-below-with-padding) is one valid pattern but reads as more aggressive / promotional than this system's overall stance, and the user's reference designs (Frames X cards, the inset-photo "Andrew S" card, Figma 295-672) showed inset visuals where the image sits inside the card's padding zone with its own rounded corners. The current rev makes inset the default. A future `--full-bleed` modifier can re-introduce the edge-to-edge pattern if a real consumer ships that needs it (matching how Banner has `--full-bleed` for the same reason); not in scope today.

**Why `radius-xs` (8) for the inset media — derived from the inverse rule**: with Card at outer `radius-l` (20) and the media inset by `--cd-pad` (12), the §2.1.2 forward formula resolves to `inner = outer − inset = 20 − 12 = 8 = radius-xs`. ½ outer = 10, inset 12 > 10, so this is technically the SOFT regime classification — but the soft-regime ideal is exactly `inner ≤ outer − inset = 8`, and `radius-xs` (8) hits that ideal exactly. The result is mathematically as good as strict-regime concentric corners (target hit, no compromise). Spread = 12. Visually parallel curve track between the card's 20-corner and the media's 8-corner around each top corner of the media (the only corners where media is corner-adjacent). The earlier rev had media at `radius-s` (12) when card was at `radius-xl` (24), which was strict-regime exact (24 − 12 = 12); the May 10 fix preserves the same "ideal target hit" property at the new outer 20 by dropping media to 8.

Default placeholder fill is `surface/subtle`, with a centered Phosphor `image` icon (`ICON_IMAGE`) at `icon-xl` (40) in `text/tertiary` — the icon is the explicit-semantic case sanctioned by Standing Rule #8 ("ICON_IMAGE specifically means 'image content goes here'"; the universal `ICON_CIRCLE` placeholder would read as "any content" rather than the specific "image" affordance the slot represents). Default aspect ratio is `16/9` (the universal "image content" ratio); consumers override by setting their own `height` or `aspect-ratio` on the media slot, and replace the placeholder by passing a string of HTML (typically `<img>`) to `cardEl({media: "<img>...</img>"})`.

**No size tiers** for Card. It's a layout primitive, not a sized control. If consumers ship a real need for tighter or roomier chrome, a future `--compact` (or `--spacious`) modifier can be added then; YAGNI for now. The earlier `--spacious` variant was removed (May 2026) on the rationale that consumers wanting feature-promo emphasis are better served by the **tone** axis than by re-padding.

#### 9.13.1 Fixed dimensions and content-fills behavior

Card has **no intrinsic dimensions** — width and height come from the consumer's wrapper. The system supports two layout modes from a single set of CSS rules; the mode is chosen automatically by whether the wrapper has an explicit height.

| Wrapper | Result | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `width: Npx` only (no height) | Card's height = sum of region heights + padding + gaps (intrinsic content sizing). Aspect-ratio on media drives a 16:9 image tile. | Cards in fluid lists where each row's height is content-driven (feed cards, doc fragments). |
| `width: Npx; height: Npx` (fixed) | Card fills the wrapper exactly. Within the fixed shell, **only the media slot shrinks** to absorb deficit; **only the body slot grows** to absorb slack. Header and footer always keep their content heights. | Comparison grids (the playground's variant demos), product card grids (e-commerce, gallery), Figma-style component libraries where every card is a fixed-size frame. |

**The four CSS rules that produce both modes**:

```css
.ds-card        { width: 100%; height: 100%; ... }            /* fill wrapper */
.ds-card__media { aspect-ratio: 16/9; min-height: 0; ... }    /* shrinks (default flex-shrink:1) */
.ds-card__header,
.ds-card__footer { flex-shrink: 0; ... }                       /* never shrink */
.ds-card__body  { flex: 1 0 auto; min-height: 0; ... }        /* grows, never shrinks */
.ds-card__footer { margin-top: auto; ... }                     /* pins to bottom edge case */
```

**Why each rule:**

1. `width: 100%; height: 100%` on the card root. **This is the load-bearing fix.** Without it, the card sizes to its content regardless of what the wrapper is — which is what the previous rev shipped, and which produced the bug where every variant rendered at a different content-driven height despite the wrapper declaring `height: 360px`. In a fluid wrapper (`height: auto`), `height: 100%` resolves back to `auto` because there's no concrete parent height to fill against, so fluid mode is unaffected.

2. `min-height: 0` on the media slot. The default `flex: 0 1 auto` already has `flex-shrink: 1`, but flex children also have a `min-height: auto` (= content's intrinsic min-height) that prevents shrinking past that floor. Setting `min-height: 0` removes that floor and lets the media shrink below its 16:9 aspect-ratio-derived height when the fixed shell can't fit the natural aspect. The trade is deliberate: fluid cards get a clean 16:9 image tile; fixed-shell cards get a media banner whose aspect adapts to fit. At the playground's current 264 × 360 rich preview, the shell is generous enough that media stays at its natural 16:9 aspect (~135 tall) and body flex-grows to absorb slack — but the deficit-absorbs-onto-media policy still fires when the shell is tighter (e.g., a 264 × 240 demo would shrink media to ~50 tall to fit avatar + media + body + footer in 240 vertical pixels).

3. `flex-shrink: 0` on header and footer. Without this, the default shrink:1 would let the title block and footer buttons compress when the shell overflows — the deficit would distribute proportionally across every shrinkable child, clipping text and squashing buttons. Locking shrink to 0 means **the deficit always lands on media**, which is the right resolution because media is the most flexible content (an image/banner can render at any aspect; text cannot wrap around clipped letters and a button cannot be 12px tall).

4. `flex: 1 0 auto` on body (grow:1, **shrink:0**, basis:auto). Body absorbs slack when present (variants without media or with short content). The `shrink: 0` is critical: if body could shrink (the default), a fixed-shell card with media + header + body + footer would distribute deficit across both media AND body, clipping body text in the process. Locking body's shrink to 0 forces the deficit to media. Body's grow:1 fires only when there's slack (no media present, content under-fills the shell).

5. `margin-top: auto` on footer. Edge case only — fires when neither media nor body is present (rare; the demos don't currently produce this combination, but the rule guards it). When a flex-growing sibling is present, `margin-top: auto` resolves to 0 because the slack is already consumed.

**Worked example — Default rich preview at 264 × 264** (square; media + title + body + stretched 2-button footer):
- Available height inside padding: 264 − 24 (12 top + 12 bottom) = 240
- Items at natural size:
  - media (16/9 × 240 = 135 tall)
  - header (title at 14/20) = **20**
  - body (14/20) = 20
  - footer (S-tier) = 28
  - sum = 203
- Flex column gaps (3 gaps × 12) = 36, BUT the header↔body gap is collapsed via `margin-top: -12` on body (text↔text adjacency rule). Net effective gap = 36 − 12 = 24.
- Total layout: 203 items + 24 visible gaps = 227
- Slack: 240 − 227 = 13. Body grows (`flex: 1 0 auto`) to absorb slack.
- Final: media 135 (full 16/9 aspect — no shrink), header 20 (title only), body 33 (= 20 + 13 slack), footer 28.

**Worked example — Tones (Primary / Secondary / Tertiary) at 264 × 264**:
Same math as Default rich preview — each tone card uses identical anatomy. Tone changes the surface fill (`surface/default` → `surface/brand-subtle` → `surface/brand`) and foreground tokens, not the layout math. All three render with media at full 16/9 aspect (135 tall), header 20, body 33 (absorbing 13px slack), footer 28.

**Worked example — Header variant at 264 × 264** (title only, no media, no body, no footer):
- Available: 240 (no inter-region gaps because only one region is present)
- Items: title 20 (header with just title at 14/20)
- Slack: 220. Title doesn't grow (no flex-grow); title sits at the top of the card and the rest is empty space.
- This is the simplest case — Card collapsed to a single-region container. The empty space below the title is acceptable in the demo because we're showing what the simplest card configuration looks like at fixed shell height; in real use, consumers would either let Card use its content height (fluid mode, no fixed wrapper) or fill the rest with body content.

**Worked example — Body variant at 264 × 264** (title + body, no media, no footer):
- Available: 240
- Items: header (title 20) + body (20) = 40
- Flex gaps: 1 gap × 12 = 12, BUT the header↔body gap is collapsed via `margin-top: -12` on body. Net visible gap = 0.
- Total: 40 items + 0 visible gap = 40
- Slack: 200. Only body grows (`flex: 1 0 auto`).
- Final: title 20 (sits at top), body 220 (= 20 + 200 slack). Title and body are flush via the gap collapse — line-box leading carries the visible separation. Body's "Body" text anchors at the top of the body region; the remaining ~200px is empty space — visible breathing room representing what longer body prose would consume.

**Worked example — Footer variant at 264 × 264** (title + body + stretched 2-button footer, no media):
- Available: 240
- Items: header (title 20) + body (20) + footer (28) = 68
- Flex gaps: 2 gaps × 12 = 24, BUT header↔body collapses to 0; body↔footer keeps its 12 (text↔controls adjacency, not collapsed). Net visible gap = 12.
- Total: 68 items + 12 visible gap = 80
- Slack: 160. Only body grows.
- Final: title 20 (top), body 180 (= 20 + 160), footer 28 (bottom, anchored via flex-grow on body absorbing all slack). Title and body flush; body and footer separated by the standard 12px gap. Cancel/Confirm-style affordance reads as a Dialog-like inline confirmation card.

**Why this is the right shape of solution instead of a card-level layout knob**:
- It mirrors the standard "Figma component frame" mental model — fixed shell, content stretches — which is how every modern component library (Untitled UI, Frames X, Material 3 docs, shadcn) ships card components in their templates.
- The consumer chooses dimensions; the system chooses behavior. The CSS rules above mean every Card "just works" at any fixed size without per-instance configuration. Change the wrapper from 264 × 264 to 320 × 480 and every region redistributes through the same flex math.
- Putting the deficit-absorbs-onto-media policy in CSS (instead of asking the consumer to set explicit media dimensions) keeps the API surface simple: `cardEl({ avatar, media, title, description, body, footerStart, footerEnd })` works the same in fluid and fixed modes.

**Playground demos use a fixed `264 × 264` square** for every Card variant — uniform, comparable at equal scale, single row when the viewport can fit them. The width was chosen as the **largest square that fits 3 tone cards in a single row at the canonical 32px frame gap** within the comp-frame__preview's ~870px inner content area (264 × 3 + 32 × 2 gap = 856). Square shape (1:1) gives a tile-style card aligning with gallery / dashboard tile / settings-card patterns (Apple HIG settings tiles, iOS quick-settings, Notion gallery view, Pinterest-square mode, Material 3 Filled card with square aspect). Earlier revs explored landscape 320 × 240 (rich content squashed media to 96 tall 3:1 banner) and portrait 264 × 360 (M3 standard-card aspect ≈ 11:15) before settling on the square 264 × 264 — the user converged on square as the right shape language for "Card as tile" (the system's general-purpose container primitive), with portrait reserved for content-heavy variants like media-card layouts that consumers can opt into via taller wrappers.

**Tones layout — responsive row, 32px frame gap (May 18, 2026)**. The Tones demo wraps three cards in `<div style="display:flex;gap:var(--space-2xl,32px);flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:stretch">`. Two load-bearing rules:
1. **`gap: var(--space-2xl, 32px)` matches the `.comp-frame__preview` direct-children gap** — per the system-wide rule in §6.5 + §7.1.1: "between separately-instantiated components in a frame is 32px (`space-2xl`) unless the cluster is a defined group with its own gap" (button group `space-s` 8 / fieldset items `space-2xs` 4 / dropdown items `space-2xs` 4 / form-field stack `space-l` 16). Tones cards are independent component instances being compared, NOT a defined group, so they use the canonical 32px frame gap. An earlier rev used `space-l` (16) which was a leftover from when the Tones row lived inside an "inner cluster" mental model; corrected to `space-2xl` (32) to align with the rule. Math: 264 × 3 + 32 × 2 = 856 ≤ 870 ✓ at the default preview width.
2. **`flex-wrap: wrap` + per-card `flex-shrink: 0`** — at the default preview width the row fits 3-up; when the viewport narrows below 856px (e.g., a sidebar collapses, a split-pane closes), cards break to a 2+1 or 1+1+1 stack instead of overflowing horizontally. The cards keep their declared 264 width via `flex-shrink: 0` (preventing the "narrow viewport squashes cards to unreadable" failure mode), and the wrapper inherits the comp-frame__preview's `flex-wrap: wrap` to handle the overflow gracefully. The May 18, 2026 rev flipped from `nowrap` to `wrap` because the previous "row scrolls horizontally" behavior was visually broken (cards extending past the preview's rounded right edge) at common laptop widths; the wrap pattern matches the system-wide stance "components compose into the available space rather than overflow it" — same instinct as `min-width: 0` on text-bearing flex children. Inline `flex-wrap:nowrap` remains available for the rare demo that genuinely needs to refuse to wrap, but it is no longer the canonical Card Tones pattern.

### 9.14 Separator — hairline divider

Separator divides content with a single hairline so related groups read as distinct. It's a definition stroke (Job C per §3.5), not an interactive surface.

**Variants**:
- **Horizontal (default)** — `<hr>` element with 1px height, full-width, `border/subtle` bg. Used between vertical sections (e.g., between two paragraphs of body content, between two card-body rows).
- **Vertical** — same hairline turned 90° via the `--vertical` modifier. 1px wide, `align-self: stretch` so it inherits the parent's height. Used between inline groups (e.g., toolbar buttons, breadcrumb segments).
- **Labeled** — when a section needs a name. Two flex-grown hairlines on either side of a centered text label. Label is `body/s` in `text/tertiary` (lighter than the previous `text/secondary` so the hairlines read as the dominant element and the label as quiet metadata, matching shadcn / Linear / Apple HIG separator-label treatment), **sentence case, no letter-spacing** (an earlier rev had `text-transform: uppercase` + `letter-spacing: 0.04em` for "section header" feel; both were removed May 2026 to align with the system-wide no-ALL-CAPS rule per §7.3 — the labeled separator is a generic primitive, not a section header). The canonical demo label is `"Label"` (slot-name convention per §7.3); the earlier `"OR"` example was retired because (a) it violated the no-ALL-CAPS rule and (b) it implied a binary-choice auth pattern when the labeled separator is generic.

**Sizing**: 1px stroke, no padding or margin of its own. The parent is responsible for the spacing around it (per §6.5 layout-gap rule — separator-to-content gap depends on context: `space-m` (12) for tight stacks, `space-l` (16) for form sections, `space-xl` (24) for major content zones).

**Color**: always `border/subtle` (definition stroke). Never escalates to `border/default` or any tonal stroke. If a tone-encoded divider is needed (e.g., "this section is in error"), use a Banner instead — Separator's job is purely structural.

#### 9.14.1 Two width modes — content-width (default) vs full-bleed

A horizontal separator inside a padded surface has two valid widths, and which one is correct is a function of **what the divider is communicating**, not a stylistic preference. Both modes already ship in the system, but the rule deciding when to use which has lived implicitly across §9.3 (Dropdown panel) and §9.14 — restated here as a single rule.

**Mode 1 — Content-width (default, inset by parent padding)**.

`.ds-separator` declares `width: 100%; margin: 0`. When the parent has padding, `width: 100%` resolves to `parent_width − 2 × padding-x` — the line is *naturally* inset to the content area. No negative margin, no escape pattern. The shorter line reads as **a soft boundary inside text flow** — "this paragraph ends, this one begins" — and respects the same indent the surrounding text already sits at.

Use for: Card body separations, Dialog body section breaks, page section dividers between prose blocks, between two stacked Cards in a vertical column, between paragraphs of body text. Anywhere the divider is "in the conversation" rather than "between rows."

Industry alignment: shadcn `<Separator />` inside Cards/content, Apple HIG paragraph breaks, Vercel Geist between blog sections — all inset.

**Mode 2 — Full-bleed (escape parent padding via negative margin)**.

When the divider is inside a *list-of-rows panel surface* (Dropdown, Select panel, Combobox panel, future ActionMenu / command palette), the line escapes the panel's padding via a negative horizontal margin matching that padding:
```css
.ds-{comp}__separator {
  height: 1px;
  background: var(--color-border-subtle);
  margin: 0 calc(-1 * var(--{comp}-pad));
}
```

The line reaches the inner border edges of the panel. The full-bleed reads as **a hard structural break between row groups** — "section of rows ends, new section begins" — matching the harder semantic of grouped lists.

Use for: panel separators only. Specifically: Dropdown menu (`.ds-menu__separator`), Select panel (`.ds-select-panel__separator`), and any future paneled list-of-rows component.

Industry alignment: shadcn / Radix `DropdownMenu.Separator` ships `-mx-1` to negate the panel's `p-1`; Linear menu divider edge-to-edge; Material 3 menu divider; Apple HIG menu separators all bleed to the inner border edge.

**Why two modes, not one:**

| Surface family | What the divider communicates | Right mode | Why the *other* mode would feel wrong |
|---|---|---|---|
| Content surfaces (Card body, Dialog body, prose sections) | Soft boundary in text flow | Content-width | Full-bleed line slicing through 16px of padding looks like a chrome inconsistency — the eye reads "why is the line longer than the text?" |
| Panel surfaces (Dropdown, Select panel, Combobox) | Hard structural break between row groups | Full-bleed | Inset line in a list of rounded rows looks weak/timid — every row visually bleeds to the panel edges, but the divider doesn't, breaking the rhythm |
| Inline groups (toolbars, breadcrumbs) | Soft separator between sibling controls | Content-width vertical | n/a — vertical separators don't have an x-padding axis to escape |

**Coverage today** (audited):

| Component | Class | Mode | Implementation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Dropdown menu | `.ds-menu__separator` | Full-bleed | `margin: 0 calc(-1 * var(--mnu-pad))` |
| Select panel | `.ds-select-panel__separator` | Full-bleed | `margin: 0 calc(-1 * var(--space-2xs, 4px))` |
| Combobox panel (future) | (will reuse Select panel pattern) | Full-bleed | n/a — composes Select panel chrome |
| Card body internal divider | `.ds-separator` (default) | Content-width | naturally inset by `--cd-pad` |
| Dialog body section break | `.ds-separator` (default) | Content-width | naturally inset by `--dlg-px` |
| Page-level section dividers | `.ds-separator` (default) | Content-width | inset by container padding |
| Toolbar / breadcrumb (vertical) | `.ds-separator--vertical` | n/a (vertical) | `align-self: stretch`; no x-padding axis |
| Labeled "Or" separator | `.ds-separator--labeled` | Content-width | flex-grown hairlines on either side of label |

**Why per-component classes for full-bleed instead of a generic `.ds-separator--full-bleed` modifier**:

The negative margin value depends on the *parent's* padding token, which varies per panel — Dropdown uses `--mnu-pad`, Select panel uses `space-2xs` directly. A generic modifier on `.ds-separator` would have to read a parent-set CSS variable (e.g., `--sep-bleed-x`) or accept the value as inline custom property — both would require every parent component to opt in. With only 2-3 consumers today (Dropdown, Select panel, future Combobox panel — which will reuse Select panel's separator), the per-component class pattern is simpler and more obvious to debug.

The §2.2.1 anti-bloat rule applies: extract to a generic modifier when ≥3 distinct consumer parents need it. Today's count is 2 (Combobox composes Select panel). Reconsider when a 4th panel-style component appears.

**a11y wiring**: `role="separator"` + `aria-orientation="horizontal|vertical"`. Labeled variants are still `role="separator"` (the visible label is decorative; if it carries semantic meaning, it should be a heading instead).

### 9.15 Combobox — searchable Select

Combobox is the **type-to-filter popup picker** counterpart to Select (§9.3). Where Select has a fixed dropdown panel, Combobox lets the user type to narrow the list — and visually communicates that affordance via a trailing `caret-up-down` icon in the trigger (the canonical "popup value picker" glyph shared with Apple HIG popup buttons, Tailwind Catalyst, Headless UI Combobox, and shadcn Combobox).

**Composition**: Combobox is mostly a composition of existing primitives, not a new visual language:
- **Trigger**: an `Input` with `trailIcon: ICON_CARET_UP_DOWN`. All Input states (default/hover/focused/error/success/disabled) apply directly. The Input's `clearable` affordance shows when the user has typed — the X chip replaces the caret-up-down for the duration of the typed value, returning to the caret on clear.

**Why a trailing caret-up-down, not a leading magnifying-glass** (rev May 2026). Earlier rev shipped Combobox with `leadIcon: ICON_MAGNIFYING_GLASS` on the rationale "the leading 🔍 says 'type to filter.'" Cross-system research showed the magnifying-glass leading affordance is associated with **dedicated Search fields** (Atlassian, GitHub, Linear's Cmd-K search palette, every IDE search) — full-width input where the user expects to type free-form text and trigger a search action. A Combobox sits inside a form as a value picker (the user is choosing *which option*, not searching free text); using the search-field iconography mis-modeled the affordance as "search this list" rather than "open this picker and the value will swap in place." Tailwind Catalyst, Headless UI, shadcn, and Material 3 Autocomplete all ship trailing carets for exactly this reason. The trailing caret-up-down also reuses the system's existing Combobox space (the clearable X already lives in the trailing slot), avoiding the leading-vs-trailing-slot competition the old layout created.
- **Panel**: a `ds-combobox__panel` matching the Select panel's chrome — `surface/raised` bg, `border/subtle` 1px border (Job C definition stroke), `radius-s` (12), `var(--shadow-3)` elevation, `var(--z-anchored, 40)` layer, `space-2xs` (4) padding, `space-2xs` (4) inter-item gap.
- **Items**: each item reuses `.ds-select-panel__item` styling — same dimensions (32-tall at M, matching the dropdown panel size matrix in §9.3), same state matrix (default / hover / pressed / focus / disabled / selected with checkmark).
- **Empty state**: when the typed query has no matches, the panel shows a single centered `ds-combobox__empty` row with `text/tertiary` "No results" copy, `body/s` (12/16). Replacing the items prevents users from seeing a falsely-empty panel that could read as "click here."

**Why reuse, not reinvent**: a Combobox that introduced its own panel, item, or trigger language would fracture the visual system. The whole point of having a tight design language is that "Select" and "Combobox" are recognizable as siblings — they ARE the same component family with one feature toggle (filter on/off). Industry consensus (Radix Combobox extends Listbox, shadcn Combobox extends Select+Command, Atlassian PopupSelect extends Select) is that Combobox === Select + filter, not Select rebuilt.

**Sizing** matches Select's tier mapping (XS/S/M/L/XL on the input ramp).

**Keyboard contract** (per §14.6 + Combobox-specific):
- Type to filter — every keystroke updates the visible item set
- ↓ to open panel and focus first item; ↑ from closed state opens panel and focuses last item
- ↑/↓ to move between items; Home/End for first/last
- Enter to select the focused item (or the only matching item if exactly one)
- Esc to close the panel without selecting
- Tab to commit and move focus to the next form field
- Type-ahead inside the panel uses the FILTER, not first-letter prefix matching — Combobox's whole job is "the search IS the navigation"

**a11y wiring**: input gets `role="combobox"`, `aria-expanded="true|false"`, `aria-controls="<panel-id>"`, `aria-autocomplete="list"`. Panel gets `role="listbox"`. Each item gets `role="option"` with `aria-selected="true|false"`. The currently-focused item is communicated to the input via `aria-activedescendant`.

**When NOT to use Combobox**: if there are fewer than ~8 fixed options, a regular Select (§9.3) is clearer and more predictable. Combobox shines when the option count is too large to scan visually (countries, time zones, users in a workspace).

### 9.16 Pagination — page navigator

Pagination steps through a sequence of pages (results 1–20, 21–40, 41–60…). It is **structurally a flex row of real Button instances** plus two inline-flex letterbox helpers (ellipsis indicator and status text). The component owns the layout decision; everything else is derived from existing tokens.

**Composition** (no new visual language):
- **Container**: `<nav role="navigation" aria-label="Pagination">` with `display: flex; gap: var(--space-2xs, 4px); align-items: center`. The 4px gap matches the "tight cluster" tier in §6.5 — pagination is a single semantic unit so its members should not visually separate beyond that.
- **Prev / Next**: real `Button {size} icon-only ghost-neutral` containing `ICON_CHEVLEFT` / `ICON_CHEVRIGHT`. Disabled at page boundaries (`current === 1` or `current === total`) using the standard Button disabled state. Optional `textLabels` mode swaps to `Button {size} soft-neutral` with `Previous` / `Next` text labels.
- **Page numbers**: real `Button {size} ghost-neutral` for both rest AND active pages in the **default** preset; the active page gets `background: var(--color-action-hover)` + `box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-border-default)` applied via `[aria-current="page"]` CSS — the canonical "current position" treatment shared with Nav active, Select panel selected, and Bottom Nav active. The **outlined** variant uses `outline-neutral` for both rest and active, with the rest stroke softened to `border/subtle` and the active stroke promoted to `border/default` + `action/hover` bg fill. The **filled** variant uses `soft-neutral` rest + `solid-neutral` active (filled-black active, matching the Linear / Vercel / Primer lane). All three presets are **fully neutral** after the May 2026 brand-color audit (§12.1) — pagination is "position in a navigable set," semantically a list-row state, not a value selection, so it gets the same treatment as Nav active. The `.ds-pagination__page` modifier overrides `--btn-px` to `space-2xs` (4) and adds `min-width: var(--btn-h)` so a single-digit page (e.g., "5") is **exactly button-height wide** (square) while a multi-digit page (e.g., "100") expands to its content within the same height. No special "circle" or "pill" treatment — the Button's tier-default `border-radius` (radius-xs at S, radius-2s at M, radius-m at L) carries the shape.
- **Ellipsis** (`…`): non-interactive `<span class="ds-pagination__ellipsis" aria-hidden="true">` sized to `min-width: var(--pg-h); height: var(--pg-h)` so it occupies the same letterbox slot as a page button, keeping the row rhythm unbroken. Displayed when the page range exceeds `maxVisible` and skips a contiguous block.
- **Status** (`Page X of Y` or `1–30 of 720`): non-interactive `<span class="ds-pagination__status">` with `height: var(--pg-h)` and `padding: 0 var(--space-s, 8px)`. Used by the compact variant in place of numbered pages, or composed alongside the nav row when paired with a page-size selector.

**Sizing — no special tier; the system's existing 5-tier ramp does the work**:

| Tier | `--pg-h` | `--pg-fs / --pg-lh` | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| XS | `control-xs` (24) | 12/16 | Ultra-dense data tables |
| **S** | **`control-s` (28)** | **12/16** | **Default — dense pagination, the role this component plays in most product surfaces** |
| M | `control-m` (32) | 14/20 | Form-paired pagination, where row should feel matched to adjacent M-tier inputs |
| L | `control-l` (40) | 14/20 | Generous spacing, large lists |

Tokens flow from `.ds-pagination--{size}` modifier on the parent, declared once at the component level — every member (Button via tier class, ellipsis via `--pg-h`, status via `--pg-h`) picks up the right height and font automatically. Swapping `size` at the parent updates the entire row in lockstep with no other config.

**Why default = S (28), not M (32)**: pagination is one of the few "always dense by role" components in the system. It's not a primary action — it's tertiary navigation through a paginated list. Industry consensus puts it at the S-tier height (Linear ~28, Vercel Geist ~28, Notion ~28, GitHub Primer ~32). M (the system's form-control default) is too heavy when seven of these stack in a row. The lever to make pagination smaller is **not** custom padding inside the Button primitive — it's choosing the right tier from the existing ramp. This is the reason §9.16 was redesigned (May 2026): an earlier rev defaulted to M because "M is the system default" — but that conflated "default form tier" with "default pagination tier." They're different roles; pagination's role wants S.

**Active-page treatment — all neutral (May 2026 brand-color audit, §12.1)**. After a system-wide audit of where brand color carries semantic weight, pagination dropped brand from every preset and now sits in the same chrome family as Nav active / Select panel selected / Bottom Nav active. The rationale: pagination's active page is a **list-row state** ("position in a navigable set"), not a value selection. Brand color is reserved in this system for true value/role selectors where commitment matters (Switch on, RadioCard selected, primary CTAs, focus halos, validation, inline links) — applying it to a list-row "you are here" indicator over-emphasizes the marker and competes with adjacent brand surfaces (filter Selects, primary actions in toolbars sharing the row with pagination). Researched against shadcn (active = `outline` variant + ghost inactive), GitHub Primer (active = filled neutral + bare-link inactive), Vercel (active = filled black `solid-neutral` + bare-text inactive), Linear (active = filled neutral with brand text + ghost inactive), Material 3 (active = `secondaryContainer` brand-tonal + text inactive), Atlassian (active = `solid-brand` + text inactive), Tailwind UI (active = saturated `solid-brand` + ghost inactive). The convergent industry split for ACTIVE is between **filled neutral** (Vercel/Linear/Primer/shadcn) and **filled brand** (Material 3 brand-tonal / Atlassian solid-brand / Tailwind solid-brand). We sit with the **filled neutral** camp — same lane as Vercel/Linear/Primer/shadcn — because brand-active conflicts with the cardinality rule documented above (pagination is list-row navigation, not value selection).

**Default preset — `ghost-neutral` rest/arrows + `ghost-neutral` active with bg + ring chrome**. Both rest and active pages use the `ghost-neutral` Button variant; the active page is overridden via `[aria-current="page"]` to paint `background: var(--color-action-hover)` + `box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-border-default)`. This is the canonical "current position" treatment used everywhere in the system for list-row active (§9.3 dropdown/select panel selected, §4.5.1 selected-row pattern, Nav vertical/horizontal/bottom active). Reads as "this is the row you're on" without any chromatic carrier. Transparent fills throughout; the inset ring + bg tint is the only chrome.

**Outlined preset (`variant: "outlined"`) — `outline-neutral` rest/arrows + `outline-neutral` active with stroke + bg chrome**. Every page carries a hairline outline at rest (calibrated down to `border/subtle`); the active page promotes its stroke to `border/default` AND adds the `action/hover` bg fill. The result is "outlined throughout with one darker-stroked, filled-bg current page" — reads as more weighted than the default ghost preset and more contained than the filled preset, useful for surfaces where pagination needs to compete visually with neighboring outlined controls.

**Filled preset (`variant: "filled"`) — `soft-neutral` rest/arrows + `solid-neutral` active**. Every page carries a filled chip at rest (`neutral/50` fill + `neutral/100` hairline); the current page swaps to a saturated `solid-neutral` (filled near-black). This matches Vercel Geist / Linear / GitHub Primer's filled-neutral-active convention. Use when the row should read more "interactive button" than "soft hairline navigation" — e.g., toolbars where pagination sits next to other filled controls.

**Why three neutral presets, no brand option**: pagination's role is positional, not committal. Adding brand chrome would either (a) double-code with adjacent brand surfaces in the same toolbar, or (b) draw the eye more than the content the user is paginating through. The three presets cover the three useful weights — quiet (default ghost), middle (outlined), weighted (filled) — without ever reaching for color as the differentiator. Granular `arrowVariant` / `activeVariant` / `inactiveVariant` props still exist for consumers who explicitly want a brand-active opt-out (e.g., for a documentation site where pagination IS the primary navigation), but the three documented presets are the system stance.

Earlier revs cycled through six brand-variant attempts (solid-brand / outline-brand / soft-brand-on-outline / soft-brand-on-ghost / solid-brand-on-ghost / soft-brand-with-selection-border) — each had a hierarchy, chrome-balance, or audit-rule failure. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.4 for the full rev-by-rev breakdown including the May 2026 brand-color audit decision.** The current neutral-only model resolves the previous "brand chrome dominates list-row state" problem at once: all three presets carry the "you are here" signal through bg + ring chrome (default), stroke + bg chrome (outlined), or fill saturation (filled), with brand reserved for the value-selection / commitment cases brand-active was originally meant for.

**Padding philosophy — keep on the Button ramp, lever is the size tier (non-negotiable)**: pagination buttons are real `Button` instances; their padding is the Button primitive's responsibility (universal optical-padding rule §6.1, tier-default `--btn-px` from §2.3). The single sanctioned cosmetic deviation is `.ds-pagination__page` overriding `--btn-px` to `space-2xs` (4) and clamping `min-width: var(--btn-h)` — that's there because labeled square buttons with single-digit content need tighter horizontal padding than text buttons or the digit floats inside a too-wide letterbox. Beyond that, **do not invent custom padding for pagination** — if the buttons feel too big in a context, drop the tier from S → XS, not from S → "S-but-tighter-padding". Inventing a Button-but-tighter primitive for pagination would force a parallel chrome model (hover, focus, disabled, pressed) that has to be maintained every time Button updates. The size-tier lever (XS/S/M/L) and the `--btn-px` override on `.ds-pagination__page` together cover the full density range without any new tokens or chrome.

**Variants documented**:
- **Numbered** (default): `[<] [1] [2] [3] … [17] [>]` — full numbered pagination with auto-ellipsis when `total > maxVisible` (default 7). Default-preset chrome: `ghost-neutral` rest + active, with active getting `action/hover` bg + `inset 0 0 0 1px border/default` ring via `[aria-current="page"]`.
- **Outlined** (`variant: "outlined"`): same content, but every page is an `outline-neutral` Button — rest stroke softened to `border/subtle`, active promoted to `border/default` + `action/hover` bg fill. Use for surfaces where pagination needs more weight than the ghost default but less than filled.
- **Filled** (`variant: "filled"`): same content, but every page is a filled chip — `soft-neutral` rest, `solid-neutral` active (filled-black, matching Vercel / Linear / Primer's filled-neutral-active convention). Use when the row should read as a button toolbar.
- **Few pages**: when `total <= maxVisible`, every page renders without ellipsis. `[<] [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [>]`.
- **Compact**: `[<] [Page X of Y] [>]` — replaces the number row with a status label. Use for narrow surfaces (data table headers, sidebar lists) where the number-rich variant would wrap or crowd.
- **Text labels**: `[Previous] [1] [2] [3] … [17] [Next]` — uses real `Button` with text labels for prev/next instead of icon-only chevrons. Pair with `arrowVariant: "soft-neutral"` so the text buttons read as the secondary tier.
- **With page size**: `<PageSizeSelect> <Status> <Pagination compact>` — composed via `.ds-pagination__group` flex container with `gap: var(--space-l, 16px)` (the form-row gap from §6.5). Pagination's component scope ENDS at the nav row; the page-size Select and status text are separate sibling elements that the consumer arranges. This matches Image 2 and Image 1 bottom-left patterns.

**Computed page list** (`paginationComputePages(current, total, maxVisible)`):
- `total <= maxVisible` → flat list, every page rendered.
- otherwise: always show first and last; show 1 sibling on each side of current (configurable, default 1); near the start, extend the right window to `[2 + 2*siblings]`; near the end, extend the left window to `[total - 1 - 2*siblings]`. Two ellipses are inserted at most.
- Reference algorithm matches shadcn `Pagination` and Vercel Geist `Pagination`.

**Math-derived values — no overrides anywhere**:
- Container gap: `space-2xs` (4) — the tight-cluster tier (§6.5)
- Compose gap (between page-size Select and pagination): `space-l` (16) — the form-row tier (§6.5)
- Page button square width: `var(--btn-h)` (the tier's `--control-{size}` token)
- Page button horizontal padding: `space-2xs` (4) — minimum to keep multi-digit pages from clipping
- Status horizontal padding: `space-s` (8) — text-breathing for the "Page X of Y" label (§6.1.1: text needs horizontal > vertical)
- Ellipsis: same dimensions as a page button — keeps row rhythm continuous

**a11y wiring**:
- Container: `<nav role="navigation" aria-label="Pagination">`
- Active page button: `aria-current="page"` + `aria-label="Current page, page N"`
- Inactive page buttons: `aria-label="Go to page N"`
- Prev / Next: `aria-label="Previous page"` / `aria-label="Next page"`
- Disabled prev/next: `disabled` attribute + `aria-disabled="true"` (matches our other disabled-button patterns; the redundant `aria-disabled` is sanctioned because some screen readers don't announce `disabled` consistently across implementations)
- Ellipsis: `aria-hidden="true"` so it isn't announced as content
- Status: regular `<span>` (announced as text inline)

**Keyboard contract** (per §14.6 + Pagination-specific):
- Tab moves focus through the buttons in order (Prev → 1 → 2 → … → N → Next)
- Enter / Space activates the focused button
- No special arrow-key navigation between pages (each button is independently focusable)

**When NOT to use Pagination**: use Combobox (§9.15) when the page count is so large that scanning even with ellipsis is impractical, or use **infinite scroll / lazy-loading** when the user's mental model is "stream of recent" rather than "discrete pages." Pagination is for catalog-style data where users want to jump directly to a numbered page.

**Cross-system alignment**: our three neutral presets sit squarely in the **filled-neutral-active camp** alongside Vercel Geist, Linear, GitHub Primer, and shadcn — all of which use neutral chrome for pagination's active page. The brand-active camp (Material 3 brand-tonal, Atlassian solid-brand, Tailwind UI solid-brand) is the minority and treats pagination as a value selector rather than a list-row navigator; we explicitly reject that framing for the reasons documented in the §12.1 audit. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.4 for the full cross-system reference table and the May 2026 audit decision history.**

### 9.17 Chip — interactive label

Chip is the **interactive** sibling of Badge. Both are rounded-rectangle at `radius-xs` (8) per §3.5.1 — neither uses pill, because pill is reserved for shape-coded primitives (Switch, Radio dial, notification dot). The differentiation is **stroke + behavior**, not shape:

| Property | Chip | Badge |
|---|---|---|
| Border | 1px `border/default` (Job A — affordance) | none — `border-color: transparent` (fill carries the boundary per §3.5) |
| Hover/focus/pressed states | yes (full state matrix) | no (read-only) |
| Selected state | yes — neutral `action/hover` surface + `text/brand` leading check (tonal variants use `feedback/{tone}/bg`) | no |
| Default sizes | XS=24 / S=28 (matches Button XS/S) | S=20 / M=24 |
| Default fill | transparent | tonal soft (`feedback/{tone}/bg`) |

At the worst-case overlap (Badge M 24 vs Button XS 24 vs Chip XS 24, all tonal soft fill), the **stroke-presence** difference (Chip carries a 1px `border/default` affordance stroke; Badge is borderless — fill carries its boundary) and the **interaction-state presence** (Chip hovers / focuses / presses; Badge doesn't) keep them distinguishable. The borderless Badge matches shadcn / Atlassian Lozenge / Vercel Geist Tag / Linear status pill / Apple HIG tag — all ship borderless badges; the missing 1px edge against a Chip's visible stroke is the instant read. The earlier "make Badge pill" attempt failed because counter Badges (single-character "3" / "8" / "12") collapsed into circles when pill — that's notification-dot iconography, the wrong identity for our Badge. Keeping both at `radius-xs` and differentiating via stroke-presence is the durable answer.

**Sizing**: XS=24 / S=28 only — Chip's design intent is "compact label that lives next to text or in a toolbar," so larger tiers (M=32, L=40) would compete with Button. If a use case ever needs M-tier chip behavior, the right answer is a `Button` with the chip's selected tonal treatment — the components are already adjacent in the visual language at the small tiers; pushing Chip into M would force a size-overlap that doesn't exist today.

**State matrix**: identical to other interactive components — default / hover (via `action/hover` overlay + `border/hover`) / focused (two-layer halo) / pressed (`brightness(.92)`) / selected / disabled.

**Selected state** — **neutral surface treatment** with a small brand-colored leading check icon as the only chromatic accent. Default selected paints `--chip-bg: action/hover` + `--chip-fg: text/primary` + `--chip-br: border/default` (the "selected current-position" surface treatment shared with Nav active, Select panel selected, and Pagination active per the May 2026 brand-color audit §12.1); the leading `ICON_CHECKMARK` glyph stays `text/brand` so the chip carries a single small brand accent that says "this one is on" without flooding the surface with brand color. A row of selected filter chips reads as a quiet sequence of marked items with restrained brand accents, not a wall of brand fills. Tonal selected variants (`info / success / warning / danger`) keep their existing `feedback/{tone}/bg + text/{tone} + feedback/{tone}/border` treatment — when chips categorize status (a "Pending" / "Approved" / "Rejected" filter row), the tone IS the message and the chromatic surface is justified by Standing Rule #4 (tone-on-tone foreground).

**Optional leading icon**: when `selected` is true and no explicit `icon` is passed, the system renders a `ICON_CHECKMARK` glyph at the leading edge in `text/brand` — the canonical "filter on" affordance shared with Material 3 Filter Chip, Linear filter chip, and shadcn. The check is the **single sanctioned brand touch** on the selected chip (Standing Rule #4 Rule C — icon = brand when icon is a state indicator). Consumers can override via `icon: ICON_*` for category cues (e.g., a "Bug" filter chip with a leading bug icon); custom icons inherit the chip's foreground color (`text/primary` when selected) per Rule A, not the brand color reserved for the check-as-state-indicator.

**Removable variant**: trailing dismiss `<button>` containing `ICON_XMARK`. Wrapper switches from `<button>` to `<div role="button">` to avoid invalid button-in-button HTML — same pattern Atlassian Tag and shadcn use. Dismiss button has its own focus halo and `aria-label="Remove {label}"`.

**Per-edge padding override** (§6.1.2): leading icon and trailing dismiss collapse the chip's horizontal padding to `(chip-h - chip-icon)/2 - 1px` so the glyph has uniform corner inset (8/8 at S, 6/6 at XS) instead of the bare 8px text padding.

**a11y wiring**:
- Filter/toggle chip: `<button role="button" aria-pressed="true|false">` — Tab to focus, Space/Enter to toggle.
- Removable chip: `<div role="button" tabindex="0" aria-pressed="…">` for the wrapper, `<button aria-label="Remove …">` for the X. The wrapper and the X are independent focus stops; Tab moves between them.
- Disabled: `disabled` on `<button>` wrappers, `aria-disabled="true"` + `tabindex="-1"` on `<div role="button">` wrappers.

### 9.18 Skeleton — loading-state placeholder

Skeleton renders a placeholder where content is loading, with a subtle shimmer sweep across `surface/subtle`. Three primitive shapes compose into any loading recipe (avatar + name + line, card + image + title + body, etc.):

| Shape | Default size | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `block` | 100% width × `space-2xl` (32) | Body content placeholder; consumers override height for image / card placeholders |
| `text` | 100% width × `space-l` (16), `radius-2xs` | Single text line at body/m or body/s tier |
| `text-lg` | 100% width × `space-xl` (24), `radius-2xs` | Title or label/l tier line |
| `circle` | `control-l` (40) × `control-l` (40), `9999px` | Avatar placeholder |

**Shimmer**: a `linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, action/hover 50%, transparent)` overlay translated from `-100%` to `100%` over 1.6s. Honors `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` via the global override (Part 4.7) — in reduced-motion mode the shimmer halts and the base surface remains visible as the loading state.

**a11y wiring**: `role="status" aria-busy="true" aria-label="Loading"`. Should not be focused or read individually for each skeleton in a list — wrap the surrounding container in a single `aria-live="polite"` region if multiple skeletons share a loading event.

**Composition rule**: don't reach for a "skeleton-card" or "skeleton-row" primitive — compose primitives. The "row" pattern is `circle 40 + flex column { text-lg 70% + text 100% }`, which Skeleton documents as a demo but doesn't ship as a separate API.

### 9.19 Spinner — circular counterpart to Progress

Spinner is the **circular counterpart to Progress** (§9.20). Same mental model (track + indicator), same track color (`surface/subtle`), same rounded end-caps — different geometry (circular vs linear) and a distinct "rail and bead" depth effect where the track ring is visibly thicker than the indicator arc.

**The pattern: track + indicator (same width).** Two stacked circles in a 24×24 SVG (cx=cy=12, r=10, circumference ≈ 62.83):
1. **Track** — full visible ring at `--spin-track` (`surface/subtle`). Same `--spin-stroke` as indicator. Static.
2. **Indicator** — same circle geometry at `--spin-stroke`, but `stroke-dasharray: 25, 100` makes only ~40% of the circumference visible. Animated via `dsSpinnerRotate` at `1s linear infinite`. The track stays put; only the indicator rotates.

**Why same stroke width (not thicker track).** A thicker track ("rail and bead") was tested but produced sub-pixel anti-aliasing at small render sizes (12–16px) — the fractional stroke widths mapped to non-integer physical pixels and created a fuzzy/pixelated appearance. Matching stroke widths keeps both layers crisp because the browser only needs to anti-alias one edge width. It also mirrors Progress directly (track and fill share the same height).

**Why 40% visible arc.** Matches Progress's 40% indeterminate-bar width exactly. Same magnitude, different geometry — linear vs circular.

**Why this differs from the prior gap-only spinner.** Earlier Spinner shipped as a **single circle with `stroke-dasharray: "60 100"`** — a ~95% visible arc with a small invisible gap. That diverged from Progress's "track + fill" mental model. The track + indicator update aligns the two indeterminate primitives so they read as siblings.

**Sizing**: XS=12 / S=16 / M=20 / L=24 — matches `--icon-{xs,s,m,l}` exactly so a spinner can replace ANY inline icon at the same tier without affecting layout.

**Stroke-linecap: round** on both layers gives the indicator's end-caps the same soft, pill-rounded look as Progress's `--pgs-rd: 9999px` fill.

**Tones — two only: brand + neutral. No semantic tones.**

| Tone | Token | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `brand` (default) | `action/solid/brand/bg` | Standard loading indicator — default CTA color |
| `neutral` | `text/secondary` | Low-emphasis: in-Button, dense toolbars, Card chrome where brand competes |

**Why no semantic tones (info/success/warning/danger).** Industry research: no major design system ships semantic tones on loading indicators — not shadcn, Material 3, Primer, Atlassian, Apple HIG, Linear, or Vercel. The reason is conceptual: both Spinner and Progress indicate a **temporal state** ("loading" / "how much is done"), not a **semantic state** ("danger" / "success"). That semantic meaning lives on the surrounding context (button label, alert container, field caption), not on the loading primitive itself. Following Primer's lead (one default color, custom via prop override), neither Spinner nor Progress ships named tones — consumers override `--spin-fill` or `--pgs-fill` locally when they need a specific color.

**Animation**: `1s linear infinite` rotation on the indicator only (track stays static). Honors `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` via global override — rotation reduces to `0.01ms`, leaving a static partial ring that still communicates "indeterminate state" through track + arc geometry alone.

**a11y wiring**: `role="status" aria-label="Loading"` on the wrapper. The inner SVG carries `aria-hidden="true"`. For Button-with-Spinner patterns, the Button itself should also carry `aria-busy="true"` + `disabled` so screen readers announce the loading state at the control level.

### 9.20 Progress — determinate or indeterminate progress bar

Progress communicates task progress with a single horizontal bar. Determinate version takes a `value` (0–100) and animates the fill width via `--motion-medium`; indeterminate version slides a 40%-wide bar across the track.

**Sizing**: S=4 / M=6 / L=8 — three thin tiers. Default is S (4) because that's the tier most modern systems converge on for inline progress (shadcn Progress 16 px container with 8 px fill, Linear 4 px, Material 3 4 px linear). L=8 is reserved for emphasized contexts (file upload "this is the main thing" surfaces).

**Track + fill**: track at `surface/subtle`, fill at `action/solid/brand/bg`. Single color, no predefined tones — following Primer's guidance (one default color, custom via prop). A progress bar communicates temporal state ("how much is done"), not semantic state. If a consumer needs a specific color (quota threshold, danger state), they override `--pgs-fill` locally. The system doesn't sanction named tones for this component for the same reason Spinner doesn't: no major design system (Primer, shadcn, Material 3, Apple HIG, Linear, Vercel) ships predefined semantic tones on a progress indicator.

**Indeterminate animation**: the 40% fill translates from `-100%` to `250%` across the track over 1.6s with `--easing-in-out`. The over-shoot creates the characteristic "fast across the middle, slow at the edges" feel modern systems use (Material 3 LinearProgress, shadcn Progress, GitHub Actions). Honors `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` — in reduced-motion mode the bar pauses at the start position and the track remains visible as the loading state.

**Determinate transition**: width changes use `var(--motion-medium) var(--easing-out)` so consumers can call `progress.style.setProperty('--pgs-value', '60%')` and get a smooth animated update without writing motion-by-hand. `--easing-out` matches Toast / Dialog enter transitions for system-wide motion-language consistency.

**a11y wiring**:
- Determinate: `role="progressbar" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100" aria-valuenow="{value}" aria-label="…"`.
- Indeterminate: `role="progressbar" aria-label="…"` only — `aria-value*` are explicitly omitted so screen readers announce indeterminate state correctly.
- Surrounding context should provide a meaningful label (e.g., `aria-label="Uploading image.jpg"`).

### 9.21 Bottom Nav — persistent mobile bottom navigation bar (Navigation > Bottom)

Bottom Nav is the **third placement of Navigation** (alongside Vertical and Horizontal), purpose-built for the persistent mobile navigation bar that switches between an app's top-level destinations (Home / Search / Create / Profile, etc.). Documented in the IA under Navigation; the implementation keeps a distinct CSS primitive (`.ds-bottom-nav`) because the layout (stacked icon-above-label, single canonical size, edge-anchored chrome) is meaningfully different from the inline `.ds-nav-item` used by Vertical and Horizontal placements. Both can be true: **the IA says "Navigation has three placements," the implementation says "three layout primitives sharing the Navigation role."** **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.8 for the May 2026 IA fold rationale (the rev that moved Bottom Nav from a parallel top-level component into Navigation > Bottom).**

The grammar table that justified the layout difference:

| Property | Bottom Nav | Horizontal Nav |
|---|---|---|
| Role | App-level mobile root navigation | Page-level section navigation (desktop/web) |
| Default chrome | Full-width edge-anchored, white bg + 1px top hairline (canonical iOS / M3 / Notion / Linear / Stripe pattern) | None — items in flow |
| Active indicator | Typography-only: `text/primary` foreground + weight 500 | bg-highlight (`action/hover`) |
| Layout | Icon stacked **above** label (vertical) | Icon **beside** label (horizontal) |
| Cardinality use | 3-5 evenly-distributed root destinations | Many items, often left-anchored |
| Position | Fixed at bottom of viewport (mobile) | Top chrome / page header (desktop) |
| Sizing model | Single canonical size | 5-tier ramp (XS/S/M/L/XL matching Button) |

The grammar prevents either placement from being collapsed into the other (you can't render an icon-stacked Mobile bottom nav using `.ds-nav-item`'s inline layout, and you can't render a desktop horizontal nav using Bottom's stacked layout); the IA fold just acknowledges they share the Navigation role.

**Why a single canonical size, not a multi-tier ramp**: Bottom Nav is mobile primary navigation with thumb-reach ergonomic constraints. Industry consensus is **unequivocally one size**:

| System | Component | Container height | Sizes shipped |
|---|---|---|---|
| Apple HIG | Tab Bar (iOS) | 49pt (fixed) | One |
| Apple HIG | Tab Bar (iOS 18 floating) | ~50pt pill | One |
| Material 3 | Navigation Bar | 80dp | One (NavigationRail is a *separate component* for tablet, not a size variant) |
| Notion (mobile) | (custom) | ~60pt | One |
| X / Twitter (mobile) | (custom) | ~50pt | One |
| Primer (GitHub) | — | — | Doesn't ship one |
| shadcn | — | — | Doesn't ship one |

A "dense" or "comfortable" bottom nav doesn't make sense for the role — there's no use case for shrinking the primary navigation in a mobile app. Multiple sizes invite consumers to pick the wrong tier and accidentally produce a harder-to-tap bar.

**Two sanctioned variants** (composable), neither changes the canonical size:
- **`--floating`** restores the smaller "floating tab bar" chrome (rounded pill container with pill-shaped per-item backplate, `surface/default` bg, uniform `border/subtle` hairline) — locally overrides `--bn-h: 56` (vs the default's 64) so items render at 48-tall (the previous chip-size geometry). Used for products where the bottom nav lives as a contained widget rather than the app's edge-anchored skeleton (e.g., a launch-screen with floating nav, a custom mobile shell with extra chrome below the bar). Items collapse to inline-flex hugging content; pill-on-pill per-item radius matches the container shape (the canonical floating-action-bar idiom from Linear / Vercel).
- **`--icon-only`** drops labels and collapses each item to a circular pill around the icon. Composes naturally with `--floating` for compact icon-bar contexts (e.g., a toolbar of nav glyphs). Has no effect on the full-width default — full-width Bottom Nav always shows labels because the canonical mobile-nav identity demands them. Per-item L/R padding derives from the optical-centering formula (§6.1): `padding = (item_area − icon) / 2`. At floating tokens (container 56 via local `--bn-h` override, pad 4, icon 20): `padding = (48 − 20) / 2 = 14 → item width = 14 + 20 + 14 = 48 = item height` (perfect circle). The `calc()` expression makes the rule self-correct under any container/icon/padding combination.

If a future "compact mobile" use case really needs a smaller height, that's a different component (a "Tab Bar mini" or a future side-anchored `NavigationRail`), not a size variant on Bottom Nav.

**Active state — typography-only (color + weight), no backplate**

The active item is signaled by a **horizontal-nav-style rounded backplate** — the same idiom used by `.ds-nav-item` (Vertical and Horizontal placements). Bottom Nav now reads as "a stacked version of the horizontal nav": the bar is a row of rounded-rectangle item backplates, each containing a stacked icon-over-label content layout. The Navigation grammar is unified across all three placements — same per-item backplate language (transparent → `action/hover` bg → `action/hover` bg + inset 1px `border/default` ring per the canonical selected-row treatment in §9.3 / §4.5.1), only the layout direction (vertical / horizontal / horizontal-bottom-anchored) and per-item content arrangement (inline / inline / stacked) differ.

**Active state**: `action/hover` bg + `text/primary` color + `box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-border-default)` ring. This is exactly the `.ds-nav-item.is-active` treatment from §9.3 / §4.5.1 — the canonical "selected list-of-rows" chrome reused here. The `--bn` token prefix is preserved for backwards compatibility with consumer CSS, but the values track Nav.

Weight stays at **400 across all states** (per §5.1.1 — components default to Regular; the backplate's bg + ring chrome carries what color alone could not, so no weight escalation is needed). Icon stays at **Phosphor Regular weight** (§5.3.1 — one weight system-wide; the foreground-color shift `text/secondary → text/primary` carries the icon's perceived darkness). **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.8 for the dropped typography-only-active rev (May 2026) and the no-Filled-icon-variant rationale.**

**Default chrome (full-width, canonical mobile pattern with horizontal-nav backplates)**:
- Container: **64 tall**, full viewport width (`width: 100%`), no outer border-radius, `surface/default` bg, **no top border** (Material 3 NavigationBar dropped the divider in v3; Apple iOS pairs hers with translucent blur, so a standalone hairline reads as imposed chrome — the bar's bottom-of-viewport position + parent shadow / tonal break is enough separation in production)
- **Outer padding `--bn-pad`** (4 = `space-2xs`) on all sides so item backplates have breathing from the bar edges
- **Inter-item gap `--bn-gap`** (4 = `space-2xs`) between siblings
- Items distribute evenly via `flex: 1 1 0` — every destination gets the same width regardless of label length (canonical iOS / M3 / Notion / Linear / Stripe behavior)
- **Per-item radius**: `--bn-item-rd: var(--radius-l, 20px)` — gives a 20/56 = 35.7% radius ratio against the 56-tall inner item. This sits intentionally one notch rounder than the control-family ramp (which holds 29-33% — Nav horizontal L is now `radius-s` 12 / 40-tall = 30%) because the Bottom Nav backplate reads as a *container surface* for stacked icon+label content, not as a control; the softer corner aligns it with the Card / Dialog `radius-l` surface family rather than the Button-tier control ramp. The backplate radius is a deliberate surface-family choice, not a control-tier derivation, so it didn't move with the May 2026 control-ramp tightening.
- Inner item content area: `64 − 2×4 = 56` tall (preserved unchanged from the prior 56-tall flat-bar geometry)
- Icon: **20 (`icon-m`)** — sits inside the 20-25 band that Apple iOS Tab Bar and Material 3 Navigation Bar both occupy
- Label: 10/12 monospace (small mobile label tier)
- Internal item gap (icon to label): 4 (`space-2xs`)
- Vertical content inside item: 20 + 4 + 12 = 36
- Visible breathing inside item: (56 − 36) / 2 = **10px above icon, 10px below label** — comfortably above the 6px breathing of the floating-pill variant because the full-width bar has the entire 56-tall area for content (the floating variant's 4px container padding consumed 8px of that budget)

**Floating chrome (`--floating` modifier)**:
- Locally overrides `--bn-h: 56` (was the previous default's bar height) to preserve the previous chip geometry where items are 48-tall (56 − 2×4 pad). The default's 64-tall bar is for full-bleed mobile chrome; floating wants the smaller, more compact chip identity.
- Container: 56 tall, `9999px` radius (full pill), 4px internal padding (`space-2xs`), `surface/default` bg, 1px `border/subtle` uniform Job C definition stroke
- Items use `flex: 0 0 auto` (hug content) — bar width grows with label width
- **Per-item radius locally overrides to `9999px`** (pill-on-pill, matching the container shape — this is the canonical "floating action bar" idiom from Linear / Vercel)
- Item area (= container minus padding × 2): 48 tall
- Visible breathing inside item: (48 − 36) / 2 = 6px above icon, 6px below label — matches Apple iOS Tab Bar's proportional breathing (~6pt internal at the canonical 49pt height) at the cost of a tighter content area than the full-width default

**Why icon-m, not icon-l**: mobile bottom nav icons across Apple iOS Tab Bar (~25pt SF Symbol) and Material 3 Navigation Bar (24dp) sit in the 20-25 band; `icon-m` (20) lands inside that range and produces the right breathing against our 56-tall inner item area. **The cascade lesson — icon size is part of the geometry, not an independent token**: when container height changes, icon size has to be re-validated against the breathing target, same as label and gap. Whenever you adjust one geometry value, walk the formula and check that the others still produce the intended breathing.

**Why 64-tall (with 4px outer pad → 56-tall inner)**: the inner content area stays at 56 (the canonical mid-tier between Apple iOS 49pt and Material 3 80dp where the bar feels like a native mobile nav, established in earlier revs). The bar grows by 8 to add the new outer padding, which gives item backplates breathing from the bar edges so they read as deliberate "contained items" rather than full-bleed strips. Every Navigation primitive in the system uses outer container padding around per-item backplates (Vertical: panel padding around items; Horizontal: bar padding); Bottom Nav now matches.

Bottom Nav has been through 7 geometry revisions (48→56→64-tall, varying icon size / gap / active-state chrome) before arriving at the current model. The current rev (May 2026) — 64-tall full-width container, 20-icon, 4-gap, horizontal-nav-style backplate active (`action/hover` bg + inset 1px `border/default` ring per §9.3) + per-item radius-l (20) — reads as "stacked version of horizontal nav" and unifies the Navigation grammar across Vertical / Horizontal / Bottom placements. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.5 for the full geometry rev log.**

**The lesson — geometry is a system, not a stack of independent tokens**: container height, icon size, gap, label tier, AND active-state chrome all participate in producing the visual identity. When an active-state rule changes, the *whole geometry* has to be re-validated.

**Item state matrix**:
| State | Treatment |
|---|---|
| Default | `text/secondary` foreground, weight 400, transparent bg, `radius-l` (20) backplate (invisible until hover/active fills it) |
| Hover (excluded when `is-active` or disabled) | `text/primary` foreground + `action/hover` bg overlay (sanctioned exception to §4.3.1 foreground-stable-on-hover, same justification as Tabs and Nav vertical/horizontal: the inactive item's chrome is intentionally muted, hover is the affordance signal that "this is clickable") |
| Pressed (`:active`) | `filter: brightness(.92)` |
| Focus (`:focus-visible`) | `outline: 2px solid var(--color-border-focus); outline-offset: -2px` (matches Nav vertical/horizontal — inset outline follows the backplate's `radius-l` curve automatically) |
| Active (`is-active`) | `text/primary` foreground + `action/hover` bg + `box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-border-default)` ring — the canonical selected-row treatment from §9.3 / §4.5.1, identical to Nav vertical/horizontal active |
| Disabled | `opacity: .5`, `cursor: not-allowed`, `pointer-events: none` |

**a11y wiring**:
- Container: `<nav role="navigation" aria-label="Main">` (landmark navigation)
- Each item: `<button>` or `<a>` (matches whether the consumer is doing client-side state or routing)
- Active item: `aria-current="page"`
- Disabled item: `disabled` attribute (button) or `aria-disabled="true"` (anchor)
- Icon: `aria-hidden="true"` on the wrapper; the visible label carries the announcement

**Keyboard contract**: Tab moves focus between items; Enter / Space activates; no special arrow-key navigation (matches the mobile-touch-primary nature of the component — keyboard is a fallback, not the primary interaction model).

### 9.22 Breadcrumb — hierarchical path indicator

Breadcrumb shows the current page's location in a hierarchy and lets users step up to ancestor pages. The grammar is **distinct from every Navigation primitive in §9.3 / Bottom Nav / Tabs / Pagination**:

| Component | Grammar | Primary affordance | Visible state |
|---|---|---|---|
| Nav vertical / horizontal | "Browse between siblings" | Switch the active leaf | Per-item rounded backplate carries the row identity |
| Bottom Nav | "Switch root routes" | Pick a top-level destination | Bar position + per-item backplate active state (matches Nav vertical/horizontal) |
| Tabs | "Switch content panels under one heading" | Reveal a different panel | Underline on the active trigger |
| Pagination | "Step through a numbered sequence" | Move to page N | Filled active page button |
| **Breadcrumb** | **"Show where I am in a hierarchy + offer ancestor links"** | **Step up to an ancestor (or open the current segment's siblings)** | **Inline text path with chevron separators; no per-item backplate** |

This list is exhaustive — adding any new "horizontal nav" component in the future MUST first prove it's distinct from these five grammars. There is no sixth slot.

**Cross-system consensus** — every modern reference converges on the same anatomy:

| System | Item color | Hover behavior | Separator | Current marker | Truncation |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Primer (GitHub) | `text/secondary` → `text/primary` on hover | Color shift only | "/" or chevron | Bold last item | n/a (paths short) |
| shadcn / Radix | `muted-foreground` → `foreground` | Color shift + underline | Chevron-right | `BreadcrumbPage` non-link | `BreadcrumbEllipsis` Dropdown |
| Atlassian | `subtle` → `default` | Color shift + underline | Chevron-right | Bold last item | Middle ellipsis with menu |
| Linear | Subdued → primary | Color shift | Chevron-right | Plain primary | Drop oldest, show last 3 |
| Vercel Geist | Muted → fg | Color shift | "/" slash | Plain primary | Truncate with `…` |
| Apple HIG (Finder path bar) | Secondary → primary | Subtle bg lift | Chevron-right | Plain primary | Auto-elide |
| Material 3 | (no formal Breadcrumb component; uses Top App Bar with hierarchy) | — | — | — | — |
| **Ours** | **`text/secondary` → `text/primary`** | **Color shift, no underline (color-only is enough at our weight 400 mono identity)** | **Chevron-right at `text/tertiary`** | **`text/primary`, non-interactive** | **`…` ellipsis Dropdown for hidden ancestors** |

Underline-on-hover is the modal industry choice; we omit it because (a) DM Mono already reads "deliberate" at weight 400, so the color shift carries the affordance without a second decoration, and (b) underlines on monospace text read as visually noisier than they do on proportional fonts. Linear and Apple HIG path bar make the same call.

**Sizes** — two tiers, both on the body type ramp. No control-tier ramp (breadcrumb is text-flow chrome, not a sized control):

| Tier | Font / line-height | Icon | Outer gap (between item and separator) | Inner gap (icon ↔ label inside one item) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| S | 12/16 (`body/s`) | `icon-xs` (12) | `space-2xs` (4) | `space-2xs` (4) |
| **M** (default) | **14/20 (`body/m`)** | **`icon-s` (16)** | **`space-xs` (6)** | **`space-2xs` (4)** |

No L/XL because hero-tier breadcrumbs aren't an idiom in any modern system. If a future marketing surface needs one, that's a new requirement to litigate, not a default to ship blind.

**Color logic — link items (ancestors):**
- Rest: `text/secondary` (subdued — these are not the focal text)
- Hover: `text/primary` (the §4.3.1 inline-link sanctioned exception applied at component scope; breadcrumb items ARE the canonical inline-navigation-link case the link-token rule exists for. Foreground stability is preserved everywhere else — no opacity, no filter on hover, just a single tonal step that mirrors `--color-text-link → --color-text-link-hover` at the neutral foreground tier)
- Pressed: `filter: brightness(.92)` (the only sanctioned brightness use, per §4.4)
- Focus: 2-layer outset halo (`box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px border/focus, 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in oklch, border/focus 30%, transparent)`) — components without their own border per §4.2
- Disabled: `text/disabled`, `cursor: not-allowed`, `pointer-events: none`

**Color logic — current page (last segment):**
- `text/primary` always — neutral, **no brand color, no chip background, no border**
- Not interactive — no hover, no focus, no cursor
- Marked `aria-current="page"` per ARIA APG

**No strokes, no rest-state backplates — anywhere on a breadcrumb (non-negotiable).**

This is the load-bearing rule the breadcrumb grammar rests on. The component's identity is "inline text path with chevron separators" (§9.22 grammar table) — strokes and rest-state chips break that identity and pull the breadcrumb visually toward the Nav / Button / Chip families instead.

| Element | Rest chrome | Hover chrome | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Link item (ancestor) | None (just text) | `action/hover` bg overlay | Hover IS the affordance signal; nothing at rest |
| Current item (last segment) | None | None (not interactive) | The `text/primary` color + position-as-last carries the "you are here" meaning; a backplate is redundant signal |
| Dropdown trigger (current segment as sibling-picker) | None | `action/hover` bg overlay | Same as link items — the trailing chevron-down IS the menu affordance; chrome would re-introduce the "is this a chip?" question and conflict with the breadcrumb identity |
| Ellipsis trigger | None | `action/hover` bg overlay | Same as link items — the `…` IS the collapsed-segments signal |
| Separator chevron | None | n/a (decorative, non-interactive) | Pure decoration between items |

**Banned configurations** (drift the implementation has had before, sanctioned for removal):
- ❌ Current segment with `action/soft/brand/{bg, text}` chip (brand-colored "you are here") — every modern system uses neutral; brand makes the segment compete with brand-colored buttons elsewhere on the page
- ❌ Dropdown trigger with `action/soft/neutral/{bg, border}` chip (visible filled chip at rest) — pulls the trigger visually away from the breadcrumb identity toward Button-soft-neutral, blurring the §9.22 grammar
- ❌ Any 1px `border` on any breadcrumb segment, including the dropdown trigger and ellipsis trigger
- ❌ Any `border-bottom` on the breadcrumb container

The implementation enforces this by setting `background: transparent; border: 0` on every breadcrumb element at rest, with `action/hover` bg overlay applied only on `:hover` for interactive segments (link / dropdown / ellipsis). The `border-radius: var(--bc-rd)` is harmless when background is transparent, but exists so the hover overlay rounds cleanly.

**Industry alignment**: Primer / shadcn / Atlassian / Linear / Vercel Geist / Apple HIG path bar / Material 3 — every modern system runs breadcrumbs without rest-state backplates or strokes. The "no per-item backplate" stance is genuinely universal at the breadcrumb level (unlike Banner ↔ Toast which has more mixed industry positions). Sentry / Bootstrap-era breadcrumbs sometimes added chips around the current page, but that's an older pattern that no modern system maintains.

**Separator chevron:**
- `ICON_CHEVRIGHT` (Phosphor `caret-right`, Regular weight)
- Color: `text/tertiary` — decorative chrome between items, not an icon *of* an item. Subduing the chevron lets the label rhythm carry the read; promoting it to `text/secondary` (matching the labels) would make every chevron compete for visual attention with every label.
- Wrapper sized via the universal `--bc-icon × --bc-lh` letterbox per §6.1.3 (icon centers in one text-line of vertical space and aligns with adjacent labels in the flex row)

**Leading icon (per item, optional):**
- Wrapper inherits item color (Standing Rule #4 Rule A — icon = label color)
- For link items: tracks `text/secondary → text/primary` with the label
- For current item: `text/primary` (matches the label)

**Variants:**

1. **Default** — text-only path with chevron separators. The canonical form.
2. **Icons** — leading icon per segment (matches reference image row 2). Useful when category cues (folder / shopping / settings) help users distinguish levels at a glance.
3. **Icon-only with tooltip** — first segment is a glyph link with the label carried in a Tooltip on hover/focus (matches reference image row 1). Composes with §9.5 Tooltip; the link gets `aria-label="<full name>"` so screen readers announce the segment regardless of whether the tooltip is open.
4. **Dropdown** — last/current segment is a Dropdown trigger (`aria-haspopup="menu"`) opening a sibling-page picker. Composes with §9.7 Dropdown. The trigger uses the **same strokeless / chiplessrest chrome as link items** (transparent bg, no border, no rest backplate); the trailing chevron-down icon IS the menu-affordance signal. On hover the trigger gets `action/hover` bg overlay, identical to link items. Color is `text/primary` (matching the current-segment color since the trigger IS the current page). The trigger carries `aria-current="page"` because it IS the current page; the dropdown lets the user *change* what "current" means.
5. **Truncated** — middle segments collapse to an `…` ellipsis trigger that opens a Dropdown listing the hidden ancestors (industry-standard pattern from shadcn / Radix / Atlassian). Use when the path exceeds ~5 segments or when the available width can't fit the full path. Always preserve the FIRST segment (root) and the LAST 1–2 segments (parent + current); collapse only the middle.

**a11y wiring (per ARIA APG breadcrumb pattern):**
- Container: `<nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">` so it appears in landmark navigation
- List: `<ol>` (ordered — the path has direction, root → current)
- Items: `<li>` with either `<a href="...">` (link) or `<span aria-current="page">` (current)
- Separators: `<li aria-hidden="true">` (decorative — the visual chevron carries no semantic meaning beyond what the order already conveys)
- Icon-only segment: link element gets `aria-label="<full name>"`; the inner icon wrapper is `aria-hidden="true"`
- Dropdown segment: button gets `aria-haspopup="menu"` + `aria-expanded="true|false"` + `aria-controls="<panel-id>"`; the panel itself gets `role="menu"` per §9.7
- Disabled link: `aria-disabled="true"` (anchors don't accept the `disabled` attribute)

**Keyboard contract:**
- Tab moves focus through interactive segments (link or dropdown trigger or ellipsis trigger) in order
- Enter activates a link (browsers handle this natively)
- Enter / Space / ↓ opens a dropdown (Dropdown / Ellipsis trigger)
- Esc closes an open dropdown and returns focus to the trigger
- Current item is NOT in the focus order (it's not a link)

**When NOT to use Breadcrumb:**
- Single-level apps without a hierarchy → use the page title alone
- Wizard flows or step indicators → use a Stepper component (not yet built) or numbered Tabs, not breadcrumbs (a wizard's "back" is "go to the previous step," not "step up the hierarchy")
- Filter/sort/view-mode pickers → use Segmented Control or Tabs

**Visual differentiation forward-looking note (extends §12.1):** Breadcrumb's "inline text path with chevrons" is the system's CANONICAL "you are here" idiom. If a future component proposes path-style chrome (e.g., a "step indicator" or "wizard progress"), it must justify visual divergence to avoid conflation with Breadcrumb. The likeliest future component is **Stepper** (numbered or labeled steps for multi-step forms) — that one differentiates by (a) carrying a state per step (complete / current / upcoming, often via filled/outlined dots), and (b) being non-link (the user can't typically jump arbitrary steps). Stepper visual: numbered dots + connector lines + per-step label. Breadcrumb visual: text labels + chevron separators + no dots. Two different idioms; do not unify.

### 9.23 Segmented Control — single-select value picker

Segmented Control picks one value from a small visible set (view mode, sort order, time range) rendered as a row of segments on a contained tonal track. Its **role is "pick a value"** — distinct from Tabs ("switch content panels," §9.11) and from Nav ("go to a destination"). The full state matrix, sizing ramp, and a11y wiring are documented in the Part 11 row; the cross-cutting rules that MUST hold:

- **Track surface = `surface/quiet`** (`neutral/50` light, `neutral/1000` dark — moved from `surface/subtle` in May 2026, see §3.1), with a 1px inset `border/subtle` definition stroke (Job C). The track is the load-bearing "these are peer options sharing one state" signal.
- **Active segment = filled raised pill** — `surface/default` fill + `var(--shadow-1)` lift + 1px inset `border/subtle` ring (the §4.5.1 selected-state pattern). This is the **filled-pill** half of the §12.1 underbar-vs-fill rule that differentiates Segmented Control from Tabs.
- **No brand color** anywhere in the component — the active state is carried entirely by the neutral raised pill, per the §3.1 brand-color discipline (brand reads as "the one chosen action," not "the current setting").
- **Foreground stays `text/primary` at every state** — unselected items are never muted (muting would imply disabled). Hover adds `action/hover` overlay on non-active segments; pressed is `brightness(.92)`; focus is `2px outline border/focus, outline-offset: -2px`.
- **Sizing** follows the §2.3 control ramp (XS–XL, default M=32); the outer track radius is `calc(item_radius + track_padding)` so concentric corners (§2.1.2) hold at every tier.
- **a11y: `role="radiogroup"`** on the track + `role="radio"` + `aria-checked` + roving `tabindex` per the ARIA APG radio pattern (NOT `tablist` — there's no content panel; it sets a value). Cardinality is single-select-always-one; see the §12.1 cardinality rule for Chip-vs-Segmented disambiguation.

### 9.24 Color Picker — popover-content color surface

Color Picker is a popover-content surface (saturation/lightness canvas + hue slider + opacity slider + a footer composing real system primitives) for choosing a color. Full anatomy lives in the Part 11 row; the cross-cutting rules:

- **Surface**: `surface/default` bg, 1px `border/subtle` Job C definition stroke, **`radius-l` (20)** matching the Dialog surface tier (§9.6), `space-m` (12) padding, `space-s` (8) inter-section gap, `var(--shadow-3)` anchored-overlay elevation.
- **Canvas**: 280×280 square, **`radius-xs` (8)** — derived per the §2.1.2 inverse rule (`outer 20 − inset 12 = 8`, soft-regime ideal hit exactly).
- **Sliders** (hue + opacity): full-width, `icon-s` (16) tall, fully rounded (`9999px`) — the shape-coded "track" idiom Switch uses (§3.5.1); a slider IS a track. Opacity slider carries a checkered transparency backdrop under the color→transparent gradient.
- **Handles are white, never brand** — a 2px `surface/default` ring keeps the thumb legible against any underlying color (a brand-tinted handle vanishes when the user picks brand). Canvas handle = ring style; slider handles = filled white with `--shadow-1`. **Handle positioning uses the `--pct` + `margin-left: calc(-1 * --pct * handle / 100)` constraint pattern**, NOT `translate(-50%)` — the latter lets the handle overhang the track ends by half its width (the canonical track-thumb positioning rule for any future Slider primitive).
- **Footer**: a standalone mode Select (Hex/RGB/HSL — a format preference) BESIDE an `InputGroup` (§9.27) holding hex value + alpha %. Per §9.27.1 the mode is SEPARATED (it reformats the same color, doesn't change it) and hex + alpha are JOINED (two aspects of one color value).
- **Composition**: most consumers wrap Color Picker in a Popover triggered by a Color Input variant (`Input + colorSwatch + mono hex`); the picker itself is decoupled from any trigger. Same relationship as Date Picker + Date Input.
- **Sanctioned hardcoded color**: the spectrum gradients (hue rainbow, SL black/white stops, opacity checker) are the one place raw color is unavoidable — they're demonstrating a color continuum, not consuming a token. Sanctioned in §1.4.5.

### 9.25 Date Picker — calendar grid

Date Picker is a calendar-grid panel for selecting a date (or range). Full anatomy in the Part 11 row; the cross-cutting rules:

- **Single canonical size** (M cell tier, 32×32) — matches Apple HIG / Linear / Atlassian / shadcn; the 7×6 grid + weekday header + nav header constrains the panel to a narrow width that doesn't benefit from a density ramp.
- **Panel**: `surface/default` bg, 1px `border/subtle` Job C definition stroke, `radius-s` (12), `space-m` (12) padding, `space-s` (8) inter-section gap — same anchored-overlay panel chrome as every other panel in the system.
- **Day cells are rounded rectangles at `radius-2s` (10)**, matching `--control-m` and Pagination cells — the selected day reads as a filled rounded rectangle, NOT a circle (per §3.5.1, pill/circle is reserved for shape-coded primitives). Circle cells (Apple HIG / M3 filled) are the outlier for systems with a rectangle-default identity, which is ours.
- **Selected day = `action/solid/brand/bg` fill** — this IS the system's sanctioned brand-for-selection case (a single chosen value, per the §3.1 brand-color discipline). **Today (unselected)** gets a low-emphasis 1px inset `border/focus` ring; today+selected drops the ring (brand fill carries it). Out-of-month days = `text/tertiary`.
- **Range variant**: start/end cells get full-brand fill with outer corners rounded + inner corners squared; mid-range cells get `action/soft/brand/bg` tint with squared corners — the "filled bar with rounded ends" range silhouette consistent across shadcn / Atlassian / Polaris.
- **Composition**: most consumers wrap Date Picker in a Popover triggered by an Input with `leadIcon: ICON_CALENDAR`; the primitive stays decoupled from any trigger.
- **a11y**: panel `role="application"` + `aria-label`; nav buttons get `aria-label="Previous month"` / `"Next month"`; selected day `aria-selected="true"`; out-of-month/disabled days `tabindex="-1"`. Keyboard: ←/→ days, ↑/↓ weeks, PgUp/PgDn months, Home/End week ends, Enter to select.

### 9.26 Drop Zone — file-upload affordance

Drop Zone is the drag-and-drop file-upload affordance with a dashed-border drop area. Full anatomy in the Part 11 row; the cross-cutting rules:

- **Single canonical size** (`width: 100%` — always sized by its container, like a layout surface), `space-2xl` (32) vertical / `space-l` (16) horizontal padding, `radius-m` (16).
- **Dashed stroke = 2px width, 8/6 dash pattern** — the dashed pattern is the load-bearing "drop content here, not a normal surface" signal (Job A affordance stroke). The 2px weight gives the dotted line enough presence to read as deliberate (matching Polaris / Material 3 / Atlassian / shadcn-extension, which all run dotted drop-zone strokes at ~2px).
- **Icon**: 24×24 (`icon-l`) glyph at `text/tertiary` (default), escalating to `text/info` on drag-over (the same blue "active/pending" family as the focus halo) and `text/danger` on error — consistent with the Standing Rule #4 Rule B chrome-icon family and the Input-validation foreground-escalation pattern.
- **State escalation**: hover/drag-over = `border/focus` dashed stroke + `feedback/info/bg` tint + info icon; focus = `border/focus` + 2px brand halo (§4.2); error = `border/danger` dashed stroke + `feedback/danger/bg` tint + danger icon. **No separate "filled" state** — after upload the dashed stroke swaps to a solid `border/subtle` and the content left-aligns next to a file list (the dashed→solid swap IS the canonical "this slot is full" affordance across Polaris / shadcn / Atlassian; dashed = "drop here," solid = "received").
- **With-files list**: each file row is modeled after the single-line Toast — filename + size on one row, trailing `Button XS icon-only ghost-neutral` with `ICON_XMARK` to remove; inter-file gap matches the drop-zone's internal gap.
- **Composition**: the optional browse button (`actionLabel`) renders as a real `Button soft-neutral S` so the secondary chip styling matches Dialog footer / Toast inline-actions.
- **a11y**: default surface is a `<button>` (keyboard-activatable for click-to-browse) with `aria-label`; `aria-disabled="true"` when disabled. Filled state switches to `<div>` (no longer interactive). Drag-and-drop is wired by the consumer; the system provides the visual states only.
- **Sanctioned hardcoded color**: the dashed-stroke color in the SVG data-URL background-image resolves to a token via `currentColor` where feasible; any residual hex in the data-URL is sanctioned in §1.4.5.

### 9.27 InputGroup — joined-field composition primitive

InputGroup visually merges two or more inline controls (Input / Select / Button) into a single bordered container with internal 1px dividers and one shared focus halo. It is **a chrome primitive**, not a new field type — the inner controls retain all their own behavior, validation, ARIA wiring, and helpers; the InputGroup only changes how they *appear* relative to each other.

**The grammar it carries**: "these 2 controls share one composite value." Canonical 2-segment patterns across the industry:

| Pattern | Inner segments | First consumer in our system |
|---|---|---|
| **Color value + opacity** | Input(hex) + Input(alpha %) | **Color Picker footer (§9.24)** — May 2026 |
| Amount + currency | Input(value, mono) + Select(currency code) | future |
| Value + unit | Input(value) + Select(unit) | future |
| URL prefix + path | Input(prefix, disabled) + Input(path) | future (URL Input pattern) |
| Search field + filter scope | Select(scope) + Input(query) | future (advanced Combobox) |
| Phone country + number | Select(country flag) + Input(number) | future |

**Two-segment maximum (non-negotiable, May 2026 revision).** An InputGroup carries exactly **two** segments separated by exactly one `border/subtle` divider. If a use case needs a third control (a mode/format/scope selector that influences the pair), that third control lives as a **standalone sibling Select** in the same row, with a `space-s` (8) gap separating it from the InputGroup — see §9.27.1's "When to JOIN vs SEPARATE" rule. Reasons the cap is two:
- Beyond two segments, the joined-field grammar starts to feel cramped — the eye loses track of which segment owns which value, and dividers stack visually instead of reading as discrete separators.
- A third selector usually represents a *format/notation/scope preference* applied to the pair (Mode applied to Hex/Alpha, Format applied to Date start/end), not a third value of the composite. Separating it makes that role explicit.
- Two segments is the unanimous industry pattern: Material 3 joined inputs, Vercel Geist Input.Group, shadcn InputGroup, Atlassian InlineGroup, Apple HIG joined fields, Figma color picker hex+alpha row — all cap at two.

**Cross-system equivalents** (this pattern is universal):

| System | Name | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| shadcn | `InputGroup` | Wrapper class + child class overrides |
| Atlassian Atlaskit | `InlineGroup` / `Connected fields` | Wrapper with shared border |
| Polaris (Shopify) | `Connected` (deprecated) → `ButtonGroup` for buttons, `TextField.Connected` for fields | Composed connected items |
| Vercel Geist | `Input.Group` | Wrapper with shed inner chrome |
| Figma color picker footer | (internal composition) | Same single-bordered row pattern |
| Adobe Color HSL row | (internal composition) | Same |
| Sketch / Affinity color editors | (internal composition) | Same |

Promoting this pattern to a system primitive in May 2026 is the right move *now* because we have one production consumer (Color Picker footer) and at least two more on the near-term roadmap (currency input, value+unit input). The previous "scoped override inside `.ds-color-picker__footer`" approach was correct as the FIRST instance (anti-bloat per §2.2.1 — don't promote until a second use case is visible), but the moment the second pattern becomes plausible the override pattern is drift. Promotion deletes the scoped CSS entirely; Color Picker becomes the first consumer of a real primitive, not a special case.

**Sizes** — matches the Input ramp (XS / S / M / L / XL):

| Tier | Outer height | Outer radius | Inner control height | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| XS | `control-xs` (24) | `radius-xs` (8) | 100% of group (24) | Ultra-dense (table filter rows) |
| S | `control-s` (28) | `radius-xs` (8) | 100% of group (28) | Dense (toolbar joined fields) |
| **M** (default) | **`control-m` (32)** | **`radius-2s` (10)** | **100% of group (32)** | **Default — matches Input M** |
| L | `control-l` (40) | `radius-s` (12) | 100% of group (40) | Standard form rows |
| XL | `control-xl` (48) | `radius-2m` (14) | 100% of group (48) | Hero-tier joined fields |

Outer radius lands on the same height-tier mapping the Input itself uses (§2.1.1). The group's OUTER height equals `--control-{size}` (group has `box-sizing: border-box`, so the 1px border is INCLUDED in that height). Inner controls override their own `height: var(--inp-h)` to `height: 100%`, filling the group's content area (30px at M tier — outer 32 minus 1px border each side). This is the only place where inner Input/Select height tokens are overridden; everywhere else the controls own their height.

**Chrome — single border, single halo, internal dividers**:

```css
.ds-input-group {
  display: inline-flex; align-items: stretch; width: 100%;
  height: var(--ig-h);
  background: var(--color-surface-default);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
  border-radius: var(--ig-rd);
  overflow: hidden;
}
.ds-input-group:focus-within {
  border-color: var(--color-border-focus);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-border-focus) 30%, transparent);
}
.ds-input-group > * + * { border-left: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle); }
```

Inner controls shed their own chrome by **direct property override with `!important`** on the rendered element, applied across ALL state selectors:
```css
.ds-input-group > .ds-input,
.ds-input-group > .ds-input:hover,
.ds-input-group > .ds-input:focus-within,
.ds-input-group > .ds-input.is-focused,
.ds-input-group > .ds-input.is-error,
.ds-input-group > .ds-input.is-success,
.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select,
.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select:hover,
.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select:focus-visible,
.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select.is-focused,
.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select.is-open,
.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select.is-error,
.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select.is-success {
  border-color: transparent !important;
  border-radius: 0 !important;
  background: transparent !important;
  box-shadow: none !important;
}
```

**Why `!important` is the right tool here, and why two simpler patterns failed.** This is the non-obvious lesson from the May 2026 audit, recorded so the next person doesn't relitigate it:

1. **Token-cascade override** (set `--sel-rd: 0` on `.ds-select-wrap`, let it cascade to `.ds-select`) — fails because `.ds-select--{size}` redeclares `--sel-rd` directly on the inner `.ds-select` element. **When a CSS custom property is redeclared on a descendant element, the element's own declaration wins over any inherited value, regardless of parent selector specificity.** Inheritance only fires when the descendant doesn't declare the property itself. So `--sel-rd: 0` set on the wrap is silently ignored by the inner button.

2. **Direct property override without `!important`** (`.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select { border-color: transparent }`) — fails on hover because the Select's hover selector is `.ds-select:hover:not(:disabled):not(.is-disabled):not(.is-focused):not(.is-error):not(.is-success):not(.is-open)` with specificity **0,6,2** (one base class + five `:not(.class)` + one `:hover` + one `:not(:disabled)`). The 3-class descendant override `.ds-input-group > .ds-select-wrap > .ds-select:hover` is only **0,3,1**. The Select's hover border-color wins, and the user sees a "rounded stroke on hover" leaking through the group's chrome.

3. **`!important` on the chrome-shed properties across all state selectors** — works because it's the right semantic tool. InputGroup is BY DEFINITION the authoritative chrome owner for its inner controls; the inner controls' own chrome is suppressed by design. `!important` expresses "InputGroup authoritatively governs this child's chrome." This matches how shadcn InputGroup, Vercel Geist Input.Group, and Material 3 joined-field CSS handle the same specificity problem. Spec-wise the "no `!important` to bypass `prefers-reduced-motion`" rule from §4.3 doesn't apply — that's a different cardinal-rule violation. Here, `!important` is enforcing the design intent, not bypassing user-agent preferences.

Two alternative patterns were considered and rejected: (a) competing-specificity chains (`.ds-select:not(.a):not(.b):not(.c):not(.d):not(.e)...` to outrun 0,6,2) — readable-hostile and brittle; (b) a render-time marker class (`.ds-input-group__child` injected by the helper) — works but adds a private-API contract between InputGroup and Input/Select, expanding the system's coupling surface for a one-rule problem.

The `box-sizing: border-box` discipline is essential here: the inner Input's 1px transparent border is still in the box model, so the inner Input's content area is 1px smaller than the group's content area on each side (group 30 content → input 28 content). This 1px is invisible (transparent border) but preserves the height math so swapping in a non-grouped Input with a visible border drops in without layout shift.

**Divider — pseudo-element, always present, tonal escalation only for persistent semantic states** (May 2026 revision). The 1px divider between adjacent segments is rendered as a `::before` pseudo-element on every non-first child (`.ds-input-group > *:not(:first-child)::before`) rather than `border-left` on the child. Three non-negotiable rules:

1. **The divider is ALWAYS present, regardless of layout.** No `--no-divider` modifier exists. Industry consensus is universal here (shadcn InputGroup, Vercel Geist Input.Group, Material 3 joined input, Atlassian InlineGroup, Apple HIG joined fields, Figma color picker footer all use subtle 1px dividers). Without dividers, the group reads as one input with affordances floating inside it (e.g., a leading swatch and a trailing `%` suffix) — the user can't see that the mode `Hex ⌄` and the value `D9D9D9` are independently editable. The divider IS the "compound field" affordance.

2. **Divider color stays subtle for transient states, escalates for persistent semantic states.** Default / hover / focus: divider = `border/subtle` (150). The outer border carries the transient state escalation (rest → hover → focus + halo); the divider stays put because escalating on every hover creates a flickering hierarchy-changing read where the segment count feels like it changes on interaction. Error / success / disabled (persistent states): divider escalates to the tone-border stop matching the outer border (`feedback/danger/border` for error, `feedback/success/border` for success, `border/disabled` for disabled) so the divider stays tonally cohesive with the outer band instead of reading as a grey line cutting through a tinted field. Without this cohesion, an error InputGroup looks like "tinted field with a misplaced grey hairline" rather than "one decisively errored field"; that's a real bug, not a stable invariant.

3. **Pseudo-element, not `border-left`, because of the chrome-shed conflict.** The chrome-shed rule (above) sets `border-color: transparent !important` on every inner `.ds-input` / `.ds-select` to suppress their own borders inside the group. Setting `border-left: 1px solid var(--ig-divider)` on the child gets overridden by the `!important` rule when the child is `.ds-input`, killing the divider between two adjacent `.ds-input`s (the Color Picker hex + opacity case). The previous Input + Select demos worked only by accident — `border-left` on `.ds-select-wrap` wasn't targeted by chrome-shed, so the divider survived there but disappeared between two `.ds-input`s. The pseudo-element bypasses descendant selectors targeting the element type, so `::before` renders consistently regardless of which two element types sit adjacent. Industry alignment: shadcn / Vercel Geist / M3 all use pseudo-element or sibling-rule dividers (not child `border-left`) for the same specificity-stability reason.

**No "borderless Select" or "borderless Input" sibling variants needed.** The system intentionally does NOT ship `.ds-select--borderless` or `.ds-input--borderless` modifiers, even though that would also solve the chrome-shed problem. Reason: a borderless variant would have exactly one consumer (InputGroup), violating the anti-bloat rule in §2.2.1 ("do not add a semantic token / class that only one component uses unless that component family will plausibly grow consumers"). The InputGroup chrome shed lives in InputGroup CSS, not in the Input/Select API. This keeps Input and Select shapes locked at their canonical chrome and pushes the "joined" exception into one place.

**One canonical chrome — no `filled` visual variant (May 2026 revision).** The system ships **one** Input/InputGroup chrome: outlined (1px `border/default` rest, escalates to `border/hover` → `border/focus` + 2-layer halo). There is no `--filled` modifier on Input, Select, Textarea, or InputGroup. An earlier May 2026 rev briefly shipped `.ds-input-group--filled` + `.ds-input--filled` (tonal `surface/subtle` fill + transparent border) to match a Color Picker compound-editor pattern; reverted after the user audit. Reasons one chrome is the right call here:

1. **Anti-bloat (§2.2.1)** — the filled variant had exactly one consumer (the briefly-joined Color Picker footer). Once Color Picker reverted to the separated pattern (mode Select + 2-segment InputGroup with default outlined chrome), the filled variant had **zero consumers**. Shipping a variant with no consumer fragments the system's vocabulary for no production benefit.
2. **Industry is split**, not unanimous, on shipping both. Material 3 ships filled as its canonical default and outlined as opt-in; Apple HIG ships filled-ish "tinted" inputs in macOS Sonoma+ settings panels; **shadcn, Vercel Geist, Linear, Atlassian, Notion, Stripe Dashboard all ship one outlined chrome only**. The single-chrome systems compose just as cleanly inside contained surfaces (Card body, popover, inspector) by leaning on the surface's own background contrast — they don't need a second visual chrome to compete with the parent. Our system follows the single-chrome-outlined camp.
3. **Two-chrome systems introduce a parallel decision tree** consumers have to walk every time they place an input ("am I in a tonal context? do I need filled? does the InputGroup match my standalone inputs?"). A single canonical chrome removes that decision entirely — every Input/Select/InputGroup looks the same regardless of context.
4. **The contained-surface concern is solvable without a chrome variant.** When an InputGroup sits inside a popover, the popover's own `surface/raised` + `border/subtle` carries the elevation; the outlined input below it doesn't double-paint because the input's border is *inside* the popover's container, not a peer. Same logic for Cards and inspector panels.

If a real consumer ever requires a tonal-filled input chrome (e.g., a future settings-app product that needs Material 3 fidelity), revisit and ship `--filled` then with proper consumer documentation. Until then, the system has one chrome.

#### 9.27.1 When to JOIN (InputGroup) vs SEPARATE (standalone field + standalone control)

A user-facing decision rule, because the difference is semantic, not aesthetic. The test is whether the two controls **share one composite value** that the user perceives as a single entity, or carry **two independent settings**.

**The two-segment-max rule (restated from §9.27 for emphasis).** InputGroup holds exactly **two** segments. If your use case has three controls in a row, **at least one of them is a format/scope preference that should sit standalone beside a 2-segment InputGroup**, not a third segment inside the joined surface.

| Pattern | Visual | When | Examples |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Joined** (2-segment InputGroup) | One bordered field with exactly one internal 1px `border/subtle` divider between exactly two segments — they share one outer chrome and one focus halo | The two segments represent **one logical value broken into independently-editable parts** — what the user is editing IS the composition of those parts, and the value is meaningless without both. | • **Color value + opacity**: `D9D9D9` + `100 %` (color notation + transparency channel — two aspects of one color value)<br>• **Amount + currency**: `100.00` + `USD` (the amount is meaningless without the denomination — `100.00` alone doesn't tell you it's dollars)<br>• **Dimension + unit**: `24` + `px` (the number is meaningless without the unit)<br>• **Phone country + number**: `+1` + `415-555-0100` (country code + number = one phone)<br>• **URL prefix + path**: `https://` + `domain.com/path` (protocol + host = one URL)<br>• **Date range start + end** (when the joined chrome reads "one date range") |
| **Separated** (standalone control + gap + standalone control or InputGroup) | Independent controls in a row, each with its own chrome and halo, separated by `space-s` / `space-m` gap | One control is a **format/notation/scope preference** that changes HOW the other(s) are interpreted but doesn't change WHAT they hold. OR the controls operate on **distinct conceptual axes** entirely. OR a third selector accompanies a 2-segment InputGroup. | • **Color mode (Hex / RGB / HSL) beside hex+alpha InputGroup** — Mode reformats the same color into different notations; the underlying color is unchanged. Mode is a notation preference, not a third aspect of the color value. (Figma, Adobe Color, Sketch all use the separated pattern here.)<br>• "Sort by" dropdown beside a "Search" input — independent affordances<br>• "Date format" dropdown above date inputs — applies to multiple downstream fields<br>• "Unit system" toggle at the top of a settings panel — page-level scope<br>• Two unrelated form fields ("First name" + "Last name") — distinct fields, not a composite value |

**The acceptance test** — for any pair of controls, ask: "Does changing one of them change WHAT the other holds (its value), or HOW the other is interpreted (its notation/format/scope)?"
- Changes **WHAT** → the controls share a composite value → JOIN them in a 2-segment InputGroup.
- Changes **HOW** → the controls are independent axes → SEPARATE them with a `space-s` gap.

**The Color Picker case, worked through.** The footer's `[Hex ⌄] [D9D9D9 │ 100 %]` is a **separated + joined** composition:

1. **Mode is separated** as a standalone Select because changing Hex → RGB → HSL doesn't change WHICH color is being edited — it changes HOW the same color is notated. `D9D9D9` (hex), `rgb(217, 217, 217)`, and `hsl(0, 0%, 85%)` are three notations of the same underlying color. That's the definition of a format/view preference, which lands in the separated bucket.
2. **Hex and opacity are joined** inside a 2-segment InputGroup because they ARE independently editable aspects of the SAME color value — hex carries the hue/saturation/lightness, opacity carries the transparency channel. Neither is complete without the other; changing one doesn't change the other's *meaning*, but together they define the color's full identity. That's the definition of a composite value, which lands in the joined bucket.
3. The chrome is the system's canonical outlined chrome — no `filled` variant. The picker's popover surface (`surface/raised`) provides the contained-context elevation; the outlined input below it doesn't compete because the popover surface and the input border are at different scales of the chrome hierarchy.

**Industry alignment for the separated mode pattern**: Figma renders the mode dropdown in its own row above or beside the color value field. Adobe Color uses a separate Mode menu beside the value. Sketch puts the mode Select beside the field. VS Code's color picker uses the same separated-mode pattern.

**Earlier rev got this wrong (recorded so the next person doesn't relitigate).** A previous May 2026 rev attempted a single joined filled InputGroup containing `[Mode │ Hex │ Alpha]` on the rationale that "even though Mode is a notation, the user perceives the whole row as 'the color editor.'" The framing was too generous — "the user perceives it as one editor" can be argued for almost any row of related controls, but the actual decision pressure is whether the chrome should imply "these aspects together define the value" (joined) or "this preference reshapes how the value is displayed" (separated). Mode is unambiguously the latter. The reverted rule is documented in this section; the failed rev is recorded in §B.25 / §B.26 of the history file.

**Failure mode to watch for**: joining a format/scope preference as a third segment. If you find yourself reaching for a 3-segment InputGroup, ask whether one of the three is actually a notation preference (changes HOW, not WHAT). If yes, separate it and use a 2-segment InputGroup for the value pair.

**Failure mode in reverse**: separating two segments that share a composite value, on the rationale that "they look heavy joined." If the field is meaningless without its partner (an unlabeled `100.00` doesn't tell you it's dollars; a bare hex `D9D9D9` doesn't tell you the opacity), they share a value and should be joined inside a 2-segment InputGroup.

**States** — the group owns the visible state chrome; inner controls' own state colors are suppressed:

| State | Treatment |
|---|---|
| Default | `border/default` border |
| Hover | `border/hover` border (only when not focused/error/success/disabled) |
| Focus-within (any inner control focused) | `border/focus` border + 2px translucent halo |
| Error (`is-error`) | `feedback/danger/border` + `feedback/danger/bg` fill; halo swaps to danger |
| Success (`is-success`) | `feedback/success/border` + `feedback/success/bg`; halo swaps to success |
| Disabled (`is-disabled`) | `surface/sunken` bg + `border/disabled` border + 0.6 opacity on inner controls + `pointer-events: none` |

Inner controls keep their own `disabled` attributes and `is-error` classes for ARIA correctness, but their visible chrome (border / halo) is suppressed — the group's halo is what the user sees.

**Segment widths** — InputGroup doesn't impose widths; consumers pass `flex` / `width` / `minWidth` to inner segments via the `flex`/`width`/`minWidth` props on `inputEl` / `selectEl`. Typical patterns:

| Segment role | Sizing |
|---|---|
| Mode / unit / currency Select (hugs content) | `width: 64px` (or 56–72 depending on label length) |
| Primary value Input (visual subject) | `flex: "1 1 0"; minWidth: "0"` (grows to fill remaining track; min-width 0 prevents text overflow from blocking shrink) |
| Secondary numeric Input (suffix-bearing) | `width: 56px` (3-digit + `%` suffix fits comfortably) |

**Composition example — Color Picker footer (canonical SEPARATED + JOINED pattern)**:
```js
// Standalone mode Select (format preference) BESIDE 2-segment InputGroup (hex value + opacity)
'<div class="ds-color-picker__footer">' +
  selectEl({ size: "m", value: mode, width: "64px" }) +
  inputGroupEl({
    size: "m",
    children: [
      inputEl({ size: "m", value: hexDisplay, mono: true, flex: "1 1 0", minWidth: "0" }),
      inputEl({ size: "m", value: String(opacity), suffix: "%", mono: true, width: "64px" })
    ]
  }) +
'</div>'
```

The wrapper `.ds-color-picker__footer` is `display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-s, 8px)` — the standard "default cluster" gap from §6.5. The Select hugs its content (`flex: 0 0 auto`); the InputGroup flex-grows to fill the rest of the track. The visual result is two independently-bordered controls with a clean 8px gap between them, NOT one wide joined field. The InputGroup itself carries the inner 1px divider between hex and alpha because they share one logical color value (hue/saturation/lightness + opacity).

**Rev history (recorded so the next person doesn't relitigate)**:
- Rev 1 (early) — One joined outlined InputGroup containing Mode + Hex + Alpha. Read as "one very wide field" rather than "one editor row" because the chrome implied all three segments defined the value when Mode was actually a notation preference.
- Rev 2 — Separated pattern: standalone Mode Select beside an InputGroup containing Hex + Alpha (default outlined chrome). Resolved Rev 1's mis-modeling and got the JOIN vs SEPARATE semantics right.
- Rev 3 (briefly) — One joined **filled** InputGroup containing Mode + Hex + Alpha. Attempted to keep the joined-editor read while solving Rev 1's "one wide field" problem via tonal filled chrome. Introduced a `--filled` variant that had only this one consumer. Reverted because (a) it still mis-modeled Mode as a value-segment when it's actually a format preference (failed the §9.27.1 acceptance test "does changing this segment change WHAT or HOW?"), and (b) shipping a `--filled` variant with one consumer violated §2.2.1 anti-bloat.
- **Rev 4 (current)** — Standalone Mode Select beside a 2-segment InputGroup containing Hex + Opacity (default outlined chrome). Matches Rev 2's correct semantics, plus the 2-segment-max rule from §9.27 caps the InputGroup at exactly two segments. The `--filled` variant was removed in lockstep; the system ships one canonical outlined chrome for all Input and InputGroup uses.

**a11y wiring** — InputGroup itself has no role; it's a chrome wrapper. The inner controls retain their own ARIA (`aria-invalid`, `aria-describedby`, `aria-haspopup`, etc.). When the whole group represents a single logical field (e.g., "Color value" = mode + hex + alpha), wrap the group in a `<div class="ds-field">` with a single `<label>` describing all three segments; the inner controls get `aria-label`s (or visually-hidden `<label>`s) describing their specific role within the group ("Color format", "Hex value", "Opacity percentage").

**Keyboard contract** — inner controls retain their own keyboard contracts (Input is native, Select opens on Space/Enter/↓ per §9.3). Tab moves focus between segments in DOM order. There is no group-level keyboard contract — each segment is independently focusable, same as any horizontally-laid form row.

**When NOT to use InputGroup**:
- **Two separate inputs with breathing room between them** (e.g., a date range with start/end) — use two standalone Inputs with a gap. InputGroup implies "these are one unit"; a date range is two units.
- **A label that prefixes an input** (e.g., "Email:" then the field) — use the field's own `prefix` prop, or the `Field` wrapper's `<label>` — not InputGroup.
- **Inline buttons that act on field content** (e.g., search Input with a "Search" button on the right) — this is the Combobox / search-field pattern; consider whether the button should be inside the Input as a trailing action affordance (§Input variants) instead of a separate joined segment.
- **More than 3–4 segments** — the joined-field grammar starts to feel cramped beyond 4 segments. Break into a row of standalone fields with explicit `space-s` gap.

**Standing rules that apply**:
- All semantic tokens (no raw hex anywhere)
- All numeric values from the spacing / radius / control ramps
- Stroke is Job A (Affordance) per §3.5 — group's border escalates rest → hover → focus + halo, just like any field control
- Inner-vs-outer-radius is irrelevant (inner controls have `border-radius: 0`, so the rounded corners are entirely owned by the outer group — §2.1.2 doesn't fire because there's no nested rounded backplate)
- Disabled fill is `surface/sunken` per §4.5 (the group has a fillable interior — the fill IS the disabled signal)

### 9.28 Composer — AI-chat-style message input

Composer is the **contained "form for typing a message"** primitive — the canonical "AI chat input" surface (Claude / ChatGPT / Cursor / Gemini). It is a **layout-and-density primitive**: it sits in the same family as Card / Dialog / Toast / Banner in that its identity is the form-pattern (not a tier on the §2.3 control ramp), but it exposes **three sizes** (S, M default, L) because the same input pattern serves three product contexts — ultra-dense product chrome where the composer is one quiet element in a busy surface (Cursor-sidebar tier, S), dense product UIs where the composer is the focal control of a panel (Cursor-compact tier, M default), and homepage / hero contexts where the composer IS the focal interactive element of the entire page (Claude-homepage tier, L). The Composer surface owns all chrome — border, background, focus halo, error tint, disabled fill — while its inner controls (Textarea / Input / Button / Select) shed their own chrome via descendant CSS using the §9.27 InputGroup `!important` pattern.

**Two layouts** chosen by content density, not user purpose (same rule as Toast inline vs footer §9.8):

| Layout | When | Anatomy |
|---|---|---|
| **`--double` (default)** | Multi-line messages with leading + trailing affordances + optional model picker. The canonical Claude / Cursor pattern. | Textarea fills the top row at **`min-height: 1 × body/m line-height (20)`** (single-row at rest per user directive — "single row at all times, expand to 2 or 3 rows if necessary") / `max-height: --cmp-text-max-rows × body/m line-height` (default 6 rows headroom, exposed as a token so consumers can tighten to 3 or 4 rows). On Chrome 133+ / Safari 18.2+ the textarea auto-grows via `field-sizing: content`; older browsers fall back to the `min-height` floor and scroll past `max-height`. Below the textarea, a control row with leading cluster on the left + trailing cluster (+ optional Select + canonical send Button) on the right. |
| **`--single`** | Single-line input where the message and the send button live in one row. The ChatGPT-mini compact pattern. | Leading cluster + single-line Input (`height: --cmp-control-h`) + trailing cluster (+ optional Select + send Button) all in one row. Container floor `--cmp-pad × 2 + --cmp-control-h` per Pattern 2 composition (§1.4.4) — S tier resolves to `4+4+28 = 36`, M tier to `6+6+32 = 44`, L tier to `8+8+40 = 56`. Off named control tier but a valid token formula. |

**Trailing area canonical order** (fixed — same as Dialog footer's "rightmost = primary"): trailing-cluster buttons → optional Select (model picker) → primary send Button. The send Button is always the rightmost element and defaults to `Button solid-brand {size} icon-only` with `ICON_CHEVUP` (where `{size}` matches the Composer's own size). The Select sits to the LEFT of the primary so the eye reads "context (model) → action (send)."

#### Three sizes — S (compact), M (default, dense), and L (spacious)

The size choice is **per-consumer per-use-case**, not per-product. A single product can host an S-sized Composer in an inspector / inline comment thread (compact, embedded chrome), an M-sized Composer in a sidebar / message thread (dense, default), AND an L-sized Composer on a hero / onboarding / empty-state page (focal, generous). The §2.3 control ramp is the lever; the inverse rule (§2.1.2) ties the rest of the surface chrome to whichever Button tier sits inside.

| Tier | Button + Select size | Outer pad (`--cmp-pad`) | `__field` pad V/H (both layouts) | Outer radius (`--cmp-rd`) | Inter-row gap (`--cmp-gap`) | Surface family | Cross-system parallel |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| S (compact) | Button S (28-tall, `radius-xs` 8) | `space-xs` (6) | **4 / 6** | `radius-s` (12) | `space-2xs` (4) | Anchored panels | Cursor sidebar / inspector composer, Linear inline comment composer |
| **M (default, dense)** | **Button M (32-tall, `radius-2s` 10)** | **`space-xs` (6)** | **6 / 8** | **`radius-m` (16)** | **`space-xs` (6)** | **Toast / Banner** | Cursor chat input, ChatGPT compose row, embedded chat widgets |
| L (spacious) | Button L (40-tall, `radius-2m` 14) | `space-s` (8) | **8 / 10** | `radius-l` (20) | `space-s` (8) | Dialog / Card | Claude homepage, ChatGPT homepage hero, Gemini hero |


**Concentric corners per tier** (§2.1.2):
- **S-tier**: outer `radius-s` (12), inset 6 (= ½ × 12, borderline strict). Inner = Button S `radius-xs` (8), spread = 4 — accepted per soft-regime tolerance (alternatives would either fragment the size family or hit non-ramp values). Outer matches anchored-panel family (Dropdown / Select / Tooltip — all `radius-s` 12); Composer in S reads as "quiet contained panel."
- **M-tier**: outer `radius-m` (16), inset 6, inner Button M `radius-2s` (10) — strict-regime ideal, hits exactly (16 − 6 = 10 ✓). Outer matches Toast / Banner surface family; Composer in M reads as "small floating contained surface."
- **L-tier**: outer `radius-l` (20), inset 8, inner Button L `radius-2m` (14) — spread = 6 (soft regime accepted; strict ideal 12 isn't on Button L's radius ramp). Spread matches Dialog precedent. Outer matches Dialog / Card surface family; Composer in L reads as "primary container surface, sized for hero presence."

**Why three sizes**: a single size can't serve all three product contexts. M dropped onto a Claude-homepage hero reads as undersized; L dropped into a Cursor sidebar reads as oversized; both feel wrong in a Linear-style inline-comment composer. Two sizes (M default + L) was an earlier rev; the S tier was added after seeing M-tier render too heavy inside inspector-style preview surfaces. **Button XS (24) considered as a hypothetical fourth size and dropped** — no canonical AI-chat-input product uses 24-tall buttons in its composer, and §2.2.1 anti-bloat kicks in without a documented consumer. Path stays open: when a real "table cell inline AI input" consumer ships, add XS with outer `radius-m` and inner Button XS (8 + 8 = 16, strict-exact).

#### Token derivations (§1.4.4 Pattern 1 + Pattern 2 only — no magic numbers)

Per-tier values flow through CSS custom properties on the `.ds-composer` root + `.ds-composer--s` and `.ds-composer--l` modifiers that override only the values that diverge from M (the default declared on the root selector):

| Token | S tier | M tier (default) | L tier | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `--cmp-rd` | `radius-s` (12) | `radius-m` (16) | `radius-l` (20) | §2.1.1 ramp |
| `--cmp-pad` | `space-xs` (6) | `space-xs` (6) | `space-s` (8) | §2.1 spacing ramp |
| `--cmp-control-h` | `control-s` (28) | `control-m` (32) | `control-l` (40) | §2.3 control ramp |
| `--cmp-gap` (inter-row, double layout) | `space-2xs` (4) | `space-xs` (6) | `space-s` (8) | §2.1 spacing ramp — scales with `--cmp-pad` (gap = pad at every tier so the inter-row gap reads as the same visual density as the outer chrome inset) |
| `--cmp-cluster-gap` (intra-cluster) | `space-2xs` (4) | `space-2xs` (4) | `space-2xs` (4) | §6.5 tight cluster tier — constant across tiers because tight-cluster gaps are density-tier-agnostic (the gap is a constant visual relationship between adjacent buttons, not a proportion of their height) |
| `--cmp-text-py` (V padding inside `__field` wrapper) | `space-2xs` (4) | `space-xs` (6) | `space-s` (8) | Scales with surface tier. Larger surfaces afford and benefit from more pronounced inward text breathing — at L, the user reads the input as the focal element of the page; at S, the input is a quiet inset chip. Applied uniformly across both `--single` and `--double` layouts (May 2026) — no longer layout-scoped. |
| `--cmp-text-px` (H padding inside `__field` wrapper) | `space-xs` (6) | `space-s` (8) | 10px (off-ramp; sanctioned per-component constant) | Case-B asymmetric breathing: H = V + 2 at every tier so visible text-to-surface H inset is 2px more than V at every tier (the line-box already provides ~3px of leading above the cap; the horizontal edge has no equivalent buffer per §6.1.1). The L-tier 10px is off the spacing ramp (ramp jumps `space-s` 8 → `space-m` 12); accepted because the alternative would force L into either 8H (uniform with V, breaking case-B) or 12H (4px asymmetry, exceeds the H = V + 2 rule shared with M/S). Documented as a per-component constant in §1.4.5. |
| `--cmp-fs / --cmp-lh` (textarea font) | 14/20 (`body/m`) | 14/20 (`body/m`) | 14/20 (`body/m`) | §5.1.1 type ramp — constant across tiers because Composer's body content is `body/m` regardless of surface size (matching Toast/Banner/Dialog body, all of which sit at 14/20) |
| `--cmp-text-min-rows` | 1 | 1 | 1 | §9.28 single-row default (user directive — "single row at all times, expand to 2 or 3 rows if necessary") |
| `--cmp-text-max-rows` | 6 | 6 | 6 | §9.28 grow ceiling (consumers may override per use case) |

**Composer height (canonical arithmetic)** — `outer-pad + field-row + outer-pad`:

Both `--single` and `--double` layouts add the `__field` wrapper's vertical padding to the field row, so the composer's outer height grows with the tier's `__field` pad. In `--single` mode the field row's content is the input/textarea at `--cmp-control-h`; in `--double` mode the field row's content is the textarea (1-line `min-height` at 20px) plus the action row (`--cmp-control-h`) plus `--cmp-gap` between them.

**Single-row height** = `outer-pad + (field-pad-V + control-h + field-pad-V) + outer-pad`:

| Tier | Outer pad × 2 | Field-pad-V × 2 | Control height | Total |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| S | 6 + 6 = 12 | 4 + 4 = 8 | 28 | **48** |
| **M (default)** | **6 + 6 = 12** | **6 + 6 = 12** | **32** | **56** |
| L | 8 + 8 = 16 | 8 + 8 = 16 | 40 | **72** |

The earlier rev's "single-row composer is exactly `2×pad + control-h`" arithmetic (S 40 / M 44 / L 56) is superseded — the May 2026 tiered-padding model adds explicit inward V breathing around the input at every tier. The trade-off: the M single-row composer grows from 44 → 56 (12px taller), but every tier now reads as a deliberately padded chip with text comfortably inset from the surface edges, matching the visual density of the surrounding ramp (Toast 40 floor + Banner-style inset chrome).

**Double-row height** = `outer-pad + (field-pad-V + textarea-min-h + field-pad-V) + cmp-gap + control-h + outer-pad`. The textarea grows past `min-height` as the user types; the headroom ceiling is `--cmp-text-max-rows × --cmp-lh` (default 120px). The double-row floor at M is `6 + 6 + 20 + 6 + 6 + 32 + 6 = 82` — meaningfully taller than single-row because the double-row identity is "this composer carries a multi-line message + a dedicated control row."

**Why padding is uniform across `--single` and `--double` layouts** (May 2026): an earlier rev applied 2V/4H only to `--double` via a layout-scoped override on the rationale that the single-row input visually fills the row (so the surface's `--cmp-pad` already provides the V breathing). User feedback rejected the layout split — the single-row composer's text glyph sat at the surface edges at `--cmp-pad` (6 at M) + ~3px leading = ~9px visible V, which read as "text flush against chrome" rather than "comfortably inset." The fix: apply the same tiered inward padding to both layouts. The single-row composer grows by `2 × field-pad-V` (12px at M), trading the canonical "44px height" identity for visual parity with the double-row composer's text breathing. Single and double now share the same `__field` padding rule and the same visible text-to-surface inset at every tier.

**Visible text breathing — outer chrome + `__field` inward padding, both layouts**:
- **Vertical** between text glyph and surface top/bottom = `--cmp-pad` + `--cmp-text-py` + line-box leading (~3px). At M: 6 + 6 + 3 = ~15px visible V from the surface edge to the cap-top. At S: 6 + 4 + 3 = ~13. At L: 8 + 8 + 3 = ~19.
- **Horizontal** between text glyph and surface L/R edges (where the text anchors an edge, e.g., double-row textarea) = `--cmp-pad` + `--cmp-text-px`. At M: 6 + 8 = 14. At S: 6 + 6 = 12. At L: 8 + 10 = 18. Case-B asymmetric: H is 2px more than V at every tier (the line-box already provides ~3px of leading above the cap; the horizontal edge has no equivalent buffer).
- **Horizontal** between text glyph and adjacent button clusters (single-row mode) = `--cmp-text-px` + `--cmp-cluster-gap` (4 at every tier per §6.5 tight-cluster rule). At M: 8 + 4 = 12px from the field's H pad to the cluster's outer edge — matches the visual rhythm modern chat composers (Claude/Cursor/ChatGPT) carry.

**Why `--cmp-pad` is symmetric on the surface** (§6.1.2 priority-rule pattern at row scope): hard-edged controls (button clusters) anchor the surface's corners and need **corner-symmetric inset** to the outer surface (`pad-x = pad-y`). Applying case-B asymmetric H padding to the surface itself would break corner-symmetry for the trailing primary button; applying V padding to the outer surface beyond the corner-symmetric value would push the buttons away from the corners and waste vertical surface real estate. Keeping `--cmp-pad` symmetric on the surface satisfies the corner rule for the buttons, and the input — which fills the row exactly — inherits the same V breathing the buttons get from `--cmp-pad`. Same pattern as Toast's leading-icon vs trailing-close per-edge override.

**Why `--cmp-gap` scales with `--cmp-pad` but `--cmp-cluster-gap` stays constant**: the inter-row gap (`--cmp-gap`, double layout only) is conceptually peer to the surface padding — both are "outer chrome to content" insets at the surface level — so scaling them together preserves the surface's overall density identity (a tighter surface has tighter inter-row gaps; a more generous surface has more generous inter-row gaps). The intra-cluster gap (`--cmp-cluster-gap`) is the gap between adjacent buttons inside a cluster, which §6.5 explicitly documents as "density-tier-agnostic for tight clusters" because button-to-button gaps don't scale linearly with button height — a 4px gap between two 28-tall buttons reads the same as a 4px gap between two 40-tall buttons. Industry alignment: Claude / ChatGPT / Cursor all run with ~4px internal button-cluster gaps regardless of composer scale.

**Textarea grow behavior** — `field-sizing: content` (Chrome 133+ / Safari 18.2+) makes the textarea auto-grow from `min-height = --cmp-text-min-rows × --cmp-lh` (default 1 × 20 = 20px) up to `max-height = --cmp-text-max-rows × --cmp-lh` (default 6 × 20 = 120px), past which the textarea scrolls. The `rows="1"` HTML attribute matches the `min-height` floor for browsers without `field-sizing` support. **Default rest state is 1 row** per user directive ("single row at all times, expand to 2 or 3 rows if necessary"); consumers wanting the older 2-row floor can override `--cmp-text-min-rows: 2`. The 6-row ceiling is a sensible default for ChatGPT-grade headroom; consumers wanting earlier scroll (e.g., tight inline chat) can override `--cmp-text-max-rows: 3` or `4`.

#### Chrome ownership and chrome-shed pattern

The Composer surface owns ALL the visible chrome — the Field-control family stroke is Job A (Affordance) per §3.5, so the border escalates rest → hover → focus + 2-layer halo per §4.2, plus error tint (`feedback/danger/bg` + `border/danger`) and disabled fill (`surface/sunken` + `border/disabled`). The inner Textarea / Input / Button / Select shed their own chrome:

| Inner control | What's shed | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Textarea / Input** | Border, padding, background, focus halo, resize handle (`resize: none`) | The text field is part of the outer surface — there's no inner-vs-outer-control distinction the user should see. `field-sizing: content` provides graceful auto-grow on Chrome 133+ / Safari 18.2+; older browsers fall back to the `min-height` floor + scroll past `max-height`. |
| **Button** | Focus halo only (`box-shadow: none !important; outline: none !important` on `:focus-visible`) | Button's own bg/border/icon carry its identity (solid-brand primary, ghost-neutral icon, soft-neutral chip). Only the focus halo is replaced because the Composer's halo IS the field's focus signal. |
| **Select (model picker)** | Full trigger chrome — bg / border / box-shadow across rest / hover / focus / open states | The Select reads as a transparent label+chevron peer to the primary button. Hover gets `action/hover` overlay so it still signals "interactive." The panel chrome (anchored overlay) is NOT shed — it lives outside the Composer surface per §9.3. |

`!important` is the right tool for the chrome-shed (same justification as §9.27 InputGroup): the parent component is the authoritative owner of its children's chrome, and the specificity arms-race against deep `:hover:not(.disabled):not(.is-focused):not(.is-error):not(.is-open)` selectors is hostile to readability.

**Foreground stability on hover (§4.3.1)**: inner Button hover does NOT change foreground color (icon stays `text/primary`/its tone). Hover on the Composer surface only changes border color. Hover on the Select shifts its bg overlay to `action/hover`. Same rule as everywhere else in the system.

**State matrix (§4.1 mandatory order)** — all on the outer `.ds-composer` surface:

| State | Treatment |
|---|---|
| Default | 1px `border/default` |
| Hover (excluded when focused / error / disabled) | 1px `border/hover` |
| Focus-within (any inner control focused) | 1px `border/focus` + 2px halo (`color-mix(border/focus 30%, transparent)`) per §4.2 |
| Error (`is-error`) | 1px `feedback/danger/border` + `feedback/danger/bg` tint; halo swaps to danger on focus |
| Disabled (`is-disabled`) | `surface/sunken` bg + `border/disabled` + 0.6 opacity on inner controls + `pointer-events: none` |
| Pressed | n/a — the Composer surface itself isn't pressed; inner Buttons handle pressed individually |

#### Rev history

The Composer went through 7 revs to arrive at the current model: XS-tier buttons → S → M default → text-padding ramp tuning (May 2026) → uniform 4px text-px → layout-scoped 2V/4H on `--double` only (May 17, 2026) → tiered V/H pad uniform across both layouts (current, May 17 evening). **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.3 and §B.22 for the full rev table and rationale.**

The lesson: surface radius is NEVER picked by surface-family taste — it's DERIVED from the inner Button tier and the surface padding via §2.1.2. Re-tiering the inner Button (a §1.4.3 customization lever per §1.4) reflows the outer chrome through the cascade; the system math doesn't fight the consumer's tier choice.

**a11y wiring**:
- Textarea / Input: `aria-label="Message"`; `placeholder` doubles as visible hint
- Icon-only Buttons (leading + trailing + send): each carries an explicit `aria-label` describing the action ("Attach file", "Voice input", "Send")
- Model-picker Select: standard Select a11y (`aria-haspopup="listbox"`, `aria-expanded`)
- Disabled state: `disabled` attribute on Textarea/Input + the `is-disabled` class which `pointer-events: none`s the inner controls

**Keyboard contract**:
- Tab moves focus through interactive elements (leading buttons → textarea → trailing buttons → model picker → send button)
- Enter submits (sends the message) — consumer-handled
- Shift+Enter inserts a newline in the textarea — native behavior
- Esc clears focus from the textarea — native behavior
- Disabled controls are skipped in the Tab order

**Variants documented** (simplified May 17, 2026 — see §B.22 for rationale):
- default — single frame showing all four states side-by-side (default / focused / error / disabled) at `--double` layout with 1 leading button + model-picker "Model" Select + primary send button
- sizes — single frame showing S / M / L tiers
- layouts — single frame showing `--double` and `--single` side-by-side
- (no separate states or variants sections — the slot permutations were removed because they were enumerating "the same slot rendered N ways" without adding pedagogical value)

**Cross-system equivalents**:

| Product | Pattern | Layout |
|---|---|---|
| **Claude** | Multi-line textarea + leading cluster + trailing model picker + send | `--double` (canonical reference) |
| ChatGPT (web) | Multi-line + few leading attachments + send | `--double` |
| ChatGPT (mobile / inline) | Single-line input + send | `--single` |
| Cursor chat | Multi-line + leading attachments + model picker + send | `--double` |
| Gemini | Multi-line + leading attachments + voice + send | `--double` |

**When NOT to use Composer**:
- Single-purpose text input (search box, email field, comment box) → use `Input` directly
- Multi-line text input without action affordances → use `Textarea` directly
- Multi-line text input with separated buttons below (not joined to the field) → use a `Field` wrapper with `Textarea` + a button row underneath

### 9.29 Table — tabular data display

Table presents rows × columns of structured data with a tinted header row, ruled cell separators, and consistent row rhythm across three density tiers. It is the system's general-purpose data primitive — what consumers reach for when a list of records (orders, users, transactions, log lines, pricing rules) needs to render with sortable / scannable columns, optional row selection, and the ability to host any system primitive (Input, Select, Badge, Avatar, Button) inside a cell.

The docs site has historically used Table to render token-value-purpose reference tables (`.ds-table--tvp`, `.ds-table--type`). After the May 2026 promotion + unification, the legacy docs tables share the new product-table primitive's chrome — same 12/12 symmetric padding, same `radius-l` (20) outer radius, same `surface/quiet` header — and the `--tvp` / `--type` class names survive only for layout-axis overrides (top-aligned cells, column ratios), not chrome. The product-table primitive is **the same component family at every size tier**, with S/M/L differentiated only by the type tier and slot-level utilities for actions / selection / numeric columns.

**Cross-system equivalents** (so we know which patterns we're inheriting and which we're departing from):

| Role | Ours | Primer | shadcn / Radix | Atlassian | Material 3 | Polaris (Shopify) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Static data display | **`Table`** | `Table` | `Table` | `Table` (dynamic + lite) | `Table` (data) | `Table` |
| Selectable rows | `Table` `kind="select"` column | `Table` row-selection | `DataTable` `RowSelection` | `Table` `selectable` | `Table` (data) | `IndexTable` |
| Editable cells | `Table` cell hosts `Input` / `Select` / `Combobox` | `Table` cell | shadcn-table custom cell | `EditableTable` | (n/a, custom) | `IndexTable` + inline-edit pattern |
| Sortable columns | (future) `Table` header click handler + caret affordance | `Table` `sort` | `DataTable` sortable | `Table` sortable | `Table` sortable | `IndexTable` sortable |
| Full data grid (virtualized + lazy-load + drag-reorder columns) | (out of scope; this is the DataGrid territory) | (separate library) | (community) | (separate `DataGrid`) | (separate `DataGrid`) | `IndexTable` (covers most cases) |

Our Table sits squarely in the **`Table` (not DataGrid)** lane — same scope as Primer `Table`, shadcn `Table`, Atlassian `Table`, Polaris `IndexTable`. Virtualization, drag-reorder, and column resize live in a future DataGrid component if a real consumer ships them; the current primitive covers the common product cases (volume-pricing rules, settings rows, transaction lists, user grids) without paying for the heavier abstractions.

**Sizes — one canonical padding across every variant; only the L tier bumps the type tier**:

| Tier | Cell padding (V / H) | Font / LH | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| `s` | `space-m` (12) / `space-m` (12) | `body/s` (12/16) | Ultra-dense — settings rows, log lines, dashboards with many records visible at once |
| **`m`** (default) | **`space-m` (12) / `space-m` (12)** | **`body/s` (12/16)** | **Default — product tables (pricing rules, transactions, items)** |
| `l` | `space-m` (12) / `space-m` (12) | `body/m` (14/20) | Spacious data surfaces — comparison tables, where breathing comes from the type tier bump (14/20 vs 12/16), not from a padding tier bump |

**Legacy docs tables** (`.ds-table--tvp`, `.ds-table--type`) **share the canonical 12/12 padding** — they no longer override. The class names are kept for back-compat layout overrides only (`table-layout: auto` for `--type`, column-width ratios for `--tvp`); padding is unified system-wide. **Vertical alignment is `top` for both `<th>` and `<td>` across every variant** as of the May 2026 audit — see "Body cells" below for the rationale.

**Why 12/12 symmetric across every tier**: padding is unified across S/M/L (size differentiation lives in the type tier — S/M at `body/s`, L at `body/m`) per the user's "padding should be consistent" directive. V = H (12/12) instead of case-B asymmetric because cells routinely contain hard-edged sub-elements (Badge, Avatar, Button, Input, Select) where §6.1.2's priority rule says hard-edged corner-symmetric inset wins over text H>V breathing — and cell content is short labels / numeric values, not prose, so the case-B "line-box provides V breathing" rationale carries less weight inside cells than inside Toast / Banner. Table now joins Card and Dialog in the "primary container surfaces use 12/12" family (Toast / Banner / Tooltip are the separate uniform-8/8 family per §6.1.5 — smaller overlay/in-flow surfaces, tighter uniform padding). T-bar positioning auto-inherits the unified tokens. See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.6 for the pre-promotion 3-pair → 8V/12H case-B → current 12/12 rev history and industry alignment.

#### Anatomy

```html
<div class="ds-table-section ds-table-section--product">
 <table class="ds-table ds-table--m ds-table--interactive">
 <colgroup>
 <col style="width: 32px"> <!-- select column -->
 <col> <!-- data columns -->
 ...
 </colgroup>
 <thead>
 <tr>
 <th class="ds-table__cell--select"><!-- master checkbox --></th>
 <th>Column 1</th>
 <th class="ds-table__cell--num">Column 3</th>
 </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody>
 <tr><td>...</td>...</tr>
 <tr class="is-selected">...</tr>
 <tr class="is-disabled">...</tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
```

**Section container** (`.ds-table-section`) — outer rounded surface that clips the table. Owns: `border-radius` (`--tbl-rd`, **`radius-l` (20) across every variant — the "primary container surfaces" family shared with Card and Dialog per §2.1.1**, derived per the §2.1.2 inverse rule for the canonical container-with-S-tier-cells case), `1px solid border/subtle` Job C definition stroke, `surface/default` bg, `overflow: hidden` to clip inner corners. No additional radius on the `<table>` itself — the section IS the rounded surface. The `.ds-table-section--product` modifier is a back-compat no-op for radius. See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.6 for the pre-May-2026 `radius-l` (product) / `radius-xl` (docs) split and the unification onto a single `radius-l` (20).

**Table** (`.ds-table`) — `width: 100%`, `border-collapse: collapse`, `table-layout: fixed` so column widths from `<colgroup>` are honored and long content wraps rather than expanding the column. `background: transparent` so the section's bg shows through (avoids double-painting).

**Header row** (`.ds-table th`) — `background: var(--color-surface-quiet)` (= `neutral/50` light / `neutral/1000` dark). The tint anchors the header as "column labels, not data." Foreground `text/primary` weight 400 (header gets prominence from surface tint + type role, not weight per §5.1.1). Padding tracks `--tbl-py` / `--tbl-px`. Bottom `1px border/subtle` separates header from first data row; right `1px border/subtle` between header cells creates vertical column dividers (with `:last-child { border-right: 0 }` keeping the right edge clean). The header uses `surface/quiet` rather than the heavier `surface/subtle` to read calm at rest without compromising the 11 other components that need `surface/subtle`'s 0.050 ΔL for active-state contrast — see §3.1 for the token-split rationale.

**Body cells** (`.ds-table td`) — same padding tokens as `th`, `text/primary` text, **`vertical-align: top` system-wide** (matches `<th>`). Same `border-right` / `border-bottom` ruling as the header; `:last-child` clears the right edge per row, `tbody tr:last-child td` clears the bottom edge of the last row. Fully ruled grid (vertical + horizontal dividers) rather than horizontal-only — pricing-rule and form-style data benefit from "each cell is independently scannable" reinforcement, and legacy docs tables already used vertical dividers. shadcn / Atlassian / Material 3 share the gridded approach; Polaris / Linear / Vercel use horizontal-only — both stances are valid, we sit with the gridded camp.

**Why `vertical-align: top` everywhere, not `middle`** (May 2026 audit): a previous rev defaulted `td` to `middle` for the single-line case and overrode to `top` only inside `--tvp` / `--type` docs tables. The middle-default broke as soon as any row contained mixed-height content — a single cell wrapping to 2-3 lines (long token name, multi-sentence description) center-aligned against a short single-line cell next door, producing visible vertical drift where the short cells appeared to "float" at the visual center of the row instead of aligning to its top edge. The legacy docs tables already needed `top` for the same reason, so the rule was inverted: **default to `top`, never override**. Single-line rows are unaffected because there's only one line to align; multi-line rows now read as crisp left-justified text blocks regardless of which column wraps. Industry alignment: shadcn / Atlassian / Linear / Vercel all default their data-table cells to top-aligned for the same reason — `vertical-align: middle` is the HTML `<td>` browser default but reads wrong as soon as data has any height variance. Right-aligned cells (`.ds-table__cell--num`, `.ds-table__cell--actions`) still use `text-align: right`: the base `.ds-table td` rule deliberately does NOT set `text-align`, so `<td>`'s browser default of `left` applies to most cells while the column-kind classes override on the cells that need right alignment. (An earlier version of this rule added `text-align: left` to `.ds-table td` — that broke right-aligned columns because `.ds-table td` (0,1,1 specificity) outranks `.ds-table__cell--num` (0,1,0); fixed by removing the redundant declaration and letting the browser default carry left alignment.)

#### States (matrix)

| State | Treatment |
|---|---|
| **Default row** | `text/primary` text, transparent row bg (section's `surface/default` shows through) |
| **Hover** (when `.ds-table--interactive` is applied) | Row bg → `action/hover` (excluded when `.is-selected` or `.is-disabled`) |
| **Selected** (`.is-selected` on `<tr>`) | Row bg → `feedback/info/bg` (light blue tint matching the validation-info family); text stays `text/primary`. Selection is conceptually a "this row is marked for an action you're about to take" — a multi-select / pending-action signal, which is why it uses the `info` tint family rather than the neutral `action/hover` chrome that single-position state (Nav active, Pagination active) uses. The blue here is forward-looking toward the reserved `selection/*` token family (§3.1) that a future Table multi-select feature will adopt. |
| **Disabled** (`.is-disabled` on `<tr>`) | All text → `text/disabled`, `cursor: not-allowed`, `pointer-events: none` so hover and click are suppressed; row bg stays transparent (the muted text + dimmed cursor carry the disabled signal — adding a `surface/sunken` row fill would compete visually with the alternating row idiom many consumers reach for) |
| **Transition** | `transition: background .15s ease` on `<tr>` so hover and selected states cross-fade |

**`interactive` is opt-in** (`.ds-table--interactive`) because many tables present read-only data (token reference, billing summary, comparison) where hover would imply rows are clickable. Polaris / Linear use the same opt-in approach. **Selected row uses `feedback/info/bg`** (light blue tint) not `action/hover` neutral — a selected table row is "marked for a pending/multi-select action" (same `info` tint family as Toast `info` tone, forward-looking toward the reserved `selection/*` family in §3.1) rather than "the current single position" (Nav active / Pagination active use neutral `action/hover` + inset ring). shadcn (`bg-muted` neutral) is the lone outlier; Linear / Atlassian / Polaris all use blue-tint selection. Note: the brand-color discipline (§3.1) keeps single-position state neutral, but multi-select "marked" rows are a different role — the info tint signals "you've flagged these," distinct from both neutral single-position and brand value-commitment.

#### Column kinds (slot utilities)

Column-level classes auto-apply to both the `<th>` and every `<td>` in that column. Set via `kind` in the `tableEl` helper or by adding the class manually:

| Column kind | Class | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `select` | `.ds-table__cell--select` | `width: 1%` (hugs content); `padding-left: var(--tbl-px)` + `padding-right: 0` so the trailing edge sits flush against the first data column's left padding (creates a uniform inset around the checkbox / radio inside) |
| `actions` | `.ds-table__cell--actions` | `text-align: right`; `width: 1%; white-space: nowrap` (hugs content, no wrap); `padding-left: space-2xs (4)` + `padding-right: space-2xs (4)` so trailing icon-button clusters sit close to the row's right edge — combined with `.ds-table__actions` (an `inline-flex` wrapper inside the cell with `gap: space-2xs (4)` and `justify-content: flex-end`) the action buttons read as a tight right-anchored cluster |
| Numeric | `.ds-table__cell--num` (or `align: "right"` on a column) | `text-align: right`; `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` so numbers line up on their decimal point across rows |

**Affix marker** — a `.ds-table__affix` `<span>` is the canonical wrapper for prefix glyphs inside a numeric cell (e.g., `$` before `100.00`, `%` after a percent). Styled at `text/tertiary` color + `space-2xs` (4) `margin-right` so the affix reads as quiet chrome and the value reads as the data. Mirrors the Input prefix / suffix convention (§Input) but living inline inside a cell. Pattern:

```html
<td class="ds-table__cell--num">
 <span class="ds-table__affix">$</span>100.00
</td>
```

#### Sticky header

For tables with many rows that scroll under a pinned header:

```html
<div class="ds-table-section">
 <div class="ds-table-scroll" style="max-height: 240px">
 <table class="ds-table ds-table--m ds-table--sticky">
 ...
 </table>
 </div>
</div>
```

`.ds-table-scroll` is a `max-height: 240px; overflow: auto; border-radius: inherit` wrapper that turns the table's body into a scroll container; `.ds-table--sticky` applies `position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: var(--z-content, 10)` to `<thead> th` so the header stays pinned while the body scrolls. `border-radius: inherit` on the scroll wrapper preserves the section's rounded corners. `--z-content` (10) sits above the table body but below anchored overlays (Dropdown / Select panel / Tooltip at `--z-anchored` 40), so a cell-anchored Dropdown floats above the sticky header — the correct stacking order.

#### Embedding system primitives inside cells

Tables routinely host other system primitives:

- **Checkbox in a `kind="select"` column** — leading checkbox per row + master checkbox in the header. Tab-traverses naturally through the cell tab order; `aria-label` on each checkbox names what it selects ("Select row 1", "Select all rows").
- **Badge** for status indicators — use `Badge S` (20-tall, fits inside the 32-tall S-tier cell or 40-tall M-tier cell with comfortable vertical breathing). Tone (`success`, `info`, `warning`, `danger`) carries the status semantics.
- **Avatar** for entity rows — `Avatar S` (28) in S-tier tables, `Avatar M` (32) in M-tier; pair with name text in the same cell using `display: inline-flex` and `gap: space-s (8)`.
- **Input + Select + Button** for editable cells — typically `size: "s"` (28-tall) to fit comfortably in M-tier rows without inflating row height beyond 44px (= 28 control + 8/8 padding). The pricing-rule pattern (Input + Input + Input + remove button in a row) is the canonical "inline editing" use case this primitive was promoted for.
- **Dropdown / Action menu trigger** for row-level actions — `Button XS icon-only ghost-neutral` containing `ICON_DOTS_THREE` (kebab) in an `actions` column; opens a Dropdown menu with row-specific actions (Edit, Duplicate, Delete).

#### a11y wiring

- Native `<table>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>`, `<tr>`, `<th>`, `<td>` — the semantic HTML carries the table model for screen readers without any ARIA. The first row of `<thead>` `<th>` cells is automatically announced as column headers; the first `<th scope="row">` in a row would be announced as a row header (we don't currently use row headers in any demo, but the spec supports it).
- **Master select checkbox** in the header gets `aria-label="Select all"` + a state of `indeterminate` when SOME but not all rows are selected (a standard tri-state pattern — checked / unchecked / indeterminate).
- **Per-row select checkboxes** get `aria-label="Select row"` (or a more specific label if the row identity is announceable, e.g., `"Select row for John Smith"`).
- **Row actions** (icon-only buttons in the actions column) carry their own `aria-label` per the icon-only-button rule (§14.3): `aria-label="Edit row"`, `aria-label="Remove row"`, `aria-label="More actions"`.
- **Disabled rows** get `aria-disabled="true"` on the `<tr>` so screen readers announce the disabled state (the `pointer-events: none` CSS doesn't reach a11y on its own).
- **Sticky header** doesn't need additional ARIA — `position: sticky` is purely visual and doesn't break the table's semantic model.

#### Keyboard contract

- Tab moves focus into and through interactive content inside cells (checkboxes, buttons, inputs) in DOM order. Tables themselves are NOT focusable; only the controls inside their cells are.
- Arrow-key navigation between cells (the DataGrid pattern) is OUT OF SCOPE for this primitive. When a consumer needs grid-style keyboard navigation, that's a DataGrid feature, not a Table feature.
- Space toggles the focused checkbox.
- Enter activates the focused button.
- Standard `:focus-visible` two-layer halo lights up around any focused cell content per §4.2.

#### Variants (canonical demos in §11)

| Demo | What it shows |
|---|---|
| Default | Three-column M-tier table with interactive hover |
| Sizes (S / M / L) | All three tiers stacked in one frame so cell-padding and font-tier differences are directly comparable |
| States — Default / Selected / Disabled | Per-row state escalation; `.is-selected` and `.is-disabled` modifiers |
| Selectable rows | Leading `kind="select"` column with master checkbox in header + per-row checkboxes; second row selected |
| Numeric columns | Right-aligned `kind="select"` columns with `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` and `.ds-table__affix` prefix glyph |
| With badges | Status indicators (`Badge S` in `success` / `info` / `warning` / `danger` tones) inside a column |
| With controls | Inputs inline-edited per row + trailing `kind="actions"` column with `Button XS icon-only ghost-neutral` remove buttons — the canonical "pricing rules" pattern from the build conversation |
| Sticky header | 12-row table inside a `ds-table-scroll` wrapper with `max-height: 240px`; header stays pinned while body scrolls |

#### When NOT to use Table

- **Single record's worth of data** (a customer's profile, a settings panel for one entity) → use a `Field` stack or a `Card` with key-value pairs, not Table.
- **Hierarchical / nested data** (a file tree, a category-and-subcategory list) → use a tree primitive (not yet built) or `Nav vertical` with collapsible groups. Tables flatten data; trees preserve hierarchy.
- **Sortable + filterable + virtualized data grid** (thousands of rows, drag-reorder columns, frozen first column) → out of scope. A future `DataGrid` component will handle that; Table is for the up-to-hundred-rows case where the native scroll viewport handles performance.
- **A list with rich per-item content blocks** (a feed of posts, a product gallery) → use a `Card` grid or list. Table's gridded chrome is the wrong idiom for content-heavy list items.

### 9.30 Navigation — vertical + horizontal placements

Navigation moves the user between destinations. It ships as **three placements** sharing the Navigation role: **Vertical** (sidebar / nested-group rows), **Horizontal** (inline page-section nav), and **Bottom** (the persistent mobile bar, documented separately in §9.21 because its stacked icon-above-label layout is a distinct primitive). Vertical + Horizontal share the `.ds-nav-item` row primitive; the cross-cutting rules:

- **Nav items ARE list-of-rows** — Vertical and Horizontal both consume the §9.3 list-of-rows state matrix (default / hover / pressed / focus / active / disabled), the per-edge inset formula (§6.1.2 / §9.3), and the nested-radius rule (§2.1.2). They are NOT a parallel implementation; the playground's own sidebar (`.nav-leaf`, `.nav-node__title`, `.nav-node__link`) MUST share this matrix (see §2.2.1 "update all consumers").
- **Active state = `action/hover` bg + inset 1px `border/default` ring** — the canonical selected-row treatment (§9.3 / §4.5.1). **No brand color** — Nav active is neutral, per the §3.1 brand-color discipline (a current page is "where you are," not "the one chosen action"). This is the deliberate asymmetry called out in the brand-color audit: Date Picker selected uses brand (a chosen value), Nav active does NOT (a location).
- **No weight bump on active** — hierarchy comes from the bg fill + inset ring, not from weight 500 (§5.1.1).
- **Sizing**: Vertical is "one step denser" (§2.4) — Nav M = Button S dimensions (28-tall), S=24, L=32, XL=40, with 12/16 font through L and the body-tier bump only at XL. Horizontal matches the Button ramp exactly (XS–XL).
- **Hover foreground change is a sanctioned §4.3.1 exception** — inactive items shift `text/secondary → text/primary` on hover (the muted inactive item invites the click; same justification as Tabs and Bottom Nav). Foreground stability holds everywhere else.
- **Trailing elements** (badge, chevron, kbd) follow the §9.3 per-edge inset formulas; nested badges/chevrons snap to `radius-3xs` (4) per the §2.1.2 strict concentric rule when the row is at `radius-xs` (8) with a 4px inset.
- **a11y**: container `<nav role="navigation" aria-label="…">`; items `<a>` or `<button>`; active item `aria-current="page"`; disclosure rows that expand nested groups get `aria-expanded`; disabled items `aria-disabled="true"`.

