## Part 12 — Next Components to Build

Based on cross-system research (Primer, shadcn/Radix, Atlassian, Material 3), the next components in priority order. Components shipped in May 2026 — Chip, Skeleton, Spinner, Progress, Banner, Tabs, Avatar, Card, Separator, Combobox, Composer, Date Picker, Color Picker, Drop Zone, and Table — are now in Part 11.

### Tier 1 — Build next (overlay surfaces)
1. **Drawer / Sheet** — Side-anchored modal counterpart to Dialog. Reuses Dialog's `--shadow-4` + scrim, swaps centered → edge-anchored layout, supports left/right/bottom anchors. Will share Dialog a11y wiring (focus trap, ESC, `role="dialog"`).
2. **Popover** — Anchored, non-modal, non-list overlay (richer content than Tooltip, less than Drawer). Reuses Dialog + Dropdown chrome at the panel level; trigger is any Button; no auto-dismiss timer.

### Tier 2 — Build after (data primitives + content)
3. **DataGrid** — Virtualized + lazy-loading + drag-reorder columns + arrow-key cell navigation + frozen columns. Out of scope of the `Table` primitive (§9.29) which covers the up-to-hundred-rows case; DataGrid is the heavier abstraction for thousands-of-rows enterprise data. Integrates with Pagination + Combobox.
4. **Tag / Lozenge** — Subtle status indicator distinct from Chip (read-only) and Badge (smaller). Evaluate whether the visual language needs a third label primitive or if Badge tonal variants cover the use cases.
5. **Accordion / Disclosure** — Expandable content section. Will share Tabs' a11y wiring at the trigger level.

### 12.1 Forward-looking differentiation rules — capture before building

These rules MUST be honored when each component ships, so that the new component slots into the existing visual language without ambiguity. They are written here to commit the design before implementation pressure tempts shortcuts.

#### Tabs vs Segmented Control — the underbar-vs-fill rule (non-negotiable)

Tabs and Segmented Control are both **horizontal groups of triggers** and would be visually identical without intentional differentiation. Industry convergence is unambiguous on the differentiator: **active indicator type AND track surface**.

| Pattern | Active indicator | Hover indicator | Track surface | Trigger radius | Role |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Tabs** | 3px rounded **neutral** underbar (`::after`, `radius-2xs`, indented by `--tab-px`) in `text/primary` (near-black — brand dropped in the May 2026 brand-color audit; see §9.11) | **Same underbar shape** in `border/default` (gray) — *no backplate* | Transparent (no track), no list `border-bottom`, triggers separated by `space-s` (8) gap | All four corners `radius-xs` (8) | Navigate between content panels — switching changes WHAT IS SHOWN below |
| **Segmented Control** | Filled background pill (active item gets `surface/default` fill + `shadow-1`) | Subtle bg on the trigger | Tonal track (`surface/quiet` bg, `border/subtle` inset stroke — moved from `surface/subtle` in May 2026; see §9.23) | Fully rounded inside the track (`radius-2s` 10 at M) | Pick a value — switching changes a SETTING (view mode, sort order, time range) |

This split exists in every modern system: shadcn (Tabs underline `variant="line"` / Toggle filled — note that shadcn's *default* "Tabs" is actually segmented and they have an open issue #8839 to rename it), Material 3 (Tabs primary/secondary both underline / Segmented Button), Atlassian (Tabs underline / SegmentedControl pill), Apple HIG (Tabs underline / SegmentedControl pill), Primer (UnderlineNav / SegmentedControl). When building Tabs, follow this convention; do NOT invent a third hybrid (filled-pill Tabs as the default flavor, or underlined Segmented Control). The semantic split between **navigation** (Tabs — "go somewhere") and **value-pick** (Segmented Control — "set something") MUST track the visual split.

**Why the hover indicator is an underbar in `border/default`, not a backplate fill** — and why this is the system-wide call. An earlier rev briefly tried `action/hover` background pill for hover (top-rounded `border-radius: 8 8 0 0`, "browser tab silhouette," with the list carrying a `border/subtle` bottom hairline that the active bar overpainted). User testing of the hover→active transition surfaced two real problems:

1. **The eye reads two different shapes.** Hover = pill, active = bar. Transitioning between them feels like "different state, different visual idiom" instead of "one indicator escalating from neutral → brand." Continuity matters more than visual interest at this scale.
2. **Backplate hover blurs §12.1's split.** The whole differentiator is "Tabs use a bar, Segmented Control uses a fill." If hover Tabs use a fill, even temporarily, the visual system has to be parsed twice: "is this a Tab whose hover is filling, or a Segmented Control whose unselected state is shaped like a tab?" Eliminating the hover backplate keeps the answer one-glance obvious.

The current treatment uses **the same `::after` shape for both hover and active**, with only the color changing (`border/default` gray → `text/primary` near-black). One indicator, two neutral shades. Hover previews a gray bar; click darkens it to near-black. No visual idiom switch, and no brand color — the underbar dropped brand in the May 2026 brand-color audit (Tabs are navigation, not value-selection; see §9.11).

Industry on this is more split than the broader bar-vs-fill question (shadcn / Atlassian backplate-hover; Linear / Vercel / Stripe / older Apple HIG underbar-hover). We sit with the underbar camp because it produces a cleaner three-way differentiation across our horizontal-trigger families:

- **Segmented Control** — contained tonal track + filled active pill + `shadow-1`. *Raised pill on a track.*
- **Tabs** — transparent track + rounded underbar active + same-shape gray underbar hover. *Bar under text.*

Tabs default tier is **M = 32**, matching Segmented Control's M and Button's M, so the three families compose naturally in adjacent toolbars.

**Future filled-Tabs flavor — deferred.** A `style="filled"` Tabs variant (filled active pill, no underbar) is plausible for projects where a softer aesthetic is wanted under the navigation role. It's not in the base system today because (a) it overlaps Segmented Control's filled-pill identity and forces a cross-component grammar reread at small surface widths, and (b) the base system stance is "one canonical default, variants only when a real consumer ships" (§2.2.1). When a real consumer needs it, build it as an explicit `style` knob alongside the underline default; until then, Tabs has one visual treatment and Segmented Control owns filled-pill.

#### Banner vs Toast — shared chrome, layout + behavior differentiates

Banner and Toast share **identical chrome** — same `surface/default` bg for neutral, same `feedback/{tone}/bg` + `feedback/{tone}/border` for tonal, same `text/{tone}` icon, same `border/subtle` neutral border. Differentiation lives entirely in **layout + behavior**:

| Property | Toast | Banner |
|---|---|---|
| Neutral bg | `surface/default` | `surface/default` (same) |
| Tonal bg | `feedback/{tone}/bg` | `feedback/{tone}/bg` (same) |
| Border | `border/subtle` neutral / `feedback/{tone}/border` tonal | Same as Toast |
| **Position** | Floating overlay, anchored to viewport edge | Inline in document flow |
| **Width** | `min-w 280 / max-w 360` | `width: 100%` of its container |
| **Persistence** | Ephemeral — auto-dismisses (4–6s) | Persistent — only user/programmatic close |
| **Shadow** | `var(--shadow-3)` | None |
| **Entry motion** | Animated slide-in (`var(--motion-medium) var(--easing-out)`) | None — appears with parent |
| **Layer** | `var(--z-toast, 80)` | `var(--z-content, 10)` or base |
| **Stacking** | Multiple in a stack | Singleton per surface |

**Why shared chrome, not differentiated**: most modern systems (shadcn / Sonner / modern Vercel / Chakra / Primer) share chrome between Toast and Banner; Material 3 is the only major outlier (Android tradition, dark Snackbar vs light Banner). We follow the dominant modern pattern because (a) Toast and Banner are never seen side-by-side in production (one floats at the viewport corner, one sits in flow at full width), and (b) the "they look identical in static docs" observation only applies to the docs grid view — a presentation artifact, not a system flaw. **See `design-system-history.mdc` §B.1 for the full cross-system audit and the two dropped Banner revisions (stripe pattern, forced-different bg).**

**Default radius is `radius-m` (16) — `--full-bleed` is the modifier**, not the other way around. Across the industry, contained-with-radius is the dominant default (Primer Banner, Atlassian SectionMessage, Vercel Note); edge-to-edge is for system-level top-of-screen banners (rare — Atlassian Banner top-of-screen, Vercel Project Banner).

**Same severity tokens.** A `Banner danger` and a `Toast danger` use the same `feedback/danger/bg`, the same `feedback/danger/border`, and the same `text/danger` for the icon — no chrome divergence anywhere.

**Same icon anatomy.** Both use the `--{comp}-lh` letterbox icon-wrapper pattern (§6.1.3) with `ICON_CIRCLE` as the universal placeholder per Standing Rule #8.

**Same action structure (with one tier asymmetry).** Inline action button (`Button soft-neutral S` or `Button soft-{tone} S`) at the right; close affordance as `Button ghost-neutral XS icon-only` if present — close is XS (24-tall) NOT S, matching Toast / Dialog close and industry consensus (Material 3 / Atlassian / Primer / shadcn all run banner close at 24 or smaller). The S→XS step between action and close is the same role-asymmetric pattern Toast uses; the actions cluster uses `align-items: center` so the height difference reads naturally.

**Banner-specific deviations** to Toast spec:
- No animated entry — Banners appear with their parent surface
- Optional **expanded/multiline content** zone below the title for longer-form messages
- `role="status"` for advisory severities, `role="alert"` for danger; same as Toast a11y wiring
- `--full-bleed` modifier drops left/right borders (viewport edges carry those sides) and keeps top + bottom borders for separation from adjacent in-flow content

#### Chip vs Badge — covered by §3.5.1, restated for completeness

- **Both Chip and Badge are rounded-rectangle** at `radius-xs` (8) — neither uses pill. Differentiation comes from **stroke + behavior**, not shape.
- **Chip is interactive** (filter on/off, autocomplete entry, multi-select tag): uses Button-style chrome (1px `border/default` Job A affordance + hover/focus/pressed states), typically 24–28 tall (Button XS/S). Selected state shows **neutral `action/hover` fill + `border/default` stroke + `text/primary` label**, with the **leading checkmark as the sole brand-colored element** (`text/brand`) — the brand fill + `selection/border` stroke approach was dropped in the May 2026 brand-color audit so that brand reads as a small confirming accent, not a full chromatic fill (see §9.17 Chip and §3.1 brand-color discipline). Removable variant adds trailing dismiss `<button>`.
- **Badge is read-only** (status indicator, count): borderless (per §3.5.1 — fill carries the boundary across all tones), no hover/focus, `<span>` element. 20–24 tall.
- Side-by-side, an interactive Chip and a read-only Badge are visually distinguished by stroke presence (Chip has it, Badge doesn't) and by behavior (Chip responds to hover/click, Badge is inert). **Do not reach for "make Chip pill because it looks cuter" or "make Badge pill because it's a label" — both break the system's shape language (§3.5.1) which reserves pill for shape-coded primitives like Switch and notification dots.**

#### Chip vs all single-select horizontal patterns (Segmented Control / Tabs / Horizontal Nav / Bottom Nav) — the cardinality rule (non-negotiable)

The **only** pattern in the system that supports multi-select is Chip. Everything else (Segmented Control, Tabs, Horizontal Nav, Bottom Nav) is single-select with a persistent value. This rule MUST hold across the system; pulling Chip into single-select land or pulling any nav pattern into multi-select land breaks the user's mental model of "what kind of control is this."

| Pattern | Cardinality | Track | Active indicator | Role |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Chip** | **Multi-select** (or zero-select) — items independent | None — gap-separated | Neutral `action/hover` fill + `border/default` stroke + `text/brand` leading check (the check is the only brand element) | Filter/tag content **within** a page |
| Segmented Control | Single-select (one always set) | Tonal track wrapping items | Filled raised pill + `shadow-1` | Pick a VALUE (setting, view mode, sort) |
| Tabs | Single-select (one always active) | Transparent, no hairline divider | 3px neutral `text/primary` underbar | Switch content panels |
| Horizontal Nav | Single-select (one current page) | None or chrome container | bg-highlight | Navigate to a destination |
| Bottom Nav (mobile) | Single-select (one root view) | Default: full-width white, no top border, with per-item `radius-l` (20) backplates (stacked horizontal-nav idiom). `--floating` modifier: `surface/default` pill track with pill items | Backplate-driven: `action/hover` bg + inset 1px `border/default` ring + `text/primary` color (matches Nav vertical/horizontal active per §9.3 / §4.5.1) | Navigate root mobile views |

**The non-negotiable: cardinality first.** A consumer choosing between Chip and Segmented Control should ask "can the user pick zero or many?" — if yes → Chip; if exactly one → Segmented Control. Same for Chip-vs-Tabs. The visual idiom (track presence, active indicator type) MUST track the cardinality decision. Don't ship a Chip that can only have one selected (use Segmented Control) and don't ship Tabs that can have many active (use Chip + content panels).

**Secondary differentiator: track presence + chrome.** Chips are independent items in flow with `gap` separation. Segmented Control has a contained tonal track that visually groups items as "peer options sharing one state." Tabs share a transparent track with no hairline divider (the active underbar carries the row's bottom edge). Bottom Nav (default) is full-width edge-anchored with no top border — items distribute evenly inside small outer padding, each with its own `radius-l` (20) backplate; the position (bottom of viewport) + the "row of stacked-content backplates" carries the "this is mobile root nav" identity. The track-vs-edge-anchored distinction reinforces "value-pick widget" (Segmented) vs "navigation skeleton" (Bottom Nav).

**Tertiary differentiator: active-indicator language.** Chip's leading check is the canonical filter-on affordance (Material 3 outlined Filter Chip / Linear / Notion). Segmented uses raised pill. Tabs use a neutral `text/primary` underbar. Nav vertical / horizontal / Bottom all use bg-highlight + inset 1px `border/default` ring (the canonical selected-row treatment from §9.3 / §4.5.1, unified across all three Nav placements). The visual idioms don't overlap; if a consumer picks the wrong indicator language, the chrome contradicts the cardinality and the user gets a mixed message.

**Worked example — picking the right pattern**:
- "Filter results by tag" → multi-select → **Chip** (zero or many tags can be active)
- "Sort by date / popularity / price" → single-select → **Segmented Control** (one sort is always applied)
- "View as grid / list" → single-select → **Segmented Control** (one view at a time)
- "Switch between Overview / Activity / Settings tabs" → single-select content panels → **Tabs**
- "Navigate to Home / Search / Profile" (mobile root) → single-select destinations → **Bottom Nav**
- "Apply a discount code, with chips showing currently applied codes" → multi-select with dismiss → **Chip removable**

**Industry alignment**: this is the dominant pattern across every modern system. Material 3 explicitly separates "Chips" (multi-select filter) from "Segmented buttons" (single-select setting) from "Navigation bar" (single-select destinations). shadcn keeps Toggle (multi) separate from Tabs / NavigationMenu. Apple HIG does the same with Tags vs SegmentedControl vs TabBar. The cardinality rule is universal.

#### Future XS-tier additions (when these consumers ship)

The 5-tier ramp (XS=24, S=28, M=32, L=40, XL=48) was deliberately exposed across **every** size-tiered control so a fully dense product can use the system end-to-end. As of May 2026, XS is implemented for: Button, Input, Select, Textarea, Segmented Control, Nav horizontal, Checkbox/Radio/Switch row, **Avatar** (24/28/32/40/48 round + square), **Combobox** (inherits Input ramp), **Pagination** (24/28/32/40 — XL not exposed because Pagination at 48 reads as oversized for a navigation row, but the underlying Button XL is available if a consumer ever needs it). Components NOT at XS by design:
- **Banner** — persistent surface, not a small-tier control; XS doesn't apply.
- **Card** — layout primitive, not a height-tiered control; XS doesn't apply. (An earlier `--spacious` modifier scaled padding/radius up; removed May 2026 — the **tone** axis covers feature-promo emphasis better than re-padding does.)
- **Separator** — 1px hairline; tiers don't apply.
- **Tabs** — currently S/M/L only because the 3px neutral underbar reads poorly below 28px height. If a future XS tabs tier is needed (e.g., for ultra-dense data tables), the indicator may need to shrink to 2px + a higher-contrast neutral to stay legible.
- **Chip** — shipped with XS=24 / S=28 (matches Button XS/S); see Part 11.
- **RadioCard** — card-family component, not a height-tiered control; XS doesn't apply.

### Building instructions for new components
1. Read this spec completely
2. Look at the most recently built component (Toast) as the template
3. Define CSS custom properties following the `--{short}-{property}` pattern
4. Implement all states in the mandatory order
5. Use only semantic tokens for all colors
6. Follow the hover treatment table exactly for the component's surface type
7. Use the two-layer focus ring
8. Build the `{name}El(opts)` helper and `render{Name}()` function
9. Register in NAV and sectionIds
10. Run through the quality checklist
11. Honor the differentiation rules in §12.1 if applicable

